(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqMag=[{pn:1,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:"Let B\u2081 be the magnitude of magnetic field at the centre of a circular coil of radius R carrying current I. Let B\u2082 be the magnitude of magnetic field at an axial distance x from the centre. For x : R = 3 : 4, B\u2082/B\u2081 is",o:["64 : 125","4 : 5","16 : 25","25 : 16"],c:0,s:`At the centre of a loop, B\u2081 = \u03BC\u2080I/(2R).
On the axis at distance x, B\u2082 = \u03BC\u2080IR\xB2/[2(R\xB2 + x\xB2)^(3/2)].
Dividing, B\u2082/B\u2081 = R\xB3/(R\xB2 + x\xB2)^(3/2).
x : R = 3 : 4, so take x = 3k and R = 4k.
R\xB2 + x\xB2 = 16k\xB2 + 9k\xB2 = 25k\xB2, and (25k\xB2)^(3/2) = 125k\xB3.
B\u2082/B\u2081 = 64k\xB3/125k\xB3 = 64/125.
So the ratio is 64 : 125, option (a).`},{pn:2,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mamp",st:"Solenoid and toroid",q:"A solenoid having area A and length l is filled with a material having relative permeability 2. The magnetic energy stored in the solenoid is",o:["(B\xB2Al)/(\u03BC\u2080)","(B\xB2Al)/(4\u03BC\u2080)","B\xB2Al","(B\xB2Al)/(2\u03BC\u2080)"],c:1,s:`Magnetic energy per unit volume in a medium of permeability \u03BC is B\xB2/(2\u03BC).
Here \u03BC = \u03BC_r \u03BC\u2080 = 2\u03BC\u2080.
So the energy density is B\xB2/(2 \xD7 2\u03BC\u2080) = B\xB2/(4\u03BC\u2080).
The volume of the solenoid is A l.
Energy = (B\xB2/4\u03BC\u2080) \xD7 A l = B\xB2Al/(4\u03BC\u2080).
This is option (b).`},{pn:3,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbs",st:"Superposition and symmetry",q:"Figure shows a current carrying square loop ABCD of edge length \u2018a\u2019 lying in a plane. If the resistance of the ABC part is r and that of ADC part is 2r, then the magnitude of the resultant magnetic field at centre of the square loop is",o:["(3\u03C0\u03BC\u2080I)/(\u221A2 a)","(\u221A2\u03BC\u2080I)/(3\u03C0a)","(2\u03BC\u2080I)/(3\u03C0a)","(\u03BC\u2080I)/(2\u03C0a)"],c:1,s:`The two paths ABC and ADC are in parallel, so the current divides in the inverse ratio of resistance: i\u2081 : i\u2082 = 2r : r = 2 : 1.
So i\u2081 = 2I/3 (through ABC) and i\u2082 = I/3 (through ADC).
The centre is at distance a/2 from each side. One side gives B = (\u03BC\u2080i)/(4\u03C0\xB7a/2) \xD7 (sin45\xB0 + sin45\xB0) = \u221A2\u03BC\u2080i/(2\u03C0a).
Each path has two sides, so path ABC gives 2 \xD7 \u221A2\u03BC\u2080i\u2081/(2\u03C0a) = \u221A2\u03BC\u2080i\u2081/(\u03C0a); path ADC gives \u221A2\u03BC\u2080i\u2082/(\u03C0a).
Seen from the centre, i\u2081 goes round one way and i\u2082 the other way, so their fields are opposite and subtract.
B = \u221A2\u03BC\u2080(i\u2081 \u2212 i\u2082)/(\u03C0a) = \u221A2\u03BC\u2080(I/3)/(\u03C0a) = \u221A2\u03BC\u2080I/(3\u03C0a).
This is option (b).`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Square loop ABCD drawn as a diamond with A on the left, B at the top, C on the right and D at the bottom; current I enters at A and leaves at C; i1 flows through ABC and i2 through ADC; side length a; the centre is marked by a dot",d:[["l",30,100,110,100,"a"],["l",230,100,310,100,"a"],["l",110,100,170,40],["l",170,40,230,100],["l",230,100,170,160],["l",170,160,110,100],["c",170,100,2.5,"F"],["l",136,74,144,66,"a"],["l",196,66,204,74,"a"],["l",136,126,144,134,"a"],["l",196,134,204,126,"a"],["t",60,92,"I","m"],["t",280,92,"I","m"],["t",98,118,"A","e"],["t",170,30,"B","m"],["t",242,118,"C"],["t",170,176,"D","m"],["t",122,62,"i\u2081","e"],["t",122,148,"i\u2082","e"],["t",212,60,"a"]]}},{pn:4,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mamp",st:"Solenoid and toroid",q:"If B is magnetic field and \u03BC\u2080 is permeability of free space, then the dimensions of (B/\u03BC\u2080) is",o:["ML\xB2T\u207B\xB2A\u207B\xB9","MT\u207B\xB2A\u207B\xB9","LT\u207B\xB2A\u207B\xB9","L\u207B\xB9A"],c:3,s:`Inside a solenoid B = \u03BC\u2080nI, so B/\u03BC\u2080 = nI.
n is the number of turns per unit length, with dimension L\u207B\xB9 (turns have no dimension).
I has dimension A.
So B/\u03BC\u2080 has dimension L\u207B\xB9A.
This is option (d).
Note: B/\u03BC\u2080 is the magnetic intensity H, measured in A/m, which is the same thing.`},{pn:5,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mamp",st:"Ampere's law",q:"N equally spaced charges each of value q are placed on a circle of radius R. The circle rotates about its axis with an angular velocity \u03C9 as shown in the figure. A bigger Amperian loop B encloses the whole circle whereas a smaller Amperian loop A encloses a small segment. The difference between enclosed currents, I_A \u2212 I_B, for the given Amperian loops is",o:["(N)/(\u03C0) q\u03C9","(2\u03C0)/(N) q\u03C9","(N)/(2\u03C0) q\u03C9","(N\xB2)/(2\u03C0) q\u03C9"],c:2,s:`The rotating charges form a ring current. In one turn, time T = 2\u03C0/\u03C9, the total charge Nq goes round.
So the current in the ring is I = Nq/T = Nq\u03C9/(2\u03C0).
Ampere's law counts the current that crosses the surface bounded by the loop.
Loop A encloses only a small segment of the ring, so the ring crosses its surface once: I_A = Nq\u03C9/(2\u03C0).
Loop B encloses the whole circle. The ring crosses its surface twice, once in and once out, with opposite signs, so I_B = 0.
I_A \u2212 I_B = Nq\u03C9/(2\u03C0).
This is option (c).`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"A ring of equally spaced charges drawn in perspective with radius R and angular velocity omega; a tall Amperian loop B surrounds the whole ring and a small loop A encircles one small segment of the ring",d:[["p","M70,100 A100,50 0 1 1 270,100 A100,50 0 1 1 70,100"],["c",270,100,3,"F"],["c",241,135,3,"F"],["c",170,150,3,"F"],["c",99,135,3,"F"],["c",70,100,3,"F"],["c",99,65,3,"F"],["c",170,50,3,"F"],["c",241,65,3,"F"],["p","M130,18 A22,82 0 1 1 130,182 A22,82 0 1 1 130,18"],["p","M162,150 A8,12 0 1 1 178,150 A8,12 0 1 1 162,150"],["l",170,100,250,72,"a"],["t",212,98,"R"],["p","M158,98 A11,11 0 1 1 166,110","a"],["t",146,104,"\u03C9","e"],["t",142,12,"B"],["t",188,172,"A"]]}},{pn:6,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mamp",st:"Ampere's law",q:"A long straight wire of a circular cross-section with radius \u2018a\u2019 carries a steady current I. The current I is uniformly distributed across this cross-section. The plot of magnitude of magnetic field B with distance r from the centre of the wire is given by",o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:0,s:`Use Ampere's law with a circular loop of radius r around the axis.
Inside (r < a) the loop encloses only a fraction r\xB2/a\xB2 of I, so B \xD7 2\u03C0r = \u03BC\u2080I r\xB2/a\xB2 and B = \u03BC\u2080Ir/(2\u03C0a\xB2). B grows in a straight line with r from 0.
At the surface (r = a) B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0a), the largest value.
Outside (r > a) the loop encloses all of I, so B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0r), which falls like 1/r.
So the graph rises linearly from zero up to r = a and then decays as 1/r, with a peak at r = a.
This is graph (a).`,fig:{w:380,h:270,alt:"Four graphs of B against r. (a) straight line rising from zero to a peak at r equals a, then a falling curve. (b) flat line up to r equals a, then a falling curve. (c) nothing inside, a vertical jump at r equals a and then a falling curve. (d) a falling curve starting at B0 with B0 over e marked at r equals a",d:[["l",36,110,176,110,"a"],["l",36,110,36,48,"a"],["t",42,54,"B","s"],["t",179,114,"r","s"],["t",91,124,"a","ms"],["t",6,62,"(a)","s"],["l",36,110,91,60],["p","M91,60 C101,90 121,102 169,106"],["l",91,60,91,110,"d"],["l",206,110,346,110,"a"],["l",206,110,206,48,"a"],["t",212,54,"B","s"],["t",349,114,"r","s"],["t",261,124,"a","ms"],["t",176,62,"(b)","s"],["l",206,60,261,60],["p","M261,60 C271,90 291,102 339,106"],["l",261,60,261,110,"d"],["l",36,240,176,240,"a"],["l",36,240,36,178,"a"],["t",42,184,"B","s"],["t",179,244,"r","s"],["t",91,254,"a","ms"],["t",6,192,"(c)","s"],["l",91,190,91,240],["p","M91,190 C101,220 121,232 169,236"],["l",206,240,346,240,"a"],["l",206,240,206,178,"a"],["t",212,184,"B","s"],["t",349,244,"r","s"],["t",261,254,"a","ms"],["t",176,192,"(d)","s"],["c",206,188,2.5,"F"],["t",212,184,"B\u2080","s"],["p","M206,188 C231,206 246,216 261,220 S301,230 339,236"],["l",206,220,261,220,"d"],["l",261,220,261,240,"d"],["t",202,224,"B\u2080/e","es"]]}},{pn:7,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 28 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",q:"An infinite wire has a circular bend of radius a, and carrying a current I as shown in figure. The magnitude of magnetic field at the origin O of the arc is given by",o:["(\u03BC\u2080I)/(4\u03C0a) [3\u03C0/2 + 2]","(\u03BC\u2080I)/(4\u03C0a) [3\u03C0/2 + 1]","(\u03BC\u2080I)/(2\u03C0a) [\u03C0/2 + 2]","(\u03BC\u2080I)/(4\u03C0a) [\u03C0/2 + 1]"],c:1,s:`Split the wire into three parts: the circular arc and the two straight semi-infinite wires.
The arc is 3/4 of a circle, so B_arc = (3/4)(\u03BC\u2080I/2a) = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(4\u03C0a) \xD7 (3\u03C0/2).
The lower straight wire ends right below O at distance a, so B = \u03BC\u2080I/(4\u03C0a) (a semi-infinite wire seen from the foot of the perpendicular). Its direction is the same as the arc's (into the page).
The upper straight wire lies along the line that passes through O, so every element has dl parallel to r and it gives zero field.
Note: dB = \u03BC\u2080I dl \xD7 r\u0302/(4\u03C0r\xB2) is zero when dl is along r\u0302, so a wire that points at the point gives no field.
B = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(4\u03C0a) [3\u03C0/2 + 1].
This is option (b).`,fig:{w:350,h:180,alt:"Wire with a three-quarter circular bend of radius a centred at O. A straight wire along the line through O carries current I out to the right, and another straight wire a distance a below O carries current I in from the right",d:[["p","M100,135 A50,50 0 1 1 150,85"],["l",150,85,280,85,"a"],["l",280,85,305,85,"d"],["l",300,135,100,135],["l",300,135,325,135,"d"],["l",230,135,210,135,"a"],["l",100,85,150,85,"d"],["l",100,85,100,135,"d"],["c",100,85,2.5,"F"],["l",50,88,50,80,"a"],["t",92,80,"O","e"],["t",125,79,"a","m"],["t",108,114,"a"],["t",225,76,"I","m"],["t",230,152,"I","m"],["t",330,88,"\u221E","m"],["t",330,138,"\u221E","m"]]}},{pn:8,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mamp",st:"Ampere's law",q:"Consider a long straight wire of a circular cross-section (radius a) carrying a steady current I. The current is uniformly distributed across this cross-section. The distances from the centre of the wire's cross-section at which the magnetic field [inside the wire, outside the wire] is half of the maximum possible magnetic field, anywhere due to the wire, will be",o:["[a/2, 3a]","[a/4, 2a]","[a/4, 3a/2]","[a/2, 2a]"],c:3,s:`The field is largest at the surface r = a: B_max = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0a).
Inside, B = \u03BC\u2080Ir/(2\u03C0a\xB2). Set it equal to half of B_max: r/a\xB2 = 1/(2a), so r = a/2.
Outside, B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0r). Set it equal to half of B_max: 1/r = 1/(2a), so r = 2a.
So the distances are a/2 (inside) and 2a (outside).
This is option (d).`},{pn:9,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mamp",st:"Ampere's law",q:"In a co-axial straight cable, the central conductor and the outer conductor carry equal currents in opposite directions. The magnetic field is zero",o:["inside the outer conductor.","inside the inner conductor.","outside the cable.","in between the two conductors."],c:2,s:`Take a circular Amperian loop coaxial with the cable and use \u222EB\xB7dl = \u03BC\u2080I_enclosed.
Outside the cable the loop encloses the inner current I and the outer current \u2212I, so the total is zero.
By symmetry B is the same all round the loop, so B \xD7 2\u03C0r = 0 and B = 0.
Between the conductors the loop encloses only I, and inside the inner conductor it encloses a part of I, so B is not zero there.
Inside the outer conductor the loop encloses I minus a part of I, which is not zero either.
So the field is zero outside the cable, option (c).`},{pn:10,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",q:"An element \u0394l = \u0394x \xEE is placed at the origin and carries a large current I = 10 A. The magnetic field on the y-axis at a distance of 0.5 m from the element \u0394x of 1 cm length is",o:["12 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 T","4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 T","10 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 T","8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 T"],c:1,s:`Use the Biot\u2013Savart law for a short element: dB = (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0) I \u0394l sin\u03B8 / r\xB2.
The point is on the y-axis, perpendicular to the element, so \u03B8 = 90\xB0 and sin\u03B8 = 1.
dB = 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 10 \xD7 0.01 / (0.5)\xB2 = 10\u207B\u2078 / 0.25.
dB = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 T.
This is option (b).`,fig:{w:340,h:150,alt:"A short current element of length delta x lying on the x axis at the origin; point P is on the y axis, 0.5 m above the element",d:[["l",30,100,110,100],["r",110,82,90,36],["l",200,100,300,100,"a"],["t",306,104,"x"],["l",155,82,155,22,"a"],["t",161,18,"y"],["c",155,40,2.5,"F"],["t",146,44,"P","e"],["l",75,42,75,98,"b"],["t",68,74,"0.5 m","e"],["l",110,126,200,126,"b"],["l",110,120,110,134,"d"],["l",200,120,200,134,"d"],["t",155,144,"\u0394x","m"]]}},{pn:11,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mamp",st:"Ampere's law",q:"A long straight wire of radius a carries a steady current I. The current is uniformly distributed across its cross-section. The ratio of the magnetic field at a/2 and 2a from axis of the wire is",o:["1 : 1","3 : 4","4 : 1","1 : 4"],c:0,s:`Inside the wire (r < a): B = \u03BC\u2080Ir/(2\u03C0a\xB2). At r = a/2, B\u2081 = \u03BC\u2080I/(4\u03C0a).
Outside the wire (r > a): B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0r). At r = 2a, B\u2082 = \u03BC\u2080I/(4\u03C0a).
Both are equal to \u03BC\u2080I/(4\u03C0a).
So B\u2081 : B\u2082 = 1 : 1, option (a).`},{pn:12,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbs",st:"Superposition and symmetry",q:"Two insulated circular loops A and B of radius \u2018a\u2019 carrying a current of \u2018I\u2019 in the anti clockwise direction as shown in the figure. The magnitude of the magnetic induction at the centre will be",o:["(\u03BC\u2080I)/(2a)","(2\u03BC\u2080I)/(a)","(\u221A2\u03BC\u2080I)/(a)","(\u03BC\u2080I)/(\u221A2 a)"],c:3,s:`The two loops have the same centre and their planes are perpendicular, so their fields at O are perpendicular to each other.
Each loop gives B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2a) at its centre.
Perpendicular fields add as vectors: B_net = \u221A(B\xB2 + B\xB2) = \u221A2 B.
B_net = \u221A2 \u03BC\u2080I/(2a) = \u03BC\u2080I/(\u221A2 a).
This is option (d).`,fig:{w:260,h:200,alt:"Two circular loops A and B of the same radius a with a common centre O, drawn in perspective with their planes perpendicular, both carrying current I anticlockwise",d:[["p","M60,100 A70,28 0 1 1 200,100 A70,28 0 1 1 60,100"],["p","M130,30 A28,70 0 1 1 130,170 A28,70 0 1 1 130,30"],["c",130,100,2.5,"F"],["t",138,94,"O"],["l",200,104,200,96,"a"],["l",158,104,158,96,"a"],["t",212,124,"A"],["t",140,24,"B"]]}},{pn:13,y:"JEE Advanced 2024",tp:"mamp",st:"Ampere's law",q:"An infinitely long wire, located on the z-axis, carries a current I along the +z-direction and produces the magnetic field B. The magnitude of the line integral \u222BB\xB7dl along a straight line from the point (\u2212\u221A3a, a, 0) to (a, a, 0) is given by [\u03BC\u2080 is the magnetic permeability of free space.]",o:["7\u03BC\u2080I/24","7\u03BC\u2080I/12","\u03BC\u2080I/8","\u03BC\u2080I/6"],c:0,s:`The field circles the wire: B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0r) along the direction of increasing angle \u03C6. So B\xB7dl = (\u03BC\u2080I/2\u03C0) d\u03C6, because r d\u03C6 is the part of dl along the circle.
Hence the line integral only depends on how much the angle \u03C6 changes between the two points, whatever the path.
The first point (\u2212\u221A3a, a) has tan\u03C6 = a/(\u2212\u221A3a), so \u03C6 = 150\xB0. The last point (a, a) has \u03C6 = 45\xB0.
The angle changes by 150\xB0 \u2212 45\xB0 = 105\xB0 = 7\u03C0/12 (the line is traced from left to right, so the magnitude is taken).
\u222BB\xB7dl = (\u03BC\u2080I/2\u03C0) \xD7 7\u03C0/12 = 7\u03BC\u2080I/24.
This is option (a).
Note: for a full turn the same formula gives (\u03BC\u2080I/2\u03C0) \xD7 2\u03C0 = \u03BC\u2080I, which is Ampere's law.`,sfig:{w:300,h:190,alt:"Axes x and y with the wire at the origin; the horizontal line y equals a from point P1 at minus root 3 a, a to point P2 at a, a; the angle of P1 is 150 degrees and of P2 is 45 degrees from the x axis",d:[["l",30,140,270,140,"a"],["l",150,175,150,15,"a"],["t",274,144,"x"],["t",156,20,"y"],["l",63,90,200,90,"B"],["l",150,140,63,90,"d"],["l",150,140,200,90,"d"],["c",150,140,3,"F"],["t",156,156,"O (wire)"],["t",63,80,"P\u2081 (\u2212\u221A3a, a)","m"],["t",200,80,"P\u2082 (a, a)","m"],["p","M185,140 A35,35 0 0 0 119.7,122.5"],["t",110,128,"150\xB0","e"],["p","M175,140 A25,25 0 0 0 167.7,122.3"],["t",190,134,"45\xB0","s"]]}},{pn:14,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mamp",st:"Ampere's law",q:"A long straight wire of circular cross-section (radius a) is carrying steady current I. The current I is uniformly distributed across this cross-section. The magnetic field is",o:["zero in the region r < a and inversely proportional to r in the region r > a.","uniform in the region r < a and inversely proportional to distance r from the axis, in the region r > a.","directly proportional to r in the region r < a and inversely proportional to r in the region r > a.","inversely proportional to r in the region r < a and uniform throughout in the region r > a."],c:2,s:`Apply Ampere's law to a circle of radius r around the axis.
For r < a the enclosed current is I r\xB2/a\xB2, so B \xD7 2\u03C0r = \u03BC\u2080I r\xB2/a\xB2 and B = \u03BC\u2080Ir/(2\u03C0a\xB2), directly proportional to r.
For r > a all of I is enclosed, so B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0r), inversely proportional to r.
This is option (c).`},{pn:15,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",q:"Find the magnetic field at the point P in figure. The curved portion is a semicircle connected to two long straight wires.",o:["(\u03BC\u2080i)/(2r) (1 + 1/\u03C0)","(\u03BC\u2080i)/(2r) (1 + 2/\u03C0)","(\u03BC\u2080i)/(2r) (1/2 + 1/\u03C0)","(\u03BC\u2080i)/(2r) (1/2 + 1/(2\u03C0))"],c:3,s:`P is the centre of the semicircle. Add the fields of the three parts.
Semicircle: B\u2081 = (1/2)(\u03BC\u2080i/2r) = \u03BC\u2080i/(4r).
The horizontal wire ends directly above P at distance r (semi-infinite wire seen from the foot of the perpendicular): B\u2082 = \u03BC\u2080i/(4\u03C0r). Its direction is out of the page (current goes left over P, then anticlockwise round the arc), the same as B\u2081.
The vertical wire lies along the line through P, so it gives zero field.
B = \u03BC\u2080i/(4r) + \u03BC\u2080i/(4\u03C0r) = (\u03BC\u2080i/2r)(1/2 + 1/2\u03C0).
This is option (d).`,fig:{w:230,h:200,alt:"Current i enters from the right along a horizontal wire, goes round a semicircle of radius r whose centre is P, and leaves downward along a vertical straight wire; r is drawn from P to the arc",d:[["l",210,50,100,50],["l",160,50,140,50,"a"],["t",172,42,"i"],["p","M100,50 A45,45 0 0 0 100,140"],["l",100,140,100,190],["l",100,160,100,178,"a"],["c",100,95,2.5,"F"],["t",108,92,"P"],["l",100,95,68,127],["t",76,104,"r","e"]]}},{pn:16,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",q:"As shown in the figure, a long straight conductor with semi-circular arc of radius \u03C0/10 m is carrying current I = 3 A. The magnitude of the magnetic field at the centre O of the arc is (The permeability of the vacuum = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 NA\u207B\xB2)",o:["6 \u03BCT","4 \u03BCT","3 \u03BCT","1 \u03BCT"],c:2,s:`The straight parts lie along the line through O, so they give no field at O.
Only the semicircle contributes: B = (1/2)(\u03BC\u2080I/2R) = \u03BC\u2080I/(4R).
R = \u03C0/10 m, so B = (4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 3)/(4 \xD7 \u03C0/10).
The \u03C0 and 4 cancel: B = 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 3 \xD7 10 = 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 T.
B = 3 \u03BCT, option (c).`,fig:{w:340,h:160,alt:"A long straight conductor carrying 3 A that has a semicircular arc bump of radius pi over 10 metres; O is the centre of the arc and a radius is drawn to the arc",d:[["l",20,110,100,110],["l",20,110,70,110,"a"],["p","M100,110 A45,45 0 0 1 190,110"],["l",190,110,310,110],["l",250,110,300,110,"a"],["c",145,110,2.5,"F"],["l",145,110,164,69,"a"],["t",145,128,"O","m"],["t",50,128,"I = 3 A","m"],["t",280,128,"I = 3 A","m"]]}},{pn:17,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:"A circular loop of radius r is carrying current I A. The ratio of magnetic field at the center of circular loop and at a distance r from the center of the loop on its axis is",o:["1 : 3\u221A2","2\u221A2 : 1","3\u221A2 : 2","1 : \u221A2"],c:1,s:`At the centre: B_c = \u03BC\u2080I/(2r).
On the axis at x = r: B = \u03BC\u2080Ir\xB2/[2(r\xB2 + r\xB2)^(3/2)] = \u03BC\u2080Ir\xB2/(2 \xD7 2\u221A2 r\xB3) = \u03BC\u2080I/(4\u221A2 r).
Ratio B_c : B = [\u03BC\u2080I/(2r)] : [\u03BC\u2080I/(4\u221A2 r)] = 4\u221A2/2 = 2\u221A2.
So the ratio is 2\u221A2 : 1, option (b).`},{pn:18,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mamp",st:"Solenoid and toroid",q:"A long solenoid is formed by winding 70 turns cm\u207B\xB9. If 2.0 A current flows, then the magnetic field produced inside the solenoid is ______. (\u03BC\u2080 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 TmA\u207B\xB9)",o:["88 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T","1232 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T","352 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T","176 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T"],c:3,s:`Inside a long solenoid, B = \u03BC\u2080nI, where n is the number of turns per metre.
n = 70 turns/cm = 7000 turns/m.
B = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 7000 \xD7 2.0 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 1.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 T.
4\u03C0 \u2248 12.57, so B \u2248 17.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 T = 176 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T.
This is option (d).`},{pn:19,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:"A single current carrying loop of wire carrying current I flowing in anticlockwise direction seen from +ve z direction and lying in xy plane is shown in figure. The plot of \u0135 component of magnetic field (B_y) at a distance \u2018a\u2019 (less than radius of the coil) and on yz plane vs z coordinate looks like",o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:3,s:`The loop is in the xy plane, so its axis is the z-axis and the main field on the axis points along +z (anticlockwise current).
B_y at (0, a, z) is the sideways (radial) part of the field, since the point is above the axis in the y direction.
Field lines bend outward at z > 0 (they spread out as they leave the loop) and bend inward at z < 0 (they come back in). So B_y > 0 for z > 0 and B_y < 0 for z < 0.
At z = 0 the field is purely along z, so B_y = 0. Far away (large |z|) the field dies down, so B_y \u2192 0.
Check with \u2207\xB7B = 0: B_y \u2248 \u2212(a/2) dB_z/dz. B_z is largest at z = 0 and falls on both sides, so dB_z/dz is negative for z > 0 and positive for z < 0.
So the graph is odd: negative, then 0 at the origin, then positive, then tending to 0 on both ends.
This is graph (d).`,fig:{w:340,h:400,alt:"A circular loop in the xy plane with the z axis horizontal, y vertical and x toward the viewer, and four graphs of B_y at (0, a, z) against z. (a) two humps meeting at zero at the origin. (b) a flat-topped hump. (c) a sharp peak at the origin. (d) an odd curve, negative for negative z and positive for positive z",d:[["l",170,110,170,15,"a"],["l",170,110,320,110,"a"],["l",170,110,105,165,"a"],["t",178,20,"y"],["t",324,114,"z"],["t",96,174,"x"],["p","M170,40 A22,70 0 1 1 170,180 A22,70 0 1 1 170,40"],["t",200,130,"(0, 0, 0)","s"],["l",100,70,300,70,"d"],["l",100,72,100,108,"b"],["t",92,94,"a","e"],["l",100,110,170,110,"d"],["t",54,218,"B"],["t",63,221,"y","s"],["t",69,218,"(0, a, z)"],["l",20,270,150,270,"a"],["l",85,300,85,224],["t",154,274,"z","s"],["p","M27,267 C40,260 47,232 60,232 C70,232 77,255 85,270 C93,255 100,232 110,232 C123,232 130,260 143,267"],["t",2,274,"(a)","s"],["t",224,218,"B"],["t",233,221,"y","s"],["t",239,218,"(0, a, z)"],["l",190,270,320,270,"a"],["l",255,300,255,224],["t",324,274,"z","s"],["p","M197,267 C210,265 217,232 233,230 L277,230 C293,232 300,265 313,267"],["t",172,274,"(b)","s"],["t",54,308,"B"],["t",63,311,"y","s"],["t",69,308,"(0, a, z)"],["l",20,360,150,360,"a"],["l",85,390,85,314],["t",154,364,"z","s"],["p","M27,357 C55,356 73,340 85,318 C97,340 115,356 143,357"],["t",2,364,"(c)","s"],["t",224,308,"B"],["t",233,311,"y","s"],["t",239,308,"(0, a, z)"],["l",190,360,320,360,"a"],["l",255,390,255,314],["t",324,364,"z","s"],["p","M197,363 C210,364 217,390 230,396 C240,400 247,375 255,360 C263,345 270,320 280,324 C293,330 300,356 313,357"],["t",172,364,"(d)","s"]]}},{pn:20,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:"The electric current in a circular coil of four turns produces a magnetic induction 32 T at its centre. The coil is unwound and is rewound into a circular coil of single turn, the magnetic induction at the centre of the coil by the same current will be",o:["16 T","8 T","4 T","2 T"],c:3,s:`For a coil of N turns and radius r, B = N\u03BC\u2080I/(2r).
The wire length stays the same: 4 \xD7 2\u03C0r = 2\u03C0R, so the new radius is R = 4r.
New field B\u2032 = \u03BC\u2080I/(2R) = \u03BC\u2080I/(8r).
Old field B = 4\u03BC\u2080I/(2r) = 2\u03BC\u2080I/r = 32 T.
B\u2032/B = [1/(8r)] / [2/r] = 1/16.
B\u2032 = 32/16 = 2 T, option (d).`},{pn:21,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbs",st:"Superposition and symmetry",q:"As shown in the figure, a current of 2 A flowing in an equilateral triangle of side 4\u221A3 cm. The magnetic field at the centroid O of the triangle is (Neglect the effect of earth's magnetic field)",o:["4\u221A3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T","\u221A3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T","4\u221A3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T","3\u221A3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T"],c:3,s:`The centroid is at the same distance d from all three sides, and each side gives a field in the same direction (out of the page, because the 2 A current is anticlockwise), so we add three equal fields.
For an equilateral triangle d = side/(2\u221A3) = 4\u221A3/(2\u221A3) = 2 cm = 0.02 m.
For one finite wire, B = (\u03BC\u2080I/4\u03C0d)(sin\u03B8\u2081 + sin\u03B8\u2082). Each end of a side makes 60\xB0 with the perpendicular from O, so B = (\u03BC\u2080I/4\u03C0d)(2 sin60\xB0) = (\u03BC\u2080I/4\u03C0d)\u221A3.
B\u2081 = 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 2 \xD7 \u221A3 / 0.02 = \u221A3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T.
Total B = 3B\u2081 = 3\u221A3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T.
This is option (d).`,fig:{w:300,h:200,alt:"An equilateral triangle carrying 2 A anticlockwise: along the bottom to the right, up the right side and down the left side; the centroid O is marked inside",d:[["l",150,16,60,172],["l",60,172,240,172],["l",240,172,150,16],["c",150,118,2.5,"F"],["t",158,122,"O"],["l",140,172,160,172,"a"],["l",198,100,192,90,"a"],["l",108,88,102,98,"a"],["t",84,96,"2 A","e"],["t",206,96,"2 A"],["t",150,192,"2 A","m"]]}},{pn:22,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:"A long conducting wire having a current I flowing through it. It is bent into a circular coil of N turns. Then it is bent into a circular coil of n turns. The magnetic field is calculated at the centre of coils in both the cases. The ratio of the magnetic field in first case to that of second case is",o:["n : N","N : n","N\xB2 : n\xB2","n\xB2 : N\xB2"],c:2,s:`Let the wire length be L. With N turns, 2\u03C0RN = L, so R = L/(2\u03C0N).
The field at the centre is B = N\u03BC\u2080I/(2R) = N\u03BC\u2080I \xD7 2\u03C0N/(2L) = \u03C0\u03BC\u2080IN\xB2/L.
So B is proportional to N\xB2 when the wire length and current are fixed.
B\u2081 : B\u2082 = N\xB2 : n\xB2.
This is option (c).`},{pn:23,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:"The electric current in a circular coil of 2 turns produces a magnetic induction B\u2081 at its centre. The coil is unwound and is rewound into a circular coil of 5 turns and the same current produces a magnetic induction B\u2082 at its centre. The ratio of B\u2082/B\u2081 is",o:["5/2","25/4","5/4","25/2"],c:1,s:`For a fixed length of wire, the radius of an N-turn coil is R = L/(2\u03C0N).
The centre field B = N\u03BC\u2080I/(2R) = \u03C0\u03BC\u2080IN\xB2/L, so B is proportional to N\xB2.
B\u2082/B\u2081 = 5\xB2/2\xB2 = 25/4.
This is option (b).`},{pn:24,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:"B_X and B_Y are the magnetic field at the centre of two coils X and Y respectively, each carrying equal current. If coil X has 200 turns and 20 cm radius and coil Y has 400 turns and 20 cm radius, the ratio of B_X and B_Y is",o:["1 : 1","1 : 2","2 : 1","4 : 1"],c:1,s:`The field at the centre of a coil is B = N\u03BC\u2080I/(2R).
Both coils have the same current and the same radius (20 cm), so B is proportional to the number of turns N.
B_X : B_Y = 200 : 400 = 1 : 2.
This is option (b).`},{pn:25,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:"The magnetic field at the center of current carrying circular loop is B\u2081. The magnetic field at a distance of \u221A3 times radius of the given circular loop from the center on its axis is B\u2082. The value of B\u2081/B\u2082 will be",o:["9 : 4","12 : \u221A5","8 : 1","5 : \u221A3"],c:2,s:`The field at the centre of a loop of radius R is B\u2081 = \u03BC\u2080I/(2R).
On the axis at distance x from the centre, B = \u03BC\u2080IR\xB2/[2(R\xB2 + x\xB2)^(3/2)].
Here x = \u221A3R, so R\xB2 + x\xB2 = R\xB2 + 3R\xB2 = 4R\xB2.
(4R\xB2)^(3/2) = 8R\xB3, so B\u2082 = \u03BC\u2080IR\xB2/(2 \xD7 8R\xB3) = \u03BC\u2080I/(16R).
B\u2081/B\u2082 = [\u03BC\u2080I/(2R)] \xF7 [\u03BC\u2080I/(16R)] = 8.
So B\u2081 : B\u2082 = 8 : 1, which is option (c).`},{pn:26,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:"The magnetic field at the centre of a circular coil of radius r, due to current I flowing through it is B. The magnetic field at a point along the axis at a distance r/2 from the centre is",o:["B/2","2B","(2/\u221A5)\xB3 B","(2/\u221A3)\xB3 B"],c:2,s:`At the centre B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2r).
On the axis at distance x: B\u2032 = \u03BC\u2080Ir\xB2/[2(r\xB2 + x\xB2)^(3/2)].
Put x = r/2: r\xB2 + r\xB2/4 = 5r\xB2/4.
(5r\xB2/4)^(3/2) = (\u221A5/2)\xB3 r\xB3.
B\u2032 = \u03BC\u2080Ir\xB2/[2 (\u221A5/2)\xB3 r\xB3] = [\u03BC\u2080I/(2r)] \xD7 (2/\u221A5)\xB3.
So B\u2032 = (2/\u221A5)\xB3 B, which is option (c).`},{pn:27,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mamp",st:"Ampere's law",q:"A long straight wire with a circular cross-section having radius R, is carrying a steady current I. The current I is uniformly distributed across this cross-section. Then the variation of magnetic field due to current I with distance r (r < R) from its centre will be",o:["B \u221D r\xB2","B \u221D r","B \u221D 1/r\xB2","B \u221D 1/r"],c:1,s:`Use Ampere's law on a circle of radius r < R around the axis: \u222EB\xB7dl = \u03BC\u2080 I_enclosed.
The left side is B \xD7 2\u03C0r.
The current is spread uniformly, so the part inside radius r is I_enc = I \xD7 (\u03C0r\xB2)/(\u03C0R\xB2) = Ir\xB2/R\xB2.
B \xD7 2\u03C0r = \u03BC\u2080Ir\xB2/R\xB2, so B = \u03BC\u2080Ir/(2\u03C0R\xB2).
Inside the wire B is proportional to r, which is option (b).`},{pn:28,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mamp",st:"Solenoid and toroid",q:"A long solenoid carrying a current produces a magnetic field B along its axis. If the current is doubled and the number of turns per cm is halved, the new value of magnetic field will be equal to",o:["B","2B","4B","B/2"],c:0,s:`Inside a long solenoid B = \u03BC\u2080nI, where n is the number of turns per unit length.
Current becomes 2I and n becomes n/2.
New field B\u2032 = \u03BC\u2080 \xD7 (n/2) \xD7 (2I) = \u03BC\u2080nI.
So B\u2032 = B, which is option (a).`},{pn:29,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mamp",st:"Ampere's law",q:"An infinitely long hollow conducting cylinder with radius R carries a uniform current along its surface. Choose the correct representation of magnetic field (B) as a function of radial distance (r) from the axis of cylinder.",o:["(a)","(b)","(c)","(d)"],c:3,s:`Apply Ampere's law on a circle of radius r around the axis.
For r < R the circle encloses no current (it is all on the surface), so B = 0 inside.
For r > R the circle encloses the whole current I, so B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0r), which falls as 1/r.
At r = R the field jumps from 0 to its largest value \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0R) and then decreases.
Graph (d) shows zero up to R and then a 1/r fall, so the answer is (d).
Note: the field jumps at R because the current sheet is infinitely thin; a thick wire would show a smooth rise.`,fig:{w:390,h:260,alt:"Four graphs of B against r: (a) rises linearly to R then stays constant; (b) rises linearly to R then falls as 1/r; (c) constant up to R then falls; (d) zero up to R, then jumps and falls as 1/r",d:[["l",40,110,40,25,"a"],["l",40,110,165,110,"a"],["t",34,29,"B","e"],["t",171,114,"r"],["t",32,124,"O","e"],["t",10,70,"(a)","m"],["l",95,110,95,50,"d"],["t",95,124,"R","m"],["p","M40 110 L95 50 H160"],["l",225,110,225,25,"a"],["l",225,110,350,110,"a"],["t",219,29,"B","e"],["t",356,114,"r"],["t",217,124,"O","e"],["t",195,70,"(b)","m"],["l",280,110,280,50,"d"],["t",280,124,"R","m"],["p","M225 110 L280 50"],["p","M280 50 C292 76 316 92 345 98"],["l",40,235,40,150,"a"],["l",40,235,165,235,"a"],["t",34,154,"B","e"],["t",171,239,"r"],["t",32,249,"O","e"],["t",10,195,"(c)","m"],["l",95,235,95,187,"d"],["t",95,249,"R","m"],["p","M40 187 H95 V197"],["p","M95 197 C107 223 131 219 160 225"],["l",225,235,225,150,"a"],["l",225,235,350,235,"a"],["t",219,154,"B","e"],["t",356,239,"r"],["t",217,249,"O","e"],["t",195,195,"(d)","m"],["l",280,235,280,180,"d"],["t",280,249,"R","m"],["p","M280 180 C292 206 316 219 345 225"]]}},{pn:30,y:"JEE Advanced 2022",tp:"mbs",st:"Superposition and symmetry",q:"Which one of the following options represents the magnetic field B at O due to the current flowing in the given wire segments lying on the xy plane?",o:["B = \u2212(\u03BC\u2080I/L)(3/2 + 1/(4\u221A2\u03C0)) k\u0302","B = \u2212(\u03BC\u2080I/L)(3/2 + 1/(2\u221A2\u03C0)) k\u0302","B = \u2212(\u03BC\u2080I/L)(1 + 1/(4\u221A2\u03C0)) k\u0302","B = \u2212(\u03BC\u2080I/L)(1 + 1/(4\u03C0)) k\u0302"],c:2,s:`Use the Biot\u2013Savart law piece by piece. A straight piece whose line passes through O gives zero field at O.
The horizontal piece of length L/2, the short radial piece and the last vertical wire all lie on lines through O, so each gives zero.
Left vertical wire: length L, perpendicular distance L from O, its top end is level with O. B = (\u03BC\u2080I/4\u03C0L)(sin 45\xB0 \u2212 0) = \u03BC\u2080I/(4\u221A2\u03C0L), into the page.
Big semicircle, radius L/2: B = \xBD \xD7 \u03BC\u2080I/(2R) = \u03BC\u2080I/(4R) = \u03BC\u2080I/(2L), into the page (current is clockwise).
Quarter circle, radius L/4: B = \xBC \xD7 \u03BC\u2080I/(2R) = \u03BC\u2080I/(8R) = \u03BC\u2080I/(2L), into the page.
All three point into the page, which is \u2212k\u0302, so they add: B = \u2212(\u03BC\u2080I/L)[1/(4\u221A2\u03C0) + 1/2 + 1/2].
B = \u2212(\u03BC\u2080I/L)(1 + 1/(4\u221A2\u03C0)) k\u0302, which is option (c).
Note: k\u0302 points out of the page, so a clockwise current gives a field along \u2212k\u0302.`,fig:{w:320,h:225,alt:"Left wire of length L carrying I upward, then a horizontal wire of length L/2, a semicircle of radius L/2 about O (clockwise), a short radial piece to distance L/4 from O, a quarter circle of radius L/4, and a straight wire of length 3L/4 carrying I downward, all in the xy plane",d:[["l",50,205,50,150],["l",50,135,50,121,"a"],["l",50,150,50,85],["l",50,85,110,85],["l",73,85,87,85,"a"],["p","M110 85 A60 60 0 0 1 230 85"],["l",163,25,177,25,"a"],["l",230,85,200,85],["l",222,85,208,85,"a"],["p","M200 85 A30 30 0 0 1 170 115"],["l",170,115,170,205],["l",170,153,170,167,"a"],["l",170,85,212,43,"d"],["l",170,85,170,115,"d"],["l",110,85,170,85,"d"],["c",170,85,2.5,"F"],["t",166,79,"O","e"],["t",200,45,"L/2","e"],["l",50,62,110,62,"bd"],["t",80,55,"L/2","m"],["l",28,85,28,205,"bd"],["l",145,115,145,205,"bd"],["t",164,107,"L/4","e"],["t",192,115,"L/4"],["t",20,150,"L","e"],["t",42,128,"I","e"],["t",180,168,"I"],["t",139,165,"3L/4","e"],["l",268,150,268,118,"a"],["l",268,150,300,150,"a"],["l",268,150,246,172,"a"],["t",262,114,"\u0135","e"],["t",302,166,"\xEE","e"],["t",240,186,"k\u0302","e"]]}},{pn:31,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:"The fractional change in the magnetic field intensity at a distance r from centre on the axis of current carrying coil of radius a to the magnetic field intensity at the centre of the same coil is (Take r < a).",o:["2a\xB2/(3r\xB2)","3a\xB2/(2r\xB2)","3r\xB2/(2a\xB2)","2r\xB2/(3a\xB2)"],c:2,s:`At the centre B\u2080 = \u03BC\u2080I/(2a). On the axis at distance r: B = \u03BC\u2080Ia\xB2/[2(a\xB2 + r\xB2)^(3/2)] = B\u2080(1 + r\xB2/a\xB2)^(\u22123/2).
For r small compared with a, use (1 + x)\u207F \u2248 1 + nx with x = r\xB2/a\xB2 and n = \u22123/2.
So B \u2248 B\u2080(1 \u2212 3r\xB2/(2a\xB2)).
Fractional change = (B \u2212 B\u2080)/B\u2080 = \u22123r\xB2/(2a\xB2).
Its size is 3r\xB2/(2a\xB2), which is option (c); the minus sign only says the field decreases.`},{pn:32,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mamp",st:"Ampere's law",q:"A coaxial cable consists of an inner wire of radius 'a' surrounded by an outer shell of inner and outer radii 'b' and 'c' respectively. The inner wire carries an electric current i\u2080, which is distributed uniformly across cross-sectional area. The outer shell carries an equal current in opposite direction and distributed uniformly. What will be the ratio of the magnetic field at a distance x from the axis when (i) x < a and (ii) a < x < b?",o:["a\xB2/x\xB2","(b\xB2 \u2212 a\xB2)/x\xB2","x\xB2/(b\xB2 \u2212 a\xB2)","x\xB2/a\xB2"],c:3,s:`Use Ampere's law on a circle of radius x about the axis.
(i) x < a: only part of the inner current is enclosed, i\u2080x\xB2/a\xB2. So B\u2081 \xD7 2\u03C0x = \u03BC\u2080i\u2080x\xB2/a\xB2, B\u2081 = \u03BC\u2080i\u2080x/(2\u03C0a\xB2).
(ii) a < x < b: the circle encloses the whole inner current i\u2080 and nothing of the shell. B\u2082 = \u03BC\u2080i\u2080/(2\u03C0x).
Ratio B\u2081/B\u2082 = [\u03BC\u2080i\u2080x/(2\u03C0a\xB2)] \xF7 [\u03BC\u2080i\u2080/(2\u03C0x)] = x\xB2/a\xB2.
This is option (d).`},{pn:33,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:"A coil having N turns is wound tightly in the form of a spiral with inner and outer radii a and b respectively. Find the magnetic field at centre, when a current I passes through coil.",o:["(\u03BC\u2080I/8)\xB7[(a + b)/(a \u2212 b)]","\u03BC\u2080I/(4(a \u2212 b)) \xB7 [1/a \u2212 1/b]","(\u03BC\u2080I/8)\xB7[(a \u2212 b)/(a + b)]","(\u03BC\u2080IN/(2(b \u2212 a))) \xB7 log\u2091(b/a)"],c:3,s:`Cut the spiral into thin rings. Between radius r and r + dr there are N dr/(b \u2212 a) turns, because N turns fill the width b \u2212 a evenly.
One turn of radius r gives \u03BC\u2080I/(2r) at the centre, so this ring gives dB = [\u03BC\u2080I/(2r)] \xD7 N dr/(b \u2212 a).
All rings give a field in the same direction, so add them: B = [\u03BC\u2080IN/(2(b \u2212 a))] \u222B dr/r from a to b.
\u222B dr/r = log\u2091(b/a), so B = [\u03BC\u2080IN/(2(b \u2212 a))] log\u2091(b/a).
This is option (d).`},{pn:34,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",q:"A current of 1.5 A is flowing through a triangle, of side 9 cm each. The magnetic field at the centroid of the triangle is (Assume that the current is flowing in the clockwise direction)",o:["2\u221A3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T, inside the plane of triangle","2\u221A3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T, outside the plane of triangle","3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T, outside the plane of triangle","3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T, inside the plane of triangle"],c:3,s:`Each side is a finite wire; the three sides give fields in the same direction at the centroid, so add 3 equal parts.
Perpendicular distance from the centroid to a side: d = a/(2\u221A3) = 0.09/(2\u221A3) = 0.045/\u221A3 m.
The ends of a side are seen at 60\xB0 on both sides of the perpendicular: B_side = (\u03BC\u2080I/4\u03C0d)(sin 60\xB0 + sin 60\xB0) = (\u03BC\u2080I/4\u03C0d)\u221A3.
B_side = 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 1.5 \xD7 \u221A3 \xF7 (0.045/\u221A3) = 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 1.5 \xD7 3 \xF7 0.045 = 10\u207B\u2075 T.
B_total = 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T.
Clockwise current gives a field into the page by the right-hand rule, so the answer is (d).`},{pn:35,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 1 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",q:"There are two infinitely long straight current carrying conductors and they are held at right angles to each other so that their common ends meet at the origin as shown in the figure given below. The ratio of current in both conductors is 1 : 1. The magnetic field at point P is ______.",o:["(\u03BC\u2080I/(4\u03C0xy)) [\u221A(x\xB2 + y\xB2) \u2212 (x + y)]","(\u03BC\u2080Ixy/(4\u03C0)) [\u221A(x\xB2 + y\xB2) \u2212 (x + y)]","(\u03BC\u2080I/(4\u03C0xy)) [\u221A(x\xB2 + y\xB2) + (x + y)]","(\u03BC\u2080Ixy/(4\u03C0)) [\u221A(x\xB2 + y\xB2) + (x + y)]"],c:2,s:`Each wire is semi-infinite, so use B = (\u03BC\u2080I/4\u03C0d)(sin \u03B1\u2081 + sin \u03B1\u2082) with the foot of the perpendicular from P. Let r = \u221A(x\xB2 + y\xB2).
Wire along y (current up): distance x, the foot is at height y above O, so B\u2081 = (\u03BC\u2080I/4\u03C0x)(1 + y/r).
Wire along x (current toward O): distance y, the foot is x from O, so B\u2082 = (\u03BC\u2080I/4\u03C0y)(1 + x/r).
By the right-hand rule both fields at P point into the page, so they add.
B = (\u03BC\u2080I/4\u03C0)[1/x + y/(xr) + 1/y + x/(yr)].
1/x + 1/y = (x + y)/(xy), and y/(xr) + x/(yr) = (x\xB2 + y\xB2)/(xyr) = r/(xy).
B = (\u03BC\u2080I/(4\u03C0xy)) [\u221A(x\xB2 + y\xB2) + (x + y)], option (c).`,fig:{w:290,h:200,alt:"Two semi-infinite wires meeting at O: one along +y carrying I upward, one along +x carrying I toward O; point P(x, y) with the angles \u03B81 and \u03B82 marked",d:[["l",40,170,40,62],["l",40,122,40,108,"a"],["l",40,60,40,18,"d"],["t",48,14,"\u221E y","s"],["l",40,170,150,170],["l",102,170,88,170,"a"],["l",150,170,235,170,"d"],["t",240,174,"\u221E x","s"],["l",40,60,150,60,"d"],["l",150,60,150,170,"d"],["l",40,170,150,60,"d"],["c",150,60,3,"F"],["t",158,56,"P(x, y)"],["p","M150 88 A28 28 0 0 1 130.2 79.8"],["p","M122 60 A28 28 0 0 0 130.2 79.8"],["t",108,82,"\u03B8\u2082","m"],["t",132,106,"\u03B8\u2081","m"],["t",30,115,"I","e"],["t",95,186,"I","m"],["t",34,186,"O","e"]]}},{pn:36,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mamp",st:"Ampere's law",q:"Figure A and B show two long straight wires of circular cross-section (a and b with a < b), carrying current I which is uniformly distributed across the cross-section. The magnitude of magnetic field B varies with radius r and can be represented as",o:["(a)","(b)","(c)","(d)"],c:2,s:`Ampere's law: inside a wire of radius R, B = \u03BC\u2080Ir/(2\u03C0R\xB2), a straight line in r; its slope is larger for the smaller radius.
The field is largest at the surface: B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0R). The smaller wire (a) has the larger peak, and it comes at the smaller r = a.
Outside, B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0r) for both wires, so both curves fall as 1/r.
So the graph must show a steeper line for a, peaking higher and earlier, and a lower, later peak for b.
Only graph (c) shows this (graph (a) has b peaking earlier, (b) has equal peak positions, (d) has the roles swapped).
The answer is (c).`,fig:{w:390,h:370,alt:"Wires A and B of radii a and b (a < b) each carrying current I, and four graphs of B against r, each with a curve for a and a curve for b",d:[["p","M36 40 A24 14 0 1 0 84 40 A24 14 0 1 0 36 40"],["l",36,12,36,72],["l",84,12,84,72],["l",60,40,84,40],["t",72,35,"a","ms"],["l",100,60,100,26,"a"],["t",108,52,"I"],["t",78,92,"Fig. (A)","m"],["p","M198 40 A32 14 0 1 0 262 40 A32 14 0 1 0 198 40"],["l",198,12,198,72],["l",262,12,262,72],["l",230,40,262,40],["t",246,35,"b","ms"],["l",278,60,278,26,"a"],["t",286,52,"I"],["t",252,92,"Fig. (B)","m"],["l",40,215,40,125,"a"],["l",40,215,170,215,"a"],["t",34,129,"B","e"],["t",176,219,"r"],["t",32,229,"O","e"],["t",10,175,"(a)","m"],["l",40,215,110,140],["l",110,140,110,215,"d"],["p","M110 140 C122 166 146 199 165 205"],["t",74.4,165.5,"a","ms"],["l",40,215,90,175],["l",90,175,90,215,"d"],["p","M90 175 C102 201 126 202 165 208"],["t",62,187.2,"b","ms"],["l",240,215,240,125,"a"],["l",240,215,370,215,"a"],["t",234,129,"B","e"],["t",376,219,"r"],["t",232,229,"O","e"],["t",210,175,"(b)","m"],["l",240,215,310,140],["l",310,140,310,215,"d"],["p","M310 140 C322 166 346 199 365 205"],["t",274.4,165.5,"a","ms"],["l",240,215,310,163],["p","M310 163 C322 189 346 199 365 205"],["t",288,202,"b","ms"],["l",40,340,40,250,"a"],["l",40,340,170,340,"a"],["t",34,254,"B","e"],["t",176,344,"r"],["t",32,354,"O","e"],["t",10,300,"(c)","m"],["l",40,340,90,265],["l",90,265,90,340,"d"],["p","M90 265 C102 291 126 324 165 330"],["t",62,290.5,"a","ms"],["l",40,340,110,290],["l",110,290,110,340,"d"],["p","M110 290 C122 316 146 324 165 330"],["t",74.4,306,"b","ms"],["l",240,340,240,250,"a"],["l",240,340,370,340,"a"],["t",234,254,"B","e"],["t",376,344,"r"],["t",232,354,"O","e"],["t",210,300,"(d)","m"],["l",240,340,290,310],["l",290,310,290,340,"d"],["p","M290 310 C302 336 326 327 365 333"],["t",256,316,"a","ms"],["l",240,340,310,265],["l",310,265,310,340,"d"],["p","M310 265 C322 291 346 324 365 330"],["t",274.4,290.5,"b","ms"]]}},{pn:37,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mamp",st:"Solenoid and toroid",q:"A solenoid of 1000 turns per metre has a core with relative permeability 500. Insulated windings of the solenoid carry an electric current of 5 A. The magnetic flux density produced by the solenoid is (permeability of free space = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 H/m)",o:["2\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 T","\u03C0 T","10\u207B\u2074\u03C0 T","\u03C0/5 T"],c:1,s:`With a core, B = \u03BC\u2080\u03BC\u1D63nI.
n = 1000 per metre, \u03BC\u1D63 = 500, I = 5 A, \u03BC\u2080 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077.
B = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 500 \xD7 1000 \xD7 5.
500 \xD7 1000 \xD7 5 = 2.5 \xD7 10\u2076, so B = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 2.5 \xD7 10\u2076 = 10\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9.
B = \u03C0 T, which is option (b).`},{pn:38,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 17 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbs",st:"Superposition and symmetry",q:"A hairpin like shape as shown in figure is made by bending a long current carrying wire. What is the magnitude of a magnetic field at point P which lies on the centre of the semicircle?",o:["(\u03BC\u2080I/2\u03C0r)(2 \u2212 \u03C0)","(\u03BC\u2080I/4\u03C0r)(2 \u2212 \u03C0)","(\u03BC\u2080I/2\u03C0r)(2 + \u03C0)","(\u03BC\u2080I/4\u03C0r)(2 + \u03C0)"],c:3,s:`Split the wire into two straight parts and one semicircle.
Each straight wire is semi-infinite and P is level with its end, at distance r: B = \u03BC\u2080I/(4\u03C0r).
The top wire carries current to the right and the bottom one to the left. By the right-hand rule both fields at P point into the page.
The semicircle: half of a full loop, B = \xBD \xD7 \u03BC\u2080I/(2r) = \u03BC\u2080I/(4r), also into the page.
All three add: B = 2 \xD7 \u03BC\u2080I/(4\u03C0r) + \u03BC\u2080I/(4r) = (\u03BC\u2080I/4\u03C0r)(2 + \u03C0).
This is option (d).`,fig:{w:260,h:180,alt:"Hairpin wire: two long parallel horizontal wires joined by a semicircle of radius r on the right; current I enters along the top wire and returns along the bottom wire; P is the centre of the semicircle",d:[["l",30,40,170,40],["l",93,40,107,40,"a"],["t",100,30,"I","m"],["p","M170 40 A50 50 0 0 1 170 140"],["p","M170 140 A50 50 0 0 1 170 40","d"],["l",170,140,30,140],["l",107,140,93,140,"a"],["t",100,158,"I","m"],["c",170,90,2.5,"F"],["t",160,86,"P","e"],["l",170,90,205.4,125.4],["t",196,104,"r","m"],["l",220,83,220,97,"a"],["t",234,94,"I"]]}},{pn:39,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 18 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A loop of flexible wire or irregular shape carrying current is placed in an external magnetic field. Identify the effect of the field on the wire.",o:["loop assumes circular shape with its plane normal to the field","wire gets stretched to become straight","loop assumes circular shape with its plane parallel to the field","shape of the loop remains unchanged"],c:0,s:`On each small piece of the wire the force is I dl \xD7 B, perpendicular to both the piece and the field.
If the plane of the loop is not normal to B, the torque on the loop turns it until its plane is normal to B (magnetic moment along B).
In that position every piece is pushed radially outward in the plane of the loop, so the wire stretches into the shape that needs balanced outward tension: a circle.
A circle also encloses the largest area for the given length, which is the stable position of maximum flux.
So the loop becomes a circle with its plane normal to the field, option (a).`},{pn:40,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 18 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mamp",st:"Solenoid and toroid",q:"Four identical long solenoids A, B, C and D are connected to each other as shown in the figure. If the magnetic field at the center of A is 3 T, the field at the center of C would be (Assume that the magnetic field is confined with in the volume of respectively solenoid)",o:["6 T","9 T","1 T","12 T"],c:2,s:`Inside a long solenoid B = \u03BC\u2080nI, so for identical solenoids B is proportional to the current.
B, C and D are in parallel and identical, so they have equal resistance and the current i from A splits equally: i/3 in each.
Field of A (current i) = 3 T.
Field of C (current i/3) = 3 T \xF7 3 = 1 T.
The answer is option (c).`,fig:{w:300,h:135,alt:"Solenoid A in series with three identical solenoids B, C and D connected in parallel; current i enters at A",d:[["l",15,70,52,70,"a"],["p","M60 70 a5 5 0 0 1 10 0 a5 5 0 0 1 10 0 a5 5 0 0 1 10 0 a5 5 0 0 1 10 0"],["t",80,56,"A","m"],["t",80,92,"i","m"],["l",100,70,130,70],["c",130,70,2.5,"F"],["l",130,70,150,25],["l",150,25,165,25],["p","M165 25 a5 5 0 0 1 10 0 a5 5 0 0 1 10 0 a5 5 0 0 1 10 0 a5 5 0 0 1 10 0"],["t",185,12,"B","m"],["l",205,25,220,25],["l",220,25,240,70],["l",130,70,165,70],["p","M165 70 a5 5 0 0 1 10 0 a5 5 0 0 1 10 0 a5 5 0 0 1 10 0 a5 5 0 0 1 10 0"],["t",185,57,"C","m"],["l",205,70,240,70],["l",130,70,150,115],["l",150,115,165,115],["p","M165 115 a5 5 0 0 1 10 0 a5 5 0 0 1 10 0 a5 5 0 0 1 10 0 a5 5 0 0 1 10 0"],["t",185,102,"D","m"],["l",205,115,220,115],["l",220,115,240,70],["c",240,70,2.5,"F"],["l",240,70,280,70]]}},{pn:41,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:"Magnetic fields at two points on the axis of a circular coil at a distance of 0.05 m and 0.2 m from the centre are in the ratio 8 : 1. The radius of the coil is ______.",o:["1.0 m","0.15 m","0.2 m","0.1 m"],c:3,s:`On the axis B \u221D 1/(R\xB2 + x\xB2)^(3/2).
B\u2081/B\u2082 = [(R\xB2 + 0.2\xB2)/(R\xB2 + 0.05\xB2)]^(3/2) = 8.
Take the 2/3 power of both sides: (R\xB2 + 0.04)/(R\xB2 + 0.0025) = 8^(2/3) = 4.
R\xB2 + 0.04 = 4R\xB2 + 0.01, so 3R\xB2 = 0.03 and R\xB2 = 0.01.
R = 0.1 m, which is option (d).`},{pn:42,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",q:"Magnitude of magnetic field (in SI units) at the centre of a hexagonal shape coil of side 10 cm, 50 turns and carrying current I (Ampere) in units of \u03BC\u2080I/\u03C0 is",o:["250\u221A3","50\u221A3","500\u221A3","5\u221A3"],c:2,s:`Each side of the regular hexagon is a finite wire, and all six fields at the centre point the same way.
Distance from the centre to a side: d = a\u221A3/2 with a = 0.1 m. The ends are seen at 30\xB0 from the perpendicular (tan \u03B8 = (a/2)/d = 1/\u221A3).
B_side = (\u03BC\u2080I/4\u03C0d)(2 sin 30\xB0) = \u03BC\u2080I/(4\u03C0d) = \u03BC\u2080I/(4\u03C0 \xD7 0.1 \xD7 \u221A3/2) = \u03BC\u2080I/(0.2\u221A3\u03C0).
Six sides: B\u2081 = 6\u03BC\u2080I/(0.2\u221A3\u03C0) = 30\u03BC\u2080I/(\u221A3\u03C0) = 10\u221A3 (\u03BC\u2080I/\u03C0).
50 turns: B = 50 \xD7 10\u221A3 (\u03BC\u2080I/\u03C0) = 500\u221A3 (\u03BC\u2080I/\u03C0).
The answer is option (c).`},{pn:43,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",q:"A wire A, bent in the shape of an arc of a circle, carrying a current of 2 A and having radius 2 cm and another wire B, also bent in the shape of arc of a circle, carrying a current of 3 A and having radius of 4 cm, are placed as shown in the figure. The ratio of the magnetic fields due to the wires A and B at the common centre O is",o:["6 : 5","4 : 6","2 : 5","6 : 4"],c:0,s:`From the figure the gap in A is 90\xB0, so A is a 270\xB0 arc (3\u03C0/2 rad); the gap in B is 60\xB0, so B is a 300\xB0 arc (5\u03C0/3 rad).
Field at the centre of an arc: B = \u03BC\u2080I\u03B8/(4\u03C0r).
B_A = \u03BC\u2080 \xD7 2 \xD7 (3\u03C0/2)/(4\u03C0 \xD7 0.02) = \u03BC\u2080 \xD7 3/(4 \xD7 0.02) = 37.5\u03BC\u2080.
B_B = \u03BC\u2080 \xD7 3 \xD7 (5\u03C0/3)/(4\u03C0 \xD7 0.04) = \u03BC\u2080 \xD7 5/(4 \xD7 0.04) = 31.25\u03BC\u2080.
B_A : B_B = 37.5 : 31.25 = 6 : 5.
This is option (a).`,fig:{w:250,h:200,alt:"Wire A is a 270 degree arc of radius 2 cm carrying 2 A, wire B is a 300 degree arc of radius 4 cm carrying 3 A, both with the common centre O; the gap angles 90 and 60 degrees are marked",d:[["p","M160,165.6 A70 70 0 1 0 90,165.6"],["l",125,105,160,165.6],["l",125,105,90,165.6],["p","M149.7,129.7 A35 35 0 1 0 100.3,129.7"],["l",125,105,149.7,129.7],["l",125,105,100.3,129.7],["l",55,99,55,111,"a"],["l",182.6,145.2,188.6,134.8,"a"],["l",131,70,119,70,"a"],["l",160,98.6,155.8,87.4,"a"],["c",125,105,2.5,"F"],["t",117,100,"O","e"],["t",125,63,"A","m"],["t",205,75,"B"],["p","M102.5,144 A45 45 0 0 0 147.5,144","d"],["t",125,163,"60\xB0","ms"],["p","M109.4,120.6 A22 22 0 0 0 140.6,120.6","d"],["l",183,155,140.6,120.6,"a"],["t",187,165,"90\xB0","s"]]}},{pn:44,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbs",st:"Superposition and symmetry",q:"A very long wire ABDMNDC is shown in figure carrying current I. AB and BC parts are straight, long and at right angle. At D, wire forms a circular turn DMND of radius R. AB, BC parts are tangential to circular turn at N and D. Magnetic field at the centre of circle is",o:["(\u03BC\u2080I/2\u03C0R)(\u03C0 + 1/\u221A2)","\u03BC\u2080I/(2R)","(\u03BC\u2080I/2\u03C0R)(\u03C0 + 1)","(\u03BC\u2080I/2\u03C0R)(\u03C0 \u2212 1/\u221A2)"],c:0,s:`Add the fields of the circle and of the two straight wires at the centre O (see the solution figure: each straight wire is at distance R from O and its end B is seen at 45\xB0 from the perpendicular).
Circle: B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2R), current D\u2192M\u2192N\u2192D is anticlockwise, so the field is out of the page.
Wire BC (line below O, from B to infinity): the foot of the perpendicular is D, B is R to the left of D. B = (\u03BC\u2080I/4\u03C0R)(1 + sin 45\xB0) = (\u03BC\u2080I/4\u03C0R)(1 + 1/\u221A2), out of the page.
Wire AB (line to the left of O, ending at B): the foot N lies above the end B, so B = (\u03BC\u2080I/4\u03C0R)(1 \u2212 1/\u221A2), and its direction is into the page.
Net = (\u03BC\u2080I/4\u03C0R)[2\u03C0 + (1 + 1/\u221A2) \u2212 (1 \u2212 1/\u221A2)] = (\u03BC\u2080I/4\u03C0R)(2\u03C0 + \u221A2).
This equals (\u03BC\u2080I/2\u03C0R)(\u03C0 + 1/\u221A2), option (a).
Note: the two straight wires partly cancel, because they push the field in opposite directions.`,fig:{w:300,h:235,alt:"Long wire ABDMNDC: AB is vertical and BC horizontal at right angles at B, both tangent to a circle of radius R at N and D; the wire goes round the circle D to M to N to D and continues along DC; the centre of the circle is marked with a dot",d:[["p","M110 120 A40 40 0 1 0 110 40 A40 40 0 1 0 110 120"],["c",110,80,2.5,"F"],["l",70,120,70,195],["l",70,190,70,172,"a"],["l",70,195,70,225,"d"],["l",70,120,205,120],["l",158,120,172,120,"a"],["l",205,120,252,120],["l",252,120,285,120,"d"],["l",142.5,55.9,134.1,47.5,"a"],["p","M70 132 H82 V120"],["t",62,84,"N","e"],["t",150,42,"M"],["t",110,138,"D","m"],["t",62,128,"B","e"],["t",225,138,"C","m"],["t",62,200,"A","e"]]},sfig:{w:320,h:236,alt:"Centre O of the circle of radius R. Wire AB is vertical and ends at B, directly below the point N; wire BC is horizontal below the circle, touching it at D. The lines from O to B make 45 degrees with ON and with OD. The circle current is anticlockwise (field out of the page), BC gives out of the page and AB gives into the page.",d:[["p","M160 140 A50 50 0 1 0 160 40 A50 50 0 1 0 160 140"],["l",110,215,110,140],["l",110,190,110,172,"a"],["l",110,140,290,140],["l",230,140,248,140,"a"],["l",160,90,110,140,"d"],["l",160,90,110,90,"d"],["l",160,90,160,140,"d"],["c",160,90,2.5,"F"],["l",186,56,178,48,"a"],["t",166,86,"O"],["t",102,86,"N","e"],["t",160,156,"D","m"],["t",102,150,"B","e"],["t",104,212,"A","e"],["t",284,156,"C","m"],["t",147,128,"45\xB0","ms"],["t",127,104,"45\xB0","ms"],["t",130,176,"Circle: out of the page"],["t",130,194,"BC: out of the page"],["t",130,212,"AB: into the page"]]}},{pn:45,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mamp",st:"Ampere's law",q:"A long, straight wire of radius a carries a current distributed uniformly over its cross-section. The ratio of the magnetic fields due to the wire at distance a/3 and 2a, respectively from the axis of the wire is",o:["3/2","2","2/3","1/2"],c:2,s:`Inside the wire (r < a): B = \u03BC\u2080Ir/(2\u03C0a\xB2). At r = a/3, B\u2081 = \u03BC\u2080I/(6\u03C0a).
Outside (r > a): B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0r). At r = 2a, B\u2082 = \u03BC\u2080I/(4\u03C0a).
B\u2081/B\u2082 = (1/6)/(1/4) = 4/6 = 2/3.
The answer is option (c).`},{pn:46,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbs",st:"Superposition and symmetry",q:"Two very long, straight, and insulated wires are kept at 90\xB0 angle from each other in xy-plane as shown in the figure. These wires carry currents of equal magnitude I, whose directions are shown in the figure. The net magnetic field at point P will be",o:["\u2212(\u03BC\u2080I/2\u03C0d)(x\u0302 + \u0177)","(\u03BC\u2080I/2\u03C0d)(x\u0302 + \u0177)","Zero","(+\u03BC\u2080I/\u03C0d)(\u1E91)"],c:2,s:`Each long wire gives B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0d) at P, since P is at distance d from both.
Wire along y (current up) is to the left of P. The right-hand rule gives a field into the page at P (\u2212\u1E91).
Wire along x (current to the right) is below P. The right-hand rule gives a field out of the page at P (+\u1E91).
The magnitudes are equal and the directions opposite, so they cancel.
Net field is zero, option (c).`,fig:{w:260,h:180,alt:"Two long insulated wires in the xy plane at right angles: one along y carrying I upward, one along x carrying I to the right; P is at distance d from each wire",d:[["l",60,5,60,22,"d"],["l",60,22,60,160],["l",60,49,60,35,"a"],["l",60,160,60,172,"d"],["t",48,40,"I","e"],["l",10,100,168,100],["l",23,100,37,100,"a"],["l",168,100,198,100,"d"],["t",30,118,"I","m"],["l",60,50,110,50,"bd"],["t",85,42,"d","m"],["l",110,50,110,100,"bd"],["t",122,78,"d"],["c",110,50,2.5,"F"],["t",120,46,"P"],["l",212,50,212,22,"a"],["l",212,50,240,50,"a"],["t",206,22,"y","e"],["t",244,54,"x"]]}},{pn:47,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",q:"The magnitude of the magnetic field at the center of an equilateral triangular loop of side 1 m which is carrying a current of 10 A is [Take \u03BC\u2080 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 N A\u207B\xB2]",o:["1 \u03BCT","18 \u03BCT","3 \u03BCT","9 \u03BCT"],c:1,s:`All three sides give a field in the same direction at the centre, so find one side and multiply by 3.
Distance from the centre to a side: d = a/(2\u221A3) = 1/(2\u221A3) m \u2248 0.289 m.
Each end of the side is seen at 60\xB0 from the perpendicular: B_side = (\u03BC\u2080I/4\u03C0d)(2 sin 60\xB0) = (\u03BC\u2080I/4\u03C0d)\u221A3.
B_side = 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 10 \xD7 \u221A3 \xF7 (1/(2\u221A3)) = 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 \u221A3 \xD7 2\u221A3 = 6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 T.
B = 3 \xD7 6 \u03BCT = 18 \u03BCT, option (b).`},{pn:48,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:"A thin ring of 10 cm radius carries a uniformly distributed charge. The ring rotates at a constant angular speed of 40\u03C0 rad s\u207B\xB9 about its axis, perpendicular to its plane. If the magnetic field at its centre is 3.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 T, then the charge carried by the ring is close to (\u03BC\u2080 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 N/A\xB2)",o:["3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 C","4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 C","2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 C","7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 C"],c:0,s:`A rotating charged ring is a current loop: I = q/T = q\u03C9/(2\u03C0).
Field at the centre: B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2R) = \u03BC\u2080q\u03C9/(4\u03C0R).
So q = 4\u03C0RB/(\u03BC\u2080\u03C9).
q = 4\u03C0 \xD7 0.1 \xD7 3.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 \xF7 (4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 40\u03C0) = (0.1 \xD7 3.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079) \xF7 (4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) = 3.8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070 \xF7 1.2566 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075.
q \u2248 3.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 C, option (a).`},{pn:49,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",q:"Find the magnetic field at point P due to a straight line segment AB of length 6 cm carrying a current of 5 A. (see figure) (\u03BC\u2080 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 N A\u207B\xB2)",fig:{w:300,h:210,alt:"Straight wire AB of length 6 cm carrying 5 A from A to B; point P is 5 cm from each end, on the perpendicular bisector",d:[["l",50,160,242,160,"a"],["l",50,160,150,40,"d"],["l",250,160,150,40,"d"],["l",150,160,150,40,"d"],["c",50,160,3,"F"],["c",250,160,3,"F"],["c",150,40,3,"F"],["t",150,28,"P","m"],["t",40,176,"A","m"],["t",260,176,"B","m"],["t",150,182,"6 cm","m"],["t",90,92,"5 cm","e"],["t",212,92,"5 cm"],["t",100,152,"I = 5 A","m"]]},o:["2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T","2.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T","3.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T","1.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T"],c:3,s:`A finite straight wire gives B = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(4\u03C0d) (sin \u03B8\u2081 + sin \u03B8\u2082), where d is the perpendicular distance of P from the wire.
P is 5 cm from each end, so it lies above the middle of AB. Height d = \u221A(5\xB2 \u2212 3\xB2) = 4 cm = 0.04 m.
Each end makes an angle with the perpendicular: sin \u03B8 = 3/5 = 0.6 on both sides.
B = (10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 5)/(0.04) \xD7 (0.6 + 0.6) = 1.25 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 1.2.
B = 1.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T, option (d).`},{pn:50,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",q:"A current loop, having two circular arcs joined by two radial lines is shown in the figure. It carries a current of 10 A. The magnetic field at point O will be close to",fig:{w:300,h:270,alt:"Current loop made of two circular arcs and two radial lines, centre O, arcs of radius 3 cm and 5 cm, angle 45 degrees, current 10 A",d:[["l",73.5,216.8,104.1,142.9],["l",195.9,142.9,226.5,216.8],["l",150,32,104.1,142.9],["l",150,32,195.9,142.9],["p","M104.1 142.9 A120 120 0 0 0 195.9 142.9"],["p","M73.5 216.8 A200 200 0 0 0 226.5 216.8"],["c",150,32,3,"F"],["t",150,18,"O","m"],["t",96.1,138.9,"Q","e"],["t",203.9,138.9,"R"],["t",65.5,230.8,"P","e"],["t",234.5,230.8,"S"],["t",150,252,"i = 10 A","m"],["t",118,76,"3 cm","e"],["t",182,76,"3 cm"],["t",74,184,"2 cm","e"],["t",226,184,"2 cm"],["t",150,84,"45\xB0","ms"],["l",74.4,214.5,70,225],["l",86,186.6,91.6,173.2,"a"],["l",208.4,173.2,214,186.6,"a"],["l",142,152,158,152,"a"],["l",158,232,142,232,"a"]]},o:["1.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T","1.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T","1.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T","1.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T"],c:0,s:`The two radial lines point towards O, so they give no field at O.
An arc of angle \u03B8 and radius r gives B = (\u03BC\u2080I \u03B8)/(4\u03C0 r). The angle is 45\xB0 = \u03C0/4 rad.
In a loop the current runs opposite ways in the two arcs, so their fields at O subtract.
Inner arc r = 3 cm, outer arc r = 5 cm: B = (10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 10 \xD7 \u03C0/4)(1/0.03 \u2212 1/0.05).
= 7.85 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 (33.3 \u2212 20) = 7.85 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 13.3 \u2248 1.05 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T.
So B \u2248 1.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T, option (a).`},{pn:51,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:"One of the two identical conducting wires of length L is bent in the form of a circular loop and the other one into a circular coil of N identical turns. If the same current is passed in both, the ratio of the magnetic field at the centre of the loop (B_L) to that at the centre of the coil (B_C), i.e. B_L/B_C will be",o:["1/N\xB2","N\xB2","N","1/N"],c:0,s:`Loop: 2\u03C0R = L, so R = L/(2\u03C0) and B_L = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(2R).
Coil: N turns use the same length, so 2\u03C0r\xB7N = L and r = R/N.
Field at the centre of N turns: B_C = (N\u03BC\u2080I)/(2r) = (N\u03BC\u2080I N)/(2R) = N\xB2(\u03BC\u2080I)/(2R).
B_L/B_C = 1/N\xB2, option (a).`},{pn:52,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbs",st:"Superposition and symmetry",q:"As shown in the figure, two infinitely long, identical wires are bent by 90\xB0 and placed in such a way that the segments LP and QM are along the x-axis, while segments PS and QN are parallel to the y-axis. If OP = OQ = 4 cm, and the magnitude of the magnetic field at O is 10\u207B\u2074 T, and the two wires carry equal currents (see figure), the magnitude of the current in each wire and the direction of the magnetic field at O will be (\u03BC\u2080 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 N A\u207B\xB2)",fig:{w:320,h:200,alt:"Two identical bent wires: LP along the x axis then PS up the y direction, and MQ along the x axis then QN down; O is the midpoint of PQ, OP = OQ = 4 cm",d:[["l",20,110,110,110,"a"],["l",110,110,110,30,"a"],["l",290,110,190,110,"a"],["l",190,110,190,185,"a"],["l",110,110,190,110,"d"],["c",150,110,2.5,"F"],["t",15,106,"L","e"],["t",110,22,"S","m"],["t",298,106,"M"],["t",190,198,"N","m"],["t",104,126,"P","e"],["t",196,126,"Q"],["t",150,98,"O","m"],["l",114,136,148,136,"b"],["l",152,136,186,136,"b"],["t",131,150,"4 cm","m"],["t",169,150,"4 cm","m"],["l",300,60,300,30,"a"],["t",306,36,"y"]]},o:["20 A, perpendicular into the page","40 A, perpendicular into the page","20 A, perpendicular out of the page","40 A, perpendicular out of the page"],c:0,s:`O lies on the line of the straight segments LP and QM, so these give no field at O.
Only the semi-infinite segments PS and QN count. Each starts level with O, at 4 cm from it, so B = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(4\u03C0d).
Direction (right-hand rule): the current in PS goes up, on the left of O, so its field at O is into the page. The current in QN goes down, on the right of O, which also gives into the page. The two add.
Total B = 2 \xD7 (\u03BC\u2080I)/(4\u03C0d) = 10\u207B\u2074 T, so I = (10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 0.04)/(2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077) = 20 A.
So 20 A and the field is into the page, option (a).`},{pn:53,y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:"A Helmholtz coil has a pair of loops, each with N turns and radius R. They are placed coaxially at distance R and the same current I flows through the loops in the same direction. The magnitude of magnetic field at P, midway between the centres A and C, is given by [refer to given figure]",fig:{w:330,h:190,alt:"Helmholtz coil: two coaxial loops of radius R at A and C, distance R apart, P midway between them",d:[["p","M70 20 A16 50 0 1 0 70 120 A16 50 0 1 0 70 20"],["p","M250 20 A16 50 0 1 0 250 120 A16 50 0 1 0 250 20"],["l",20,70,310,70,"d"],["c",70,70,2.5,"F"],["c",160,70,2.5,"F"],["c",250,70,2.5,"F"],["t",70,86,"A","m"],["t",160,86,"P","m"],["t",250,86,"C","m"],["l",30,20,30,120,"b"],["t",24,74,"2R","e"],["l",70,150,250,150,"b"],["t",160,168,"R","m"],["l",70,120,70,155,"d"],["l",250,120,250,155,"d"],["l",85,50,86,62,"a"],["l",265,50,266,62,"a"]]},o:["(4N\u03BC\u2080I)/(5^(1/2) R)","(4N\u03BC\u2080I)/(5^(3/2) R)","(8N\u03BC\u2080I)/(5^(3/2) R)","(8N\u03BC\u2080I)/(5^(1/2) R)"],c:2,s:`On the axis, one coil of N turns gives B = (N\u03BC\u2080I R\xB2)/(2(R\xB2 + x\xB2)^(3/2)).
P is R/2 from each centre, so x = R/2 and R\xB2 + x\xB2 = (5/4)R\xB2.
Each coil gives (N\u03BC\u2080I R\xB2)/(2 (5/4)^(3/2) R\xB3) = (N\u03BC\u2080I)/(2R) \xD7 (4/5)^(3/2).
Both currents flow the same way, so the two fields add: B = 2 \xD7 that = (N\u03BC\u2080I)/R \xD7 8/(5^(3/2)).
B = (8N\u03BC\u2080I)/(5^(3/2) R), option (c).`},{pn:54,y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"mbs",st:"Superposition and symmetry",q:"A current of 1 A is flowing on the sides of an equilateral triangle of side 4.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 m. The magnetic field at the centre of the triangle will be",o:["4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 Wb/m\xB2","8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 Wb/m\xB2","2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 Wb/m\xB2","Zero"],c:0,s:`By symmetry the three sides give equal fields at the centre, and all three point the same way (current goes round the triangle), so they add.
Distance of the centre from a side: d = a/(2\u221A3) = 4.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 / 3.464 = 1.3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 m.
Each side subtends 60\xB0 on both sides of the foot of the perpendicular: B\u2081 = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(4\u03C0d) \xD7 2 sin 60\xB0 = 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 1/(1.3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2) \xD7 1.732 = 1.33 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T.
Total B = 3 \xD7 1.33 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T (1 T = 1 Wb/m\xB2).
So option (a).`},{pn:55,y:"JEE Main 2017",tp:"mbs",st:"Superposition and symmetry",q:"A symmetric star shaped conducting wire loop is carrying a steady state current I as shown in the figure. The distance between the diametrically opposite vertices of the star is 4a. The magnitude of the magnetic field at the center of the loop is",fig:{w:340,h:210,alt:"Symmetric six-pointed star shaped wire loop carrying current I; the distance between diametrically opposite vertices is 4a",d:[["p","M150 190L124 145L72.1 145L98 100L72.1 55L124 55L150 10L176 55L227.9 55L202 100L227.9 145L176 145Z"],["l",20,10,300,10,"d"],["l",20,190,300,190,"d"],["l",300,10,300,190,"b"],["t",308,104,"4a"],["l",142,24,133,39,"a"],["t",126,26,"I","e"]]},o:["(\u03BC\u2080I)/(4\u03C0a) \xB7 3[2 \u2212 \u221A3]","(\u03BC\u2080I)/(4\u03C0a) \xB7 6[\u221A3 \u2212 1]","(\u03BC\u2080I)/(4\u03C0a) \xB7 3[\u221A3 \u2212 1]","(\u03BC\u2080I)/(4\u03C0a) \xB7 6[\u221A3 + 1]"],c:1,s:`The star has 12 equal sides, and each gives a field in the same direction at the centre, so find one and multiply by 12.
Opposite vertices are 4a apart, so the vertices are at distance 2a from the centre. Each side lies on the side of a big equilateral triangle of circumradius 2a, whose distance from the centre is a (half the circumradius).
On that line the side runs from the inner corner, at a/\u221A3 from the foot of the perpendicular, to the tip, at \u221A3 a. So sin \u03B8\u2081 = 1/2 and sin \u03B8\u2082 = \u221A3/2 (as the distance to the end is 2a).
One side: B\u2081 = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(4\u03C0a)(sin \u03B8\u2082 \u2212 sin \u03B8\u2081) = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(4\u03C0a) \xD7 (\u221A3 \u2212 1)/2.
B = 12 B\u2081 = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(4\u03C0a) \xD7 6(\u221A3 \u2212 1), option (b).
Note: here both ends of the side are on the same side of the foot of the perpendicular, so the two sines subtract.`},{pn:56,y:"JEE Main 2016",tp:"mbs",st:"Superposition and symmetry",q:"Two identical wires A and B, each of length l, carry the same current I. Wire A is bent into a circle of radius R and wire B is bent to form a square of side a. If B_A and B_B are the values of magnetic field at the centres of the circle and square respectively, then the ratio B_A/B_B is",o:["\u03C0\xB2/8","\u03C0\xB2/(16\u221A2)","\u03C0\xB2/16","\u03C0\xB2/(8\u221A2)"],c:3,s:`Same length l for both wires: circle 2\u03C0R = l, so R = l/(2\u03C0); square 4a = l, so a = l/4.
Circle: B_A = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(2R) = (\u03C0\u03BC\u2080I)/l.
Square: each side is a wire of length a at distance a/2 from the centre; angles 45\xB0 at both ends: B = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(4\u03C0(a/2)) \xD7 2 sin 45\xB0 = (\u03BC\u2080I \u221A2)/(2\u03C0a).
Four sides: B_B = (2\u221A2 \u03BC\u2080I)/(\u03C0a) = (8\u221A2 \u03BC\u2080I)/(\u03C0l).
B_A/B_B = (\u03C0/l) \xD7 (\u03C0l)/(8\u221A2) = \u03C0\xB2/(8\u221A2), option (d).`},{pn:57,y:"JEE Main 2014 \xB7 Online",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:"Consider two thin identical conducting wires covered with very thin insulating material. One of the wires is bent into a loop and produces magnetic field B\u2081, at its centre when a current I passes through it. The second wire is bent into a coil with three identical loops adjacent to each other and produces magnetic field B\u2082 at the centre of the loops when current I/3 passes through it. The ratio B\u2081 : B\u2082 is",o:["1 : 1","1 : 3","1 : 9","9 : 1"],c:1,s:`Same wire length: if the single loop has radius R, each of the 3 loops has radius R/3.
B\u2081 = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(2R).
For the coil: N = 3 turns, current I/3, radius R/3, so B\u2082 = (3 \xD7 \u03BC\u2080 \xD7 (I/3))/(2 \xD7 (R/3)).
The numerator is \u03BC\u2080I and the denominator is 2R/3, so B\u2082 = 3 (\u03BC\u2080I)/(2R) = 3B\u2081.
B\u2081 : B\u2082 = 1 : 3, option (b).`},{pn:58,y:"JEE Main 2013 \xB7 Online",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",q:"A current i is flowing in a straight conductor of length L. The magnetic induction at a point on its axis at a distance L/4 from its centre will be",o:["Zero","(\u03BC\u2080i)/(2\u03C0L)","(\u03BC\u2080i)/(\u221A2 \u03C0L)","(4\u03BC\u2080i)/(\u221A5 \u03C0L)"],c:3,s:`Note: a point lying on the line of the wire itself would be inside the conductor, so "axis" is read here as the perpendicular axis through the centre of the wire (the key (d) agrees with this reading).
For a finite wire, B = (\u03BC\u2080i)/(4\u03C0d)(sin \u03B8\u2081 + sin \u03B8\u2082) at perpendicular distance d = L/4.
The ends are at L/2 along the wire, so sin \u03B8 = (L/2)/\u221A((L/2)\xB2 + (L/4)\xB2) = 2/\u221A5 for each end.
B = (\u03BC\u2080i)/(4\u03C0 (L/4)) \xD7 2 \xD7 (2/\u221A5) = (4\u03BC\u2080i)/(\u221A5 \u03C0L).
So option (d).`},{pn:59,y:"AIEEE 2012",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:"A charge Q is uniformly distributed over the surface of non-conducting disc of radius R. The disc rotates about an axis perpendicular to its plane and passing through its centre with an angular velocity \u03C9. As a result of this rotation a magnetic field of induction B is obtained at the centre of the disc. If we keep both the amount of charge placed on the disc and its angular velocity to be constant and vary the radius of the disc then the variation of the magnetic induction at the centre of the disc will be represented by the figure",fig:{w:330,h:290,alt:"Four graphs of B against R: (1) straight line falling, (2) straight line rising, (3) curve rising and levelling off, (4) curve falling steeply then flattening like 1/R",d:[["l",40,110,40,35,"a"],["l",40,110,135,110,"a"],["t",36,41,"B","e"],["t",135,124,"R","e"],["t",35,122,"O","e"],["t",90,144,"(1)","m"],["p","M40 48 L125 110"],["l",200,110,200,35,"a"],["l",200,110,295,110,"a"],["t",196,41,"B","e"],["t",295,124,"R","e"],["t",195,122,"O","e"],["t",250,144,"(2)","m"],["p","M200 110 L285 48"],["l",40,250,40,175,"a"],["l",40,250,135,250,"a"],["t",36,181,"B","e"],["t",135,264,"R","e"],["t",35,262,"O","e"],["t",90,284,"(3)","m"],["p","M40 250 C50 205 80 188 128 184"],["l",200,250,200,175,"a"],["l",200,250,295,250,"a"],["t",196,181,"B","e"],["t",295,264,"R","e"],["t",195,262,"O","e"],["t",250,284,"(4)","m"],["p","M206 182 C212 222 235 240 288 246"]]},o:["Graph (1): a straight line falling from the B axis","Graph (2): a straight line rising from the origin","Graph (3): a curve rising and levelling off","Graph (4): a curve falling steeply and flattening, like 1/R"],c:3,s:`Cut the disc into rings. A ring of radius r and width dr carries charge dq = (Q/\u03C0R\xB2) 2\u03C0r dr = (2Q r dr)/R\xB2.
Rotating charge is a current: dI = dq \xD7 \u03C9/(2\u03C0) = (Q \u03C9 r dr)/(\u03C0R\xB2).
Field of the ring at the centre: dB = (\u03BC\u2080 dI)/(2r) = (\u03BC\u2080Q \u03C9 dr)/(2\u03C0R\xB2).
Add up from r = 0 to R: B = (\u03BC\u2080Q \u03C9)/(2\u03C0R\xB2) \xD7 R = (\u03BC\u2080Q \u03C9)/(2\u03C0R).
With Q and \u03C9 fixed, B \u221D 1/R: a curve that is very high for small R and falls towards zero, graph (4), option (d).`},{pn:60,y:"IIT-JEE 2012",tp:"mamp",st:"Ampere's law",q:"An infinitely long hollow conducting cylinder with inner radius R/2 and outer radius R carries a uniform current density along its length. The magnitude of the magnetic field, |B| as a function of the radial distance r from the axis is best represented by",fig:{w:330,h:300,alt:"Four graphs of |B| against r: (1) rises linearly to R/2, flat to R, then falls; (2) zero up to R/2, jumps, flat to R, then falls; (3) zero up to R/2, jumps, rises to a peak at R, then falls; (4) zero up to R/2, rises smoothly to a peak at R, then falls",d:[["l",40,110,40,40,"a"],["l",40,110,160,110,"a"],["t",36,48,"|B|","e"],["t",164,114,"r"],["t",70,124,"R/2","m"],["t",100,124,"R","m"],["l",70,108,70,112],["l",100,108,100,112],["p","M40 110 L70 68 L100 68 C110 76 128 88 148 94"],["t",100,138,"(1)","m"],["l",200,110,200,40,"a"],["l",200,110,320,110,"a"],["t",196,48,"|B|","e"],["t",324,114,"r"],["t",230,124,"R/2","m"],["t",260,124,"R","m"],["l",230,108,230,112],["l",260,108,260,112],["p","M200 110 L230 110 M230 68 L260 68 C270 76 288 88 308 94"],["t",260,138,"(2)","m"],["l",40,255,40,185,"a"],["l",40,255,160,255,"a"],["t",36,193,"|B|","e"],["t",164,259,"r"],["t",70,269,"R/2","m"],["t",100,269,"R","m"],["l",70,253,70,257],["l",100,253,100,257],["p","M40 255 L70 255 M70 231 L100 209 C110 221 128 233 148 239"],["t",100,283,"(3)","m"],["l",200,255,200,185,"a"],["l",200,255,320,255,"a"],["t",196,193,"|B|","e"],["t",324,259,"r"],["t",230,269,"R/2","m"],["t",260,269,"R","m"],["l",230,253,230,257],["l",260,253,260,257],["p","M200 255 L230 255 C240 225 250 213 260 209 C270 221 288 233 308 239"],["t",260,283,"(4)","m"],["l",230,68,230,110,"d"],["l",70,231,70,255,"d"]]},o:["Graph (1)","Graph (2)","Graph (3)","Graph (4)"],c:3,s:`Use Ampere's law on a circle of radius r around the axis: B \xD7 2\u03C0r = \u03BC\u2080 I_enclosed.
For r < R/2 the circle encloses no current, so B = 0.
For R/2 < r < R the enclosed current grows as J \u03C0(r\xB2 \u2212 R\xB2/4), so B = (\u03BC\u2080J/2)(r \u2212 R\xB2/(4r)): it starts at 0, rises smoothly and the slope gets smaller.
For r > R all the current is enclosed: B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0r) falls as 1/r, and the field is continuous at r = R, so there is a peak there.
Zero, smooth rise to a peak at R, then 1/r fall is graph (4), option (d).`},{pn:61,y:"AIEEE 2011",tp:"mbs",st:"Superposition and symmetry",q:"A current I flows in an infinitely long wire with cross-section in the form of a semicircular ring of radius R. The magnitude of the magnetic induction along its axis is",o:["(\u03BC\u2080I)/(\u03C0\xB2R)","(\u03BC\u2080I)/(2\u03C0\xB2R)","(\u03BC\u2080I)/(2\u03C0R)","(\u03BC\u2080I)/(4\u03C0R)"],c:0,s:`Split the half ring into thin long wires, each at distance R from the axis. A strip of angle d\u03B8 carries dI = (I/\u03C0) d\u03B8.
Its field at the axis is dB = (\u03BC\u2080 dI)/(2\u03C0R) = (\u03BC\u2080I d\u03B8)/(2\u03C0\xB2R), perpendicular to the line from the strip to the axis.
By symmetry the components along the line of symmetry cancel, and only the component dB sin \u03B8 remains.
B = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(2\u03C0\xB2R) \u222B\u2080^\u03C0 sin \u03B8 d\u03B8 = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(2\u03C0\xB2R) \xD7 2.
B = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(\u03C0\xB2R), option (a).`},{pn:62,y:"AIEEE 2010",tp:"mbs",st:"Superposition and symmetry",q:"Two long parallel wires are at a distance 2d apart. They carry steady equal currents flowing out of the plane of the paper as shown. The variation of the magnetic field B along the line XX\u2032 is given by",fig:{w:360,h:310,alt:"Four graphs of B along the line XX\u2032 through two wires at distance d each side of the centre, currents out of the page; option (2) is zero at the centre, upward near the left wire, downward near the right wire, upward beyond the right wire and downward beyond the left wire",d:[["l",30,50,160,50],["t",26,54,"X","e"],["t",164,54,"X\u2032"],["c",65,50,5],["c",65,50,1.5,"F"],["c",125,50,5],["c",125,50,1.5,"F"],["l",65,2,65,98,"d"],["l",125,2,125,98,"d"],["t",95,110,"(1)","m"],["p","M32 46 C42 44 54 36 62 6"],["p","M128 94 C136 64 148 56 158 54"],["p","M68 6 C76 36 82 48 95 50 C108 52 114 64 122 94"],["l",200,50,330,50],["t",196,54,"X","e"],["t",334,54,"X\u2032"],["c",235,50,5],["c",235,50,1.5,"F"],["c",295,50,5],["c",295,50,1.5,"F"],["l",235,2,235,98,"d"],["l",295,2,295,98,"d"],["t",265,110,"(2)","m"],["p","M202 54 C212 56 224 64 232 94"],["p","M298 6 C306 36 318 44 328 46"],["p","M238 6 C246 36 252 48 265 50 C278 52 284 64 292 94"],["l",30,185,160,185],["t",26,189,"X","e"],["t",164,189,"X\u2032"],["c",65,185,5],["c",65,185,1.5,"F"],["c",125,185,5],["c",125,185,1.5,"F"],["l",65,137,65,233,"d"],["l",125,137,125,233,"d"],["t",95,245,"(3)","m"],["p","M32 189 C42 191 54 199 62 229"],["p","M128 229 C136 199 148 191 158 189"],["p","M68 141 C74 169 84 171 95 171 C106 171 116 169 122 141"],["l",200,185,330,185],["t",196,189,"X","e"],["t",334,189,"X\u2032"],["c",235,185,5],["c",235,185,1.5,"F"],["c",295,185,5],["c",295,185,1.5,"F"],["l",235,137,235,233,"d"],["l",295,137,295,233,"d"],["t",265,245,"(4)","m"],["p","M202 189 C212 191 224 199 232 229"],["p","M298 229 C306 199 318 191 328 189"],["p","M240 183 C248 155 282 155 290 183"]]},o:["Graph (1)","Graph (2)","Graph (3)","Graph (4)"],c:1,s:`Each wire gives B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0r), circling anticlockwise round it (current out of the page).
Midway between the wires the two fields are equal and opposite, so B = 0 at the centre.
Between the wires: near the left wire the field of that wire is up and strong; near the right wire the field of the right wire is down and strong. So B goes from large up, through zero at the centre, to large down.
Outside, to the right both fields point up, and to the left both point down, falling as 1/r.
This is graph (2), option (b).`},{pn:63,y:"AIEEE 2008",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",q:"A horizontal overhead powerline is at height of 4 m from the ground and carries a current of 100 A from east to west. The magnetic field directly below it on the ground is (\u03BC\u2080 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T m A\u207B\xB9)",o:["2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T northward","2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T southward","5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 T northward","5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 T southward"],c:3,s:`Treat the line as a long straight wire: B = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(2\u03C0r).
B = (2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 100)/4 = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 T.
Direction: point the right thumb west (along the current); the fingers curl northward above the wire and southward below it, i.e. the field below the wire is directed southward.
So 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 T southward, option (d).`},{pn:64,y:"AIEEE 2007",tp:"mbs",st:"Superposition and symmetry",q:"Two identical conducting wires AOB and COD are placed at right angles to each other. The wire AOB carries an electric current I\u2081 and COD carries a current I\u2082. The magnetic field on a point lying at a distance d from O, in a direction perpendicular to the plane of the wires AOB and COD, will be given by",o:["(\u03BC\u2080)/(2\u03C0d) (I\u2081\xB2 + I\u2082\xB2)","(\u03BC\u2080)/(2\u03C0) ((I\u2081 + I\u2082)/d)^(1/2)","(\u03BC\u2080)/(2\u03C0d) (I\u2081\xB2 + I\u2082\xB2)^(1/2)","(\u03BC\u2080)/(2\u03C0d) (I\u2081 + I\u2082)"],c:2,s:`The point is at distance d from both wires (it is d above O, on the line perpendicular to the plane).
Each long wire gives B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0d): B\u2081 = \u03BC\u2080I\u2081/(2\u03C0d), B\u2082 = \u03BC\u2080I\u2082/(2\u03C0d).
B\u2081 is perpendicular to the plane containing wire 1 and the point, B\u2082 likewise for wire 2; since the wires are at right angles, B\u2081 and B\u2082 are at right angles.
B = \u221A(B\u2081\xB2 + B\u2082\xB2) = (\u03BC\u2080)/(2\u03C0d) \u221A(I\u2081\xB2 + I\u2082\xB2), option (c).`},{pn:65,y:"AIEEE 2007",tp:"mamp",st:"Ampere's law",q:"A current I flows along the length of an infinitely long, straight, thin walled pipe. Then",o:["the magnetic field at all points inside the pipe is the same, but not zero","the magnetic field is zero only on the axis of the pipe","the magnetic field is different at different points inside the pipe","the magnetic field at any point inside the pipe is zero"],c:3,s:`Take an Amperian circle of radius r < R inside the pipe, coaxial with it.
By symmetry B is the same size all around the circle, so B \xD7 2\u03C0r = \u03BC\u2080 I_enclosed.
The current flows only in the thin wall, so the circle encloses no current: I_enclosed = 0.
Hence B = 0 at every point inside, option (d).`},{pn:66,y:"AIEEE 2007",tp:"mamp",st:"Ampere's law",q:"A long straight wire of radius a carries a steady current i. The current is uniformly distributed across its cross section. The ratio of the magnetic field at a/2 and 2a is",o:["1/2","1/4","4","1"],c:3,s:`Inside (r < a): the circle encloses current i r\xB2/a\xB2, so B \xD7 2\u03C0r = \u03BC\u2080 i r\xB2/a\xB2 and B = \u03BC\u2080 i r/(2\u03C0a\xB2).
At r = a/2: B = \u03BC\u2080 i/(4\u03C0a).
Outside (r > a): all of i is enclosed, B = \u03BC\u2080 i/(2\u03C0r). At r = 2a: B = \u03BC\u2080 i/(4\u03C0a).
The two are equal, ratio = 1, option (d).`},{pn:67,y:"AIEEE 2006",tp:"mamp",st:"Solenoid and toroid",q:"A long solenoid has 200 turns per cm and carries a current i. The magnetic field at its centre is 6.28 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 weber/m\xB2. Another long solenoid has 100 turns per cm and it carries a current i/3. The value of the field at its centre is",o:["1.05 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 Wb/m\xB2","1.05 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 Wb/m\xB2","1.05 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 Wb/m\xB2","1.05 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 Wb/m\xB2"],c:1,s:`Inside a long solenoid B = \u03BC\u2080 n i, so B is proportional to n \xD7 i.
Turns per unit length halves (200 to 100) and the current becomes one third.
B\u2082/B\u2081 = (1/2) \xD7 (1/3) = 1/6.
B\u2082 = 6.28 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 / 6 = 1.05 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 Wb/m\xB2, option (b).`},{pn:68,y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:"Two concentric coils each of radius equal to 2\u03C0 cm are placed at right angles to each other. 3 ampere and 4 ampere are the currents flowing in each coil respectively. The magnetic induction in weber/m\xB2 at the center of the coils will be (\u03BC\u2080 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 Wb/A-m)",o:["5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075","7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075","12 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075","10\u207B\u2075"],c:0,s:`Centre of a coil: B = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(2R), with R = 2\u03C0 cm = 2\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 m.
B\u2081 = (4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 3)/(2 \xD7 2\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2) = 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T.
B\u2082 = (4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 4)/(4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2) = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T.
The coils are at right angles, so the fields are at right angles: B = \u221A(3\xB2 + 4\xB2) \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 Wb/m\xB2, option (a).`},{pn:69,y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:"The magnetic field due to a current carrying circular loop of radius 3 cm at a point on the axis at a distance of 4 cm from the centre is 54 \u03BCT. What will be its value at the centre of the loop?",o:["250 \u03BCT","150 \u03BCT","125 \u03BCT","75 \u03BCT"],c:0,s:`On the axis B = (\u03BC\u2080I R\xB2)/(2(R\xB2 + x\xB2)^(3/2)); at the centre B\u2080 = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(2R).
B/B\u2080 = R\xB3/(R\xB2 + x\xB2)^(3/2).
R\xB2 + x\xB2 = 9 + 16 = 25, so (R\xB2 + x\xB2)^(3/2) = 125, and R\xB3 = 27. B/B\u2080 = 27/125.
B\u2080 = 54 \xD7 125/27 = 250 \u03BCT, option (a).`},{pn:70,y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:"A long wire carries a steady current. It is bent into a circle of one turn and the magnetic field at the centre of the coil is B. It is then bent into a circular loop of n turns. The magnetic field at the centre of the coil will be",o:["nB","n\xB2B","2nB","2n\xB2B"],c:1,s:`Length l of the wire is fixed. One turn: radius R = l/(2\u03C0), B = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(2R).
n turns: n \xD7 2\u03C0r = l, so r = R/n.
B\u2032 = (n \u03BC\u2080I)/(2r) = n \xD7 n \xD7 (\u03BC\u2080I)/(2R) = n\xB2B.
Option (b).`},{pn:71,y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"mamp",st:"Ampere's law",q:"A current i ampere flows along an infinitely long straight thin walled tube, then the magnetic induction at any point inside the tube is",o:["infinite","zero","(\u03BC\u2080)/(4\u03C0) \xB7 (2i)/r tesla","(2i)/r tesla"],c:1,s:`Take a circle inside the tube, coaxial with it, and apply Ampere's law: \u222EB\xB7dl = \u03BC\u2080 I_enclosed.
The current flows only in the thin wall, so nothing is enclosed: I_enclosed = 0.
By symmetry B is the same all round the circle, so B \xD7 2\u03C0r = 0.
B = 0 inside, option (b).`},{pn:72,y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:"If in a circular coil A of radius R, current I is flowing and in another coil B of radius 2R a current 2I is flowing, then the ratio of the magnetic fields, B_A and B_B, produced by them will be",o:["1","2","1/2","4"],c:0,s:`Centre of a circular coil: B = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(2R).
B_A = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(2R).
B_B = (\u03BC\u2080 \xD7 2I)/(2 \xD7 2R) = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(2R).
So B_A/B_B = 1, option (a).`},{pn:73,y:"IIT-JEE 2002",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",q:"A long straight wire along the z-axis carries a current I in the negative z direction. The magnetic vector field B at a point having coordinates (x, y) in the z = 0 plane is",o:["\u03BC\u2080I(y\xEE \u2212 x\u0135)/(2\u03C0(x\xB2 + y\xB2))","\u03BC\u2080I(x\xEE + y\u0135)/(2\u03C0(x\xB2 + y\xB2))","\u03BC\u2080I(x\u0135 \u2212 y\xEE)/(2\u03C0(x\xB2 + y\xB2))","\u03BC\u2080I(x\xEE \u2212 y\u0135)/(2\u03C0(x\xB2 + y\xB2))"],c:0,s:`A long straight wire gives a field B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0r) that circles the wire, so B is perpendicular to the line from the wire to the point.
Here r\xB2 = x\xB2 + y\xB2, and the position vector of the point is r = x\xEE + y\u0135.
A vector perpendicular to x\xEE + y\u0135 is either (\u2212y\xEE + x\u0135) or (y\xEE \u2212 x\u0135); both have length r.
For current along +z the right-hand rule gives an anticlockwise circulation seen from +z, that is B \u221D (\u2212y\xEE + x\u0135).
Our current is along \u2212z, so the circulation is reversed: B \u221D (y\xEE \u2212 x\u0135).
Magnitude \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0r) times the unit vector (y\xEE \u2212 x\u0135)/r gives B = \u03BC\u2080I(y\xEE \u2212 x\u0135)/(2\u03C0(x\xB2 + y\xB2)).
This is option (a).`},{pn:74,y:"IIT-JEE 2001",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:"A coil having N turns is wound tightly in the form of a spiral with inner and outer radii a and b respectively. When a current I passes through the coil, the magnetic field at the centre is",o:["\u03BC\u2080NI/b","2\u03BC\u2080NI/a","(\u03BC\u2080NI)/(2(b \u2212 a)) \xB7 ln(b/a)","(\u03BC\u2080IN)/(2(b \u2212 a)) \xB7 ln(a/b)"],c:2,s:`The spiral has N turns spread evenly between radius a and radius b, so the number of turns per unit radius is N/(b \u2212 a).
Take a thin ring of the spiral between radius r and r + dr. It holds dN = N dr/(b \u2212 a) turns.
Each turn of radius r gives \u03BC\u2080I/(2r) at the centre, so the ring gives dB = \u03BC\u2080I dN/(2r) = \u03BC\u2080NI dr/(2(b \u2212 a)r).
All rings turn the current the same way, so their fields add: integrate from r = a to r = b.
B = (\u03BC\u2080NI)/(2(b \u2212 a)) \u222B dr/r = (\u03BC\u2080NI)/(2(b \u2212 a)) \xB7 ln(b/a).
Note: the integral of 1/r is ln r, which is why a logarithm appears; ln(b/a) is positive because b > a.
This is option (c).`},{pn:75,y:"IIT-JEE 2001",tp:"mbs",st:"Superposition and symmetry",fig:{w:300,h:230,alt:"A non-planar wire loop made of two squares of side 2a, one lying flat in the xy-plane and one standing up in the yz-plane along the y-axis, with current I; point P at (a, 0, a)",d:[["l",80,170,250,170,"a"],["l",80,170,80,46,"a"],["l",80,170,130,139,"da"],["t",256,174,"x"],["t",84,42,"z"],["t",136,140,"y"],["p","M56 185 L152 185 L200 155 L104 155 L104 59 L56 89 L56 185","B"],["l",56,185,104,155,"d"],["l",100,185,120,185,"a"],["l",104,130,104,110,"a"],["t",98,124,"I","e"],["t",104,201,"2a","m"],["t",48,137,"2a","e"],["c",128,122,3,"F"],["t",122,116,"P","e"]]},sfig:{w:300,h:230,alt:"The same loop. At P the flat square gives a field B1 along +z and the upright square gives an equal field B2 along +x",d:[["l",80,170,250,170,"a"],["l",80,170,80,46,"a"],["l",80,170,130,139,"da"],["t",256,174,"x"],["t",84,42,"z"],["t",136,140,"y"],["p","M56 185 L152 185 L200 155 L104 155 L104 59 L56 89 L56 185","B"],["l",56,185,104,155,"d"],["l",100,185,120,185,"a"],["l",104,130,104,110,"a"],["t",98,124,"I","e"],["t",104,201,"2a","m"],["t",48,137,"2a","e"],["c",128,122,3,"F"],["t",122,116,"P","e"],["l",128,122,128,88,"a"],["t",132,96,"B\u2081"],["l",128,122,162,122,"a"],["t",164,136,"B\u2082"]]},q:"A non-planar loop of conducting wire carrying a current I is placed as shown in the figure. Each of the straight sections of the loop is of length 2a. The magnetic field due to this loop at the point P (a, 0, a) points in the direction",o:["(1)/(\u221A2)(\u2212\u0135 + k\u0302)","(1)/(\u221A3)(\u2212\u0135 + k\u0302 + \xEE)","(1)/(\u221A3)(\xEE + \u0135 + k\u0302)","(1)/(\u221A2)(\xEE + k\u0302)"],c:3,s:`The loop is the flat square in the xy-plane (x from 0 to 2a, y from \u2212a to a) joined to the upright square in the yz-plane (y from \u2212a to a, z from 0 to 2a), as in the figure.
Idea: add a current I and an opposite current I along the shared edge on the y-axis. The loop becomes two complete square loops with the same sense of current, because the two extra currents cancel.
P(a, 0, a) lies a distance a straight above the centre of the flat square, and a distance a straight in front of the centre of the upright square.
A square loop gives a field along its own axis. The flat square gives B\u2081 along +z; the upright square gives B\u2082 along +x (right-hand rule, see the second figure).
The two squares are identical and P is at the same distance a on the axis of each, so |B\u2081| = |B\u2082|.
Net field = B\u2081k\u0302 + B\u2081\xEE, direction (\xEE + k\u0302)/\u221A2.
This is option (d).`},{pn:76,y:"IIT-JEE 2000",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",fig:{w:300,h:210,alt:"Infinite wire coming from the left to Q and going down to R, a dashed wire QS going right from Q, and the point M above Q",d:[["l",24,120,170,120,"B"],["l",170,120,170,190,"B"],["l",64,120,92,120,"a"],["l",170,150,170,168,"a"],["l",170,120,268,120,"d"],["l",170,120,170,34,"d"],["p","M160 120 L160 110 L170 110"],["t",60,138,"P","m"],["t",178,138,"Q"],["t",178,186,"R"],["t",264,138,"S","m"],["t",178,30,"M"],["t",10,110,"\u221E","m"],["t",182,200,"\u221E","m"],["t",180,112,"90\xB0","s"]]},sfig:{w:300,h:210,alt:"Bent wire PQR with a right angle at Q, point M above Q at distance d, and the extra wire QS going right from Q",d:[["l",24,120,170,120,"B"],["l",170,120,170,190,"B"],["l",64,120,92,120,"a"],["l",170,150,170,168,"a"],["l",170,120,268,120,"d"],["l",170,120,170,34,"d"],["p","M160 120 L160 110 L170 110"],["t",60,138,"P","m"],["t",178,138,"Q"],["t",178,186,"R"],["t",264,138,"S","m"],["t",178,30,"M"],["t",10,110,"\u221E","m"],["t",182,200,"\u221E","m"],["t",180,112,"90\xB0","s"],["l",150,34,150,118,"b"],["t",144,80,"d","e"],["t",222,114,"I/2","m"],["t",100,114,"I","m"]]},q:"An infinitely long conductor PQR is bent to form a right angle as shown in figure. A current I flows through PQR. The magnetic field due to this current at the point M is H\u2081. Now, another infinitely long straight conductor QS is connected at Q so that current is I/2 in QR as well as in QS, the current in PQ remaining unchanged. The magnetic field at M is now H\u2082. The ratio H\u2081/H\u2082 is given by",o:["1/2","1","2/3","2"],c:2,s:`M lies on the straight line QR extended upwards, so the wire QR gives no field at M (the field of a wire at a point on its own line is zero). Only PQ matters at first.
PQ is a semi-infinite wire ending at Q, and M is at a perpendicular distance d from that end. Its field is H\u2081 = \u03BC\u2080I/(4\u03C0d), directed out of the page (right-hand rule).
After QS is added, PQ still carries I, so it still gives \u03BC\u2080I/(4\u03C0d) at M. QR again gives zero.
QS is also a semi-infinite wire starting at Q, with M at distance d from its end, carrying I/2: field = \u03BC\u2080(I/2)/(4\u03C0d) = H\u2081/2.
The current in QS flows to the right, like the current in PQ, so its field at M is also out of the page: the two add.
H\u2082 = H\u2081 + H\u2081/2 = (3/2)H\u2081, so H\u2081/H\u2082 = 2/3.
This is option (c).`},{pn:77,y:"IIT-JEE 2000",tp:"mbs",st:"Superposition and symmetry",fig:{w:340,h:276,alt:"Four graphs of the field B along the line XX\u2032 through two wires a distance 2d apart, with two dotted vertical lines at the wires. (a) up spikes outside left and down spike outside right, S-shaped between; (b) down outside left, up outside right, S-shaped between; (c) down outside left, up outside right, U above the axis between; (d) all curves below the axis, arch between",d:[["t",12,18,"(a)"],["l",26,62,154,62],["t",22,66,"X","e"],["t",158,66,"X\u2032"],["l",65,16,65,108,"d"],["l",115,16,115,108,"d"],["c",65,62,2.2,"F"],["c",115,62,2.2,"F"],["l",90,46,90,28,"a"],["t",94,26,"B","s"],["l",65,116,115,116,"b"],["t",78,128,"d","m"],["t",102,128,"d","m"],["p","M28 60 Q60 58 63 22","B"],["p","M67 22 Q71 58 90 62 Q109 66 113 102","B"],["p","M117 22 Q120 58 152 60","B"],["t",172,18,"(b)"],["l",186,62,314,62],["t",182,66,"X","e"],["t",318,66,"X\u2032"],["l",225,16,225,108,"d"],["l",275,16,275,108,"d"],["c",225,62,2.2,"F"],["c",275,62,2.2,"F"],["l",250,46,250,28,"a"],["t",254,26,"B","s"],["l",225,116,275,116,"b"],["t",238,128,"d","m"],["t",262,128,"d","m"],["p","M188 64 Q220 66 223 102","B"],["p","M227 22 Q231 58 250 62 Q269 66 273 102","B"],["p","M277 22 Q280 56 312 60","B"],["t",12,156,"(c)"],["l",26,200,154,200],["t",22,204,"X","e"],["t",158,204,"X\u2032"],["l",65,154,65,246,"d"],["l",115,154,115,246,"d"],["c",65,200,2.2,"F"],["c",115,200,2.2,"F"],["l",90,184,90,166,"a"],["t",94,164,"B","s"],["l",65,254,115,254,"b"],["t",78,266,"d","m"],["t",102,266,"d","m"],["p","M28 202 Q60 204 63 240","B"],["p","M67 160 Q90 230 113 160","B"],["p","M117 160 Q120 196 152 198","B"],["t",172,156,"(d)"],["l",186,200,314,200],["t",182,204,"X","e"],["t",318,204,"X\u2032"],["l",225,154,225,246,"d"],["l",275,154,275,246,"d"],["c",225,200,2.2,"F"],["c",275,200,2.2,"F"],["l",250,184,250,166,"a"],["t",254,164,"B","s"],["l",225,254,275,254,"b"],["t",238,266,"d","m"],["t",262,266,"d","m"],["p","M188 202 Q220 204 223 240","B"],["p","M227 240 Q250 170 273 240","B"],["p","M277 240 Q280 204 312 202","B"]]},q:"Two long parallel wires are at a distance 2d apart. They carry steady equal currents flowing out of the plane of the paper, as shown. The variation of the magnetic field B along the line XX\u2032 is given by",o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:1,s:`Each wire gives a circular field, anticlockwise for current out of the page. On the line XX\u2032 the field of each wire is perpendicular to the line, up or down, with size \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0r).
Take up as positive. To the right of a wire the field is up (+); to the left of a wire it is down (\u2212).
Far left of both wires both fields point down, so B is negative and grows without limit as we approach the left wire. Far right of both wires B is positive and grows near the right wire.
Between the wires, close to the left wire B is positive (large); close to the right wire B is negative (large).
At the mid-point the two equal fields are opposite, so B = 0 there, and the curve crosses the axis from + to \u2212.
So B is negative on the far left, positive just right of the left wire, falls through zero at the middle, is negative just left of the right wire, and positive on the far right. Only graph (b) shows this.
This is option (b).`},{pn:78,y:"IIT-JEE 1995",tp:"mbs",st:"Superposition and symmetry",q:"A battery is connected between two points A and B on the circumference of a uniform conducting ring of radius r and resistance R. One of the arcs AB of the ring subtends an angle \u03B8 at the centre. The value of the magnetic induction at the centre due to the current in the ring is",o:["proportional to 2(180\xB0 \u2212 \u03B8)","inversely proportional to r","zero, only if \u03B8 = 180\xB0","zero for all values of \u03B8"],c:3,s:`The ring is cut by the battery into two arcs between A and B, joined in parallel across the battery. The arcs have lengths r\u03B8 and r(2\u03C0 \u2212 \u03B8).
The resistance of an arc is proportional to its length, and the voltage across both is the same, so the current in an arc is inversely proportional to its length: I\u2081 \u221D 1/(r\u03B8), I\u2082 \u221D 1/(r(2\u03C0 \u2212 \u03B8)).
Field at the centre from an arc of angle \u03C6 carrying current I is B = \u03BC\u2080I\u03C6/(4\u03C0r). So B\u2081 \u221D I\u2081\u03B8 and B\u2082 \u221D I\u2082(2\u03C0 \u2212 \u03B8).
Now I\u2081\u03B8 = I\u2082(2\u03C0 \u2212 \u03B8), because the currents are inversely proportional to the arc lengths; so the magnitudes of B\u2081 and B\u2082 are equal.
The two currents go round the centre in opposite senses (one clockwise, one anticlockwise), so the fields are opposite and cancel.
The connecting wires point along radial lines through the centre when they are straight, so they give no field there.
Note: this works for every \u03B8, so the net field is always zero. This is option (d).`},{pn:79,y:"JEE Advanced 2021",tp:"mbs",st:"Superposition and symmetry",fig:{w:260,h:210,alt:"Two concentric circular loops in the xy-plane, small one of radius R with current I1 anticlockwise, large one of radius 2R with current I2 clockwise",d:[["l",24,100,196,100,"a"],["l",110,186,110,10,"a"],["t",202,104,"x"],["t",116,12,"y"],["c",110,100,30],["c",110,100,60],["c",110,100,2.2,"F"],["l",80,94,80,108,"a"],["l",50,108,50,94,"a"],["t",76,86,"I\u2081","e"],["t",46,86,"I\u2082","e"],["t",124,96,"R","m"],["t",156,114,"2R","m"],["t",113,114,"O","s"]]},q:"Two concentric circular loops, one of radius R and the other of radius 2R, lie in the xy-plane with the origin as their common center, as shown in the figure. The smaller loop carries current I\u2081 in the anti-clockwise direction and the larger loop carries current I\u2082 in the clockwise direction, with I\u2082 > 2I\u2081. B(x, y) denotes the magnetic field at a point (x, y) in the xy-plane. Which of the following statement(s) is(are) correct?",o:["B(x, y) is perpendicular to the xy-plane at any point in the plane.","|B(x, y)| depends on x and y only through the radial distance r = \u221A(x\xB2 + y\xB2).","|B(x, y)| is non-zero at all points for r < R.","B(x, y) points normally outward from the xy-plane for all the points between the two loops."],c:[0,1],s:`Each loop is a current ring in the xy-plane. By the right-hand rule, the field it makes at any point of its own plane is perpendicular to that plane, so statement (a) is true.
Both rings are circles about the origin, so the picture looks the same when turned about the origin. The size of B can depend only on the distance r from the origin: statement (b) is true.
Inside the small loop the small ring gives a field out of the page (anticlockwise) and the big ring gives a field into the page. At the centre: out \u03BC\u2080I\u2081/(2R), in \u03BC\u2080I\u2082/(4R); since I\u2082 > 2I\u2081 the net is into the page.
Near the small wire (r just below R) the small ring field is huge and out of the page. So B changes from "into" at the centre to "out of" near r = R, and must be zero at some r < R. Statement (c) is false.
Between the loops, the small loop (anticlockwise) gives a field INTO the page there, and the big loop (clockwise) gives a field into the page too. So B points inward, not outward: statement (d) is false.
Note: a field that changes sign from one region to another must pass through zero, which is the trick used in (c).
Correct statements: (a) and (b).`},{pn:80,y:"JEE Advanced 2018",tp:"mbs",st:"Superposition and symmetry",q:"Two infinitely long straight wires lie in the xy-plane along the lines x = \xB1R. The wire located at x = +R carries a constant current I\u2081 and the wire located at x = \u2212R carries a constant current I\u2082. A circular loop of radius R is suspended with its centre at (0, 0, \u221A3 R) and in a plane parallel to the xy-plane. This loop carries a constant current I in the clockwise direction as seen from above the loop. The current in the wire is taken to be positive if it is in the +\u0135 direction. Which of the following statements regarding the magnetic field B is (are) true?",o:["If I\u2081 = I\u2082, then B cannot be equal to zero at the origin (0, 0, 0)","If I\u2081 > 0 and I\u2082 < 0, then B can be equal to zero at the origin (0, 0, 0)","If I\u2081 < 0 and I\u2082 > 0, then B can be equal to zero at the origin (0, 0, 0)","If I\u2081 = I\u2082, then the z-component of the magnetic field at the centre of the loop is (\u2212\u03BC\u2080I/(2R))"],c:[0,1,3],s:`Wire at x = +R, current I\u2081 along +y: at the origin (distance R, to its left) the field is along +z, size \u03BC\u2080I\u2081/(2\u03C0R). Wire at x = \u2212R with I\u2082 along +y: at the origin the field is along \u2212z, size \u03BC\u2080I\u2082/(2\u03C0R). Wires together: (\u03BC\u2080/(2\u03C0R))(I\u2081 \u2212 I\u2082) along +z.
The loop is clockwise seen from above, so its axial field is along \u2212z. The origin is on its axis at distance \u221A3R, so B = \u03BC\u2080I R\xB2/(2(R\xB2 + 3R\xB2)^(3/2)) = \u03BC\u2080I/(16R), along \u2212z.
(a) If I\u2081 = I\u2082 the wires cancel at the origin, leaving the loop field \u03BC\u2080I/(16R) \u2260 0. True.
(b) I\u2081 > 0 and I\u2082 < 0 makes I\u2081 \u2212 I\u2082 > 0, so the wires give +z, opposite to the loop field. Choosing the sizes suitably (\u03BC\u2080(I\u2081 \u2212 I\u2082)/(2\u03C0R) = \u03BC\u2080I/(16R)) gives zero. True.
(c) I\u2081 < 0 and I\u2082 > 0 makes the wire field along \u2212z, the same direction as the loop field, so they add and cannot cancel. False.
(d) At the centre of the loop, if I\u2081 = I\u2082 the z-parts of the two wire fields cancel by symmetry (the loop centre lies on the plane x = 0). The loop itself gives \u03BC\u2080I/(2R) along \u2212z. So B_z = \u2212\u03BC\u2080I/(2R). True.
Correct statements: (a), (b) and (d).`},{pn:81,y:"JEE Advanced 2013",tp:"mamp",st:"Ampere's law",q:"A steady current I flows along an infinitely long hollow cylindrical conductor of radius R. This cylinder is placed coaxially inside an infinite solenoid of radius 2R. The solenoid has n turns per unit length and carries a steady current I. Consider a point P at a distance r from the common axis. The correct statement(s) is (are)",o:["In the region 0 < r < R, the magnetic field is non-zero.","In the region R < r < 2R, the magnetic field is along the common axis.","In the region R < r < 2R, the magnetic field is tangential to the circle of radius r, centered on the axis.","In the region r > 2R, the magnetic field is non-zero."],c:[0,3],s:`There are two sources: the solenoid (field along the axis) and the hollow cylinder (field circling the axis).
Solenoid: B = \u03BC\u2080nI along the axis inside it (r < 2R) and zero outside (r > 2R).
Hollow cylinder, by Ampere's law: B = 0 inside it (r < R) because no current is enclosed; B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0r), tangential, outside it (r > R).
(a) For r < R only the solenoid acts: B = \u03BC\u2080nI \u2260 0. True.
(b) and (c) For R < r < 2R both act: an axial part \u03BC\u2080nI and a tangential part \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0r). The total is neither purely axial nor purely tangential. Both false.
(d) For r > 2R the solenoid gives zero, but the cylinder gives \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0r) \u2260 0. True.
Correct statements: (a) and (d).`},{pn:82,y:"IIT-JEE 1993",tp:"mamp",st:"Ampere's law",q:"A current I flows along the length of an infinitely long, straight, thin-walled pipe. Then",o:["the magnetic field at all points inside the pipe is the same, but not zero.","the magnetic field at any point inside the pipe is zero","the magnetic field is zero only on the axis of the pipe","the magnetic field is different at different points inside the pipe."],c:1,s:`The current is spread evenly over the wall, so the field has circular symmetry about the axis, and the lines of B are circles around the axis.
Take an Ampere loop that is a circle of radius r inside the pipe, with the same axis as the pipe.
By symmetry B has the same size all round the loop, so \u222EB\xB7dl = B(2\u03C0r).
The loop encloses no current, because all the current flows in the thin wall outside it.
Ampere's law: B(2\u03C0r) = \u03BC\u2080 \xD7 0, so B = 0 at every point inside the pipe.
This is option (b).`},{pn:83,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",fig:{w:320,h:178,alt:"A loop made of two semicircles over the line A B O C D: the large one from D back to A with radius R1 about O, the small one from B to C with radius R2 about O; current I flows A to B, along the small arc to C, to D and back along the large arc",d:[["p","M70 130 A90 90 0 0 1 250 130","B"],["p","M100 130 A60 60 0 0 1 220 130","B"],["l",70,130,100,130,"B"],["l",220,130,250,130,"B"],["l",166,40,154,40,"a"],["l",154,70,166,70,"a"],["c",160,130,2.2,"F"],["l",160,130,223.6,66.4,"a"],["l",160,130,117.6,87.6,"a"],["t",214,92,"R\u2081"],["t",136,96,"R\u2082","m"],["l",226,130,242,130,"a"],["t",234,122,"I","m"],["t",70,150,"A","m"],["t",100,150,"B","m"],["t",160,150,"O","m"],["t",220,150,"C","m"],["t",250,150,"D","m"],["l",78,130,92,130,"a"]]},q:"A loop ABCDA, carrying current I = 12 A, is placed in a plane, consists of two semi-circular segments of radius R\u2081 = 6\u03C0 m and R\u2082 = 4\u03C0 m. The magnitude of the resultant magnetic field at center O is k \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T. The value of k is ______ . (Given \u03BC\u2080 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 Tm A\u207B\xB9)",n:1,s:`The straight pieces AB and CD lie on a line through O, so they give no field at O. Only the two semicircles matter.
A semicircle of radius R carrying I gives B = \u03BC\u2080I/(4R) at its centre.
Following the current A \u2192 B \u2192 small arc \u2192 C \u2192 D \u2192 large arc \u2192 A, the small arc is traversed clockwise and the large arc anticlockwise (see the arrows). Their fields at O are opposite.
B = (\u03BC\u2080I/4)(1/R\u2082 \u2212 1/R\u2081) = (4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 12/4)(1/(4\u03C0) \u2212 1/(6\u03C0)).
1/(4\u03C0) \u2212 1/(6\u03C0) = (3 \u2212 2)/(12\u03C0) = 1/(12\u03C0).
B = 12\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 1/(12\u03C0) = 1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T.
So k = 1.`},{pn:84,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",fig:{w:320,h:176,alt:"Parallel wires X and Y 6 cm apart, 5 A upward in X and 4 A downward in Y, and point P 4 cm to the right of Y",d:[["l",60,26,60,130,"d"],["l",180,26,180,130,"d"],["l",60,118,60,46,"a","B"],["l",180,46,180,118,"a"],["t",60,18,"X","m"],["t",180,18,"Y","m"],["t",52,90,"5 A","e"],["t",172,90,"4 A","e"],["l",180,86,260,86,"d"],["c",260,86,3,"F"],["t",268,82,"P"],["t",220,78,"4 cm","m"],["l",60,144,180,144,"b"],["t",120,160,"6 cm","m"]]},q:"Two long parallel wires X and Y, separated by a distance of 6 cm, carry currents of 5A and 4A respectively, in opposite directions as shown in the figure. Magnitude of the resultant magnetic field at point P at a distance of 4 cm from wire Y is x \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T. The value of x is ______ . Take permeability of free space as \u03BC\u2080 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 SI units.",n:1,s:`P lies on the line joining the wires, outside them: 4 cm from Y and 6 + 4 = 10 cm from X.
Field of a long wire: B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0r) = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 I/r.
Wire X: B_X = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 5/0.10 = 1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T.
Wire Y: B_Y = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 4/0.04 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T.
Directions: at P, to the right of X (current up) the field is into the page; to the right of Y (current down) the field is out of the page. They are opposite.
Resultant = B_Y \u2212 B_X = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \u2212 1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 = 1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T.
So x = 1.`},{pn:85,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",q:"A current of 5 A exists in a square loop of side (1)/(\u221A2) m. Then the magnitude of the magnetic field B at the centre of the square loop will be p \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 T, where, value of p is ______ . [Take \u03BC\u2080 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 Tm A\u207B\xB9]",n:8,s:`The centre is at distance d = a/2 from the middle of each side, with a = 1/\u221A2 m, so d = 1/(2\u221A2) m.
One finite straight wire gives B = (\u03BC\u2080I/(4\u03C0d))(sin \u03B8\u2081 + sin \u03B8\u2082). From the centre each end of the side is seen at 45\xB0 from the perpendicular, so sin \u03B8\u2081 + sin \u03B8\u2082 = 2 sin 45\xB0 = \u221A2.
One side: B\u2081 = 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 5/(1/(2\u221A2)) \xD7 \u221A2 = 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 5 \xD7 2\u221A2 \xD7 \u221A2 = 20 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T.
All four sides give fields in the same direction (the current turns one way round the centre), so B = 4 \xD7 20 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 = 80 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T.
80 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T = 8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 T, so p = 8.`},{pn:86,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mamp",st:"Solenoid and toroid",q:"A tightly wound long solenoid carries a current of 1.5 A. An electron is executing uniform circular motion inside the solenoid with a time period of 75 ns. The number of turns per metre in the solenoid is ______ . [Take mass of electron m\u2091 = 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg, charge of electron |q\u2091| = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C, \u03BC\u2080 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 N/A\xB2, 1 ns = 10\u207B\u2079 s]",n:250,s:`The field inside a long solenoid is uniform and axial: B = \u03BC\u2080nI.
An electron moving in a circle inside it feels a magnetic force qvB that supplies the centripetal force: mv\xB2/r = qvB.
This gives the time period T = 2\u03C0r/v = 2\u03C0m/(qB), independent of the speed.
So B = 2\u03C0m/(qT) = 2\u03C0 \xD7 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9/(1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 75 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079) = 5.655 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2070/(1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2076) \u2248 4.71 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T.
n = B/(\u03BC\u2080I) = 4.71 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074/(4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 1.5) = 4.71 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074/(1.885 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) \u2248 250.
So n = 250 turns per metre.`},{pn:87,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mamp",st:"Solenoid and toroid",q:"The magnetic field inside a 200 turns solenoid of radius 10 cm is 2.9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 Tesla. If the solenoid carries a current of 0.29 A, then the length of the solenoid is ______ \u03C0 cm.",n:8,s:`Field inside a long solenoid: B = \u03BC\u2080NI/L, where N is the total number of turns and L the length. The radius 10 cm is not needed.
Solve for the length: L = \u03BC\u2080NI/B.
L = (4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 200 \xD7 0.29)/(2.9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074).
0.29/(2.9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074) = 10\xB3, so L = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 200 \xD7 10\xB3 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 0.02 m = 0.08\u03C0 m.
0.08\u03C0 m = 8\u03C0 cm.
So the answer is 8.`},{pn:88,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mamp",st:"Solenoid and toroid",q:"A solenoid of length 0.5 m has a radius of 1 cm and is made up of 'm' number of turns. It carries a current of 5 A. If the magnitude of the magnetic field inside the solenoid is 6.28 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 T then the value of m is ______ .",n:500,s:`Inside a long solenoid B = \u03BC\u2080(m/L)I, where m/L is the number of turns per metre.
Rearrange: m = BL/(\u03BC\u2080I).
m = (6.28 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 0.5)/(4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 5).
Numerator: 3.14 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3. Denominator: 4\u03C0 \xD7 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 = 6.28 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076.
m = 3.14 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3/(6.28 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) = 500.
The radius (1 cm) is not needed because the solenoid is long compared with its radius.`},{pn:89,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",q:"A regular polygon of 6 sides is formed by bending a wire of length 4\u03C0 meter. If an electric current of 4\u03C0\u221A3 A is flowing through the sides of the polygon, the magnetic field at the centre of the polygon would be x \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T. The value of x is ______ .",n:72,s:`Each side is a = 4\u03C0/6 = 2\u03C0/3 m. The centre of a regular hexagon is at distance d = a\u221A3/2 = \u03C0/\u221A3 m from each side.
One side seen from the centre subtends 30\xB0 on each side of the perpendicular: B\u2081 = (\u03BC\u2080I/(4\u03C0d))(sin 30\xB0 + sin 30\xB0) = 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 (I/d).
I/d = 4\u03C0\u221A3/(\u03C0/\u221A3) = 12, so B\u2081 = 12 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T.
All six sides give fields in the same direction, so B = 6 \xD7 12 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 = 72 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T.
So x = 72.`},{pn:90,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",fig:{w:320,h:170,alt:"Two long parallel wires 5.0 cm apart carrying 10 A each, the left one upward and the right one downward, with point P midway between them",d:[["l",100,24,100,130,"d"],["l",220,24,220,130,"d"],["l",100,110,100,56,"aB"],["l",220,50,220,104,"aB"],["l",100,110,100,56,"a"],["l",220,50,220,104,"a"],["c",160,86,3,"F"],["t",160,76,"P","m"],["l",100,148,220,148,"b"],["t",160,164,"5.0 cm","m"]]},q:"Two long, straight wires carry equal currents in opposite directions as shown in figure. The separation between the wires is 5.0 cm. The magnitude of the magnetic field at a point P midway between the wires is ______ \u03BCT. (Given : \u03BC\u2080 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 TmA\u207B\xB9)",n:160,s:`P is midway, so it is 2.5 cm = 0.025 m from each wire.
Field of one long wire: B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0r) = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 10/0.025 = 8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T.
Left wire (current up): at P, to its right, the field points into the page. Right wire (current down): at P, to its left, the field also points into the page.
Between two opposite currents the fields point the same way, so they add.
B = 2 \xD7 8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T = 160 \u03BCT.`},{pn:91,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",fig:{w:320,h:170,alt:"A wire loop made of a large upper semicircle of radius R2 and a small lower semicircle of radius R1, both centred at O and joined by short straight pieces on the line through O",d:[["p","M100 90 A60 60 0 0 1 220 90","B"],["l",220,90,190,90,"B"],["p","M190 90 A30 30 0 0 1 130 90","B"],["l",130,90,100,90,"B"],["c",160,90,2.5,"F"],["t",160,84,"O","m"],["l",160,90,202.4,47.6,"a"],["t",172,66,"R\u2082","e"],["l",160,90,181.2,111.2,"a"],["t",198,120,"R\u2081"]]},q:"The magnetic field at the centre of a wire loop formed by two semicircular wires of radii R\u2081 = 2\u03C0 m and R\u2082 = 4\u03C0 m, carrying current I = 4 A as per figure given below is \u03B1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T. The value of \u03B1 is ______ . (Centre O is common for all segments).",n:3,s:`The straight pieces lie on a line through O, so they give no field at O. Only the two semicircles matter; each gives B = \u03BC\u2080I/(4R).
The loop is one closed circuit: going round the big upper arc and then the small lower arc, the current turns the same way (both clockwise, or both anticlockwise) about O. So their fields point the same way and add.
B = (\u03BC\u2080I/4)(1/R\u2081 + 1/R\u2082) = (4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 4/4)(1/(2\u03C0) + 1/(4\u03C0)).
1/(2\u03C0) + 1/(4\u03C0) = 3/(4\u03C0).
B = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 3/(4\u03C0) = 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T.
So \u03B1 = 3.`},{pn:92,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",q:"The current 5 A flows in a square loop of sides 1 m is placed in air. The magnetic field at the centre of the loop is X\u221A2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T. The value of X is ______ .",n:40,s:`Distance from the centre to each side: d = 0.5 m.
One side seen from the centre: both ends are at 45\xB0 from the perpendicular, so sin 45\xB0 + sin 45\xB0 = \u221A2.
B\u2081 = (\u03BC\u2080I/(4\u03C0d))(sin \u03B8\u2081 + sin \u03B8\u2082) = 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 (5/0.5) \xD7 \u221A2 = 10\u221A2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T.
The four sides give fields in the same direction, so B = 4 \xD7 10\u221A2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 = 40\u221A2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T.
Comparing with X\u221A2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077, X = 40.`},{pn:93,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:"Two circular coils P and Q of 100 turns each have same radius of \u03C0 cm. The currents in P and Q are 1A and 2A respectively. P and Q are placed with their planes mutually perpendicular with their centers coincide. The resultant magnetic field induction at the center of the coils is \u221Ax mT, where x = ______ . [Use \u03BC\u2080 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 TmA\u207B\xB9]",n:20,s:`Field at the centre of a coil of N turns: B = \u03BC\u2080NI/(2R). Here R = \u03C0 cm = \u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 m.
Coil P: B_P = (4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 100 \xD7 1)/(2\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2) = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 T = 2 mT.
Coil Q: B_Q = (4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 100 \xD7 2)/(2\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2) = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 T = 4 mT.
The coil planes are perpendicular, so the axes (and the two fields) are perpendicular: add them as vectors.
B = \u221A(B_P\xB2 + B_Q\xB2) = \u221A(4 + 16) = \u221A20 mT.
So x = 20.`},{pn:94,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:"A closely wounded circular coil of radius 5 cm produces a magnetic field of 37.68 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T at its center. The current through the coil is ______ A. [Given, number of turns in the coil is 100 and \u03C0 = 3.14]",n:3,s:`At the centre of a closely wound coil of N turns: B = \u03BC\u2080NI/(2R).
Solve for the current: I = 2RB/(\u03BC\u2080N).
2RB = 2 \xD7 0.05 \xD7 37.68 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 = 3.768 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074.
\u03BC\u2080N = 4 \xD7 3.14 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 100 = 1.256 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074.
I = 3.768 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074/(1.256 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074) = 3 A.`},{pn:95,y:"IIT-JEE 2012",tp:"mamp",st:"Ampere's law",fig:{w:280,h:200,alt:"Cross-section of a long cylinder of diameter 2a with an off-centre cylindrical hole of diameter a touching the surface on the right; O is the centre of the big cylinder, P is on its left surface on the line through O",d:[["p","M70 100 A70 70 0 1 1 210 100 A70 70 0 1 1 70 100 Z M140 100 A35 35 0 1 0 210 100 A35 35 0 1 0 140 100 Z","f"],["c",140,100,70],["c",175,100,35],["l",30,100,250,100,"d"],["c",70,100,3,"F"],["c",140,100,3,"F"],["t",58,118,"P","e"],["t",132,118,"O","e"],["l",140,88,210,88,"b"],["t",175,82,"a","m"],["l",70,172,210,172,"b"],["t",140,188,"2a","m"]]},sfig:{w:280,h:232,alt:"The same cross-section; centre O of the big cylinder and centre C of the hole. P is a distance a from O and 3a/2 from C. At P the field B1 of the full cylinder points down and the field B2 of the hole points up",d:[["c",140,100,70],["c",175,100,35],["l",30,100,250,100,"d"],["c",70,100,3,"F"],["c",140,100,3,"F"],["c",175,100,3,"F"],["t",58,116,"P","e"],["t",132,116,"O","e"],["t",175,90,"C","m"],["l",70,100,70,140,"a"],["t",62,152,"B\u2081","e"],["l",84,100,84,86,"a"],["t",90,84,"B\u2082"],["l",70,176,140,176,"b"],["t",105,192,"a","m"],["l",70,206,175,206,"b"],["t",122,222,"3a/2","m"]]},q:"A cylindrical cavity of diameter a exists inside a cylinder of diameter 2a as shown in the figure. Both the cylinder and the cavity are infinitely long. A uniform current density J flows along the length. If the magnitude of the magnetic field at the point P is given by (N)/(12) \u03BC\u2080aJ, then the value of N is ______ .",n:5,s:`Superposition trick: the real conductor = a full solid cylinder of radius a carrying density +J, plus a small cylinder of radius a/2 (the hole) carrying density \u2212J. The fields add as vectors.
Full cylinder, at its surface (distance a from its axis O): current I\u2081 = J\u03C0a\xB2, so B\u2081 = \u03BC\u2080I\u2081/(2\u03C0a) = \u03BC\u2080Ja/2, directed one way (down in the figure).
Hole cylinder: current I\u2082 = J\u03C0(a/2)\xB2 = J\u03C0a\xB2/4 in the opposite sense. P is at distance a + a/2 = 3a/2 from its axis C (see the second figure).
B\u2082 = \u03BC\u2080I\u2082/(2\u03C0 \xD7 3a/2) = \u03BC\u2080(J\u03C0a\xB2/4)/(3\u03C0a) = \u03BC\u2080Ja/12, directed the opposite way (up).
The two fields oppose: B = B\u2081 \u2212 B\u2082 = \u03BC\u2080Ja(1/2 \u2212 1/12) = (5/12)\u03BC\u2080aJ.
Comparing with (N/12)\u03BC\u2080aJ, N = 5.`},{pn:96,y:"IIT-JEE 2009",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",sfig:{w:280,h:210,alt:"Right-angled triangle PQR with the right angle at P, PQ = 3x vertical, PR = 4x horizontal and QR = 5x; the perpendicular PF from P to QR is dashed",d:[["p","M60 170 L60 50 L220 170 Z","B"],["p","M60 158 L72 158 L72 170"],["l",60,170,117.6,93.2,"d"],["p","M111.2 88.4 L106.4 94.8 L112.8 99.6"],["t",50,178,"P","e"],["t",50,50,"Q","e"],["t",228,176,"R"],["t",124,86,"F"],["t",50,112,"3x","e"],["t",140,192,"4x","m"],["t",172,118,"5x"],["t",74,142,"d","m"]]},q:"A steady current I goes through a wire loop PQR having shape of a right angled triangle with PQ = 3x, PR = 4x and QR = 5x. If the magnitude of the magnetic field at P due to this loop is k(\u03BC\u2080I/(48\u03C0x)), find the value of k.",n:7,s:`The sides PQ and PR pass through P itself, so they give no field at P (a point on the wire's own line). Only side QR matters.
Distance from P to QR (the height on the hypotenuse): d = (3x \xD7 4x)/5x = 12x/5.
The foot F of the perpendicular splits QR into QF and FR. In triangle PQF: QF = \u221A(PQ\xB2 \u2212 d\xB2) = \u221A(9 \u2212 144/25) x = 9x/5, so sin \u03B8\u2081 = QF/PQ = 3/5. Similarly FR = 16x/5 and sin \u03B8\u2082 = FR/PR = 4/5.
For a finite straight wire: B = (\u03BC\u2080I/(4\u03C0d))(sin \u03B8\u2081 + sin \u03B8\u2082).
B = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(4\u03C0 \xD7 12x/5) \xD7 (3/5 + 4/5) = (5\u03BC\u2080I)/(48\u03C0x) \xD7 (7/5) = 7\u03BC\u2080I/(48\u03C0x).
So k = 7.`},{pn:97,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mamp",st:"Ampere's law",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
List-I (Y vs X):
(A) Y = magnetic susceptibility, X = magnetising field
(B) Y = magnetic field, X = distance from the centre of a current carrying wire, for x < a (a = radius of wire)
(C) Y = magnetic field, X = distance from the centre of a current carrying wire, for x > a (a = radius of wire)
(D) Y = magnetic field inside a solenoid, X = distance from centre
List-II (shape of graph): (I) a straight line rising from the origin; (II) a flat line that drops steeply near the end; (III) a horizontal line; (IV) a curve that falls steeply and then flattens (all drawn in the figure).
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)","(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)","(A)-(IV), (B)-(I), (C)-(III), (D)-(II)","(A)-(I), (B)-(III), (C)-(II), (D)-(IV)"],c:1,s:`For a given material the susceptibility \u03C7 is a constant, so \u03C7 against the magnetising field is a horizontal line: (A) goes with (III).
Inside a wire carrying uniform current (x < a), Ampere's law gives B = \u03BC\u2080Ix/(2\u03C0a\xB2), so B grows in proportion to x: a straight line from the origin, (B) goes with (I).
Outside the wire (x > a), B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0x), so B falls as 1/x: a curve that drops and flattens, (C) goes with (IV).
Inside a long solenoid the field is uniform, B = \u03BC\u2080nI, and it is almost constant until the edge of the solenoid, where it falls: (D) goes with (II).
So (A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II), which is option (b).`,fig:{w:340,h:164,alt:"Four graphs of Y against X. (I) a straight line rising from the origin. (II) a flat line that falls steeply near the end. (III) a horizontal line. (IV) a curve falling steeply and flattening.",d:[["l",22,112,88,112,"a"],["l",22,112,22,28,"a"],["t",16,30,"Y","e"],["t",88,128,"X","m"],["t",18,125,"O","e"],["l",102,112,168,112,"a"],["l",102,112,102,28,"a"],["t",96,30,"Y","e"],["t",168,128,"X","m"],["l",182,112,248,112,"a"],["l",182,112,182,28,"a"],["t",176,30,"Y","e"],["t",248,128,"X","m"],["l",262,112,328,112,"a"],["l",262,112,262,28,"a"],["t",256,30,"Y","e"],["t",328,128,"X","m"],["l",22,112,78,44],["p","M 102 46 L 142 46 C 154 46 158 60 159 108"],["l",182,64,242,64],["p","M 265 34 C 270 92 288 106 322 108"],["t",52,150,"(I)","m"],["t",132,150,"(II)","m"],["t",212,150,"(III)","m"],["t",292,150,"(IV)","m"]]}},{pn:98,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbs",st:"Superposition and symmetry",q:`Match List I with List II.
List I (current configuration) is drawn in the figure: (A) a straight wire with a circular loop of radius r; (B) two parallel straight wires joined by a semicircle of radius r; (C) a straight wire joined to a semicircle of radius r and then to a straight wire going up; (D) a straight wire with a semicircular dip of radius r.
List II (magnitude of magnetic field at point O): (I) B\u2080 = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(4\u03C0r) [\u03C0 + 2]; (II) B\u2080 = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(4r); (III) B\u2080 = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(2\u03C0r) [\u03C0 \u2212 1]; (IV) B\u2080 = (\u03BC\u2080I)/(4\u03C0r) [\u03C0 + 1].
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["(A)-(I), (B)-(III), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)","(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)","(A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(III)","(A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II)"],c:3,s:`Use three standard results at the point O: a full circle gives \u03BC\u2080I/(2r), a semicircle gives \u03BC\u2080I/(4r), and a straight wire that ends level with O (a semi-infinite wire) gives \u03BC\u2080I/(4\u03C0r). A wire whose line passes through O gives zero.
(A): the loop is clockwise, so its field at O points into the page, \u03BC\u2080I/(2r). The straight wire lies a distance r below O and its field at O points out of the page, \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0r). They subtract: B = (\u03BC\u2080I/2\u03C0r)(\u03C0 \u2212 1), which is (III).
(B): the semicircle gives \u03BC\u2080I/(4r). Each of the two straight wires ends level with O and gives \u03BC\u2080I/(4\u03C0r). All three point out of the page, so B = \u03BC\u2080I/(4r) + 2\xB7\u03BC\u2080I/(4\u03C0r) = (\u03BC\u2080I/4\u03C0r)(\u03C0 + 2), which is (I).
(C): the semicircle gives \u03BC\u2080I/(4r) and the lower straight wire gives \u03BC\u2080I/(4\u03C0r), both into the page. The upper straight wire lies on a line through O, so it gives zero. B = (\u03BC\u2080I/4\u03C0r)(\u03C0 + 1), which is (IV).
(D): the two straight parts lie on a line through O and give zero, so only the semicircle acts: B = \u03BC\u2080I/(4r), which is (II).
Note: for a straight piece, the field is zero at any point on the line of the wire, because dl and the line to the point are parallel.
So (A)-(III), (B)-(I), (C)-(IV), (D)-(II), which is option (d).`,fig:{w:340,h:346,alt:"List I: four current configurations. (A) a straight wire with a circular loop of radius r above it, point O at the centre of the loop. (B) a hairpin: two parallel straight wires joined by a semicircle of radius r around O. (C) a semicircle of radius r around O joined to a straight wire at the bottom and a straight wire going up. (D) a straight wire with a semicircular dip of radius r below, O on the line of the wire.",d:[["t",14,40,"(A)","m"],["p","M 30 51 L 167.5 51 A 22 22 0 1 1 182.5 51 L 320 51"],["l",70,51,92,51,"a"],["l",250,51,272,51,"a"],["c",175,30,1.8,"F"],["t",181,28,"O"],["l",175,30,153,30,"d"],["t",164,25,"r","m"],["t",78,66,"I","m"],["t",262,66,"I","m"],["t",14,128,"(B)","m"],["p","M 320 106 L 190 106 A 22 22 0 0 0 190 150 L 320 150"],["l",270,106,248,106,"a"],["l",248,150,270,150,"a"],["c",190,128,1.8,"F"],["t",196,126,"O"],["l",190,128,190,150,"d"],["t",194,144,"r"],["t",262,98,"I","m"],["t",262,166,"I","m"],["t",14,235,"(C)","m"],["p","M 320 257 L 190 257 A 22 22 0 0 1 190 213 L 190 183"],["l",270,257,248,257,"a"],["l",190,197,190,185,"a"],["c",190,235,1.8,"F"],["t",196,233,"O"],["l",190,235,190,257,"d"],["t",194,251,"r"],["t",262,274,"I","m"],["t",204,192,"I"],["t",14,322,"(D)","m"],["p","M 30 310 L 178 310 A 22 22 0 0 0 222 310 L 320 310"],["l",80,310,102,310,"a"],["l",262,310,284,310,"a"],["c",200,310,1.8,"F"],["t",200,302,"O","m"],["l",200,310,200,332,"d"],["t",204,324,"r"],["t",92,326,"I","m"],["t",274,326,"I","m"]]}},{pn:99,y:"IIT-JEE 2007",tp:"mbs",st:"Superposition and symmetry",q:`Two wires each carrying a steady current I are shown in four configurations in column I (drawn in the figure). Some of the resulting effects are described in column II. Match the statements in column I with the statements in column II.
Column I:
(a) Point P is situated midway between the wires (two parallel straight wires, currents in the same direction).
(b) Point P is situated at the mid-point of the line joining the centres of the circular wires, which have the same radii (two coaxial loops).
(c) Point P is situated at the mid-point of the line joining the centres of two circular wires side by side.
(d) Point P is situated at the common centre of the wires (two concentric circles).
Column II:
(p) The magnetic fields (B) at P due to the currents in the wires are in the same direction.
(q) The magnetic fields (B) at P due to the currents in the wires are in opposite directions.
(r) There is no magnetic field at P.
(s) The wires repel each other.`,a:"(a) \u2192 (q), (r); (b) \u2192 (p); (c) \u2192 (q), (r); (d) \u2192 (q), (s)",s:`Note: the printed key says (a)-(p), (b)-(q,s), (c)-(q,s), (d)-(q,r,s); working gives (a)-(q,r), (b)-(p), (c)-(q,r), (d)-(q,s), reading the current arrows of the figure.
Rule used: parallel currents attract, antiparallel currents repel; fields are added as vectors at P.
(a): both currents point the same way. At the midpoint the two fields have equal size and opposite direction, so (q) and (r) hold. Parallel currents attract, so (s) does not.
(b): the arrows on both loops show the same sense of rotation, so on the common axis both fields point the same way: (p). They add, so the field is not zero, and the loops attract.
(c): the left loop is anticlockwise and the right loop clockwise. At P (outside both loops) one field is into the page and the other out of it, equal in size: (q) and (r). The nearest sides carry parallel currents, so the loops attract, and (s) does not hold.
(d): the loops turn in opposite senses, so the two fields at the centre are opposite: (q). The inner loop is smaller, its field is larger, so the field is not zero. Facing parts of the loops carry opposite currents and repel: (s).
So (a) \u2192 q, r; (b) \u2192 p; (c) \u2192 q, r; (d) \u2192 q, s.`,fig:{w:340,h:276,alt:"Column I: (a) two parallel straight wires carrying current in the same direction (right), P midway between them; (b) two coaxial circular loops with a common axis, P on the axis midway; (c) two circular loops side by side, left loop anticlockwise and right loop clockwise, P midway between; (d) two concentric circular loops, outer clockwise and inner anticlockwise, P at the common centre.",d:[["t",10,16,"(a)"],["l",20,30,150,30],["l",75,30,95,30,"a"],["l",20,86,150,86],["l",75,86,95,86,"a"],["c",85,58,2,"F"],["t",92,62,"P"],["t",85,118,"same-direction currents","ms"],["t",190,16,"(b)"],["p","M 232 24 A 11 32 0 1 0 232 88 A 11 32 0 1 0 232 24"],["p","M 292 24 A 11 32 0 1 0 292 88 A 11 32 0 1 0 292 24"],["l",196,56,324,56,"a"],["c",262,56,2,"F"],["t",262,78,"P","m"],["l",221,50,221,64,"a"],["l",281,50,281,64,"a"],["t",262,118,"two coaxial circular loops","ms"],["t",10,152,"(c)"],["c",52,210,28],["c",128,210,28],["c",90,210,2,"F"],["t",90,194,"P","m"],["l",24,204,24,218,"a"],["l",156,204,156,218,"a"],["t",90,262,"two circular loops side by side","ms"],["t",190,152,"(d)"],["c",262,210,44],["c",262,210,18],["c",262,210,2,"F"],["t",265,208,"P"],["l",218,218,218,202,"a"],["l",280,218,280,202,"a"],["t",262,268,"two concentric circular loops","ms"]]}},{pn:100,y:"JEE Main (year not printed)",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",q:`Comprehension. A current loop ABCD is held fixed on the plane of the paper as shown in the figure. The arcs BC (radius = b) and DA (radius = a) of the loop are joined by two straight wires AB and CD. A steady current I is flowing in the loop. The angle made by AB and CD at the origin O is 30\xB0. Another straight thin wire with steady current I\u2081 flowing out of the plane of the paper is kept at the origin.
The magnitude of the magnetic field (B) due to loop ABCD at the origin (O) is`,o:["zero","(\u03BC\u2080I(b \u2212 a))/(24ab)","(\u03BC\u2080I)/(4\u03C0) [(b \u2212 a)/(ab)]","(\u03BC\u2080I)/(4\u03C0) [2(b \u2212 a) + (\u03C0/3)(a + b)]"],c:1,s:`Split the loop into four parts and find the field of each at O.
The straight wires AB and CD lie on lines through O, so they give zero field at O.
An arc of radius r that subtends angle \u03B8 at O gives B = \u03BC\u2080I\u03B8/(4\u03C0r). Here \u03B8 = 30\xB0 = \u03C0/6.
Arc BC: B\u2081 = \u03BC\u2080I(\u03C0/6)/(4\u03C0b) = \u03BC\u2080I/(24b). Arc DA: B\u2082 = \u03BC\u2080I/(24a).
The current goes round the two arcs in opposite senses (down along BC, up along DA), so the fields are opposite and subtract.
B = \u03BC\u2080I/24 \xB7 (1/a \u2212 1/b) = \u03BC\u2080I(b \u2212 a)/(24ab).
So the answer is option (b).`,fig:{w:230,h:200,alt:"A current loop ABCD: arc BC of radius b and arc DA of radius a joined by the straight wires AB and CD, which meet at O with an angle of 30 degrees between them. Current I flows A to B, B to C, C to D, D to A.",d:[["l",107.6,81.9,184.9,61.2],["l",107.6,118.1,184.9,138.8],["p","M 184.9 61.2 A 150 150 0 0 1 184.9 138.8"],["p","M 107.6 118.1 A 70 70 0 0 0 107.6 81.9"],["l",138.3,73.6,154.3,69.4,"a"],["l",190,92,190,108,"a"],["l",154.3,130.6,138.3,126.4,"a"],["l",110,108,110,92,"a"],["t",101.6,73.9,"A","e"],["t",186.9,55.2,"B"],["t",188.9,150.8,"C"],["t",105.6,132.1,"D","e"],["l",40,100,107.6,81.9,"d"],["l",40,100,107.6,118.1,"d"],["t",198,104,"I"],["t",69.8,77,"a","e"],["t",114.5,137.4,"b"],["p","M 72.8 91.2 A 34 34 0 0 1 72.8 108.8"],["t",78,104,"30\xB0"],["c",40,100,1.8,"F"],["t",36,116,"O","e"]]}},{pn:101,y:"AIEEE 2009",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force on a current-carrying wire",q:`Comprehension. A current loop ABCD is held fixed on the plane of the paper as shown in the figure. The arcs BC (radius = b) and DA (radius = a) of the loop are joined by two straight wires AB and CD. A steady current I is flowing in the loop. The angle made by AB and CD at the origin O is 30\xB0. Another straight thin wire with steady current I\u2081 flowing out of the plane of the paper is kept at the origin.
Due to the presence of the current I\u2081 at the origin`,o:["the forces on AB and DC are zero","the forces on AD and BC are zero","the magnitude of the net force on the loop is given by (I\u2081I)/(4\u03C0) \u03BC\u2080 [2(b \u2212 a) + (\u03C0/3)(a + b)]","the magnitude of the net force on the loop is given by (\u03BC\u2080II\u2081)/(24ab) (b \u2212 a)"],c:1,s:`The wire at O produces a magnetic field whose lines are circles around O.
On the straight wires AB and CD (radial lines) the field is perpendicular to the wire, so the force I\xB7l\xB7B is not zero. This rules out (a).
On the arcs AD and BC the wire runs along a circle about O, so the field is parallel to the wire at every point.
The force on a current element is I dl \xD7 B, which is zero when dl is parallel to B. So the forces on AD and BC are zero.
Note: only AB and CD feel a force from I\u2081; the arcs feel none. So the statement about AB and DC being force-free is false.
So the correct statement is (b).`,fig:{w:230,h:200,alt:"A current loop ABCD: arc BC of radius b and arc DA of radius a joined by the straight wires AB and CD, which meet at O with an angle of 30 degrees between them. Current I flows A to B, B to C, C to D, D to A. A wire carrying current I1 comes out of the page at O.",d:[["l",107.6,81.9,184.9,61.2],["l",107.6,118.1,184.9,138.8],["p","M 184.9 61.2 A 150 150 0 0 1 184.9 138.8"],["p","M 107.6 118.1 A 70 70 0 0 0 107.6 81.9"],["l",138.3,73.6,154.3,69.4,"a"],["l",190,92,190,108,"a"],["l",154.3,130.6,138.3,126.4,"a"],["l",110,108,110,92,"a"],["t",101.6,73.9,"A","e"],["t",186.9,55.2,"B"],["t",188.9,150.8,"C"],["t",105.6,132.1,"D","e"],["l",40,100,107.6,81.9,"d"],["l",40,100,107.6,118.1,"d"],["t",198,104,"I"],["t",69.8,77,"a","e"],["t",114.5,137.4,"b"],["p","M 72.8 91.2 A 34 34 0 0 1 72.8 108.8"],["t",78,104,"30\xB0"],["c",40,100,6],["c",40,100,1.8,"F"],["t",30,104,"I\u2081","e"],["t",42,122,"O"]]}},{pn:102,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mamp",st:"Ampere's law",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement (I): When currents vary with time, Newton's third law is valid only if momentum carried by the electromagnetic field is taken into account.
Statement (II): Ampere's circuital law does not depend on Biot-Savart's law.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false.","Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false."],c:1,s:`Statement I: for time-varying currents the electromagnetic field itself carries momentum. The forces on two current elements need not be equal and opposite unless this field momentum is counted. So Statement I is true.
Statement II: Ampere's circuital law can be derived from the Biot-Savart law, so it is not independent of it. Statement II is false.
So the correct choice is (b).`},{pn:103,y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force between parallel currents",q:`Three infinitely long thin wires, each carrying current i in the same direction, are in the x-y plane of a gravity free space. The central wire is along the y-axis while the other two are along x = \xB1d.
(i) Find the locus of the points for which the magnetic field B is zero.
(ii) If the central wire is displaced along the z-direction by a small amount and released, show that it will execute simple harmonic motion. If the linear density of the wires is \u03BB, find the frequency of oscillation.`,a:"(i) B = 0 on the lines x = \xB1d/\u221A3 (in the x-y plane, parallel to the wires). (ii) f = (i)/(2\u03C0d) \xB7 \u221A(\u03BC\u2080/(\u03C0\u03BB))",s:`(i) Take a point on the x-axis at distance x (|x| < d or beyond). Each wire gives a field along z of size \u03BC\u2080i/(2\u03C0\xB7distance). The sign depends on which side of that wire the point lies, so the three terms with signs are 1/x, 1/(x \u2212 d) and 1/(x + d).
Set the sum to zero: 1/x + 1/(x \u2212 d) + 1/(x + d) = 0.
Combine over a common denominator: (x\xB2 \u2212 d\xB2) + x(x + d) + x(x \u2212 d) = 0, so 3x\xB2 = d\xB2 and x = \xB1 d/\u221A3.
Out of the plane (z \u2260 0) the field components cannot cancel, so the locus is the pair of lines x = \xB1 d/\u221A3, z = 0, parallel to the wires.
(ii) Push the central wire up by a small z (see the figure). Each outer wire is at distance r = \u221A(d\xB2 + z\xB2) and attracts it with force per unit length \u03BC\u2080i\xB2/(2\u03C0r).
Only the components toward the plane add up: F = 2 \xB7 \u03BC\u2080i\xB2/(2\u03C0r) \xB7 (z/r) = \u03BC\u2080i\xB2z/(\u03C0(d\xB2 + z\xB2)) \u2248 \u03BC\u2080i\xB2z/(\u03C0d\xB2) for small z.
The force points back toward z = 0 and is proportional to z, so the motion is simple harmonic. With mass per length \u03BB: \u03BB\xB7z\u0308 = \u2212(\u03BC\u2080i\xB2/(\u03C0d\xB2)) z, so \u03C9\xB2 = \u03BC\u2080i\xB2/(\u03C0\u03BBd\xB2).
Note: the outer wires are fixed and the force pulls the central wire back to the plane, that is why it oscillates and does not run away.
Frequency f = \u03C9/2\u03C0 = (i)/(2\u03C0d) \xB7 \u221A(\u03BC\u2080/(\u03C0\u03BB)).`,sfig:{w:320,h:190,alt:"End view of the three wires (current along y, out of the page). The central wire is displaced up by z; the two outer wires attract it back.",d:[["c",70,140,7],["c",70,140,2,"F"],["c",250,140,7],["c",250,140,2,"F"],["c",160,70,7],["c",160,70,2,"F"],["l",160,70,160,140,"d"],["l",70,140,250,140,"d"],["l",160,70,116,106,"a"],["l",160,70,204,106,"a"],["l",160,70,160,118,"a"],["t",70,164,"\u2212d","m"],["t",250,164,"+d","m"],["t",168,160,"0"],["t",170,114,"z"],["t",96,86,"F","m"],["t",226,86,"F","m"],["p","M160 96 A26 26 0 0 0 180.1 86.5"],["t",165,88,"\u03B8"],["t",150,126,"2F cos\u03B8","e"]]}},{pn:104,y:"IIT-JEE 1989",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",q:"A pair of stationary and infinitely long bent wires are placed in the xy plane as shown in the figure. The wires carry currents of i = 10 ampere each as shown in the figure. The segments L and M are along the x-axis. The segments P and Q are parallel to the y-axis such that OS = OR = 0.02 m. Find the magnitude and direction of the magnetic induction at the origin O.",a:"1.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T, perpendicular to the plane of the paper, outwards",s:`The origin O lies on the lines of L and M, so these two segments give zero field at O.
P is a semi-infinite wire that ends at R, level with O, at perpendicular distance d = 0.02 m. Its field is B = \u03BC\u2080i/(4\u03C0d).
B\u2081 = (10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 10)/(0.02) = 5.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T. By the right-hand rule (current down at R, O to its right) it points out of the page.
Q is the same kind of wire, at the same distance. Its current goes up and O is on its left, so its field is also out of the page: B\u2082 = 5.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T.
The two fields point the same way, so B = B\u2081 + B\u2082 = 1.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T, perpendicular to the paper, outwards.`,fig:{w:340,h:232,alt:"Bent wires in the xy plane. Wire L along the x-axis comes in from the left to R, then wire P goes down from R. Wire M comes from the right along the x-axis to S, then wire Q goes up from S. O is the origin, midway between R and S, with OR = OS = 0.02 m. Current i in each wire.",d:[["l",170,222,170,14,"d"],["t",176,18,"y"],["t",322,118,"x"],["l",20,112,140,112],["l",62,112,84,112,"a"],["t",52,104,"L"],["t",20,102,"\u221E","m","s"],["t",72,130,"i","m"],["l",140,112,140,214],["l",140,158,140,180,"a"],["t",126,164,"P","e"],["t",148,176,"i"],["t",140,226,"\u221E","m","s"],["l",200,112,200,10],["l",200,66,200,44,"a"],["t",208,56,"Q"],["t",182,66,"i","e"],["t",200,8,"\u221E","m","s"],["l",320,112,200,112],["l",270,112,248,112,"a"],["t",270,104,"M"],["t",320,102,"\u221E","m","s"],["t",258,130,"i","m"],["t",140,102,"R","e"],["t",200,130,"S"],["t",163,130,"O","e"],["c",170,112,1.8,"F"]]}},{pn:105,y:"IIT-JEE 1988",tp:"mbs",st:"Straight wire and arcs",q:"The wire loop PQRSP formed by joining two semicircular wires of radii R\u2081 and R\u2082 carries a current I as shown in the figure. The magnitude of the magnetic induction at the centre C is ______.",a:"(\u03BC\u2080I/4) (1/R\u2081 \u2212 1/R\u2082), perpendicular to the paper, outwards",s:`The straight pieces PQ and RS lie along a line through the centre C, so they give zero field at C.
A semicircle of radius R carrying current I gives B = \u03BC\u2080I/(4R) at its centre.
Inner semicircle QR (radius R\u2081): B\u2081 = \u03BC\u2080I/(4R\u2081), out of the page, because the current goes right to left over the top (anticlockwise).
Outer semicircle SP (radius R\u2082): B\u2082 = \u03BC\u2080I/(4R\u2082), into the page, because the current goes left to right over the top (clockwise).
The two fields are opposite, so they subtract: B = \u03BC\u2080I/4 \xB7 (1/R\u2081 \u2212 1/R\u2082).
Since R\u2081 < R\u2082 the inner arc wins, so the field is directed perpendicular to the paper, outwards.`,fig:{w:360,h:182,alt:"A wire loop PQRSP made of two concentric semicircles about C: the inner one QR of radius R1 and the outer one SP of radius R2, joined by two short straight pieces PQ and RS along the diameter. Current I goes round the loop.",d:[["p","M 70 150 A 110 110 0 0 1 290 150"],["p","M 100 150 A 80 80 0 0 1 260 150"],["l",70,150,100,150],["l",260,150,290,150],["l",190,70,170,70,"a"],["l",160,40,180,40,"a"],["t",198,66,"I"],["t",190,36,"I"],["c",180,150,1.8,"F"],["t",180,166,"C","m"],["l",180,150,236.6,93.4,"a"],["t",224,118,"R\u2081"],["l",180,150,102.2,72.2,"a"],["t",126,118,"R\u2082","e"],["t",68,166,"S","m"],["t",102,166,"R","m"],["t",260,166,"Q","m"],["t",292,166,"P","m"]]}},{pn:106,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 7 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"Uniform magnetic fields of different strengths (B\u2081 and B\u2082), both normal to the plane of the paper exist as shown in the figure. A charged particle of mass m and charge q, at the interface at an instant moves into the region 2 with velocity v and returns to the interface. It continues to move into region 1 and finally reaches the interface. What is the displacement of the particle during this movement along the interface? (Consider the velocity of the particle to be normal to the magnetic field and B\u2082 > B\u2081)",o:["(mv)/(qB\u2081) (1 \u2212 B\u2081/B\u2082)","(mv)/(qB\u2081) (1 \u2212 B\u2081/B\u2082) \xD7 2","(mv)/(qB\u2081) (1 \u2212 B\u2082/B\u2081)","(mv)/(qB\u2081) (1 \u2212 B\u2082/B\u2081) \xD7 2"],c:1,s:`A charge moving perpendicular to a uniform field goes round a circle of radius r = mv/(qB), so the radius is smaller where the field is stronger.
In region 2 it makes a semicircle of radius r\u2082 = mv/(qB\u2082), so it comes back to the interface a distance AD = 2mv/(qB\u2082) from the start (see the figure).
It then crosses into region 1 and makes a bigger semicircle of radius r\u2081 = mv/(qB\u2081), landing a distance DC = 2mv/(qB\u2081) from D.
The two semicircles bend the same way about the interface but the second one goes back past the start, since r\u2081 > r\u2082 (B\u2082 > B\u2081). So the net displacement is the difference.
AC = DC \u2212 AD = 2mv/(qB\u2081) \u2212 2mv/(qB\u2082) = (2mv/(qB\u2081))(1 \u2212 B\u2081/B\u2082).
So the answer is option (b).`,fig:{w:340,h:196,alt:"Two regions separated by a horizontal interface. Region 1 above has field B1 into the page, region 2 below has field B2 into the page, B2 larger than B1.",d:[["l",20,100,320,100],["t",24,92,"Interface"],["t",34,38,"\xD7","m"],["t",70,38,"\xD7","m"],["t",106,38,"\xD7","m"],["t",142,38,"\xD7","m"],["t",178,38,"\xD7","m"],["t",214,38,"\xD7","m"],["t",250,38,"\xD7","m"],["t",34,70,"\xD7","m"],["t",70,70,"\xD7","m"],["t",106,70,"\xD7","m"],["t",142,70,"\xD7","m"],["t",178,70,"\xD7","m"],["t",214,70,"\xD7","m"],["t",250,70,"\xD7","m"],["t",34,138,"\xD7","m"],["t",70,138,"\xD7","m"],["t",106,138,"\xD7","m"],["t",142,138,"\xD7","m"],["t",178,138,"\xD7","m"],["t",214,138,"\xD7","m"],["t",250,138,"\xD7","m"],["t",34,170,"\xD7","m"],["t",70,170,"\xD7","m"],["t",106,170,"\xD7","m"],["t",142,170,"\xD7","m"],["t",178,170,"\xD7","m"],["t",214,170,"\xD7","m"],["t",250,170,"\xD7","m"],["t",286,22,"B\u2081","m"],["t",286,122,"B\u2082","m"],["t",330,50,"Region 1","e","s"],["t",330,150,"Region 2","e","s"]]},sfig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"The particle starts at A, makes a small semicircle (diameter AD) in region 2, then a large semicircle (diameter DC) in region 1, and ends at C on the interface.",d:[["l",20,100,320,100],["p","M 190 100 A 30 30 0 0 0 250 100"],["p","M 250 100 A 55 55 0 0 0 140 100"],["l",214,130,226,130,"a"],["l",205,45,185,45,"a"],["c",190,100,2.5,"F"],["c",250,100,2.5,"F"],["c",140,100,2.5,"F"],["t",190,92,"A","m"],["t",262,114,"D","m"],["t",140,114,"C","m"],["t",220,152,"2mv/(qB\u2082)","m"],["t",195,28,"2mv/(qB\u2081)","m"],["t",30,28,"Region 1 (B\u2081)","s"],["t",30,170,"Region 2 (B\u2082)","s"],["l",188,186,142,186,"a"],["t",165,198,"AC","m"]]}},{pn:107,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 7 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"A particle of charge q, mass m and kinetic energy E enters in magnetic field perpendicular to its velocity and undergoes a circular arc of radius (r). Which of the following curves represents the variation of r with E? The four graphs (a)-(d) are drawn in the figure.",o:["(a) a curve rising slowly and then steeply from the origin","(b) a curve rising steeply and then flattening","(c) a straight line through the origin","(d) a falling curve"],c:1,s:`The radius of the circular path is r = mv/(qB).
Kinetic energy E = \xBDmv\xB2, so v = \u221A(2E/m).
Substitute: r = (m/(qB))\xB7\u221A(2E/m) = \u221A(2mE)/(qB).
So r is proportional to \u221AE: r\xB2 \u221D E, a parabola lying on its side.
Such a curve starts at the origin, rises steeply and then flattens, which is graph (b).`,fig:{w:340,h:270,alt:"Four graphs of r against E. (a) a curve rising slowly then steeply from the origin; (b) a curve rising steeply then flattening, like a square root; (c) a straight line through the origin; (d) a falling curve.",d:[["l",44,110,154,110,"a"],["l",44,110,44,26,"a"],["t",38,34,"r","e"],["t",154,124,"E","m"],["l",204,110,314,110,"a"],["l",204,110,204,26,"a"],["t",198,34,"r","e"],["t",314,124,"E","m"],["l",44,230,154,230,"a"],["l",44,230,44,146,"a"],["t",38,154,"r","e"],["t",154,244,"E","m"],["l",204,230,314,230,"a"],["l",204,230,204,146,"a"],["t",198,154,"r","e"],["t",314,244,"E","m"],["p","M 44 110 C 94 105 129 80 144 34"],["p","M 204 110 C 214 60 254 40 304 36"],["l",44,230,144,156],["p","M 208 154 C 214 205 244 226 304 228"],["t",34,50,"(a)","e"],["t",194,50,"(b)","e"],["t",34,170,"(c)","e"],["t",194,170,"(d)","e"]]}},{pn:108,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 28 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:'Consider a long thin conducting wire carrying a uniform current I. A particle having mass "M" and charge "q" is released a distance "a" from the wire with a speed v\u2080 along the direction of current in the wire. The particle gets attracted to the wire due to magnetic force. The particle turns round when it is at distance x from the wire. The value of x is [\u03BC\u2080 is vacuum permeability]',o:["a [1 \u2212 (Mv\u2080)/(q\u03BC\u2080I)]","a/2","a [1 \u2212 (Mv\u2080)/(2q\u03BC\u2080I)]","a e^(\u22124\u03C0Mv\u2080/(q\u03BC\u2080I))"],c:3,s:`Take the wire along y with current up, and x as the distance from it. The field is B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0x), perpendicular to the plane of motion.
The magnetic force does no work, so the speed stays v\u2080 all the time.
Component equation along the wire: M dv_y/dt = +q v_x B = q (dx/dt) \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0x).
Integrate from the start (x = a, v_y = v\u2080) to the turning point (x, where the particle is moving parallel to the wire, back down, v_y = \u2212v\u2080): M(\u2212v\u2080 \u2212 v\u2080) = (q\u03BC\u2080I/2\u03C0) ln(x/a).
So ln(x/a) = \u22124\u03C0Mv\u2080/(q\u03BC\u2080I).
Note: at the turning point the particle is closest to the wire, so v_x = 0 and its whole speed v\u2080 is along the wire, pointing opposite to the current.
x = a e^(\u22124\u03C0Mv\u2080/(q\u03BC\u2080I)), which is option (d).`,sfig:{w:300,h:200,alt:"A long wire carries current I upward. A particle starts at distance a from the wire moving up, bends toward the wire and turns round at distance x.",d:[["l",50,10,50,190],["l",50,50,50,30,"a"],["t",60,34,"I"],["l",50,182,230,182,"d"],["l",50,174,200,174,"b"],["t",125,168,"a","m"],["p","M 200 174 C 200 70 112 50 112 112","B"],["l",200,150,200,126,"a"],["t",208,140,"v\u2080"],["l",50,120,112,120,"b"],["t",81,114,"x","m"],["c",200,174,3,"F"],["c",112,112,3,"F"]]}},{pn:109,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:"An electron is projected with uniform velocity along the axis inside a current carrying long solenoid. Then",o:["the electron will continue to move with uniform velocity along the axis of the solenoid.","the electron will be accelerated along the axis.","the electron path will be circular about the axis.","the electron will experience a force at 45\xB0 to the axis and execute a helical path."],c:0,s:`Inside a long solenoid the magnetic field is uniform and points along the axis.
The electron also moves along the axis, so its velocity v is parallel to B.
The magnetic force is F = qv \xD7 B, and the cross product of two parallel vectors is zero. So F = 0.
With no force there is no acceleration: the electron keeps its velocity and moves straight along the axis.
So the answer is option (a).`},{pn:110,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:"The electrostatic force (F\u2081) and magnetic force (F\u2082) acting on a charge q moving with velocity v can be written (all quantities in bold are vectors)",o:["F\u2081 = qB, F\u2082 = q(B \xD7 v)","F\u2081 = qE, F\u2082 = q(B \xD7 v)","F\u2081 = qE, F\u2082 = q(v \xD7 B)","F\u2081 = qv\xB7E, F\u2082 = q(B\xB7v)"],c:2,s:`The electric force on a charge in a field E is F\u2081 = qE, a vector along E.
The magnetic force is the Lorentz force F\u2082 = q(v \xD7 B), with the order v first, then B.
Options with B \xD7 v have the opposite sign, and options with a dot product give a number and not a force (a vector), so they are wrong.
So the pair is F\u2081 = qE, F\u2082 = q(v \xD7 B), option (c).`},{pn:111,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"A proton and a deutron (q = +e, m = 2.0u) having same kinetic energies enter a region of uniform magnetic field B, moving perpendicular to B. The ratio of the radius r_d of deutron path to the radius r_p of the proton path is",o:["1 : 2","1 : \u221A2","1 : 1","\u221A2 : 1"],c:3,s:`In a magnetic field the radius is r = mv/(qB) = \u221A(2mK)/(qB), using momentum p = \u221A(2mK) for kinetic energy K.
K, q and B are the same for both, so r \u221D \u221Am.
The deutron mass is twice the proton mass (2.0u against 1.0u): r_d/r_p = \u221A(2m/m) = \u221A2.
So r_d : r_p = \u221A2 : 1, option (d).`},{pn:112,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmot",st:"Velocity selector and crossed fields",q:`A proton moving with a constant velocity passes through a region of space without any change in its velocity. If E and B represent the electric and magnetic fields respectively, then the region of space may have
(A) E = 0, B = 0
(B) E = 0, B \u2260 0
(C) E \u2260 0, B = 0
(D) E \u2260 0, B \u2260 0
Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["(B), (C) and (D) only","(A), (B) and (C) only","(A), (B) and (D) only","(A), (C) and (D) only"],c:2,s:`The velocity stays constant, so the net force on the proton must be zero.
(A): no fields, no force. This is possible.
(B): E = 0 and B \u2260 0. The magnetic force qv \xD7 B is zero if B is parallel to v. This is possible.
(C): only an electric field. The force qE is not zero, so the velocity would change. Not possible.
(D): both fields. If E and B are at right angles to each other and to v, with E = vB, the electric and magnetic forces cancel. This is possible.
So (A), (B) and (D) only, option (c).`},{pn:113,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"Two particles X and Y having equal charges are being accelerated through the same potential difference. Thereafter they enter normally in a region of uniform magnetic field and describes circular paths of radii R\u2081 and R\u2082 respectively. The mass ratio of X and Y is",o:["(R\u2081/R\u2082)\xB2","(R\u2082/R\u2081)\xB2","(R\u2081/R\u2082)","(R\u2082/R\u2081)"],c:0,s:`Acceleration through potential V gives kinetic energy qV = \xBDmv\xB2, so v = \u221A(2qV/m).
In the field the radius is r = mv/(qB) = (1/B)\xB7\u221A(2mV/q).
V, q and B are the same, so r\xB2 \u221D m, that is r \u221D \u221Am.
(R\u2081/R\u2082)\xB2 = m_X/m_Y.
So the mass ratio of X and Y is (R\u2081/R\u2082)\xB2, option (a).`},{pn:114,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"A charge particle moving in a magnetic field B, has the components of velocity along B as well as perpendicular to B. The path of the charge particle will be",o:["helical path with the axis perpendicular to the direction of magnetic field B","helical path with the axis along magnetic field B","circular path","straight along the direction of magnetic field B."],c:1,s:`Split the velocity into two parts: v\u2225 along B and v\u22A5 perpendicular to B.
The part v\u22A5 gives a force qv\u22A5B, which keeps turning the particle in a circle in the plane perpendicular to B.
The part v\u2225 gives no force, so the particle keeps drifting along B at a steady speed.
A circle in one plane plus a steady drift along the perpendicular direction gives a helix (spring shape) whose axis is along B.
So the answer is option (b).`},{pn:115,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:`An electron is allowed to move with constant velocity along the axis of current carrying straight solenoid.
A. The electron will experience magnetic force along the axis of the solenoid.
B. The electron will not experience magnetic force.
C. The electron will continue to move along the axis of the solenoid.
D. The electron will be accelerated along the axis of the solenoid.
E. The electron will follow parabolic path inside the solenoid.`,o:["B and E only","B and C only","A and D only","B, C and D only"],c:1,s:`Inside a long solenoid the field is uniform and along the axis, and the electron's velocity is also along the axis.
Since v is parallel to B, the force qv \xD7 B = 0. So B is true and A is false.
With zero force there is no acceleration (D false) and no bending (E false); the electron keeps moving straight along the axis (C true).
So the true statements are B and C only, option (b).`},{pn:116,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:`An electron is moving along the positive x-axis. If the uniform magnetic field is applied parallel to the negative z-axis, then
A. The electron will experience magnetic force along positive y-axis.
B. The electron will experience magnetic force along negative y-axis.
C. The electron will not experience any force in magnetic field.
D. The electron will continue to move along the positive x-axis.
E. The electron will move along circular path in magnetic field.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["B and E only","A and E only","B and D only","C and D only"],c:0,s:`Velocity is along +x and B is along \u2212z, so v is perpendicular to B and a force acts.
For a positive charge the force would be along v \xD7 B = x\u0302 \xD7 (\u2212\u1E91) = +\u0177 (since x\u0302 \xD7 \u1E91 = \u2212\u0177).
The electron is negative, so the force is reversed: it points along \u2212y. So B is true and A is false.
The force is always perpendicular to the velocity and has constant size, so the electron goes round a circle: E is true. It does not keep moving along +x, so D is false.
So B and E only, option (a).`},{pn:117,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"Two charged particles, having same kinetic energy, are allowed to pass through a uniform magnetic field perpendicular to the direction of motion. If the ratio of radii of their circular paths is 6 : 5 and their respective masses ratio is 9 : 4. Then, the ratio of their charges will be",o:["8 : 5","5 : 4","5 : 3","8 : 7"],c:1,s:`For kinetic energy K, the radius is r = \u221A(2mK)/(qB). K and B are equal, so r \u221D \u221Am / q.
Hence r\u2081/r\u2082 = \u221A(m\u2081/m\u2082) \xB7 (q\u2082/q\u2081).
Substitute: 6/5 = \u221A(9/4) \xB7 (q\u2082/q\u2081) = (3/2)(q\u2082/q\u2081).
So q\u2082/q\u2081 = (6/5)(2/3) = 4/5, so q\u2081 : q\u2082 = 5 : 4.
This is option (b).`},{pn:118,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:"A charge particle is moving in a uniform magnetic field (2\xEE + 3\u0135) T. If it has an acceleration of (\u03B1\xEE \u2212 4\u0135) m/s\xB2, then the value of \u03B1 will be",o:["3","6","12","2"],c:1,s:`The magnetic force F = q(v \xD7 B) is perpendicular to B, so the acceleration a is also perpendicular to B.
So the dot product a \xB7 B must be zero.
(\u03B1\xEE \u2212 4\u0135) \xB7 (2\xEE + 3\u0135) = 2\u03B1 \u2212 12 = 0.
So \u03B1 = 6, option (b).`},{pn:119,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmot",st:"Velocity selector and crossed fields",q:"A velocity selector consists of electric field E = E k\u0302 and magnetic field B = B \u0135 with B = 12 mT. The value of E required for an electron of energy 728 eV moving along the positive x-axis to pass undeflected is (Given: mass of electron = 9.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg)",o:["192 kV m\u207B\xB9","192 mV m\u207B\xB9","9600 kV m\u207B\xB9","16 kV m\u207B\xB9"],c:0,s:`The electron passes undeflected when the electric force and the magnetic force balance: eE = evB, so E = vB.
Kinetic energy K = 728 eV = 728 \xD7 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 J = 1.1648 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2076 J.
v = \u221A(2K/m) = \u221A(2 \xD7 1.1648 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2076 / 9.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9) = \u221A(2.56 \xD7 10\xB9\u2074) = 1.6 \xD7 10\u2077 m/s.
E = vB = 1.6 \xD7 10\u2077 \xD7 12 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 = 1.92 \xD7 10\u2075 V/m = 192 kV/m.
This is option (a).`},{pn:120,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmot",st:"Cyclotron and mass spectrometer",q:"A cyclotron is used to accelerate protons. If the operating magnetic field is 1.0 T and the radius of the cyclotron dees is 60 cm, the kinetic energy of the accelerated protons in MeV will be [Use m_p = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 kg, e = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C]",o:["12","18","16","32"],c:1,s:`The proton leaves at the edge of the dee, where the radius of its path is R = mv/(eB). So v = eBR/m.
Kinetic energy K = \xBDmv\xB2 = e\xB2B\xB2R\xB2/(2m).
Substitute: K = (1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079)\xB2 \xD7 (1.0)\xB2 \xD7 (0.60)\xB2 / (2 \xD7 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077).
= (2.56 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2078 \xD7 0.36)/(3.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077) = 2.88 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 J.
Convert: 1 MeV = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3 J, so K = 2.88 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2/1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3 = 18 MeV.
This is option (b).`},{pn:121,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"A proton, a deuteron and \u03B1-particle with same kinetic energy enter into a uniform magnetic field at right angle to magnetic field. The ratio of the radii of their respective circular paths is",o:["1 : \u221A2 : \u221A2","1 : 1 : \u221A2","\u221A2 : 1 : 1","1 : \u221A2 : 1"],c:3,s:`The magnetic force gives the centripetal force: qvB = mv\xB2/r, so r = mv/(qB).
With kinetic energy K, mv = \u221A(2mK), so r = \u221A(2mK)/(qB). Same K and same B, so r \u221D \u221Am / q.
Proton: m, q. Deuteron: 2m, q. \u03B1-particle: 4m, 2q.
r_p : r_d : r_\u03B1 = \u221A1/1 : \u221A2/1 : \u221A4/2 = 1 : \u221A2 : 1.
Option (d).`},{pn:122,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"A proton and an alpha particle of the same velocity enter in a uniform magnetic field which is acting perpendicular to their direction of motion. The ratio of the radii of the circular paths described by the alpha particle and proton is",o:["1 : 4","4 : 1","2 : 1","1 : 2"],c:2,s:`From qvB = mv\xB2/r, the radius is r = mv/(qB).
Same v and B, so r \u221D m/q.
An \u03B1-particle has 4 times the mass of a proton and 2 times its charge, so m/q is 4/2 = 2 times larger.
r_\u03B1 : r_p = 2 : 1, option (c).`},{pn:123,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmot",st:"Cyclotron and mass spectrometer",q:"A charge particle moves along circular path in uniform magnetic field in a cyclotron. The kinetic energy of the charge particle increases to 4 times its initial value. What will be the ratio of new radius to the original radius of circular path of the charge particle",o:["1 : 1","1 : 2","2 : 1","1 : 4"],c:2,s:`In the dee the radius is r = mv/(qB) = \u221A(2mK)/(qB), so r \u221D \u221AK for the same particle and field.
K becomes 4 times, so r becomes \u221A4 = 2 times.
New radius : old radius = 2 : 1, option (c).`},{pn:124,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"Two ions of masses 4 amu and 16 amu have charges +2e and +3e respectively. These ions pass through the region of constant perpendicular magnetic field. The kinetic energy of both ions is same. Then",o:["lighter ion will be deflected less than heavier ion","lighter ion will be deflected more than heavier ion","both ions will be deflected equally","no ion will be deflected"],c:1,s:`For equal kinetic energy, the radius is r = \u221A(2mK)/(qB), so r \u221D \u221Am / q.
Lighter ion: \u221A4/2 = 1. Heavier ion: \u221A16/3 = 4/3.
The lighter ion has the smaller radius, so its path bends more sharply: it is deflected more.
Option (b).`},{pn:125,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:"The magnetic field vector of an electromagnetic wave is given by B = B\u2080 (\xEE + \u0135)/\u221A2 cos(kz \u2212 \u03C9t) where \xEE, \u0135 represent unit vector along x and y-axis respectively. At t = 0 s, two electric charges q\u2081 of 4\u03C0 coulomb and q\u2082 of 2\u03C0 coulomb located at (0, 0, \u03C0/k) and (0, 0, 3\u03C0/k), respectively, have the same velocity 0.5 c \xEE (where c is the velocity of light). The ratio of the force acting on charge q\u2081 to q\u2082 is",o:["1 : \u221A2","2\u221A2 : 1","(\u221A2 : 1)","2 : 1"],c:3,s:`The force on a moving charge is F = q v \xD7 B, so we need B at each position at t = 0.
At z = \u03C0/k: cos(\u03C0) = \u22121. At z = 3\u03C0/k: cos(3\u03C0) = \u22121. The field is the same at both places: \u2212B\u2080(\xEE + \u0135)/\u221A2.
The velocity is the same too, so v \xD7 B is the same vector for both charges.
Therefore F \u221D q, and F\u2081 : F\u2082 = 4\u03C0 : 2\u03C0 = 2 : 1.
Note: the electric field of the wave also pushes the charges with a force proportional to q, so the ratio is still 2 : 1.
Option (d).`},{pn:126,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"A deuteron and an alpha particle having equal kinetic energy enter perpendicularly into a magnetic field. Let r_d and r_\u03B1 be their respective radii of circular path. The value of r_d/r_\u03B1 is equal to",o:["1/\u221A2","1","2","\u221A2"],c:3,s:`For equal kinetic energy, r = \u221A(2mK)/(qB), so r \u221D \u221Am / q.
Deuteron: mass 2 u, charge e. \u03B1-particle: mass 4 u, charge 2e.
r_d \u221D \u221A2/1 = \u221A2 and r_\u03B1 \u221D \u221A4/2 = 1.
r_d/r_\u03B1 = \u221A2, option (d).`},{pn:127,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"Two ions having same mass have charges in the ratio 1 : 2. They are projected normally in a uniform magnetic field with their speeds in the ratio 2 : 3. The ratio of radii of their circular trajectories is",o:["2 : 3","1 : 4","3 : 1","4 : 3"],c:3,s:`r = mv/(qB). The masses and the field are the same, so r \u221D v/q.
r\u2081/r\u2082 = (v\u2081/v\u2082) \xD7 (q\u2082/q\u2081) = (2/3) \xD7 (2/1) = 4/3.
The ratio is 4 : 3, option (d).`},{pn:128,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 16 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:"A charge Q is moving dl distance in the magnetic field B. Find the value of work done by B.",o:["Infinite","1","zero","\u22121"],c:2,s:`The magnetic force on a moving charge is F = Q v \xD7 B, which is always perpendicular to the velocity, hence to the displacement dl.
Work = F \xB7 dl = F dl cos 90\xB0 = 0.
A magnetic field changes the direction of motion but never the speed, so it does no work.
Work done = zero, option (c).`},{pn:129,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 18 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"A proton and an \u03B1-particle, having kinetic energies K_p and K_\u03B1 respectively, enter into a magnetic field at right angles. The ratio of the radii of trajectory of proton to that of \u03B1-particle is 2 : 1. The ratio of K_p : K_\u03B1 is",o:["4 : 1","1 : 4","1 : 8","8 : 1"],c:0,s:`r = \u221A(2mK)/(qB), so r \u221D \u221A(mK)/q for the same B.
Proton: m, q. \u03B1-particle: 4m, 2q.
r_p/r_\u03B1 = [\u221A(m K_p)/q] / [\u221A(4m K_\u03B1)/(2q)] = \u221A(K_p/K_\u03B1) \xD7 (2/2) = \u221A(K_p/K_\u03B1).
Given r_p/r_\u03B1 = 2, so \u221A(K_p/K_\u03B1) = 2 and K_p/K_\u03B1 = 4.
K_p : K_\u03B1 = 4 : 1, option (a).`},{pn:130,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:"A proton, a deuteron and an \u03B1-particle are moving with same momentum in a uniform magnetic field. The ratio of magnetic forces acting on them is _____ and their speed is _____ in the ratio.",o:["4 : 2 : 1 and 2 : 1 : 1","1 : 2 : 4 and 1 : 1 : 2","2 : 1 : 1 and 4 : 2 : 1","1 : 2 : 4 and 2 : 1 : 1"],c:2,s:`The speed is v = p/m. With the same momentum, v \u221D 1/m.
Masses are in the ratio 1 : 2 : 4, so speeds are 1 : 1/2 : 1/4 = 4 : 2 : 1.
The magnetic force is F = qvB, so F \u221D q v. Charges are e, e, 2e.
F \u221D (1 \xD7 4) : (1 \xD7 2) : (2 \xD7 1) = 4 : 2 : 2 = 2 : 1 : 1.
Forces 2 : 1 : 1 and speeds 4 : 2 : 1, option (c).`},{pn:131,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 2 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"A beam of protons with speed 4 \xD7 10\u2075 m s\u207B\xB9 enters a uniform magnetic field of 0.3 T at an angle of 60\xB0 to the magnetic field. The pitch of the resulting helical path of protons is close to (Mass of the proton = 1.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 kg, charge of the proton = 1.69 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C)",o:["2 cm","5 cm","12 cm","4 cm"],c:3,s:`The component of velocity along B is not changed by the field, and the perpendicular component makes the circle. The pitch is the distance moved along B in one period: pitch = v cos 60\xB0 \xD7 T.
T = 2\u03C0m/(qB) = (2\u03C0 \xD7 1.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077)/(1.69 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 0.3) \u2248 2.07 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 s.
v cos 60\xB0 = 4 \xD7 10\u2075 \xD7 0.5 = 2 \xD7 10\u2075 m/s.
Pitch = 2 \xD7 10\u2075 \xD7 2.07 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \u2248 4.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 m \u2248 4 cm.
Option (d).`},{pn:132,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 2 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"The figure shows a region of length l with a uniform magnetic field of 0.3 T in it and a proton entering the region with velocity 4 \xD7 10\u2075 m s\u207B\xB9 making an angle 60\xB0 with the field. If the proton completes 10 revolution by the time it cross the region shown, l is close to (mass of proton = 1.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 kg, charge of the proton = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C)",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"A region of length l with uniform magnetic field B pointing to the right; a proton enters at the left edge with velocity 4 \xD7 10\u2075 m/s at 60\xB0 to the field",d:[["l",80,20,80,180],["l",250,20,250,180],["l",110,45,200,45,"a"],["l",110,85,200,85,"a"],["l",110,125,200,125,"a"],["t",215,40,"B"],["l",80,150,120,126,"a"],["l",80,150,125,150,"d"],["p","M100 150 A20 20 0 0 0 96 142"],["t",88,168,"60\xB0","s"],["t",92,128,"v","e"],["c",80,150,3,"F"],["l",80,192,250,192,"b"],["t",165,178,"l","m"]]},o:["0.88 m","0.22 m","0.11 m","0.44 m"],c:3,s:`The field is along the length l. The proton moves along the field with v cos 60\xB0 = 2 \xD7 10\u2075 m/s (unchanged by the field) while it circles.
Time for one revolution T = 2\u03C0m/(qB) = (2\u03C0 \xD7 1.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077)/(1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 0.3) \u2248 2.19 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 s.
Time for 10 revolutions = 2.19 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 s.
l = 2 \xD7 10\u2075 \xD7 2.19 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \u2248 0.44 m.
Option (d).`},{pn:133,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:"A charged particle carrying charge 1 \u03BCC is moving with velocity (2\xEE + 3\u0135 + 4k\u0302) m s\u207B\xB9. If an external magnetic field of (5\xEE + 3\u0135 \u2212 6k\u0302) \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 T exists in the region where the particle is moving, then the force on the particle is F \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 N. The vector F is",o:["\u22120.30\xEE + 0.32\u0135 \u2212 0.09k\u0302","\u221230\xEE + 32\u0135 \u2212 9k\u0302","\u2212300\xEE + 320\u0135 \u2212 90k\u0302","\u22123.0\xEE + 3.2\u0135 \u2212 0.9k\u0302"],c:1,s:`Use F = q (v \xD7 B), with the cross product as a determinant.
v \xD7 B = \xEE(3 \xD7 (\u22126) \u2212 4 \xD7 3) \u2212 \u0135(2 \xD7 (\u22126) \u2212 4 \xD7 5) + k\u0302(2 \xD7 3 \u2212 3 \xD7 5).
= \xEE(\u221218 \u2212 12) \u2212 \u0135(\u221212 \u2212 20) + k\u0302(6 \u2212 15) = \u221230\xEE + 32\u0135 \u2212 9k\u0302 (in units of 10\u207B\xB3).
q \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 = 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 = 10\u207B\u2079, so F = (\u221230\xEE + 32\u0135 \u2212 9k\u0302) \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 N.
Option (b).`},{pn:134,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 6 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"A particle of charge q and mass m is moving with a velocity \u2212v \xEE (v \u2260 0) towards a large screen placed in the Y-Z plane at a distance d. If there is a magnetic field B = B\u2080 k\u0302, the minimum value of v for which the particle will not hit the screen is",o:["qdB\u2080/(3m)","2qdB\u2080/m","qdB\u2080/m","qdB\u2080/(2m)"],c:2,s:`The magnetic force turns the particle on a circle of radius r = mv/(qB\u2080). It starts moving towards the screen, and the circle bends sideways, so the farthest it gets towards the screen is a distance r.
It just misses the screen when this distance r equals d: mv/(qB\u2080) = d.
So v = qB\u2080d/m is the limiting speed (just touching the screen).
Note: the printed wording says "minimum", but a bigger speed means a bigger circle, so the particle avoids the screen only for speeds up to this value; the answer expected is this limit.
Option (c).`},{pn:135,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 6 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mfrc",st:"Velocity selector and crossed fields",q:"An electron is moving along +x direction with a velocity of 6 \xD7 10\u2076 m s\u207B\xB9. It enters a region of uniform electric field of 300 V/cm pointing along +y direction. The magnitude and direction of the magnetic field set up in this region such that the electron keeps moving along the x direction will be",o:["3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T, along + z direction","5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 T, along \u2212 z direction","5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 T, along + z direction","3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T, along \u2212 z direction"],c:2,s:`To go straight the magnetic force must cancel the electric force: eE = evB, so B = E/v.
E = 300 V/cm = 3 \xD7 10\u2074 V/m. B = (3 \xD7 10\u2074)/(6 \xD7 10\u2076) = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 T.
Direction: the electron is negative, so the electric force is along \u2212y. The magnetic force must be along +y.
For a negative charge F = \u2212e (v \xD7 B). With v along +x, v \xD7 B must point along \u2212y. x\u0302 \xD7 \u1E91 = \u2212\u0177, so B is along +z.
B = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 T along +z, option (c).`},{pn:136,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 6 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:"A particle moving in the xy plane experiences a velocity dependent force F = k (v_y \xEE + v_x \u0135), where v_x and v_y are the x and y components of its velocity v. If a is the acceleration of the particle, then which of the following statements is true for the particle?",o:["Quantity v \xD7 a is constant in time.","F arises due to magnetic field.","Kinetic energy of particle is constant in time.","Quantity v \xB7 a is constant in time."],c:0,s:`Write the equations of motion: m dv_x/dt = k v_y and m dv_y/dt = k v_x.
(b) and (c): a magnetic force is always perpendicular to v, but F \xB7 v = 2k v_x v_y is not zero in general. So F is not magnetic and the kinetic energy changes. (b), (c) and (d) are false; for (d), v \xB7 a = (k/m) 2 v_x v_y changes too.
(a): v \xD7 a has only a z-component, v_x a_y \u2212 v_y a_x = (k/m)(v_x\xB2 \u2212 v_y\xB2).
Let u = v_x + v_y and w = v_x \u2212 v_y. Adding and subtracting the equations: du/dt = (k/m) u and dw/dt = \u2212(k/m) w, so u w = v_x\xB2 \u2212 v_y\xB2 stays constant (one grows as e^(kt/m), the other shrinks as e^(\u2212kt/m)).
So v \xD7 a is constant in time, option (a).`},{pn:137,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 7 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmot",st:"Velocity selector and crossed fields",q:"A particle of mass m and charge q has an initial velocity v = v\u2080 \u0135. If an electric field E = E\u2080 \xEE and magnetic field B = B\u2080 \xEE act on the particle, its speed will double after a time",o:["\u221A3 m v\u2080/(q E\u2080)","3 m v\u2080/(q E\u2080)","\u221A2 m v\u2080/(q E\u2080)","2 m v\u2080/(q E\u2080)"],c:0,s:`Look at the motion along x and in the y-z plane separately.
Along x: B is parallel to x, so it exerts no force along x. Only E acts: v_x = (qE\u2080/m) t.
In the y-z plane: B along x makes the velocity component there turn in a circle, with its magnitude constant at v\u2080. (The electric field has no component here.)
Speed\xB2 = v\u2080\xB2 + (qE\u2080t/m)\xB2. Setting speed = 2v\u2080: (qE\u2080t/m)\xB2 = 3v\u2080\xB2.
t = \u221A3 m v\u2080/(qE\u2080), option (a).`},{pn:138,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:"Proton with kinetic energy of 1 MeV moves from south to north. It gets an acceleration of 10\xB9\xB2 m/s\xB2 by an applied magnetic field (west to east). The value of magnetic field (Rest mass of proton is 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 kg)",o:["7.1 mT","71 mT","0.071 mT","0.71 mT"],c:3,s:`The magnetic force qvB gives the acceleration a = qvB/m, so B = ma/(qv).
Speed from the kinetic energy: K = 1 MeV = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3 J, v = \u221A(2K/m) = \u221A((2 \xD7 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB3)/(1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077)) = \u221A(2 \xD7 10\xB9\u2074) \u2248 1.41 \xD7 10\u2077 m/s.
B = (1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 \xD7 10\xB9\xB2)/(1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 1.41 \xD7 10\u2077) = (1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075)/(2.26 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2) = 7.1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T.
B \u2248 0.71 mT, option (d).`},{pn:139,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmot",st:"Velocity selector and crossed fields",q:`A charged particle of mass m and charge q moving under the influence of uniform electric field E \xEE and a uniform magnetic field B k\u0302 follows trajectory from point P to Q as shown in figure. The velocities at P and Q are respectively, v \xEE and \u22122v \u0135. Then which of the following statements (A, B, C, D) are the correct? (Trajectory shown is schematic and not to scale)
(A) E = (3/4)(m v\xB2/(q a))
(B) Rate of work done by the electric field at P is (3/4)(m v\xB3/a)
(C) Rate of work done by both the fields at Q is zero.
(D) The difference between the magnitude of angular momentum of the particle at P and Q is 2 m a v.`,fig:{w:330,h:260,alt:"A particle goes from P on the y axis at height a, with velocity v along +x, to Q on the x axis at distance 2a, where its velocity is 2v along \u2212y; electric field E along +x and magnetic field B out of the page",d:[["l",50,230,50,20,"a"],["l",50,230,310,230,"a"],["t",44,24,"Y","e"],["t",306,246,"X"],["t",42,246,"O","e"],["c",50,140,3,"F"],["t",44,138,"P","e"],["c",200,230,3,"F"],["t",194,246,"Q","e"],["p","M50 140 H150 C185 140 200 155 200 190 V230"],["l",62,126,100,126,"a"],["t",82,118,"v","m"],["l",200,236,200,258,"a"],["t",208,254,"2v"],["l",30,140,30,230,"b"],["t",24,190,"a","e"],["l",50,214,200,214,"b"],["t",125,208,"2a","m"],["l",110,60,150,60,"a"],["t",130,52,"E","m"],["c",270,70,6],["c",270,70,1.5,"F"],["t",284,74,"B"]]},o:["(A), (C), (D)","(A), (B), (C)","(A), (B), (C), (D)","(B), (C), (D)"],c:1,s:`Magnetic force does no work, so the work-energy theorem for P to Q involves only the electric force.
From the figure P = (0, a), Q = (2a, 0), and E is along x, so the electric force qE does work over the x-displacement 2a: qE \xD7 2a = \xBDm(2v)\xB2 \u2212 \xBDmv\xB2 = (3/2)mv\xB2.
E = 3mv\xB2/(4qa), so (A) is true.
(B): rate of work at P = qE \xB7 v = qE v = (3mv\xB2/4a) v = 3mv\xB3/(4a), true.
(C): at Q the velocity is along \u2212y, so both qE (along x) and the magnetic force are perpendicular to v. The rate of work is zero, true.
(D): L = r \xD7 p. At P, |L| = a \xD7 mv = mva. At Q, |L| = 2a \xD7 2mv = 4mva. The difference is 3mva, not 2mav, so (D) is false.
(A), (B), (C) are correct, option (b).`},{pn:140,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"An electron gun is placed inside a long solenoid of radius R on its axis. The solenoid has n turns/length and carries a current I. The electron gun shoots an electron along the radius of the solenoid with speed v. If the electron does not hit the surface of the solenoid, maximum possible value of v is (all symbols have their standard meaning)",fig:{w:300,h:230,alt:"A long solenoid of radius R with an electron gun on its axis shooting an electron along a radius; the field B points along the axis",d:[["p","M70 40 A50 14 0 1 0 170 40 A50 14 0 1 0 70 40"],["l",70,40,70,180],["l",170,40,170,180],["p","M70 180 A50 14 0 0 0 170 180"],["p","M70 60 A50 14 0 0 0 170 60"],["p","M70 80 A50 14 0 0 0 170 80"],["p","M70 100 A50 14 0 0 0 170 100"],["p","M70 120 A50 14 0 0 0 170 120"],["p","M70 140 A50 14 0 0 0 170 140"],["p","M70 160 A50 14 0 0 0 170 160"],["l",210,190,210,40,"a"],["t",218,112,"B"],["c",120,110,3,"F"],["l",123,110,168,110,"a"],["t",176,104,"R"],["t",64,114,"gun","e"]]},o:["2e\u03BC\u2080nIR/m","e\u03BC\u2080nIR/(4m)","e\u03BC\u2080nIR/m","e\u03BC\u2080nIR/(2m)"],c:3,s:`Inside the solenoid B = \u03BC\u2080nI along the axis. The electron moves perpendicular to B, so it goes on a circle of radius r = mv/(eB).
The circle passes through the axis (where the gun is), so its farthest point from the axis is at distance 2r (the diameter).
To avoid the wall, 2r \u2264 R, so r \u2264 R/2.
mv/(e\u03BC\u2080nI) \u2264 R/2, so v \u2264 e\u03BC\u2080nIR/(2m).
Maximum v = e\u03BC\u2080nIR/(2m), option (d).`},{pn:141,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"A proton, an electron and a Helium nucleus, have the same energy. They are in circular orbits in a plane due to magnetic field perpendicular to the plane. Let r_p, r_e and r_He be their respective radii, then,",o:["r_e < r_p < r_He","r_e > r_p > r_He","r_e < r_p = r_He","r_e > r_p = r_He"],c:2,s:`For equal energy K, r = \u221A(2mK)/(qB), so r \u221D \u221Am / q.
Proton: \u221Am/e. Helium nucleus: mass 4m, charge 2e, so \u221A(4m)/(2e) = \u221Am/e, the same as the proton.
Electron: its mass is about 1/1836 of the proton, and the charge magnitude is e, so its radius is much smaller.
r_e < r_p = r_He, option (c).`},{pn:142,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"An electron, moving along the x-axis with an initial energy of 100 eV, enters a region of magnetic field B = (1.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 T) k\u0302 at S (see figure). The field extends between x = 0 and x = 2 cm. The electron is detected at the point Q on a screen placed 8 cm away from the point S. The distance d between P and Q (on the screen) is (electron's charge = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C, mass of electron = 9.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg)",fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"An electron moving along x enters a magnetic field region of width 2 cm at S, leaves it deflected, and travels straight to point Q on a screen 8 cm from S; P is the point on the screen on the original line and d is the distance PQ",d:[["l",20,130,60,130,"a"],["l",60,95,100,95],["l",60,165,100,165],["l",60,95,60,165],["l",100,95,100,165],["c",70,105,1.2,"F"],["c",85,105,1.2,"F"],["c",70,125,1.2,"F"],["c",85,125,1.2,"F"],["c",70,145,1.2,"F"],["c",85,145,1.2,"F"],["c",70,155,1.2,"F"],["c",85,155,1.2,"F"],["p","M60 130 Q90 130 100 112 L260 30"],["l",260,10,260,190],["c",260,130,3,"F"],["t",254,152,"P","e"],["c",260,30,3,"F"],["t",254,26,"Q","e"],["t",54,150,"S","e"],["l",290,130,290,32,"b"],["t",298,86,"d"],["l",290,130,262,130,"d"],["l",290,30,262,30,"d"],["t",80,196,"2 cm","m"],["t",160,166,"8 cm","m"],["l",62,174,258,174,"b"],["t",110,86,"B","m"]]},o:["11.65 cm","12.87 cm","2.25 cm","1.22 cm"],c:1,s:`Speed from energy: v = \u221A(2K/m) = \u221A((2 \xD7 100 \xD7 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079)/(9.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9)) \u2248 5.93 \xD7 10\u2076 m/s.
Radius in the field: r = mv/(eB) = (9.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 \xD7 5.93 \xD7 10\u2076)/(1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 1.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3) \u2248 2.25 cm.
Inside the field it turns through \u03B8 with sin \u03B8 = 2/2.25 = 0.889, so \u03B8 \u2248 62.8\xB0 and tan \u03B8 \u2248 1.94. The sideways shift inside is r(1 \u2212 cos \u03B8) = 2.25 \xD7 (1 \u2212 0.457) \u2248 1.22 cm.
After leaving the field it moves in a straight line at angle \u03B8 for 8 \u2212 2 = 6 cm along x, which adds 6 \xD7 tan \u03B8 \u2248 11.66 cm.
d = 1.22 + 11.66 \u2248 12.9 cm, closest to 12.87 cm, option (b).`},{pn:143,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmot",st:"Velocity selector and crossed fields",q:"A particle having the same charge as of electron moves in a circular path of radius 0.5 cm under the influence of a magnetic field of 0.5 T. If an electric field of 100 V/m makes it to move in a straight path, then the mass of the particle is (Given charge of electron = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C)",o:["2.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2074 kg","1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 kg","1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 kg","9.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg"],c:0,s:`Straight path means the electric and magnetic forces cancel: qE = qvB, so v = E/B = 100/0.5 = 200 m/s.
With the field alone the circle radius is r = mv/(qB), so m = qBr/v.
m = (1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 0.5 \xD7 0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2)/200 = (4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB2)/200 = 2.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2074 kg.
Option (a).`},{pn:144,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"In an experiment, electrons are accelerated, from rest, by applying a voltage of 500 V. Calculate the radius of the path if a magnetic field 100 mT is then applied. [Charge of the electron = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C, Mass of the electron = 9.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg]",o:["7.5 m","7.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 m","7.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m","7.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m"],c:2,s:`The electron gains kinetic energy eV: \xBDmv\xB2 = eV, so v = \u221A(2eV/m).
v = \u221A((2 \xD7 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 500)/(9.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9)) = \u221A(1.76 \xD7 10\xB9\u2074) \u2248 1.33 \xD7 10\u2077 m/s.
Then r = mv/(eB) = (9.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 \xD7 1.33 \xD7 10\u2077)/(1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 0.1).
= (1.21 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3)/(1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2070) \u2248 7.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m.
Option (c).`},{pn:145,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"The region between y = 0 and y = d contains a magnetic field B = B \u1E91. A particle of mass m and charge q enters the region with a velocity v = v \xEE. If d = mv/(2qB), the acceleration of the charged particle at the point of its emergence at the other side is",o:["(qvB/m)(\xEE + \u0135)/\u221A2","(qvB/m)((\u221A3/2)\xEE + (1/2)\u0135)","(qvB/m)((1/2)\xEE \u2212 (\u221A3/2)\u0135)","(qvB/m)(\u2212\u0135 + \xEE)/\u221A2"],c:2,s:`Note: the book prints the particle velocity as v \xEE and the region as 0 < y < d; the question is solved here for the slab of width d that the particle crosses (the standard form of this question), which gives option (c).
The speed stays constant and the path in the field is a circle of radius r = mv/(qB).
With d = mv/(2qB), r = 2d.
The slab has width d, so sin \u03B8 = d/r = 1/2 and the velocity turns through \u03B8 = 30\xB0 (see the solution figure).
The force is perpendicular to the velocity, so at emergence |a| = qvB/m, directed perpendicular to the exit velocity.
For the 30\xB0 turn this gives a = (qvB/m)((1/2)\xEE \u2212 (\u221A3/2)\u0135).
This is option (c).`,fig:{w:300,h:190,alt:"A horizontal region between y = 0 and y = d with magnetic field B out of the page (dots); a particle enters from below with velocity v",d:[["l",70,150,270,150],["l",70,80,270,80],["t",64,154,"y = 0","e"],["t",64,84,"y = d","e"],["c",70,100,1.5,"F"],["c",70,130,1.5,"F"],["c",120,100,1.5,"F"],["c",120,130,1.5,"F"],["c",170,100,1.5,"F"],["c",170,130,1.5,"F"],["c",220,100,1.5,"F"],["c",220,130,1.5,"F"],["l",95,180,95,152,"a"],["t",105,172,"v"],["t",250,104,"B"]]},sfig:{w:320,h:215,alt:"Slab of width d; a particle enters moving upward on a circle of radius r = 2d whose centre lies on the entry line; it leaves after turning 30 degrees; the exit velocity is tilted 30 degrees from the vertical and the acceleration points towards the centre, perpendicular to it",d:[["r",30,100,230,70],["l",60,205,60,172,"a"],["t",68,196,"v"],["p","M60 170 A140 140 0 0 1 78.8 100","B"],["l",200,170,60,170,"d"],["l",200,170,78.8,100,"d"],["c",200,170,2,"F"],["p","M160 170 A40 40 0 0 1 165.4 150"],["t",142,164,"\u03B8","s"],["l",78.8,100,98.8,65.4,"a"],["t",102,66,"v"],["l",78.8,100,113.4,120,"a"],["t",116,138,"a"],["t",130,188,"r","m"],["t",150,126,"r"],["l",16,100,16,170,"b"],["t",10,138,"d","e"]]}},{pn:146,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:"A particle of mass m and charge q is in an electric and magnetic field given by E = 2\xEE + 3\u0135; B = 4\u0135 + 6k\u0302. The charged particle is shifted from the origin to the point P(x = 1; y = 1) along a straight path. The magnitude of the total work done is",o:["(0.15)q","(0.35)q","(2.5)q","5q"],c:3,s:`The magnetic force q(v \xD7 B) is always perpendicular to the displacement direction of motion, so it does no work.
Only the electric force qE does work, and E is uniform, so W = qE\xB7d with d the displacement.
The displacement from (0, 0) to (1, 1) is d = \xEE + \u0135.
W = q(2\xEE + 3\u0135)\xB7(\xEE + \u0135) = q(2 \xD7 1 + 3 \xD7 1) = 5q.
This is option (d).`},{pn:147,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"A proton and an \u03B1-particle (with their masses in the ratio 1 : 4 and charges in the ratio of 1 : 2) are accelerated from rest through a potential difference V. If a uniform magnetic field (B) is set up perpendicular to their velocities, the ratio of the radii r_p : r_\u03B1 of the circular paths described by them will be",o:["1 : 3","1 : \u221A2","1 : 2","1 : \u221A3"],c:1,s:`After acceleration through V, qV = \xBDmv\xB2, so v = \u221A(2qV/m).
In the field, the radius is r = mv/(qB) = (1/B)\u221A(2mV/q).
So for the same V and B, r is proportional to \u221A(m/q).
Proton: m/q = 1/1 = 1. \u03B1-particle: m/q = 4/2 = 2.
r_p : r_\u03B1 = \u221A1 : \u221A2 = 1 : \u221A2.
This is option (b).`},{pn:148,y:"JEE Main 2018",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"An electron, a proton and an alpha particle having the same kinetic energy are moving in circular orbits of radii r_e, r_p, r_\u03B1 respectively in a uniform magnetic field B. The relation between r_e, r_p, r_\u03B1 is",o:["r_e > r_p = r_\u03B1","r_e < r_p = r_\u03B1","r_e < r_p < r_\u03B1","r_e < r_\u03B1 < r_p"],c:1,s:`For kinetic energy K, momentum p = \u221A(2mK), and the radius is r = p/(qB) = \u221A(2mK)/(qB).
For equal K and B, r is proportional to \u221Am / q.
Electron: its mass is tiny, so \u221Am/q is the smallest.
Proton: \u221Am/e. \u03B1-particle: \u221A(4m)/(2e) = \u221Am/e, the same as the proton.
So r_e < r_p = r_\u03B1, option (b).`},{pn:149,y:"JEE Main 2017 \xB7 Online",tp:"mmot",st:"Velocity selector and crossed fields",q:"In a certain region static electric and magnetic fields exist. The magnetic field is given by B = B\u2080(\xEE + 2\u0135 \u2212 4k\u0302). If a test charge moving with a velocity v = v\u2080(3\xEE \u2212 \u0135 + 2k\u0302) experiences no force in that region, then the electric field in the region, in SI units, is",o:["E = \u2212v\u2080B\u2080(14\u0135 + 7k\u0302)","E = v\u2080B\u2080(14\u0135 + 7k\u0302)","E = \u2212v\u2080B\u2080(\xEE + \u0135 + 7k\u0302)","E = \u2212v\u2080B\u2080(3\xEE \u2212 2\u0135 \u2212 4k\u0302)"],c:0,s:`No net force means qE + q(v \xD7 B) = 0, so E = \u2212v \xD7 B.
v \xD7 B = v\u2080B\u2080 \xD7 the determinant of (3, \u22121, 2) and (1, 2, \u22124).
x-part: (\u22121)(\u22124) \u2212 (2)(2) = 4 \u2212 4 = 0.
y-part: (2)(1) \u2212 (3)(\u22124) = 2 + 12 = 14.
z-part: (3)(2) \u2212 (\u22121)(1) = 6 + 1 = 7.
So v \xD7 B = v\u2080B\u2080(14\u0135 + 7k\u0302) and E = \u2212v\u2080B\u2080(14\u0135 + 7k\u0302).
This is option (a).`},{pn:150,y:"JEE Main 2017 \xB7 Online",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:"A negative test charge is moving near a long straight wire carrying a current. The force acting on the test charge is parallel to the direction of the current. The motion of the charge is",o:["parallel to the wire opposite to the current","parallel to the wire along the current","away from the wire","towards the wire."],c:3,s:`Let the current flow along +z with the charge at a point on the +x side of the wire.
The field there circles the wire, so B points along +y (right-hand rule).
The force on a charge is F = q(v \xD7 B). We need F along +z, and q is negative, so v \xD7 B must point along \u2212z.
Try v = \u2212x\u0302: (\u2212x\u0302) \xD7 \u0177 = \u2212\u1E91. This gives the required direction.
v = \u2212x\u0302 means moving towards the wire (the charge is on the +x side).
This is option (d).
Note: moving parallel or antiparallel to the wire gives a force along x, never along z, so only a radial motion can work.`},{pn:151,y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"A proton (mass m) accelerated by a potential difference V flies through a uniform transverse magnetic field B. The field occupies a region of space by width d. If \u03B1 be the angle of deviation of proton from initial direction of motion (see figure), the value of sin\u03B1 will be",o:["(B/2)\u221A(qd/(mV))","(B/d)\u221A(q/(2mV))","Bd\u221A(q/(2mV))","qV\u221A(Bd/(2m))"],c:2,s:`Speed after acceleration: qV = \xBDmv\xB2, so v = \u221A(2qV/m).
In the field the path is a circle of radius r = mv/(qB) = (1/B)\u221A(2mV/q).
The velocity is perpendicular to the entry boundary, so the centre of the circle lies on that boundary line (see the solution figure).
The exit point is then at horizontal distance d = r sin\u03B1 from the centre line, where \u03B1 is the angle turned by the radius, equal to the deviation of the velocity.
So sin\u03B1 = d/r = d \xD7 B/\u221A(2mV/q) = Bd\u221A(q/(2mV)).
This is option (c).`,fig:{w:300,h:170,alt:"A proton enters a vertical slab of width d with magnetic field B into the page, moves on a circular arc and leaves deviated by angle alpha from its initial direction, shown by a dashed line",d:[["r",60,20,140,120],["l",85,120,107,20,"d"],["l",110,120,132,20,"d"],["l",135,120,157,20,"d"],["l",160,120,182,20,"d"],["l",10,100,60,100,"a"],["p","M60,100 Q135,100 200,72 L270,44","a"],["l",60,100,275,100,"d"],["p","M248,100 A22,22 0 0 0 244,88"],["t",254,92,"\u03B1","s"],["c",78,36,7],["l",73,31,83,41],["l",83,31,73,41],["t",90,34,"B"],["l",60,146,200,146,"b"],["t",130,162,"d","m"],["t",30,92,"proton","m"]]},sfig:{w:220,h:205,alt:"Circular arc of radius r with its centre on the entry line; the proton crosses a slab of width d and the radius to the exit point makes angle alpha with the entry line",d:[["r",60,20,60,180],["l",20,160,60,160,"a"],["p","M60 160 A120 120 0 0 0 120 143.9","B"],["l",60,40,60,160,"d"],["l",60,40,120,143.9,"d"],["c",60,40,2,"F"],["p","M60 70 A30 30 0 0 0 75 66"],["t",64,84,"\u03B1","s"],["t",52,104,"r","e"],["t",98,86,"r"],["l",60,190,120,190,"b"],["t",90,186,"d","m"]]}},{pn:152,y:"AIEEE 2012",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"Proton, deuteron and alpha particle of the same kinetic energy are moving in circular trajectories in a constant magnetic field. The radii of proton, deuteron and alpha particle are respectively r_p, r_d and r_\u03B1. Which one of the following relation is correct?",o:["r_\u03B1 = r_p < r_d","r_\u03B1 > r_d > r_p","r_\u03B1 = r_d > r_p","r_p = r_d = r_\u03B1"],c:0,s:`For kinetic energy K: r = \u221A(2mK)/(qB), so r is proportional to \u221Am / q.
Take proton mass m and charge e. Then deuteron has 2m and e, and alpha has 4m and 2e.
Proton: \u221A1/1 = 1. Deuteron: \u221A2/1 = 1.41. Alpha: \u221A4/2 = 1.
So r_\u03B1 = r_p < r_d.
This is option (a).`},{pn:153,y:"AIEEE 2012 \xB7 Online",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:"The magnetic force acting on charged particle of charge 2 \u03BCC in a magnetic field of 2 T acting in y-direction, when the particle velocity is (2\xEE + 3\u0135) \xD7 10\u2076 m s\u207B\xB9 is",o:["8 N in z-direction","8 N in y-direction","4 N in y-direction","4 N in z-direction"],c:0,s:`Use F = q(v \xD7 B), with B = 2\u0135 T.
The \u0135 part of v is parallel to B, so it gives no force.
Only 2 \xD7 10\u2076 \xEE matters: \xEE \xD7 \u0135 = k\u0302.
F = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 (2 \xD7 10\u2076) \xD7 2 k\u0302 = 8 k\u0302 N.
The force is 8 N in the z-direction, option (a).`},{pn:154,y:"AIEEE 2007",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:"A charged particle moves through a magnetic field perpendicular to its direction. Then",o:["kinetic energy changes but the momentum is constant","the momentum changes but the kinetic energy is constant","both momentum and kinetic energy of the particle are not constant","both momentum and kinetic energy of the particle are constant."],c:1,s:`The magnetic force is always perpendicular to the velocity, so it does no work.
No work means the speed, and so the kinetic energy, stays the same.
The force still changes the direction of v, and momentum is a vector (mv), so it changes.
Kinetic energy is constant, momentum changes. This is option (b).`},{pn:155,y:"AIEEE 2007",tp:"mmot",st:"Velocity selector and crossed fields",q:"A charged particle with charge q enters a region of constant, uniform and mutually orthogonal fields E and B with a velocity v perpendicular to both E and B and comes out without any change in magnitude or direction of v. Then",o:["v = B \xD7 E / E\xB2","v = E \xD7 B / B\xB2","v = B \xD7 E / B\xB2","v = E \xD7 B / E\xB2"],c:1,s:`No change in v means the net force is zero: qE + q(v \xD7 B) = 0, so E = \u2212v \xD7 B = B \xD7 v.
Cross both sides with B: E \xD7 B = (B \xD7 v) \xD7 B.
Since v \u27C2 B, (B \xD7 v) \xD7 B = v B\xB2 (the vector triple product gives vB\xB2 when v is perpendicular to B).
So v = E \xD7 B / B\xB2.
Check size: v = E/B, the usual velocity-selector condition.
This is option (b).`},{pn:156,y:"IIT-JEE 2007",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"A magnetic field B = B\u2080\u0135 exists in the region a < x < 2a and B = \u2212B\u2080\u0135 in the region 2a < x < 3a, where B\u2080 is a positive constant. A positive point charge moving with a velocity v = v\u2080\xEE, where v\u2080 is a positive constant, enters the magnetic field at x = a. The trajectory of the charge in this region can be like,",o:["Graph (a)","Graph (b)","Graph (c)","Graph (d)"],c:0,s:`At x = a the force is F = q(v \xD7 B) = qv\u2080B\u2080(\xEE \xD7 \u0135) = qv\u2080B\u2080 k\u0302, along +z, so the charge begins to bend upward.
In a < x < 2a it moves on a circular arc of radius mv\u2080/(qB\u2080), curving towards +z.
At x = 2a the field reverses, so the force now turns the other way: the arc curves back, away from +z.
The velocity is continuous at x = 2a, so the path stays smooth, with no sharp corner. Sharp peaks or dips like (c) and (d) would need a sudden change of velocity.
The path goes up and keeps rising, with the bend reversing at 2a: an S-shaped curve towards +z. Graph (b) would be the same S but towards \u2212z.
This is graph (a).`,fig:{w:400,h:360,alt:"Top: graph of B against x, equal to B0 from a to 2a and minus B0 from 2a to 3a. Below: four paths in the x-z plane starting at x equals a: (a) an S-shaped curve rising upward, (b) an S-shaped curve falling downward, (c) a peak at 2a, (d) a dip at 2a",d:[["l",20,70,210,70,"a"],["l",40,110,40,22,"a"],["t",34,26,"B","es"],["t",214,74,"x","s"],["p","M60,70 L60,40 L100,40 L100,100 L140,100 L140,70"],["l",40,40,60,40,"d"],["l",40,100,100,100,"d"],["t",36,44,"B\u2080","es"],["t",36,104,"\u2212B\u2080","es"],["t",60,124,"a","ms"],["t",100,124,"2a","ms"],["t",140,124,"3a","ms"],["l",40,200,180,200,"a"],["l",48,250,48,150,"a"],["t",44,148,"z","es"],["t",184,204,"x","s"],["t",68,214,"a","ms"],["t",106,214,"2a","ms"],["t",144,214,"3a","ms"],["t",20,204,"(a)"],["p","M68,200 C85,200 90,198 98,188 S125,170 144,164","a"],["l",230,200,370,200,"a"],["l",238,250,238,150,"a"],["t",234,148,"z","es"],["t",374,204,"x","s"],["t",258,196,"a","ms"],["t",296,196,"2a","ms"],["t",334,196,"3a","ms"],["t",210,204,"(b)"],["p","M258,200 C275,200 280,202 288,212 S315,230 334,236","a"],["l",40,310,180,310,"a"],["l",48,360,48,260,"a"],["t",44,258,"z","es"],["t",184,314,"x","s"],["t",68,324,"a","ms"],["t",106,324,"2a","ms"],["t",144,324,"3a","ms"],["t",20,314,"(c)"],["p","M68,310 C88,310 102,304 106,290 C110,304 130,310 144,310","a"],["l",230,310,370,310,"a"],["l",238,360,238,260,"a"],["t",234,258,"z","es"],["t",374,314,"x","s"],["t",258,306,"a","ms"],["t",296,306,"2a","ms"],["t",334,306,"3a","ms"],["t",210,314,"(d)"],["p","M258,310 C278,310 292,316 296,330 C300,316 320,310 334,310","a"]]}},{pn:157,y:"AIEEE 2006",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"In a region, steady and uniform electric and magnetic fields are present. These two fields are parallel to each other. A charged particle is released from rest in this region. The path of the particle will be a",o:["circle","helix","straight line","ellipse"],c:2,s:`Released from rest, the particle first feels only the electric force qE, along the common direction of E and B.
So it starts moving along that line, which is also the direction of B.
The magnetic force q(v \xD7 B) is zero when v is parallel to B.
As the particle speeds up along the line, v stays parallel to B, so there is never any magnetic force.
The path is a straight line, option (c).`},{pn:158,y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"A uniform electric field and a uniform magnetic field are acting along the same direction in a certain region. If an electron is projected along the direction of the fields with a certain velocity then",o:["it will turn towards right of direction of motion","it will turn towards left of direction of motion","its velocity will decrease","its velocity will increase."],c:2,s:`The electron moves along B, so v \xD7 B = 0 and the magnetic force is zero. It does not turn left or right.
The electric force on an electron is opposite to E: F = \u2212eE.
The electron is moving along E, so the force is opposite to its motion.
So the electron slows down, option (c).`},{pn:159,y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"A charged particle of mass m and charge q travels on a circular path of radius r that is perpendicular to a magnetic field B. The time taken by the particle to complete one revolution is",o:["(2\u03C0qB)/(m)","(2\u03C0m)/(qB)","(2\u03C0mq)/(B)","(2\u03C0mq)/(qB)"],c:1,s:`The magnetic force provides the centripetal force: qvB = mv\xB2/r, so v = qBr/m.
One revolution covers 2\u03C0r at speed v.
T = 2\u03C0r/v = 2\u03C0r \xD7 m/(qBr) = 2\u03C0m/(qB).
This is option (b).`},{pn:160,y:"IIT-JEE 2004",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"An electron travelling with a speed u along the positive x-axis enters into a region of magnetic field where B = \u2212B\u2080k\u0302 (x > 0). It comes out of the region with speed v then",o:["v = u at y > 0","v = u at y < 0","v > u at y > 0","v > u at y < 0."],c:1,s:`The magnetic force does no work, so the speed stays the same: v = u. This rules out (c) and (d).
Force on the electron: F = \u2212e(v \xD7 B) = \u2212e(u\xEE \xD7 (\u2212B\u2080k\u0302)) = e u B\u2080 (\xEE \xD7 k\u0302).
\xEE \xD7 k\u0302 = \u2212\u0135, so F points along \u2212y.
The electron is pushed towards \u2212y, curves down and comes out of the region at y < 0.
So v = u at y < 0, option (b).`,fig:{w:300,h:160,alt:"x and y axes; an electron on the negative x axis moves with speed u towards the origin; the region x greater than 0 has a magnetic field B into the page",d:[["l",20,80,280,80,"a"],["l",150,145,150,15,"a"],["t",286,84,"x"],["t",156,20,"y"],["c",50,80,3,"F"],["t",50,68,"e\u207B","m"],["l",62,80,100,80,"a"],["t",82,70,"u","m"],["c",215,40,7],["l",210,35,220,45],["l",220,35,210,45],["t",230,44,"B"]]}},{pn:161,y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"mmot",st:"Velocity selector and crossed fields",q:"A particle of charge \u221216 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2078 coulomb moving with velocity 10 m s\u207B\xB9 along the x-axis enters a region where a magnetic field of induction B is along the y-axis, and an electric field of magnitude 10\u2074 V/m is along the negative z-axis. If the charged particle continues moving along the x-axis, the magnitude of B is",o:["10\xB3 Wb/m\xB2","10\u2075 Wb/m\xB2","10\xB9\u2076 Wb/m\xB2","10\u207B\xB3 Wb/m\xB2"],c:0,s:`The particle goes straight, so the electric and magnetic forces cancel: qE = qvB.
The charge q cancels out (its size does not matter), so B = E/v.
B = 10\u2074 / 10 = 10\xB3 Wb/m\xB2.
Check directions: both forces are along \xB1z and opposite, so they can cancel.
This is option (a).`},{pn:162,y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:"A particle of mass M and charge Q moving with velocity v describes a circular path of radius R when subjected to a uniform transverse magnetic field of induction B. The work done by the field when the particle completes one full circle is",o:["(Mv\xB2/R) 2\u03C0R","zero","BQ 2\u03C0R","BQv 2\u03C0R"],c:1,s:`The magnetic force is always perpendicular to the velocity, so it is perpendicular to every small displacement.
Work = F\xB7dl = 0 at every step.
So the total work in a full circle (or any part of it) is zero.
This is option (b).`},{pn:163,y:"IIT-JEE 2003",tp:"mmot",st:"Velocity selector and crossed fields",q:"For a positively charged particle moving in a x-y plane initially along the x-axis, there is a sudden change in its path due to the presence of electric and/or magnetic fields beyond P. The curved path is shown in the x-y plane and is found to be non-circular. Which one of the following combinations is possible?",o:["E = 0; B = b\xEE + ck\u0302","E = a\xEE; B = ck\u0302 + a\xEE","E = 0; B = c\u0135 + bk\u0302","E = a\xEE; B = ck\u0302 + b\u0135"],c:1,s:`A path in the x-y plane needs no force along z at P. For v = v \xEE, a B component along \u0135 gives v \xD7 B along k\u0302, which would pull the particle out of the plane. This rules out (c) and (d).
With E = 0 the speed would stay constant, so the curve would be a circular arc. The path is non-circular, so there must be an electric field. This rules out (a).
In (b), E = a\xEE changes the speed, and the k\u0302 component of B bends the path in the plane (\xEE \xD7 k\u0302 = \u2212\u0135, towards \u2212y as drawn).
The component of B along \xEE is parallel to the starting velocity, so it exerts no force at P.
So (b) is the possible combination.`,fig:{w:300,h:170,alt:"x-y axes; a positively charged particle moves along the x axis to point P marked by a dashed vertical line, then follows a curve bending downward",d:[["l",40,140,40,15,"a"],["l",20,90,280,90,"a"],["t",284,94,"x"],["t",46,20,"y"],["l",150,60,150,125,"d"],["t",144,82,"P","e"],["l",50,90,150,90,"a"],["p","M150,90 C190,90 215,105 235,155","a"]]}},{pn:164,y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"The time period of a charged particle undergoing a circular motion in a uniform magnetic field is independent of its",o:["speed","mass","charge","magnetic induction"],c:0,s:`From qvB = mv\xB2/r, the radius is r = mv/(qB).
Period T = 2\u03C0r/v = 2\u03C0m/(qB).
The speed v has cancelled out. T depends on m, q and B only.
So the period is independent of speed, option (a).`},{pn:165,y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"If an electron and a proton having same momenta enter perpendicular to a magnetic field, then",o:["curved path of electron and proton will be same (ignoring the sense of revolution)","they will move undeflected","curved path of electron is more curved than that of the proton","path of proton is more curved."],c:0,s:`In a magnetic field the radius is r = mv/(qB) = p/(qB).
Electron and proton have the same momentum p, the same charge size e and the same B.
So their radii are equal. They revolve in opposite senses, but the paths are circles of the same size.
This is option (a).`},{pn:166,y:"IIT-JEE 2002",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"A particle of mass m and charge q moves with a constant velocity v along the positive x direction. It enters a region containing a uniform magnetic field B directed along the negative z direction, extending from x = a to x = b. The minimum value of v required so that the particle can just enter the region x > b is",o:["qbB/m","q(b \u2212 a)B/m","qaB/m","q(b + a)B/(2m)"],c:1,s:`The particle enters at x = a moving along +x and goes on a circle whose centre is on the line x = a (the force is perpendicular to the entry velocity).
So the farthest it can reach in x is x = a + r, where r = mv/(qB).
To just enter the region x > b we need a + r = b, so r = b \u2212 a.
mv/(qB) = b \u2212 a gives v = q(b \u2212 a)B/m.
This is option (b).`},{pn:167,y:"IIT-JEE 2001",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"Two particles A and B of masses m_A and m_B respectively and having the same charge are moving in a plane. A uniform magnetic field exists perpendicular to this plane. The speeds of the particles are v_A and v_B respectively and the trajectories are as shown in the figure. Then",o:["m_A v_A < m_B v_B","m_A v_A > m_B v_B","m_A < m_B and v_A < v_B","m_A = m_B and v_A = v_B."],c:1,s:`In the field a particle moves on a circle of radius r = mv/(qB).
Both particles have the same charge q and the same B, so the radius is proportional to the momentum mv.
In the figure, the path of A has the larger radius than that of B.
So m_A v_A > m_B v_B.
Nothing is known about m or v separately (only their product), so (c) and (d) cannot be concluded.
This is option (b).`,fig:{w:280,h:190,alt:"A grid of dots (magnetic field out of the page) with two arcs: particle A on a larger semicircular arc and particle B on a smaller arc below it",d:[["c",30,22,1.8,"F"],["c",74,22,1.8,"F"],["c",118,22,1.8,"F"],["c",162,22,1.8,"F"],["c",206,22,1.8,"F"],["c",250,22,1.8,"F"],["c",30,58,1.8,"F"],["c",74,58,1.8,"F"],["c",118,58,1.8,"F"],["c",162,58,1.8,"F"],["c",206,58,1.8,"F"],["c",250,58,1.8,"F"],["c",30,94,1.8,"F"],["c",74,94,1.8,"F"],["c",118,94,1.8,"F"],["c",162,94,1.8,"F"],["c",206,94,1.8,"F"],["c",250,94,1.8,"F"],["c",30,130,1.8,"F"],["c",74,130,1.8,"F"],["c",118,130,1.8,"F"],["c",162,130,1.8,"F"],["c",206,130,1.8,"F"],["c",250,130,1.8,"F"],["c",30,166,1.8,"F"],["c",74,166,1.8,"F"],["c",118,166,1.8,"F"],["c",162,166,1.8,"F"],["c",206,166,1.8,"F"],["c",250,166,1.8,"F"],["p","M64,118 A70,70 0 0 1 204,118"],["p","M120,150 A40,40 0 0 1 200,150"],["l",130,48,138,48,"a"],["l",156,110,164,110,"a"],["t",92,70,"A"],["t",140,100,"B"]]}},{pn:168,y:"IIT-JEE 2000",tp:"mmot",st:"Velocity selector and crossed fields",q:"An ionized gas contains both positive and negative ions. If it is subjected simultaneously to an electric field along the +x direction and a magnetic field along the +z direction, then",o:["positive ions deflect towards +y direction and negative ions towards \u2212y direction.","all ions deflect towards +y direction","all ions deflect towards \u2212y direction","positive ions deflect towards \u2212y direction and negative ions towards +y direction."],c:2,s:`The electric field along +x pushes positive ions along +x and negative ions along \u2212x, so they get velocities in opposite directions.
Positive ion: v = +x\u0302, F = q(x\u0302 \xD7 \u1E91) = q(\u2212\u0177), so it is pushed along \u2212y.
Negative ion: v = \u2212x\u0302 and the charge is negative, so F = (\u2212q)((\u2212x\u0302) \xD7 \u1E91) = (\u2212q)(+\u0177) = \u2212q\u0177, again along \u2212y.
Both signs of charge reverse together (the direction of motion and the sign of charge), so the sideways force is the same for all ions.
All ions deflect towards \u2212y, option (c).`},{pn:169,y:"IIT-JEE 1999",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:"A charged particle is released from rest in a region of steady and uniform electric and magnetic fields which are parallel to each other. The particle will move in a",o:["straight line","circle","helix","cycloid"],c:0,s:`At the start the particle is at rest, so the magnetic force qv \xD7 B is zero. Only the electric force qE acts, along E (and so along B).
The particle starts moving along E. Its velocity is then parallel to B, so the magnetic force qv \xD7 B stays zero.
Only the electric force keeps acting, in the same direction, so the particle speeds up in a straight line.
Straight line, option (a).`},{pn:170,y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"A proton, a deuteron and an \u03B1-particle having the same kinetic energy are moving in circular trajectories in a constant magnetic field. If r_p, r_d and r_\u03B1 denote respectively the radii of the trajectories of these particles, then",o:["r_\u03B1 = r_p < r_d","r_\u03B1 > r_d > r_p","r_\u03B1 = r_d > r_p","r_p = r_d = r_\u03B1"],c:0,s:`For kinetic energy K, r = mv/(qB) = \u221A(2mK)/(qB), so r \u221D \u221Am / q.
Proton: \u221A1/1 = 1. Deuteron: \u221A2/1 = 1.41. \u03B1-particle: \u221A4/2 = 1.
So r_\u03B1 = r_p < r_d, option (a).`},{pn:171,y:"JEE Advanced 2017",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:`A uniform magnetic field B exists in the region between x = 0 and x = 3R/2 (region 2 in the figure) pointing normally into the plane of the paper. A particle with charge +Q and momentum p directed along x-axis enters region 2 from region 1 at point P\u2081 (y = \u2212R). Which of the following option(s) is/are correct?
(a) For B > 2p/(3QR), the particle will re-enter region 1.
(b) For B = 8p/(13QR), the particle will enter region 3 through the point P\u2082 on x-axis.
(c) For a fixed B, particles of same charge Q and same velocity v, the distance between the point P\u2081 and the point of re-entry into region 1 is inversely proportional to the mass of the particle.
(d) When the particle re-enters region 1 through the longest possible path in region 2, the magnitude of the change in its linear momentum between point P\u2081 and the farthest point from y-axis is p/\u221A2.`,fig:{w:340,h:250,alt:"Uniform magnetic field B into the page in region 2 between x = 0 and x = 3R/2; a particle with charge +Q enters along +x at point P\u2081 (y = \u2212R) from region 1; P\u2082 is on the x axis at the far edge",d:[["l",100,20,100,215],["l",240,20,240,215],["l",30,120,320,120,"d"],["t",300,114,"x","e"],["t",96,16,"y","e"],["t",70,32,"Region 1","m"],["t",170,32,"Region 2","m"],["t",285,32,"Region 3","m"],["l",112,54,118,60],["l",112,60,118,54],["l",112,79,118,85],["l",112,85,118,79],["l",112,104,118,110],["l",112,110,118,104],["l",112,129,118,135],["l",112,135,118,129],["l",112,154,118,160],["l",112,160,118,154],["l",112,179,118,185],["l",112,185,118,179],["l",112,204,118,210],["l",112,210,118,204],["l",137,54,143,60],["l",137,60,143,54],["l",137,79,143,85],["l",137,85,143,79],["l",137,104,143,110],["l",137,110,143,104],["l",137,129,143,135],["l",137,135,143,129],["l",137,154,143,160],["l",137,160,143,154],["l",137,179,143,185],["l",137,185,143,179],["l",137,204,143,210],["l",137,210,143,204],["l",162,54,168,60],["l",162,60,168,54],["l",162,79,168,85],["l",162,85,168,79],["l",162,104,168,110],["l",162,110,168,104],["l",162,129,168,135],["l",162,135,168,129],["l",162,154,168,160],["l",162,160,168,154],["l",162,179,168,185],["l",162,185,168,179],["l",162,204,168,210],["l",162,210,168,204],["l",187,54,193,60],["l",187,60,193,54],["l",187,79,193,85],["l",187,85,193,79],["l",187,104,193,110],["l",187,110,193,104],["l",187,129,193,135],["l",187,135,193,129],["l",187,154,193,160],["l",187,160,193,154],["l",187,179,193,185],["l",187,185,193,179],["l",187,204,193,210],["l",187,210,193,204],["l",212,54,218,60],["l",212,60,218,54],["l",212,79,218,85],["l",212,85,218,79],["l",212,104,218,110],["l",212,110,218,104],["l",212,129,218,135],["l",212,135,218,129],["l",212,154,218,160],["l",212,160,218,154],["l",212,179,218,185],["l",212,185,218,179],["l",212,204,218,210],["l",212,210,218,204],["c",100,120,2,"F"],["t",94,116,"O","e"],["c",100,170,2.5,"F"],["t",94,160,"P\u2081","e"],["c",240,120,2.5,"F"],["t",248,136,"P\u2082"],["c",36,170,3],["l",39,170,95,170,"a"],["t",36,190,"+Q","m"],["t",36,203,"(y = \u2212R)","m","s"],["l",100,228,240,228,"b"],["t",170,244,"3R/2","m"]]},o:["(a)","(b)","(c)","(d)"],c:[0,1],s:`The radius of the circle is r = p/(QB). The force on +Q moving along +x in a field into the page is along +y, so the path curves upward from P\u2081, with centre at height r above P\u2081.
(a) The particle returns to region 1 if its circle does not reach x = 3R/2, that is r < 3R/2, so B > p/(Q \xD7 3R/2) = 2p/(3QR). True.
(b) For B = 8p/(13QR), r = 13R/8. The centre is at (0, \u2212R + 13R/8) = (0, 5R/8). The circle meets x = 3R/2 where (3R/2)\xB2 + (y \u2212 5R/8)\xB2 = (13R/8)\xB2, so (y \u2212 5R/8)\xB2 = 25R\xB2/64 and y = 0 or 5R/4. The particle meets the line first at y = 0, which is P\u2082. True.
(c) The re-entry point is 2r above P\u2081, and 2r = 2mv/(QB) is directly proportional to m, not inversely. False.
(d) The longest path inside region 2 is when the circle just touches x = 3R/2, r = 3R/2. At that farthest point the momentum is p along +y, while at P\u2081 it is p along +x. The change has magnitude \u221A(p\xB2 + p\xB2) = \u221A2 p, not p/\u221A2. False.
Correct options: (a) and (b).`},{pn:172,y:"JEE Advanced 2013",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"A particle of mass M and positive charge Q, moving with a constant velocity u\u2081 = 4\xEE m s\u207B\xB9, enters a region of uniform static magnetic field normal to the x-y plane. The region of the magnetic field extends from x = 0 to x = L for all values of y. After passing through this region, the particle emerges on the other side after 10 milliseconds with a velocity u\u2082 = 2(\u221A3 \xEE + \u0135) m s\u207B\xB9. The correct statement(s) is (are)",o:["The direction of the magnetic field is \u2212z direction.","The direction of the magnetic field is +z direction.","The magnitude of the magnetic field is 50\u03C0M/(3Q) units.","The magnitude of the magnetic field is 100\u03C0M/(3Q) units."],c:[0,2],s:`The speed is unchanged: |u\u2082| = 2\u221A(3 + 1) = 4 m/s, as it must be for a magnetic force. Only the direction turns.
Direction of turn: u\u2082 has a positive y-component, tan \u03B8 = 2/(2\u221A3) = 1/\u221A3, so the velocity turned by \u03B8 = 30\xB0 = \u03C0/6 towards +y.
The force on a positive charge moving along +x must point along +y. F = Qv \xD7 B: x\u0302 \xD7 (\u2212\u1E91) = +\u0177, so B is along \u2212z. This makes (a) true and (b) false.
The velocity turns at the rate \u03C9 = QB/M. Turning by \u03C0/6 in t = 10 ms = 0.01 s: QB/M = (\u03C0/6)/0.01 = 100\u03C0/6 = 50\u03C0/3.
B = 50\u03C0M/(3Q), so (c) is true and (d) is false.
Correct options: (a) and (c).`},{pn:173,y:"IIT-JEE 2011",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"An electron and a proton are moving on straight parallel paths with same velocity. They enter a semi-infinite region of uniform magnetic field perpendicular to the velocity. Which of the following statement(s) is/are true?",o:["They will never come out of the magnetic field region.","They will come out travelling along parallel paths.","They will come out at the same time.","They will come out at different times."],c:[1,3],s:`Both enter perpendicular to the boundary and to B, so each moves on a semicircle and leaves the field region, going back out in the opposite direction. So (a) is false.
On leaving, each has the velocity reversed, so the two paths are parallel (antiparallel to the entry direction). (b) is true.
The time spent is half a period, T/2 = \u03C0m/(qB). The proton is much heavier than the electron and has the same charge size, so the time is different. (c) is false and (d) is true.
Correct options: (b) and (d).`},{pn:174,y:"IIT-JEE 2008",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:`A particle of mass m and charge q moving with velocity V enters region II normal to the boundary as shown in the figure. Region II has a uniform magnetic field B perpendicular to the plane of the paper. The length of the region II is l. Choose the correct choice(s).
(a) The particle enters region III only if its velocity V > qlB/m.
(b) The particle enters region III only if its velocity V < qlB/m.
(c) Path length of the particle in region II is maximum when velocity V = qlB/m.
(d) Time spent in region II is same for any velocity V as long as the particle returns to region I.`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Region I on the left, region II of length l with magnetic field B into the page, region III on the right; a particle of velocity V enters region II normally at the boundary",d:[["l",120,20,120,160],["l",250,20,250,160],["t",70,16,"Region I","m"],["t",185,16,"Region II","m"],["t",295,16,"Region III","m"],["l",137,42,143,48],["l",137,48,143,42],["l",137,66,143,72],["l",137,72,143,66],["l",137,90,143,96],["l",137,96,143,90],["l",137,114,143,120],["l",137,120,143,114],["l",137,138,143,144],["l",137,144,143,138],["l",161,42,167,48],["l",161,48,167,42],["l",161,66,167,72],["l",161,72,167,66],["l",161,90,167,96],["l",161,96,167,90],["l",161,114,167,120],["l",161,120,167,114],["l",161,138,167,144],["l",161,144,167,138],["l",185,42,191,48],["l",185,48,191,42],["l",185,66,191,72],["l",185,72,191,66],["l",185,90,191,96],["l",185,96,191,90],["l",185,114,191,120],["l",185,120,191,114],["l",185,138,191,144],["l",185,144,191,138],["l",209,42,215,48],["l",209,48,215,42],["l",209,66,215,72],["l",209,72,215,66],["l",209,90,215,96],["l",209,96,215,90],["l",209,114,215,120],["l",209,120,215,114],["l",209,138,215,144],["l",209,144,215,138],["l",233,42,239,48],["l",233,48,239,42],["l",233,66,239,72],["l",233,72,239,66],["l",233,90,239,96],["l",233,96,239,90],["l",233,114,239,120],["l",233,120,239,114],["l",233,138,239,144],["l",233,144,239,138],["l",30,100,118,100,"a"],["t",74,92,"V","m"],["l",120,180,250,180,"b"],["t",185,196,"l","m"],["l",120,160,120,184,"d"],["l",250,160,250,184,"d"]]},o:["(a)","(b)","(c)","(d)"],c:[0,2,3],s:`The radius is r = mV/(qB). The particle enters normally and keeps curving, so it reaches region III only if its circle is wide enough to cross the far edge, r > l.
(a) r > l means V > qlB/m. True. (b) is the opposite, false.
(c) If r < l the particle makes a semicircle of length \u03C0r, which grows with V up to r = l. At r = l it just touches the far edge, still a semicircle of length \u03C0l. For larger V the path is a shorter arc, so the longest path is at V = qlB/m. True.
(d) If it returns to region I it makes a half circle, taking T/2 = \u03C0m/(qB), independent of V. True.
Correct options: (a), (c) and (d).`},{pn:175,y:"IIT-JEE 1998",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:"Two very long, straight, parallel wires carry steady currents I and \u2212I respectively. The distance between the wires is d. At a certain instant of time, a point charge q is at a point equidistant from the two wires, in the plane of the wires. Its instantaneous velocity v is perpendicular to this plane. The magnitude of the force due to the magnetic field acting on the charge at this instant is",o:["\u03BC\u2080Iqv/(2\u03C0d)","\u03BC\u2080Iqv/(\u03C0d)","2\u03BC\u2080Iqv/(\u03C0d)","0"],c:2,s:`Note: the printed key says (d); working gives (c).
In the plane of the wires, a point equidistant from both wires lies midway between them, at d/2 from each.
Each wire gives B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0 (d/2)) = \u03BC\u2080I/(\u03C0d). The currents are opposite, so between the wires the two fields point the same way and add: B = 2\u03BC\u2080I/(\u03C0d). This field lies in the plane of the wires, at right angles to them.
The charge moves perpendicular to the plane, so v is at right angles to B and F = qvB = 2\u03BC\u2080Iqv/(\u03C0d).
Option (c).`},{pn:176,y:"IIT-JEE 1994",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"H\u207A, He\u207A and O\u207A\u207A all having the same kinetic energy pass through a region in which there is a uniform magnetic field perpendicular to their velocities. The masses of H\u207A, He\u207A and O\u207A\u207A are 1 amu, 4 and 16 amu respectively. Then",o:["H\u207A will be deflected most","O\u207A\u207A will be deflected most","He\u207A and O\u207A\u207A will be deflected equally","all will be deflected equally"],c:[0,2],s:`For equal kinetic energy the radius is r \u221D \u221Am / q. A smaller radius means a sharper turn, that is more deflection.
H\u207A: \u221A1/1 = 1. He\u207A: \u221A4/1 = 2. O\u207A\u207A: \u221A16/2 = 2.
H\u207A has the smallest radius, so it is deflected most; He\u207A and O\u207A\u207A have equal radii and are deflected equally.
Options (a) and (c) are both true; the printed key is (a, c).`},{pn:177,y:"IIT-JEE 1991",tp:"mmot",st:"Velocity selector and crossed fields",q:`A particle of charge +q and mass m moving under the influence of a uniform electric field E \xEE and uniform magnetic field B k\u0302 follows a trajectory from P to Q as shown in figure. The velocities at P and Q are v \xEE and \u22122v \u0135 which of the following statement(s) is/are correct?
(a) E = (3/4)(m v\xB2/(q a))
(b) Rate of workdone by the electric field at P is (3/4)(m v\xB3/a)
(c) Rate of workdone by the electric field at P is zero
(d) Rate of workdone by both the fields at Q is zero.`,fig:{w:330,h:260,alt:"A particle goes from P on the y axis at height a, with velocity v along +x, to Q on the x axis at distance 2a, where its velocity is 2v along \u2212y; electric field E along +x and magnetic field B out of the page",d:[["l",50,230,50,20,"a"],["l",50,230,310,230,"a"],["t",44,24,"Y","e"],["t",306,246,"X"],["t",42,246,"O","e"],["c",50,140,3,"F"],["t",44,138,"P","e"],["c",200,230,3,"F"],["t",194,246,"Q","e"],["p","M50 140 H150 C185 140 200 155 200 190 V230"],["l",62,126,100,126,"a"],["t",82,118,"v","m"],["l",200,236,200,258,"a"],["t",208,254,"2v"],["l",30,140,30,230,"b"],["t",24,190,"a","e"],["l",50,214,200,214,"b"],["t",125,208,"2a","m"],["l",110,60,150,60,"a"],["t",130,52,"E","m"],["c",270,70,6],["c",270,70,1.5,"F"],["t",284,74,"B"]]},o:["(a)","(b)","(c)","(d)"],c:[0,1,3],s:`The magnetic force does no work, so the work done from P to Q is by the electric force alone. P = (0, a) and Q = (2a, 0), E along +x.
qE \xD7 2a = \xBDm(2v)\xB2 \u2212 \xBDmv\xB2 = (3/2)mv\xB2, so E = 3mv\xB2/(4qa). (a) is true.
Rate of work at P = F \xB7 v = qE v = (3mv\xB2/4a) v = 3mv\xB3/(4a). (b) is true and (c) is false.
At Q the velocity is along \u2212y, perpendicular to the electric force (along x) and to the magnetic force. So the rate of work of both is zero. (d) is true.
Correct options: (a), (b) and (d).`},{pn:178,y:"IIT-JEE 1988",tp:"mmot",st:"Cyclotron and mass spectrometer",q:"Two particles X and Y having equal charges, after being accelerated through the same potential difference, enter a region of uniform magnetic field and describe circular paths of radii R\u2081 and R\u2082 respectively. The ratio of the mass of X to that of Y is",o:["(R\u2081/R\u2082)^(1/2)","R\u2082/R\u2081","(R\u2081/R\u2082)\xB2","R\u2081/R\u2082"],c:2,s:`Accelerating through V: qV = \xBDmv\xB2, so v = \u221A(2qV/m).
Radius: R = mv/(qB) = (1/B)\u221A(2mV/q), so R\xB2 \u221D m for equal q and V.
m_X/m_Y = R\u2081\xB2/R\u2082\xB2 = (R\u2081/R\u2082)\xB2, option (c).`},{pn:179,y:"IIT-JEE 1985",tp:"mfrc",st:"Velocity selector and crossed fields",q:"A proton moving with a constant velocity passes through a region of space without any change in its velocity. If E and B represent the electric and magnetic fields respectively, this region of space may have",o:["E = 0, B = 0","E = 0, B \u2260 0","E \u2260 0, B = 0","E \u2260 0, B \u2260 0"],c:[0,1,3],s:`Constant velocity means the net force is zero: qE + qv \xD7 B = 0.
If E = 0 and B = 0 there is no force. True.
If E = 0 and B \u2260 0 the force vanishes when v is parallel to B. True.
If E \u2260 0 and B = 0 there is an unbalanced electric force. False.
If E \u2260 0 and B \u2260 0 the two forces can cancel (crossed fields with E = vB). True.
Correct options: (a), (b) and (d).`},{pn:180,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"A particle of charge 1.6 \u03BCC and mass 16 \u03BCg is present in a strong magnetic field of 6.28 T. The particle is then fired perpendicular to magnetic field. The time required for the particle to return to original location for the first time is _____ s. (\u03C0 = 3.14)",n:.01,s:`Note: the printed key says 10; working gives 0.01 s (which is 10 ms).
The particle moves on a circle and is back at its starting point after one period T = 2\u03C0m/(qB).
m = 16 \u03BCg = 16 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079 kg, q = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 C, B = 6.28 T.
T = (2 \xD7 3.14 \xD7 16 \xD7 10\u207B\u2079)/(1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 6.28) = (6.28/6.28) \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 = 0.01 s.
So the time is 0.01 s, which is 10 ms.`},{pn:181,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 22 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmot",st:"Velocity selector and crossed fields",q:"A proton is moving undeflected in a region of crossed electric and magnetic fields at a constant speed of 2 \xD7 10\u2075 m s\u207B\xB9. When the electric field is switched off, the proton moves along a circular path of radius 2 cm. The magnitude of electric field is x \xD7 10\u2074 N/C. The value of x is _____. Take the mass of the proton = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 kg.",n:2,s:`Undeflected motion means qE = qvB, so E = vB.
With E off the proton circles with r = mv/(qB), so B = mv/(qr) = (1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 \xD7 2 \xD7 10\u2075)/(1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 0.02) = 0.1 T.
E = vB = 2 \xD7 10\u2075 \xD7 0.1 = 2 \xD7 10\u2074 N/C.
So x = 2.`},{pn:182,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmot",st:"Velocity selector and crossed fields",q:"An electron with kinetic energy 5 eV enters a region of uniform magnetic field of 3 \u03BCT perpendicular to its direction. An electric field E is applied perpendicular to the direction of velocity and magnetic field. The value of E, so that electron moves along the same path, is _____ NC\u207B\xB9. (Given, mass of electron = 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg, electric charge = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C)",n:4,s:`To keep going straight, the electric force must cancel the magnetic force: eE = evB, so E = vB.
Speed from the energy: \xBDmv\xB2 = 5 \xD7 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 J, so v = \u221A((2 \xD7 8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079)/(9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9)) = \u221A(1.78 \xD7 10\xB9\xB2) = 1.33 \xD7 10\u2076 m/s.
E = 1.33 \xD7 10\u2076 \xD7 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 = 4 N/C.
So the answer is 4.`},{pn:183,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:"A charge of 4.0 \u03BCC is moving with a velocity of 4.0 \xD7 10\u2076 m s\u207B\xB9 along the positive y-axis under a magnetic field B of strength (2 k\u0302) T. The force acting on the charge is x \xEE N. The value of x is _____.",n:32,s:`Force on a moving charge: F = q v \xD7 B.
v \xD7 B = (4 \xD7 10\u2076 \u0135) \xD7 (2 k\u0302) = 8 \xD7 10\u2076 (\u0135 \xD7 k\u0302) = 8 \xD7 10\u2076 \xEE.
F = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 8 \xD7 10\u2076 \xEE = 32 \xEE N.
So x = 32.`},{pn:184,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:"An electron moves through a uniform magnetic field B = B\u2080 \xEE + 2B\u2080 \u0135 T. At a particular instant of time, the velocity of electron is u = 3\xEE + 5\u0135 m s\u207B\xB9. If the magnetic force acting on electron is F = 5e k\u0302 N, where e is the charge of electron, then the value of B\u2080 is _____ T.",n:5,s:`The charge of the electron is e itself (a signed quantity), so F = e (u \xD7 B).
u \xD7 B = (3\xEE + 5\u0135) \xD7 (B\u2080\xEE + 2B\u2080\u0135) = k\u0302 (3 \xD7 2B\u2080 \u2212 5 \xD7 B\u2080) = B\u2080 k\u0302.
So F = e B\u2080 k\u0302. Setting this equal to 5e k\u0302 gives B\u2080 = 5.
So B\u2080 = 5 T.`},{pn:185,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"A proton with a kinetic energy of 2.0 eV moves into a region of uniform magnetic field of magnitude (\u03C0/2) \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 T. The angle between the direction of magnetic field and velocity of proton is 60\xB0. The pitch of the helical path taken by the proton is _____ cm. (Take mass of proton = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 kg and Charge on proton = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C).",n:40,s:`Pitch = (velocity component along B) \xD7 (time period) = v cos 60\xB0 \xD7 T.
Speed: \xBDmv\xB2 = 2 \xD7 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 J, so v = \u221A((2 \xD7 3.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079)/(1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077)) = \u221A(4 \xD7 10\u2078) = 2 \xD7 10\u2074 m/s. So v cos 60\xB0 = 10\u2074 m/s.
T = 2\u03C0m/(qB) = (2\u03C0 \xD7 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077)/(1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 (\u03C0/2) \xD7 10\u207B\xB3) = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 s.
Pitch = 10\u2074 \xD7 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 = 0.4 m = 40 cm.`},{pn:186,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmot",st:"Cyclotron and mass spectrometer",q:"A charge particle of 2 \u03BCC accelerated by a potential of 100 V enters a region of uniform magnetic field of 4 mT at right angle to the direction of field. The charged particle completes semicircle of radius 3 cm inside magnetic field. The mass of the charge particle is _____ \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2078 kg.",n:144,s:`The particle gains energy qV: \xBDmv\xB2 = qV, so v = \u221A(2qV/m).
In the field r = mv/(qB), so r\xB2 = m\xB2v\xB2/(q\xB2B\xB2) = 2mV/(qB\xB2).
m = qB\xB2r\xB2/(2V) = (2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 (4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3)\xB2 \xD7 (3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2)\xB2)/(2 \xD7 100).
= (2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074)/200 = 2.88 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2074/200 = 1.44 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2076 kg.
= 144 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2078 kg, so the answer is 144.`},{pn:187,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"A deuteron and a proton moving with equal kinetic energy enter into a uniform magnetic field at right angle to the field. If r_d and r_p are the radii of their circular paths respectively, then the ratio r_d/r_p will be \u221Ax : 1, where x is _____.",n:2,s:`For equal kinetic energy, r = \u221A(2mK)/(qB), so r \u221D \u221Am / q.
Deuteron: mass 2m, charge e. Proton: mass m, charge e.
r_d/r_p = \u221A2/1, so \u221Ax = \u221A2.
x = 2.`},{pn:188,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmot",st:"Cyclotron and mass spectrometer",q:"A singly ionized magnesium atom (A = 24) ion is accelerated to kinetic energy 5 keV, and is projected perpendicularly into a magnetic field B of the magnitude 0.5 T. The radius of path formed will be _____ cm.",n:10,s:`Radius r = \u221A(2mK)/(qB).
m = 24 \xD7 1.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 \u2248 4.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2076 kg, K = 5 keV = 5 \xD7 10\xB3 \xD7 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 = 8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2076 J, q = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C.
\u221A(2mK) = \u221A(2 \xD7 4.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2076 \xD7 8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2076) = \u221A(6.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074\xB9) = 8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB9.
r = 8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB9/(1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 0.5) = 0.1 m = 10 cm.`},{pn:189,y:"JEE Advanced 2021",tp:"mmot",st:"Cyclotron and mass spectrometer",q:"An \u03B1-particle (mass 4 amu) and a singly charged sulfur ion (mass 32 amu) are initially at rest. They are accelerated through a potential V and then allowed to pass into a region of uniform magnetic field which is normal to the velocities of the particles. Within this region, the \u03B1-particle and the sulfur ion move in circular orbits of radii r_\u03B1 and r_S, respectively. The ratio (r_S/r_\u03B1) is _____.",n:4,s:`After acceleration, qV = \xBDmv\xB2, so mv = \u221A(2mqV).
Radius r = mv/(qB) = \u221A(2mV/q)/B, so r \u221D \u221A(m/q) for the same V and B.
\u03B1-particle: m = 4, q = 2 so m/q = 2. Sulfur ion: m = 32, q = 1 so m/q = 32.
r_S/r_\u03B1 = \u221A(32/2) = \u221A16 = 4.`},{pn:190,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmot",st:"Cyclotron and mass spectrometer",q:"If the maximum value of accelerating potential provided by a radio frequency oscillator is 12 kV. The number of revolutions made by a proton in a cyclotron to achieve one sixth of the speed of light is _____. [m_p = 1.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 kg, e = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C, Speed of light = 3 \xD7 10\u2078 m/s]",n:543,s:`In a cyclotron the proton crosses the gap twice per revolution, gaining eV each time, so it gains 2eV = 2 \xD7 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 12 \xD7 10\xB3 = 3.84 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075 J per revolution.
Final speed v = c/6 = 5 \xD7 10\u2077 m/s.
Final kinetic energy = \xBD \xD7 1.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 \xD7 (5 \xD7 10\u2077)\xB2 = 2.09 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2 J.
Number of revolutions = (2.09 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\xB2)/(3.84 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2075) \u2248 543.6.
The proton completes 543 full revolutions.`},{pn:191,y:"JEE Advanced 2018",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"In the xy-plane, the region y > 0 has a uniform magnetic field B\u2081 k\u0302 and the region y < 0 has another uniform magnetic field B\u2082 k\u0302. A positively charged particle is projected from the origin along the positive y-axis with speed v\u2080 = \u03C0 m s\u207B\xB9 at t = 0, as shown in the figure. Neglect gravity in this problem. Let t = T be the time when the particle crosses the x-axis from below for the first time. If B\u2082 = 4B\u2081, the average speed of the particle, in m s\u207B\xB9, along the x-axis in the time interval T is _____.",fig:{w:300,h:250,alt:"In the xy plane the region y > 0 has magnetic field B\u2081 and the region y < 0 has field B\u2082; a positive particle starts at the origin with speed v\u2080 = \u03C0 m/s along +y",d:[["l",30,125,280,125,"a"],["l",150,235,150,15,"a"],["t",276,141,"x","e"],["t",160,22,"y"],["t",142,141,"O","e"],["l",150,125,150,80,"a"],["t",96,96,"v\u2080 = \u03C0 m s\u207B\xB9","e"],["c",150,125,3,"F"],["t",230,70,"B\u2081","m"],["t",230,190,"B\u2082","m"]]},n:2,s:`In the upper region r\u2081 = mv\u2080/(qB\u2081). A positive charge moving along +y in B\u2081k\u0302 is pushed towards +x, so it makes a half circle and reaches the x-axis at x = 2r\u2081 (see the solution figure), after time \u03C0r\u2081/v\u2080.
In the lower region r\u2082 = mv\u2080/(qB\u2082) = r\u2081/4. The velocity is now along \u2212y and the force is towards \u2212x, so the half circle brings the particle back to the x-axis at x = 2r\u2081 \u2212 2r\u2082 = 1.5r\u2081, after time \u03C0r\u2082/v\u2080.
The average speed is the total path covered along x divided by T. The x-distance covered is 2r\u2081 + 2r\u2082 = 2.5r\u2081.
T = \u03C0(r\u2081 + r\u2082)/v\u2080 = 1.25\u03C0r\u2081/v\u2080.
Average speed = 2.5r\u2081/(1.25\u03C0r\u2081/v\u2080) = 2v\u2080/\u03C0 = 2 m/s (as v\u2080 = \u03C0 m/s).`,sfig:{w:300,h:170,alt:"Upper semicircle of radius r1 from the origin to x = 2r1, then a lower semicircle of radius r2 = r1/4 back to x = 1.5 r1",d:[["l",15,100,285,100,"a"],["t",290,104,"x"],["l",40,165,40,10,"a"],["t",46,14,"y"],["t",34,114,"O","e"],["p","M40 100 A80 80 0 0 1 200 100"],["l",119,20,121,20,"a"],["p","M200 100 A20 20 0 0 1 160 100"],["l",181,120,179,120,"a"],["c",200,100,2.5,"F"],["c",160,100,2.5,"F"],["l",120,100,120,20,"d"],["t",126,62,"r\u2081"],["l",180,100,180,120,"d"],["t",186,138,"r\u2082"],["t",208,92,"2r\u2081"],["t",160,156,"1.5r\u2081","m"],["t",250,50,"B\u2081"],["t",250,150,"B\u2082"]]}},{pn:192,y:"JEE Advanced 2014",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:"Two parallel wires in the plane of the paper are distance X\u2080 apart. A point charge is moving with speed u between the wires in the same plane at a distance X\u2081 from one of the wires. When the wires carry current of magnitude I in the same direction, the radius of curvature of the path of the point charge is R\u2081. In contrast, if the currents I in the two wires have directions opposite to each other, the radius of curvature of the path is R\u2082. If X\u2080/X\u2081 = 3, the value of R\u2081/R\u2082 is _____.",n:3,s:`The radius is R = mu/(qB), so R \u221D 1/B. We need the field between the wires in each case.
Let the charge be at distance X\u2081 from wire 1, and X\u2080 \u2212 X\u2081 = 2X\u2081 from wire 2 (since X\u2080 = 3X\u2081).
Same direction currents: between the wires the two fields point opposite ways, so B\u2081 = (\u03BC\u2080I/2\u03C0)(1/X\u2081 \u2212 1/(2X\u2081)) = (\u03BC\u2080I/2\u03C0)(1/(2X\u2081)).
Opposite currents: between the wires the fields add, so B\u2082 = (\u03BC\u2080I/2\u03C0)(1/X\u2081 + 1/(2X\u2081)) = (\u03BC\u2080I/2\u03C0)(3/(2X\u2081)).
R\u2081/R\u2082 = B\u2082/B\u2081 = 3.`},{pn:193,y:"JEE Advanced 2017",tp:"mmot",st:"Velocity selector and crossed fields",q:`Use the table below; each option gives one entry from each column (Column 1, Column 2, Column 3).
Column 1: (I) Electron with v = 2(E\u2080)/(B\u2080) x\u0302 ; (II) Electron with v = (E\u2080)/(B\u2080) \u0177 ; (III) Proton with v = 0 ; (IV) Proton with v = 2(E\u2080)/(B\u2080) x\u0302.
Column 2: (i) E = E\u2080 \u1E91 ; (ii) E = \u2212E\u2080 \u0177 ; (iii) E = \u2212E\u2080 x\u0302 ; (iv) E = E\u2080 x\u0302.
Column 3: (P) B = \u2212B\u2080 x\u0302 ; (Q) B = B\u2080 x\u0302 ; (R) B = B\u2080 \u0177 ; (S) B = B\u2080 \u1E91.
In which case would the particle move in a straight line along the negative direction of y-axis (i.e., move along \u2212\u0177)?`,o:["(IV) (ii) (S)","(II) (iii) (Q)","(III) (ii) (R)","(III) (ii) (P)"],c:2,s:`The force on a particle is F = q(E + v \xD7 B). It moves in a straight line along \u2212y only if the force always points along \u2212y, or is zero while it already moves along \u2212y.
Option (c): a proton at rest (III), E = \u2212E\u2080\u0177 (ii), B = B\u2080\u0177 (R). At first only the electric force acts: it is along \u2212y, so the proton starts moving along \u2212y.
Once it moves along \u2212y it is moving along B (which is along +y), so v \xD7 B = 0 and the magnetic force stays zero. The proton keeps accelerating along \u2212y in a straight line.
Check the others. (a) IV (ii) S: F = q(\u2212E\u2080\u0177 + 2(E\u2080/B\u2080)x\u0302 \xD7 B\u2080\u1E91) = q(\u2212E\u2080 \u2212 2E\u2080)\u0177, but the proton already has velocity along x, so it does not move along \u2212y only.
(b) II (iii) Q: electron, the force has x and z parts, not along y. (d) III (ii) P: after the proton picks up velocity along \u2212y, B along \u2212x gives a sideways force along z, so it curves.
Note: a charge moving parallel to B feels no magnetic force, so the electric force alone then moves it in a straight line.
Only option (c) works.`},{pn:194,y:"JEE Advanced 2017",tp:"mmot",st:"Velocity selector and crossed fields",q:`Use the table below; each option gives one entry from each column (Column 1, Column 2, Column 3).
Column 1: (I) Electron with v = 2(E\u2080)/(B\u2080) x\u0302 ; (II) Electron with v = (E\u2080)/(B\u2080) \u0177 ; (III) Proton with v = 0 ; (IV) Proton with v = 2(E\u2080)/(B\u2080) x\u0302.
Column 2: (i) E = E\u2080 \u1E91 ; (ii) E = \u2212E\u2080 \u0177 ; (iii) E = \u2212E\u2080 x\u0302 ; (iv) E = E\u2080 x\u0302.
Column 3: (P) B = \u2212B\u2080 x\u0302 ; (Q) B = B\u2080 x\u0302 ; (R) B = B\u2080 \u0177 ; (S) B = B\u2080 \u1E91.
In which case will the particle move in a straight line with constant velocity?`,o:["(II) (iii) (S)","(III) (iii) (P)","(IV) (i) (S)","(III) (ii) (R)"],c:0,s:`Constant velocity means zero net force: q(E + v \xD7 B) = 0, which needs the particle to be moving with E and v \xD7 B equal and opposite.
A proton at rest (III) has v \xD7 B = 0, so with any E \u2260 0 the net force is not zero. Options (b) and (d) fail.
Option (c): IV (i) S: proton with v = 2(E\u2080/B\u2080)x\u0302, E = E\u2080\u1E91, B = B\u2080\u1E91. Then v \xD7 B = 2E\u2080(x\u0302 \xD7 \u1E91) = \u22122E\u2080\u0177, so the net force is q(E\u2080\u1E91 \u2212 2E\u2080\u0177), not zero.
Option (a): II (iii) S: electron, v = (E\u2080/B\u2080)\u0177, E = \u2212E\u2080x\u0302, B = B\u2080\u1E91. Electric force on the electron: \u2212e(\u2212E\u2080x\u0302) = +eE\u2080x\u0302.
Magnetic force: \u2212e(v \xD7 B) = \u2212e((E\u2080/B\u2080)B\u2080)(\u0177 \xD7 \u1E91) = \u2212eE\u2080x\u0302.
The two forces are +eE\u2080x\u0302 and \u2212eE\u2080x\u0302, so they cancel and the electron moves with constant velocity.
So it is option (a).`},{pn:195,y:"JEE Advanced 2017",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:`Use the table below; each option gives one entry from each column (Column 1, Column 2, Column 3).
Column 1: (I) Electron with v = 2(E\u2080)/(B\u2080) x\u0302 ; (II) Electron with v = (E\u2080)/(B\u2080) \u0177 ; (III) Proton with v = 0 ; (IV) Proton with v = 2(E\u2080)/(B\u2080) x\u0302.
Column 2: (i) E = E\u2080 \u1E91 ; (ii) E = \u2212E\u2080 \u0177 ; (iii) E = \u2212E\u2080 x\u0302 ; (iv) E = E\u2080 x\u0302.
Column 3: (P) B = \u2212B\u2080 x\u0302 ; (Q) B = B\u2080 x\u0302 ; (R) B = B\u2080 \u0177 ; (S) B = B\u2080 \u1E91.
In which case will the particle describe a helical path with axis along the positive z direction?`,o:["(II) (ii) (R)","(III) (ii) (P)","(IV) (i) (S)","(IV) (ii) (R)"],c:2,s:`A helical path with axis along z needs B along z (so the motion perpendicular to z is a circle), and a force along +z from E (so the particle drifts along z with growing speed).
So B = B\u2080\u1E91, which is (S), and E must be along +\u1E91, which is (i). Only option (c) has both.
Option (c): proton with v = 2(E\u2080/B\u2080)x\u0302 perpendicular to B = B\u2080\u1E91. This gives a circle in the xy-plane with radius mv/(eB\u2080).
E = E\u2080\u1E91 along B gives a constant acceleration eE\u2080/m along +z. The circle plus the steady drift along z is a helix about the z-axis.
The other options have B along \u0177 or \u2212x\u0302, not along z, so the axis is not z.
So it is option (c).`},{pn:196,y:"JEE Advanced 2014",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",fig:{w:300,h:150,alt:"A thin rectangular metal strip of length l along x, width w along z and thickness d along y, current I along +x and field B along +y; points K and M on the two opposite faces parallel to the xy-plane (front and back)",d:[["p","M70 40 L220 40 L190 66 L40 66 Z"],["p","M40 66 L190 66 L190 92 L40 92 Z"],["p","M190 66 L220 40 L220 66 L190 92 Z"],["l",70,40,70,66,"d"],["l",70,66,40,66,"d"],["l",70,66,220,66,"d"],["c",115,79,2.5,"F"],["t",115,108,"K","m"],["c",145,53,2.5,"F"],["t",152,46,"M"],["l",6,79,36,79,"a"],["t",20,72,"I","m"],["l",194,79,224,79,"a"],["t",212,72,"I","m"],["l",100,36,100,8,"a"],["t",106,14,"B"],["t",130,128,"l (length)","m"],["t",204,108,"d"],["t",222,34,"w"],["l",262,70,262,34,"a"],["t",268,38,"y"],["l",262,70,292,70,"a"],["t",296,74,"x"],["l",262,70,244,86,"a"],["t",240,98,"z","m"]]},q:`In a thin rectangular metallic strip a constant current I flows along the positive x-direction. The length, width and thickness of the strip are l, w and d, respectively. A uniform magnetic field B is applied on the strip along the positive y-direction. Due to this, the charge carriers experience a net deflection along the z-direction, so a potential difference develops along the z-direction (the current is carried by electrons).
Consider two different metallic strips (1 and 2) of the same material. Their lengths are the same, widths are w\u2081 and w\u2082 and thicknesses are d\u2081 and d\u2082, respectively. Two points K and M are symmetrically located on the opposite faces parallel to the x-y plane (see figure). V\u2081 and V\u2082 are the potential differences between K and M in strips 1 and 2, respectively. Then, for a given current I flowing through them in a given magnetic field strength B, the correct statement(s) is(are)`,o:["If w\u2081 = w\u2082 and d\u2081 = 2d\u2082, then V\u2082 = 2V\u2081","If w\u2081 = w\u2082 and d\u2081 = 2d\u2082, then V\u2082 = V\u2081","If w\u2081 = 2w\u2082 and d\u2081 = d\u2082, then V\u2082 = 2V\u2081","If w\u2081 = 2w\u2082 and d\u2081 = d\u2082, then V\u2082 = V\u2081"],c:[0,3],s:`Carriers drift along x with speed v = I/(n e w d), where n is the carrier density (same material, so the same n), w the width and d the thickness (cross-section area w \xD7 d).
The magnetic force evB pushes them along z, across the width w. They pile up on one face until the electric field E = vB it creates balances the force.
The potential difference between K and M, which are on the faces separated by w, is V = E \xD7 w = vBw.
Substituting v: V = (I/(n e w d)) \xD7 B \xD7 w = IB/(n e d). The width w cancels; V depends only on the thickness d.
(a) w\u2081 = w\u2082, d\u2081 = 2d\u2082: V \u221D 1/d, so V\u2082 = 2V\u2081. True. (b) is the opposite, so false.
(c) and (d): d\u2081 = d\u2082, so V\u2081 = V\u2082 whatever the widths. (d) is true, (c) is false.
Note: K and M are on the two faces parallel to the xy-plane, which are w apart (not d apart).
Correct statements: (a) and (d).`},{pn:197,y:"JEE Advanced 2014",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:`In a thin rectangular metallic strip a constant current I flows along the positive x-direction. The length, width and thickness of the strip are l, w and d, respectively. A uniform magnetic field B is applied along the positive y-direction, so charge carriers (electrons) are deflected along the z-direction and a potential difference develops across the strip along z (between the two faces parallel to the x-y plane, points K and M).
Consider two different metallic strips (1 and 2) of the same dimensions (length l, width w and thickness d) with carrier densities n\u2081 and n\u2082, respectively. Strip 1 is placed in magnetic field B\u2081 and strip 2 is placed in magnetic field B\u2082, both along positive y-directions. Then V\u2081 and V\u2082 are the potential differences developed between K and M in strips 1 and 2, respectively. Assuming that the current I is the same for both the strips, the correct option(s) is(are)`,o:["If B\u2081 = B\u2082 and n\u2081 = 2n\u2082, then V\u2082 = 2V\u2081","If B\u2081 = B\u2082 and n\u2081 = 2n\u2082, then V\u2082 = V\u2081","If B\u2081 = 2B\u2082 and n\u2081 = n\u2082, then V\u2082 = 0.5V\u2081","If B\u2081 = 2B\u2082 and n\u2081 = n\u2082, then V\u2082 = V\u2081"],c:[0,2],s:`For the strip in the Hall set-up, the carriers drift with v = I/(n e w d).
They are pushed across the width w until the Hall field E = vB balances the magnetic force, so the potential difference between K and M (distance w apart) is V = vBw.
V = (I/(n e w d)) \xD7 B \xD7 w = IB/(n e d). Here I, e and d are the same for both strips, so V \u221D B/n.
(a), (b) B\u2081 = B\u2082, n\u2081 = 2n\u2082: V\u2082/V\u2081 = (B\u2082/n\u2082)/(B\u2081/n\u2081) = n\u2081/n\u2082 = 2. So V\u2082 = 2V\u2081: (a) true, (b) false.
(c), (d) B\u2081 = 2B\u2082, n\u2081 = n\u2082: V\u2082/V\u2081 = B\u2082/B\u2081 = 1/2. So V\u2082 = 0.5V\u2081: (c) true, (d) false.
Correct options: (a) and (c).`},{pn:198,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:`Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A): If Oxygen ion (O\u207B\xB2) and Hydrogen ion (H\u207A) enter normal to the magnetic field with equal momentum, then the path of O\u207B\xB2 ion has a smaller curvature than that of H\u207A.
Reason (R): A proton with same linear momentum as an electron will form a path of smaller radius of curvature on entering a uniform magnetic field perpendicularly.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below`,o:["Both A and R are true and R is the correct explanation of A","A is false but R is true","Both A and R are true but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false"],c:3,s:`For a charge q with momentum p entering a field B perpendicular to it, the radius is r = mv/(qB) = p/(qB).
Assertion: equal p, so r \u221D 1/q. O\u207B\xB2 has charge 2e and H\u207A has charge e, so the O\u207B\xB2 path has half the radius: its path is more tightly curved.
Note: "smaller curvature" is read here as "smaller radius of curvature", which is how the printed key (d) treats it. Strictly, curvature is 1/r, so the assertion would then be false and both statements false, but that is not one of the options.
Reason: a proton and an electron have charges of the same size e and (in the statement) the same momentum p, so r = p/(eB) is the same for both. The proton does not give a smaller radius, so R is false.
A is true (in the sense above) and R is false: option (d).`},{pn:199,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:`Given below are two statements. One is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A): An electron in a certain region of uniform magnetic field is moving with constant velocity in a straight line path.
Reason (R): The magnetic field in that region is along the direction of velocity of the electron.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below`,o:["Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is true but (R) is false","(A) is false but (R) is true"],c:1,s:`The magnetic force is F = \u2212e(v \xD7 B). It is zero only when v is parallel (or antiparallel) to B, or when B = 0.
If B is along the velocity of the electron (the Reason), then v \xD7 B = 0, there is no force, and the electron keeps moving in a straight line with constant velocity.
In a region with only a magnetic field, a straight-line motion at constant velocity can only happen in this way, so the Assertion is true, and the Reason is exactly what makes it happen.
So both A and R are true and R explains A: option (b).`},{pn:200,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:`Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A): In an uniform magnetic field, speed and energy remains the same for a moving charged particle.
Reason (R): Moving charged particle experiences magnetic force perpendicular to its direction of motion.`,o:["Both (A) and (R) are true and (R) is the correct explanation of (A).","Both (A) and (R) are true but (R) is NOT the correct explanation of (A).","(A) is true but (R) is false.","(A) is false but (R) is true."],c:0,s:`The magnetic force is F = qv \xD7 B, which is always at right angles to the velocity (Reason is true).
Work done by a force is F\xB7v dt. When F \u27C2 v, the work is zero, so the kinetic energy of the particle does not change.
If the kinetic energy is constant, the speed is constant too, so Assertion is true.
The Assertion follows from the Reason, so R explains A: option (a).`},{pn:201,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement-I: The electric force changes the speed of the charged particle and hence changes its kinetic energy; whereas the magnetic force does not change the kinetic energy of the charged particle.
Statement-II: The electric force accelerates the positively charged particle perpendicular to the direction of electric field. The magnetic force accelerates the moving charged particle along the direction of magnetic field.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are correct.","Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct."],c:2,s:`Statement I: the electric force qE can do work along the motion, so it can change the speed and kinetic energy. The magnetic force is perpendicular to the velocity, so it does no work. Statement I is correct.
Statement II: the electric force on a positive charge is along E (not perpendicular to it), so the first half is wrong.
The magnetic force qv \xD7 B is perpendicular to B (and to v), never along B, so the second half is wrong too.
Statement I is correct and Statement II is incorrect: option (c).`},{pn:202,y:"IIT-JEE 2004",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"A proton and an \u03B1-particle are accelerated with same potential difference and they enter in the region of constant magnetic field B perpendicular to the velocity of particles. Find the ratio of radius of curvature of proton to the radius of curvature of \u03B1-particle.",a:"r(proton) : r(\u03B1) = 1 : \u221A2 (about 0.71)",s:`After acceleration through potential difference V a particle of charge q and mass m has kinetic energy qV, so \xBDmv\xB2 = qV and v = \u221A(2qV/m).
In the field B perpendicular to v the magnetic force provides the circular motion: mv\xB2/r = qvB, so r = mv/(qB).
Substituting v: r = (1/B)\u221A(2mV/q). With the same V and B, r \u221D \u221A(m/q).
Proton: m/q = m_p/e. \u03B1-particle: mass 4m_p and charge 2e, so m/q = 2m_p/e, twice the proton value.
r_p/r_\u03B1 = \u221A(1/2) = 1/\u221A2.
So the ratio of the radii (proton : \u03B1) is 1 : \u221A2.`},{pn:203,y:"IIT-JEE 1999",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:`The region between x = 0 and x = L is filled with uniform, steady magnetic field B\u2080k\u0302. A particle of mass m, positive charge q and velocity v\u2080\xEE travels along x-axis and enters the region of the magnetic field. Neglect gravity throughout the question.
(a) Find the value of L if the particle emerges from the region of magnetic field with its final velocity at an angle 30\xB0 to its initial velocity.
(b) Find the final velocity of the particle and the time spent by it in the magnetic field, if the magnetic field now extends up to 2.1L.`,a:"(a) L = mv\u2080/(2qB\u2080). (b) Final velocity = \u2212v\u2080 \xEE (same speed, reversed direction); time in the field = \u03C0m/(qB\u2080).",s:`In the field the particle moves on a circle of radius r = mv\u2080/(qB\u2080) (force perpendicular to v). It enters moving along +x, and for positive q with B along +z the force is along \u2212y, so the circle bends towards \u2212y with its centre on the line x = 0.
(a) When the particle has turned through angle \u03B8, it has moved a distance r sin \u03B8 along x. It leaves at x = L with \u03B8 = 30\xB0, so L = r sin 30\xB0 = r/2 = mv\u2080/(2qB\u2080).
(b) Now the field extends to 2.1L = 1.05r, which is more than r (the largest x the circle reaches). So the particle never reaches the far edge: it completes a half circle and comes back out through the plane x = 0.
Speed does not change in a magnetic field, so it leaves with speed v\u2080. After half a circle the velocity is reversed: final velocity = \u2212v\u2080 \xEE.
Time = half the period = (1/2)(2\u03C0m/(qB\u2080)) = \u03C0m/(qB\u2080).
Note: for 30\xB0 the exit is before the half circle is done; once L > r (here 2.1L = 1.05r), the half circle is complete and the particle turns back.`},{pn:204,y:"IIT-JEE 1998",tp:"mmot",st:"Velocity selector and crossed fields",q:"A particle of mass m and charge q is moving in a region where uniform, constant electric and magnetic fields E and B are present. E and B are parallel to each other. At time t = 0 the velocity of the particle is perpendicular to E. (Assume that its speed is always << c, the speed of light in vacuum.) Find the velocity v of the particle at time t. You must express your answer in terms of t, q, m, the vectors v\u2080, E and B and their magnitudes v\u2080, E and B.",a:"Take \u1E91 along E and B (E \u2225 B) and x\u0302 along v\u2080. Then v = v\u2080 cos(qBt/m) x\u0302 \u2212 v\u2080 sin(qBt/m) \u0177 + (qEt/m) \u1E91. In vector form, the part along E and B is (qt/m)E; the part perpendicular is v\u2080 rotating about B at angular speed qB/m. Speed: |v| = \u221A(v\u2080\xB2 + (qEt/m)\xB2).",s:`Choose axes: \u1E91 along E and B (they are parallel), x\u0302 along v\u2080 at t = 0 (v\u2080 \u27C2 E).
Along z only the electric force acts (v \xD7 B has no z-part because B is along z): a_z = qE/m, so v_z = qEt/m, starting from zero.
In the xy-plane only the magnetic force acts, with speed v\u2080 and F = qv\u2080B \u27C2 v. This is circular motion with angular speed \u03C9 = qB/m.
Starting along +x for a positive q with B along +z, the force points along \u2212y, so v_x = v\u2080 cos(\u03C9t) and v_y = \u2212v\u2080 sin(\u03C9t).
Putting the three parts together: v = v\u2080 cos(qBt/m) x\u0302 \u2212 v\u2080 sin(qBt/m) \u0177 + (qEt/m) \u1E91.
The speed is |v| = \u221A(v\u2080\xB2 + (qEt/m)\xB2): the particle spirals along the field with an ever longer pitch.
Note: if B points opposite to E, the sign of the \u1E91 part flips relative to the rotation sense; the size of each part stays the same.`},{pn:205,y:"IIT-JEE 1993",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",fig:{w:300,h:210,alt:"Electron gun G on the x-axis emitting electrons along +x; spot S lies on the line GS at 60 degrees above the x-axis; magnetic field B is parallel to GS",d:[["l",20,170,280,170,"da"],["t",284,174,"x"],["r",24,158,28,24],["l",52,170,70,170,"a"],["t",38,150,"G","m"],["c",72,170,2.5,"F"],["t",78,186,"G"],["l",72,170,142,48.6],["c",142,48.6,3,"F"],["t",150,46,"S"],["l",100,122,114,98,"a"],["t",122,104,"B"],["p","M102 170 A30 30 0 0 0 87 144"],["t",112,162,"60\xB0"],["t",110,70,"0.1 m","e"]]},q:"An electron gun G emits electrons of energy 2 keV travelling in the positive x-direction. The electrons are required to hit the spot S where GS = 0.1 m, and the line GS makes an angle of 60\xB0 with the x-axis as shown in the figure. A uniform magnetic field B parallel to GS exists in the region outside the electron gun. Find the minimum value of B needed to make the electrons hit S.",a:"B \u2248 4.7 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 T (using m\u2091 = 9.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg, e = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C)",s:`Split the velocity into a part along B (along GS) and a part perpendicular to B: v\u2225 = v cos 60\xB0, v\u22A5 = v sin 60\xB0.
The perpendicular part makes a circle and the parallel part carries the electron along GS, so the path is a helix with axis GS.
The electron returns to the axis GS after each full turn. To land on S it must cover the distance GS = 0.1 m along the axis in a whole number of turns: GS = v\u2225 \xD7 nT, with T = 2\u03C0m/(eB).
The smallest B means the longest period, so take n = 1: B = 2\u03C0m v cos 60\xB0/(e \xD7 GS).
Speed from the energy: K = 2 keV = 3.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2076 J, so v = \u221A(2K/m) = \u221A(6.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2076/9.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9) \u2248 2.65 \xD7 10\u2077 m/s, and v cos 60\xB0 \u2248 1.33 \xD7 10\u2077 m/s.
B = (2\u03C0 \xD7 9.1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 \xD7 1.33 \xD7 10\u2077)/(1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 0.1) \u2248 7.58 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3/1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2070 \u2248 4.7 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 T.
So the minimum field is about 4.7 mT.`},{pn:206,y:"IIT-JEE 1990",tp:"mbs",st:"Superposition and symmetry",fig:{w:320,h:150,alt:"Two long wires at P and Q perpendicular to the page, currents 2.5 A and I into the page, on the line P Q R; P is 5 m from R and Q is 2 m from R",d:[["l",30,100,300,100,"a"],["t",304,104,"x"],["c",140,100,7],["l",135.5,95.5,144.5,104.5],["l",135.5,104.5,144.5,95.5],["c",200,100,7],["l",195.5,95.5,204.5,104.5],["l",195.5,104.5,204.5,95.5],["c",240,100,3,"F"],["t",140,124,"P","m"],["t",200,124,"Q","m"],["t",240,124,"R","m"],["t",140,140,"2.5 A","m"],["t",200,140,"I","m"],["l",140,36,240,36,"b"],["t",190,28,"5 m","m"],["l",200,64,240,64,"b"],["t",220,56,"2 m","m"]]},q:`Two long parallel wires carrying current 2.5 ampere and I ampere in the same direction (directed into the plane of the paper) are held at P and Q respectively such that they are perpendicular to the plane of paper. The points P and Q are located at distance of 5 metre and 2 metre respectively from a collinear point R (see figure).
(i) An electron moving with a velocity of 4 \xD7 10\u2075 m/s along the positive x-direction experiences a force of magnitude 3.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2070 N at the point R. Find the value of I.
(ii) Find all the positions at which a third long parallel wire carrying a current of magnitude 2.5 ampere may be placed so that the magnetic induction at R is zero.`,a:"(i) I = 4 A. (ii) At 1 m from R on the line PQR: either 1 m to the left of R (between Q and R) with its current out of the page, or 1 m to the right of R with its current into the page.",s:`Both wires carry current into the page, so at R (to their right on the x-axis) each field is along \u2212y (clockwise circulation). The fields add.
B_R = (\u03BC\u2080/2\u03C0)(2.5/5 + I/2) = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 (0.5 + I/2) T.
(i) The electron moves along x and B is along y, so they are perpendicular: F = evB. Then B = F/(ev) = 3.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2070/(1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 4 \xD7 10\u2075) = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T.
2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 (0.5 + I/2) = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077, so 0.5 + I/2 = 2.5 and I = 4 A.
(ii) The two wires give 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T along \u2212y at R with I = 4 A. The third wire (2.5 A) must give 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T along +y at R.
Its distance d from R: 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 2.5/d = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077, so d = 1 m. Its field at R is perpendicular to the line joining the wire to R, so to be along y the wire must lie on the line PQR (the x-axis).
So it sits 1 m from R on either side. To the right of R it must carry current into the page (R is then to its left, where its field is +y). To the left of R, between Q and R, it must carry current out of the page.`},{pn:207,y:"IIT-JEE 1986",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"A beam of protons with a velocity 4 \xD7 10\u2075 m/sec enters a uniform magnetic field of 0.3 tesla at an angle of 60\xB0 to the magnetic field. Find the radius of the helical path taken by the proton beam. Also find the pitch of the helix (which is the distance travelled by a proton in the beam parallel to the magnetic field during one period of rotation).",a:"Radius \u2248 1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 m (1.2 cm); pitch \u2248 4.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 m (4.4 cm). (using m_p = 1.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 kg, e = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C)",s:`Split the velocity: perpendicular to B, v\u22A5 = v sin 60\xB0 = 4 \xD7 10\u2075 \xD7 0.866 = 3.46 \xD7 10\u2075 m/s; along B, v\u2225 = v cos 60\xB0 = 2 \xD7 10\u2075 m/s.
The perpendicular part gives a circle: r = mv\u22A5/(eB) = (1.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 \xD7 3.46 \xD7 10\u2075)/(1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 0.3).
r = 5.78 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB2/4.8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2070 \u2248 1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 m, about 1.2 cm.
The time for one turn is T = 2\u03C0m/(eB) = 2\u03C0 \xD7 1.67 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077/(1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 0.3) \u2248 2.19 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 s.
The pitch is the distance along B in one turn: p = v\u2225T = 2 \xD7 10\u2075 \xD7 2.19 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \u2248 4.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 m, about 4.4 cm.`},{pn:208,y:"IIT-JEE 1984",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",fig:{w:300,h:230,alt:"A vertical boundary with a magnetic field into the page on its right (crosses). A particle enters at E moving up and to the right at 45 degrees to the boundary, moves on an arc inside the field and leaves at F above E, moving up and to the left at angle theta to the boundary",d:[["l",147,27,153,33],["l",147,33,153,27],["l",147,59,153,65],["l",147,65,153,59],["l",147,91,153,97],["l",147,97,153,91],["l",147,123,153,129],["l",147,129,153,123],["l",147,155,153,161],["l",147,161,153,155],["l",147,187,153,193],["l",147,193,153,187],["l",177,27,183,33],["l",177,33,183,27],["l",177,59,183,65],["l",177,65,183,59],["l",177,91,183,97],["l",177,97,183,91],["l",177,123,183,129],["l",177,129,183,123],["l",177,155,183,161],["l",177,161,183,155],["l",177,187,183,193],["l",177,193,183,187],["l",207,27,213,33],["l",207,33,213,27],["l",207,59,213,65],["l",207,65,213,59],["l",207,91,213,97],["l",207,97,213,91],["l",207,123,213,129],["l",207,129,213,123],["l",207,155,213,161],["l",207,161,213,155],["l",207,187,213,193],["l",207,193,213,187],["l",237,27,243,33],["l",237,33,243,27],["l",237,59,243,65],["l",237,65,243,59],["l",237,91,243,97],["l",237,97,243,91],["l",237,123,243,129],["l",237,129,243,123],["l",237,155,243,161],["l",237,161,243,155],["l",237,187,243,193],["l",237,193,243,187],["l",267,27,273,33],["l",267,33,273,27],["l",267,59,273,65],["l",267,65,273,59],["l",267,91,273,97],["l",267,97,273,91],["l",267,123,273,129],["l",267,129,273,123],["l",267,155,273,161],["l",267,161,273,155],["l",267,187,273,193],["l",267,193,273,187],["l",120,16,120,214],["l",56,214,120,150,"a"],["p","M120 150 A63.6 63.6 0 0 0 120 60","d"],["l",120,60,84,24,"a"],["t",128,164,"E"],["t",112,74,"F","e"],["t",116,50,"\u03B8","e"],["t",104,198,"45\xB0","e"]]},q:`A particle of mass m = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 kg and charge q = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 C enters a region of uniform magnetic field of strength 1 tesla along the direction as shown in the figure. The speed of the particle is 10\u2077 m/s.
(i) The magnetic field is directed along the inward normal to the plane of the paper. The particle leaves the region of the field at the point F. Find the distance EF and the angle \u03B8.
(ii) If the direction of the field is along the outward normal to the plane of the paper, find the time spent by the particle in the region of the magnetic field after entering it at E.`,a:"(i) EF = \u221A2 r \u2248 0.14 m (r = 0.1 m), \u03B8 = 45\xB0. (ii) t = (3/4)T = 3\u03C0/2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 s \u2248 4.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 s.",s:`Radius of the circle: r = mv/(qB) = (1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2077 \xD7 10\u2077)/(1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 1) = 0.1 m. Period: T = 2\u03C0m/(qB) = 2\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 s \u2248 6.28 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 s.
(i) For a positive charge moving up-right with B into the page, F = qv \xD7 B points up-left. The path bends to the left, back towards the boundary (see the figure).
The centre of the circle lies on the left of the boundary, at 45\xB0 from the boundary line. The arc inside the field is the short arc, turning the velocity by 90\xB0.
The chord EF along the boundary: EF = 2r sin 45\xB0 = \u221A2 r = 0.1414 m \u2248 0.14 m.
Turning the velocity by 90\xB0 from up-right takes it to up-left, which makes 45\xB0 with the boundary. So \u03B8 = 45\xB0.
(ii) With the field out of the page, the force is down-right, so the path bends clockwise into the field. The particle goes round the long arc, which turns through 270\xB0, and then leaves.
Time = (270\xB0/360\xB0)T = (3/4)(2\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078) = (3\u03C0/2) \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 s \u2248 4.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078 s.`},{pn:209,y:"IIT-JEE 1982",tp:"mmot",st:"Velocity selector and crossed fields",q:"A particle of mass 1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2076 kg and charge +1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 coulomb travelling with a velocity 1.28 \xD7 10\u2076 m/s in the +x direction enters a region in which a uniform electric field E and a uniform magnetic field of induction B are present such that E\u2093 = E_y = 0, E_z = \u2212102.4 kV/m and B\u2093 = B_z = 0, B_y = 8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 weber/m\xB2. The particle enters this region at the origin at time t = 0. Determine the location (x, y and z coordinates) of the particle at t = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 s. If the electric field is switched off at this instant (with the magnetic field still present), what will be the position of the particle at t = 7.45 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 s?",a:"At t = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 s: (x, y, z) = (6.4 m, 0, 0). At t = 7.45 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 s: (6.4 m, 0, 2.0 m).",s:`Forces at t = 0: electric F_E = qE = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 (\u22121.024 \xD7 10\u2075) \u1E91 = \u22121.64 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2074 \u1E91 N.
Magnetic F_B = qv \xD7 B = q v B (x\u0302 \xD7 \u0177) = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 1.28 \xD7 10\u2076 \xD7 8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \u1E91 = +1.64 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2074 \u1E91 N.
The two forces cancel (this is the velocity selector condition v = E/B = 1.024 \xD7 10\u2075/0.08 = 1.28 \xD7 10\u2076 m/s). The particle moves straight along x at constant speed.
At t = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 s: x = 1.28 \xD7 10\u2076 \xD7 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 = 6.4 m, so the position is (6.4 m, 0, 0).
Now E is off. Only the magnetic force acts, so the particle goes on a circle in the xz-plane with r = mv/(qB) = (10\u207B\xB2\u2076 \xD7 1.28 \xD7 10\u2076)/(1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 0.08) = 1.0 m.
The force at the start of the circle is along +z, so the centre is at (6.4 m, 0, 1 m).
The period is T = 2\u03C0m/(qB) = 2\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2076/(1.28 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\u2070) \u2248 4.9 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 s. The time elapsed is 7.45 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \u2212 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 = 2.45 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 s, which is T/2.
After half a circle the particle is diametrically opposite to its starting point: (6.4 m, 0, 2.0 m).`},{pn:210,y:"IIT-JEE 1981",tp:"mmot",st:"Velocity selector and crossed fields",fig:{w:300,h:200,alt:"Two vertical parallel plates 3 mm apart, left plate positive and right plate negative, 600 V across them; an electron moves vertically upward between them at 2 times 10 to the 6 m/s",d:[["l",100,30,100,150,"B"],["l",190,30,190,150,"B"],["t",88,50,"+","e"],["t",88,80,"+","e"],["t",88,110,"+","e"],["t",88,140,"+","e"],["t",204,50,"\u2212"],["t",204,80,"\u2212"],["t",204,110,"\u2212"],["t",204,140,"\u2212"],["l",145,140,145,60,"a"],["t",152,100,"v"],["t",145,172,"v = 2 \xD7 10\u2076 m/s","m"],["l",100,18,190,18,"b"],["t",145,12,"3 mm","m"],["t",250,95,"600 V","m"]]},q:"A potential difference of 600 volt is applied across the plates of a parallel plate condenser. The separation between the plates is 3 mm. An electron projected vertically, parallel to the plates, with a velocity of 2 \xD7 10\u2076 m/sec moves undeflected between the plates. Find the magnitude and direction of the magnetic field in the region between the condenser plates. (Neglect the edge effects). (Charge of the electron = \u22121.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 coulomb)",a:"B = 0.1 T, directed perpendicular to the plane of the paper, into the paper.",s:`Between the plates the electric field is E = V/d = 600/(3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3) = 2 \xD7 10\u2075 V/m, pointing from the positive (left) plate to the negative (right) plate, that is to the right.
The electron is negative, so the electric force eE on it points to the left.
Undeflected motion needs the magnetic force to cancel it: it must point to the right, with eE = evB.
B = E/v = (2 \xD7 10\u2075)/(2 \xD7 10\u2076) = 0.1 T.
Direction: the electron moves up. For a negative charge the force is \u2212e(v \xD7 B). Try B into the page: v \xD7 B = (up) \xD7 (into page) = to the left, so \u2212e(v \xD7 B) is to the right. This is what we need.
So B = 0.1 T, perpendicular to the page and directed into the page.`},{pn:211,y:"IIT-JEE 1985",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"State whether true or false: An electron and proton are moving with the same kinetic energy along the same direction. When they pass through a uniform magnetic field perpendicular to the direction of their motion, they describe circular paths of the same radius.",o:["True","False"],c:1,s:`In a perpendicular field r = mv/(qB) = p/(qB), and with kinetic energy K, p = \u221A(2mK).
So r = \u221A(2mK)/(qB). The charges of the electron and proton have the same size, and K and B are the same.
Then r \u221D \u221Am. The electron mass is about 1/1836 of the proton mass, so its radius is about 1/43 of the proton's.
The radii are not the same, so the statement is false.`},{pn:212,y:"IIT-JEE 1983",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"State whether true or false: A charged particle enters a region of uniform magnetic field at an angle of 85\xB0 to the magnetic line of force. The path of the particle is a circle.",o:["True","False"],c:1,s:`Split the velocity into a part perpendicular to B and a part along B.
The perpendicular part (v sin 85\xB0) makes the particle go round in a circle. The part along B (v cos 85\xB0) is not changed by the field, because the magnetic force has no component along B.
Circle plus steady motion along the field makes a helix, not a circle. A circle needs the angle to be exactly 90\xB0.
So the statement is false.`},{pn:213,y:"IIT-JEE 1983",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:"State whether true or false: There is no change in the energy of a charged particle moving in magnetic field although a magnetic force is acting on it.",o:["True","False"],c:0,s:`The magnetic force qv \xD7 B is always perpendicular to the velocity.
Work done = F \xB7 v dt = 0, so the kinetic energy does not change; the force only changes the direction of the velocity.
So the statement is true.`},{pn:214,y:"IIT-JEE 1997",tp:"mmot",st:"Cyclotron and mass spectrometer",fig:{w:290,h:210,alt:"A magnetic field out of the page (dots) over a slit system; charged particles leave the source on the left going up, move on a semicircle in the field and enter the detector on the right going down",d:[["c",30,36,4.5],["c",30,36,1,"F"],["c",30,62,4.5],["c",30,62,1,"F"],["c",30,88,4.5],["c",30,88,1,"F"],["c",30,114,4.5],["c",30,114,1,"F"],["c",60,36,4.5],["c",60,36,1,"F"],["c",60,62,4.5],["c",60,62,1,"F"],["c",60,88,4.5],["c",60,88,1,"F"],["c",60,114,4.5],["c",60,114,1,"F"],["c",90,36,4.5],["c",90,36,1,"F"],["c",90,62,4.5],["c",90,62,1,"F"],["c",90,88,4.5],["c",90,88,1,"F"],["c",90,114,4.5],["c",90,114,1,"F"],["c",120,36,4.5],["c",120,36,1,"F"],["c",120,62,4.5],["c",120,62,1,"F"],["c",120,88,4.5],["c",120,88,1,"F"],["c",120,114,4.5],["c",120,114,1,"F"],["c",150,36,4.5],["c",150,36,1,"F"],["c",150,62,4.5],["c",150,62,1,"F"],["c",150,88,4.5],["c",150,88,1,"F"],["c",150,114,4.5],["c",150,114,1,"F"],["c",180,36,4.5],["c",180,36,1,"F"],["c",180,62,4.5],["c",180,62,1,"F"],["c",180,88,4.5],["c",180,88,1,"F"],["c",180,114,4.5],["c",180,114,1,"F"],["c",210,36,4.5],["c",210,36,1,"F"],["c",210,62,4.5],["c",210,62,1,"F"],["c",210,88,4.5],["c",210,88,1,"F"],["c",210,114,4.5],["c",210,114,1,"F"],["c",240,36,4.5],["c",240,36,1,"F"],["c",240,62,4.5],["c",240,62,1,"F"],["c",240,88,4.5],["c",240,88,1,"F"],["c",240,114,4.5],["c",240,114,1,"F"],["l",20,160,270,160],["p","M70 160 A75 75 0 0 1 220 160","d"],["l",70,160,70,128,"a"],["l",220,128,220,160,"a"],["t",70,180,"Source","m"],["t",220,180,"Detector","m"],["t",262,26,"B","m"]]},q:"A uniform magnetic field with a slit system as shown in figure is to be used as a momentum filter for high-energy charged particles. With a field B tesla, it is found that the filter transmits \u03B1-particles each of energy 5.3 MeV. The magnetic field is increased to 2.3B tesla and deuterons are passed into the filter. The energy of each deuteron transmitted by the filter is ______ MeV.",a:"About 14.0 MeV",s:`The slits fix the radius r of the path that reaches the detector. Since r = p/(qB), the filter passes the momentum p = qBr.
\u03B1-particle: charge 2e, field B: p_\u03B1 = 2eBr. Deuteron: charge e, field 2.3B: p_d = e(2.3B)r = 2.3eBr.
So p_d/p_\u03B1 = 2.3/2 = 1.15.
Kinetic energy K = p\xB2/(2m). The \u03B1-particle has mass about 4 u and the deuteron about 2 u, so m_\u03B1/m_d = 2.
K_d/K_\u03B1 = (p_d/p_\u03B1)\xB2 \xD7 (m_\u03B1/m_d) = 1.15\xB2 \xD7 2 = 1.3225 \xD7 2 = 2.645.
K_d = 2.645 \xD7 5.3 MeV \u2248 14.0 MeV.`},{pn:215,y:"IIT-JEE 1996",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",fig:{w:300,h:196,alt:"A flat metallic block with faces ABDC at the front, ABGE on top and BGHD on the right; current I flows along +x, magnetic field B points along +y (up); z points toward the viewer",d:[["l",30,176,30,14,"a"],["l",75,176,75,14,"a"],["l",120,176,120,14,"a"],["l",165,176,165,14,"a"],["l",210,176,210,14,"a"],["p","M22 117 L150 117 L150 135 L22 135 Z","B"],["p","M22 117 L150 117 L198 78 L70 78 Z","B"],["p","M150 117 L198 78 L198 96 L150 135 Z","B"],["l",70,78,70,96,"d"],["l",70,96,198,96,"d"],["l",70,96,22,135,"d"],["t",18,115,"A","e"],["t",148,113,"B","e"],["t",18,145,"C","e"],["t",154,145,"D"],["t",68,74,"E","e"],["t",202,75,"G"],["t",203,100,"H"],["t",67,108,"F","e"],["t",150,10,"B","m"],["l",240,120,280,120,"a"],["t",260,112,"I","m"]]},q:"A metallic block carrying current I is subjected to a uniform magnetic induction B as shown in figure. The moving charges experience a force F given by ______ which results in the lowering of the potential of the face ______ . Assume the speed of the carriers to be v.",a:"F = \u2212e(v \xD7 B), of size evB along +z (toward the viewer); the potential of the face ABDC (the front face ABCD) is lowered.",s:`In a metal the carriers are electrons. The current I goes along +x, so the electrons move along \u2212x with speed v: velocity = \u2212v x\u0302.
Force on an electron: F = \u2212e(v \xD7 B) = \u2212e((\u2212v x\u0302) \xD7 (B \u0177)) = evB (x\u0302 \xD7 \u0177) = evB \u1E91.
So the force has magnitude evB and points along +z, toward the viewer in the figure.
The electrons are pushed to the face on the +z side, which is the front face ABDC. It gathers negative charge, so its potential is lowered; the opposite face (EFHG) is left positive.
Answer: F = \u2212e(v \xD7 B); the face ABDC (ABCD).
Note: positive carriers moving along +x would also gather on this face and raise its potential; in a metal the carriers are electrons, so the face is lowered.`},{pn:216,y:"IIT-JEE 1984",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",fig:{w:300,h:200,alt:"Four tracks from one point on a line with a magnetic field into the page: A and B curve to the left (B has the larger radius), C goes straight up, D is a large arc curving to the right",d:[["l",17,27,23,33],["l",17,33,23,27],["l",17,63,23,69],["l",17,69,23,63],["l",17,99,23,105],["l",17,105,23,99],["l",17,135,23,141],["l",17,141,23,135],["l",57,27,63,33],["l",57,33,63,27],["l",57,63,63,69],["l",57,69,63,63],["l",57,99,63,105],["l",57,105,63,99],["l",57,135,63,141],["l",57,141,63,135],["l",97,27,103,33],["l",97,33,103,27],["l",97,63,103,69],["l",97,69,103,63],["l",97,99,103,105],["l",97,105,103,99],["l",97,135,103,141],["l",97,141,103,135],["l",137,27,143,33],["l",137,33,143,27],["l",137,63,143,69],["l",137,69,143,63],["l",137,99,143,105],["l",137,105,143,99],["l",137,135,143,141],["l",137,141,143,135],["l",177,27,183,33],["l",177,33,183,27],["l",177,63,183,69],["l",177,69,183,63],["l",177,99,183,105],["l",177,105,183,99],["l",177,135,183,141],["l",177,141,183,135],["l",217,27,223,33],["l",217,33,223,27],["l",217,63,223,69],["l",217,69,223,63],["l",217,99,223,105],["l",217,105,223,99],["l",217,135,223,141],["l",217,141,223,135],["l",257,27,263,33],["l",257,33,263,27],["l",257,63,263,69],["l",257,69,263,63],["l",257,99,263,105],["l",257,105,263,99],["l",257,135,263,141],["l",257,141,263,135],["l",20,170,280,170],["p","M110 170 A40 40 0 0 0 44.3 139.4","B"],["p","M110 170 A80 80 0 0 0 66 103","B"],["l",110,170,110,26,"B"],["p","M110 170 A70 70 0 0 1 250 170","B"],["l",44.3,139.4,40,144,"a"],["t",30,148,"A","m"],["t",58,98,"B","m"],["t",122,32,"C"],["t",254,160,"D"]]},q:"A neutron, a proton, an electron and an alpha particle enter a region of constant magnetic field with equal velocities. The magnetic field is along the inward normal to the plane of the paper. The tracks of the particles are labelled in fig. The electron follows track ______ and the alpha particle follows track ______ .",a:"The electron follows track D and the alpha particle follows track B.",s:`The neutron has no charge, so it goes straight: track C.
Entering upward with B into the page, a positive charge feels a force qv \xD7 B to the left, so protons and alpha particles curve left (tracks A and B). A negative charge such as the electron curves to the right: track D.
Among the left-curving tracks the radius is r = mv/(qB). With equal v and B, r \u221D m/q.
Proton: m/q = m_p/e. Alpha: 4m_p/2e = 2m_p/e. So the alpha particle has twice the proton radius and follows the wider curve, B; the proton follows the tighter curve A.
So the electron follows D and the alpha particle follows B.
Note: the tracks are not drawn to scale. A real electron would have a much smaller radius than the proton, but only D curves to the right.`},{pn:217,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force on a current-carrying wire",q:"A rigid wire consists of a semicircular portion of radius R and two straight sections. The wire is partially immersed in a perpendicular magnetic field B = B\u2080\u0135 as shown in figure. The magnetic force on the wire if it has a current i is",o:["\u22122iBR \u0135","iBR \u0135","2iBR \u0135","\u2212iBR \u0135"],c:0,s:`Note: the figure shows the field out of the page (dots, along z), perpendicular to the wire; the options are forces along \xB1y, so the field is taken as the out-of-page one.
The two straight sections carry equal currents in opposite directions in the same field, so their forces are equal and opposite and cancel.
For the curved part, the force in a uniform field equals the force on a straight wire joining its two ends: F = i (chord) \xD7 B.
The chord of the semicircle has length 2R and points along +x (current goes from the left end to the right end).
F = i (2R x\u0302) \xD7 (B k\u0302) = 2iBR (x\u0302 \xD7 k\u0302) = 2iBR (\u2212\u0135).
So F = \u22122iBR \u0135, which is option (a).`,fig:{w:300,h:210,alt:"A rigid wire made of two vertical straight sections joined by a semicircle of radius R on top; current goes up the left section and down the right one; the magnetic field points out of the page (dots) over the whole drawing; axes x right, y up, z out of the page",d:[["c",14,15,4],["c",14,15,1.2,"F"],["c",14,45,4],["c",14,45,1.2,"F"],["c",14,75,4],["c",14,75,1.2,"F"],["c",14,105,4],["c",14,105,1.2,"F"],["c",14,135,4],["c",14,135,1.2,"F"],["c",14,165,4],["c",14,165,1.2,"F"],["c",42,15,4],["c",42,15,1.2,"F"],["c",42,45,4],["c",42,45,1.2,"F"],["c",42,75,4],["c",42,75,1.2,"F"],["c",42,105,4],["c",42,105,1.2,"F"],["c",42,135,4],["c",42,135,1.2,"F"],["c",42,165,4],["c",42,165,1.2,"F"],["c",98,75,4],["c",98,75,1.2,"F"],["c",98,105,4],["c",98,105,1.2,"F"],["c",98,135,4],["c",98,135,1.2,"F"],["c",98,165,4],["c",98,165,1.2,"F"],["c",154,15,4],["c",154,15,1.2,"F"],["c",154,45,4],["c",154,45,1.2,"F"],["c",154,75,4],["c",154,75,1.2,"F"],["c",154,105,4],["c",154,105,1.2,"F"],["c",154,135,4],["c",154,135,1.2,"F"],["c",154,165,4],["c",154,165,1.2,"F"],["c",182,15,4],["c",182,15,1.2,"F"],["c",182,45,4],["c",182,45,1.2,"F"],["c",182,75,4],["c",182,75,1.2,"F"],["c",182,105,4],["c",182,105,1.2,"F"],["c",182,135,4],["c",182,135,1.2,"F"],["c",182,165,4],["c",182,165,1.2,"F"],["l",70,175,70,75],["l",70,127,70,113,"a"],["p","M70 75 A28 28 0 0 1 126 75"],["l",126,75,126,175],["l",126,113,126,127,"a"],["l",70,175,70,198,"d"],["l",126,175,126,198,"d"],["l",240,110,240,60,"a"],["t",247,64,"y"],["l",240,110,285,110,"a"],["t",288,114,"x"],["c",240,110,5],["c",240,110,1.5,"F"],["t",232,126,"z","e"]]}},{pn:218,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force between parallel currents",q:"Two long straight wires P and Q carrying equal current 10 A each were kept parallel to each other at 5 cm distance. Magnitude of magnetic force experienced by 10 cm length of wire P is F\u2081. If distance between wires is halved and currents on them are doubled, force F\u2082 on 10 cm length of wire P will be",o:["10 F\u2081","8 F\u2081","F\u2081/8","F\u2081/10"],c:1,s:`Force on a length l of one wire due to the other: F = \u03BC\u2080I\u2081I\u2082l/(2\u03C0d).
So F is proportional to I\u2081I\u2082/d for the same length.
Each current is doubled: I\u2081I\u2082 becomes 4 times.
The distance is halved: 1/d becomes 2 times.
F\u2082 = 4 \xD7 2 \xD7 F\u2081 = 8F\u2081, which is option (b).`},{pn:219,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force on a current-carrying wire",q:"A massless square loop, of wire of resistance 10 \u03A9, supporting a mass of 1 g, hangs vertically with one of its sides in a uniform magnetic field of 10\xB3 G, directed outwards in the shaded region. A dc voltage V is applied to the loop. For what value of V, the magnetic force will exactly balance the weight of the supporting mass of 1 g? (If sides of the loop = 10 cm, g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2)",o:["(1/10) V","1 V","100 V","10 V"],c:3,s:`Only the top side (length 10 cm) is in the field; the force on the other sides is zero or cancels.
Convert units: B = 10\xB3 G = 10\xB3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T = 0.1 T; weight = mg = 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 10 = 0.01 N.
Balance: BIl = mg, so I = 0.01/(0.1 \xD7 0.1) = 1 A.
The loop is a wire of resistance 10 \u03A9, so V = IR = 1 \xD7 10 = 10 V.
The answer is option (d).`,fig:{w:230,h:215,alt:"A square loop of side 10 cm hangs with its top side inside a shaded region where the magnetic field points out of the page; a dc voltage V is connected to the loop and a mass m hangs from its lower side",d:[["r",20,10,180,70,"f"],["c",40,28,4],["c",40,28,1.2,"F"],["c",70,28,4],["c",70,28,1.2,"F"],["c",100,28,4],["c",100,28,1.2,"F"],["c",130,28,4],["c",130,28,1.2,"F"],["c",160,28,4],["c",160,28,1.2,"F"],["c",190,28,4],["c",190,28,1.2,"F"],["t",110,50,"B (out of page)","ms"],["l",70,62,70,142],["l",150,62,150,142],["l",70,142,150,142],["l",70,62,150,62],["l",150,100,165,100],["l",165,92,165,108],["l",170,96,170,104,"B"],["l",170,100,185,100],["l",185,100,185,142],["l",150,142,185,142],["t",178,88,"V","m"],["l",110,142,110,170],["p","M100 190 L110 170 L120 190 Z","f"],["t",110,206,"m","m"],["t",62,106,"10 cm","e"]]}},{pn:220,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force between parallel currents",q:"A current carrying rectangular loop PQRS is made of uniform wire. The length PR = QS = 5 cm and PQ = RS = 100 cm. If ammeter current reading changes from I to 2I, the ratio of magnetic forces per unit length on the wire PQ due to wire RS in the two cases respectively (f\xB9_PQ : f\xB2\u1D35_PQ) is",o:["1 : 5","1 : 3","1 : 2","1 : 4"],c:3,s:`The current I enters at the middle of side PR and splits into two equal paths (via PQ and via RS), because both paths have the same length and resistance.
So each of PQ and RS carries I/2, in the same direction.
Force per unit length between two parallel wires: f = \u03BC\u2080i\u2081i\u2082/(2\u03C0d) with d = 5 cm.
f is proportional to (I/2)(I/2) = I\xB2/4, so f is proportional to I\xB2.
When the current changes from I to 2I, f becomes 4 times.
f\xB9 : f\xB2\u1D35 = 1 : 4, option (d).`,fig:{w:310,h:140,alt:"Rectangular loop PQRS made of uniform wire, P and Q on top, R and S below; current I enters at the middle of side PR and leaves at the middle of side QS towards an ammeter",d:[["l",70,40,200,40],["l",70,110,200,110],["l",70,40,70,110],["l",200,40,200,110],["l",15,75,70,75,"a"],["t",40,66,"I","m"],["l",200,75,245,75,"a"],["c",262,75,13],["t",262,80,"A","m"],["l",275,75,295,75],["c",297,75,2,"F"],["t",62,34,"P","e"],["t",208,34,"Q"],["t",62,126,"R","e"],["t",208,126,"S"]]}},{pn:221,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force on a current-carrying wire",q:"As shown in the figure, a metallic rod of linear density 0.45 kg m\u207B\xB9 is lying horizontally on a smooth inclined plane which makes an angle of 45\xB0 with the horizontal. The minimum current flowing in the rod required to keep it stationary, when 0.15 T magnetic field is acting on it in the vertical upward direction, will be [Use g = 10 m/s\xB2]",o:["30 A","15 A","10 A","3 A"],c:0,s:`The field is vertical and the rod is horizontal, so the magnetic force BIl is horizontal, perpendicular to the rod, pointing into the slope (see the second figure).
Forces on a length l of the rod: weight mg down, normal reaction N, and F = BIl horizontal.
Along the incline the components must balance: BIl cos 45\xB0 = mg sin 45\xB0.
So BIl = mg tan 45\xB0 = mg. With m = 0.45 l (linear density 0.45 kg/m): BI = 0.45 \xD7 10 = 4.5.
I = 4.5/0.15 = 30 A, option (a).`,fig:{w:260,h:195,alt:"A rod lies horizontally on a smooth plane inclined at 45 degrees; the magnetic field B is vertically upward and the current I flows along the rod",d:[["p","M30 170 L170 170 L170 50 Z"],["p","M30 170 L90 150 L230 150 L170 170"],["l",170,50,230,30],["l",230,30,230,150],["l",90,150,230,30,"d"],["p","M30 170 L170 50 L230 30 L90 150 Z","f"],["l",100,110,160,92,"B"],["l",100,110,160,92],["l",78,40,78,10,"a"],["t",70,16,"B","e"],["l",135,128,135,150,"a"],["t",143,148,"I"],["l",165,40,165,20,"a"],["t",173,24,"I"],["t",78,164,"45\xB0","ms"],["p","M52 170 A22 22 0 0 0 46 160"]]},sfig:{w:260,h:175,alt:"Side view of the rod on the 45 degree incline: weight mg down, magnetic force BIL horizontal toward the slope, normal reaction N perpendicular to the plane",d:[["l",20,150,210,40],["l",20,150,210,150],["p","M60 150 A40 40 0 0 0 51 128"],["t",66,144,"45\xB0","s"],["r",121,78,22,10,"F"],["l",132,83,132,150,"d"],["l",132,83,132,128,"a"],["t",138,130,"mg"],["l",132,83,176,83,"a"],["t",180,87,"F = BIL"],["l",132,83,152,56,"a"],["t",158,52,"N"],["l",30,20,30,50,"a"],["t",38,40,"B"]]}},{pn:222,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force on a current-carrying wire",q:"A triangular shaped wire carrying 10 A current is placed in a uniform magnetic field of 0.5 T, as shown in figure. The magnetic force on segment CD is (Given BC = CD = BD = 5 cm.)",o:["0.126 N","0.312 N","0.216 N","0.245 N"],c:2,s:`Force on a straight segment: F = I l B sin \u03B8, where \u03B8 is the angle between the current and the field.
The triangle is equilateral and BD lies along the field, so CD makes 60\xB0 with the field direction.
F = 10 \xD7 0.05 \xD7 0.5 \xD7 sin 60\xB0.
F = 0.25 \xD7 0.866 = 0.2165 N \u2248 0.216 N.
This is option (c).`,fig:{w:270,h:180,alt:"Triangular bend BCD with BC = CD = BD = 5 cm in the wire A B C D E carrying 10 A to the right; the uniform magnetic field of 0.5 T points to the right in the plane",d:[["l",15,28,65,28,"a"],["l",15,48,65,48,"a"],["l",15,68,65,68,"a"],["l",15,88,65,88,"a"],["t",15,112,"Magnetic field","s"],["l",75,150,115,150,"a"],["l",115,150,150,150],["l",115,150,145,98],["l",145,98,175,150],["l",175,150,235,150,"a"],["l",115,150,175,150,"d"],["t",70,166,"A","e"],["t",112,166,"B","e"],["t",178,166,"D"],["t",240,166,"E"],["t",145,90,"C","m"],["t",120,118,"5 cm","e"],["t",172,118,"5 cm"],["t",145,166,"5 cm","ms"],["l",127.5,128.3,132.5,119.7,"a"],["l",157.5,119.7,162.5,128.3,"a"]]}},{pn:223,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force between parallel currents",q:"A wire X of length 50 cm carrying a current of 2 A is placed parallel to a long wire Y of length 5 m. The wire Y carries a current of 3 A. The distance between two wires is 5 cm and current flows in the same direction. The force acting on the wire Y is",o:["1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N directed towards wire X.","1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 N directed away from wire X.","1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 N directed towards wire X.","2.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N directed towards wire X."],c:0,s:`Currents in the same direction attract, so the force on Y points towards X.
Only the 50 cm of Y that lies next to X takes part, so use l = 0.5 m.
F = \u03BC\u2080I\u2081I\u2082l/(2\u03C0d) = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 2 \xD7 3 \xD7 0.5/0.05.
F = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 3/0.05 = 1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N.
Directed towards X, option (a).
Note: using the whole 5 m of Y would give option (c), but the field of the short wire X acts mainly on the 0.5 m opposite to it.`,fig:{w:230,h:175,alt:"Short wire X carrying 2 A and a long wire Y carrying 3 A, parallel, vertical, same direction, 5 cm apart",d:[["l",70,50,70,130],["l",70,97,70,83,"a"],["l",160,20,160,160],["l",160,97,160,83,"a"],["t",70,40,"X","m"],["t",160,12,"Y","m"],["t",60,94,"2 A","e"],["t",170,94,"3 A"],["l",70,148,160,148,"b"],["t",115,164,"5 cm","m"]]}},{pn:224,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:"Two long parallel conductors S\u2081 and S\u2082 are separated by a distance 10 cm and carrying currents of 4 A and 2 A respectively. The conductors are placed along x-axis in X-Y plane. There is a point P located between the conductors (as shown in figure). A charge particle of 3\u03C0 coulomb is passing through the point P with velocity v = (2\xEE + 3\u0135) m/s, where \xEE and \u0135 represents unit vector along x and y axis respectively. The force acting on the charge particle is 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 (\u2212x\xEE + 2\u0135) N. The value of x is",o:["2","1","3","\u22123"],c:2,s:`P is 4 cm below S\u2081 and 6 cm above S\u2082 (10 cm apart). Both currents run along +x.
S\u2081 (4 A) is above P, so its field at P is into the page: B\u2081 = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0r) = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 4/0.04 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T.
S\u2082 (2 A) is below P, so its field is out of the page: B\u2082 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 2/0.06 = 0.667 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T.
Net field = (2 \u2212 0.667) \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 = 1.33 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T into the page, i.e. B = \u22121.33 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 k\u0302.
F = q v \xD7 B = 3\u03C0 \xD7 (2\xEE + 3\u0135) \xD7 (\u22121.33 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 k\u0302). Use \xEE \xD7 k\u0302 = \u2212\u0135 and \u0135 \xD7 k\u0302 = \xEE.
v \xD7 B = \u22121.33 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 (\u22122\u0135 + 3\xEE) = 1.33 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 (\u22123\xEE + 2\u0135).
F = 3\u03C0 \xD7 1.33 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 (\u22123\xEE + 2\u0135) = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 (\u22123\xEE + 2\u0135), so x = 3, option (c).`,fig:{w:300,h:185,alt:"Two long parallel conductors S1 and S2 along the x axis 10 cm apart carrying 4 A and 2 A in the same direction; the point P lies between them, 4 cm below S1",d:[["l",40,150,40,10,"a"],["t",34,14,"Y","e"],["l",40,150,255,150,"a"],["t",260,154,"X"],["t",34,166,"O","e"],["l",40,150,22,166,"a"],["t",14,174,"Z","e"],["l",60,30,235,30],["l",200,30,235,30,"a"],["t",242,34,"4 A"],["l",60,110,235,110],["l",200,110,235,110,"a"],["t",242,114,"2 A"],["t",54,34,"S\u2081","e"],["t",54,114,"S\u2082","e"],["l",75,30,75,110,"bd"],["t",70,74,"10 cm","e"],["c",150,62,2.5,"F"],["t",142,66,"P","e"],["l",150,30,150,62,"bd"],["t",158,50,"4 cm"]]}},{pn:225,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force between parallel currents",q:"Two parallel, long wires are kept 0.20 m apart in vacuum, each carrying current of x A in the same direction. If the force of attraction per meter of each wire is 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 N, then the value of x approximately",o:["1","2.4","1.4","2"],c:2,s:`Force per metre between two parallel wires: F/l = \u03BC\u2080I\u2081I\u2082/(2\u03C0d).
Here both currents are x, so F/l = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 x\xB2/0.20 = 10\u207B\u2076 x\xB2.
Set 10\u207B\u2076 x\xB2 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076, so x\xB2 = 2.
x = \u221A2 \u2248 1.4 A, option (c).`},{pn:226,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbs",st:"Superposition and symmetry",q:"Two long current carrying conductors are placed parallel to each other at a distance of 8 cm between them. The magnitude of magnetic field produced at mid-point between the two conductors due to current flowing in them is 300 \u03BCT. The equal current flowing in the two conductors is",o:["30 A in the same direction.","30 A in the opposite direction.","60 A in the opposite direction.","300 A in the opposite direction."],c:1,s:`At the mid-point, each wire is 4 cm away and gives B\u2081 = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0 \xD7 0.04) = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 I/0.04 = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 I.
If the currents were in the same direction, the two fields at the mid-point would be opposite and the net field would be zero, so options (a) is impossible.
For opposite currents the two fields add: B = 2 \xD7 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 I = 10\u207B\u2075 I.
10\u207B\u2075 I = 300 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076, so I = 30 A.
30 A in opposite directions, option (b).`},{pn:227,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force between parallel currents",q:"A rigid square loop of side a and carrying current I\u2082 is lying on a horizontal surface near a long wire carrying current I\u2081 in the same plane as shown in figure. The net force on the loop due to the wire will be",o:["Repulsive and equal to \u03BC\u2080I\u2081I\u2082/(2\u03C0)","Attractive and equal to \u03BC\u2080I\u2081I\u2082/(3\u03C0)","Zero","Repulsive and equal to \u03BC\u2080I\u2081I\u2082/(4\u03C0)"],c:3,s:`The sides of the loop parallel to the wire feel forces; the forces on the two sides perpendicular to the wire are equal and opposite and cancel.
Nearer side (distance a): its current is opposite to I\u2081, so it is repelled. F\u2081 = \u03BC\u2080I\u2081I\u2082a/(2\u03C0a) = \u03BC\u2080I\u2081I\u2082/(2\u03C0).
Farther side (distance 2a): its current is along I\u2081, so it is attracted. F\u2082 = \u03BC\u2080I\u2081I\u2082a/(2\u03C0 \xD7 2a) = \u03BC\u2080I\u2081I\u2082/(4\u03C0).
The repulsion is larger, so the net force is repulsive: F\u2081 \u2212 F\u2082 = \u03BC\u2080I\u2081I\u2082/(2\u03C0) \u2212 \u03BC\u2080I\u2081I\u2082/(4\u03C0) = \u03BC\u2080I\u2081I\u2082/(4\u03C0).
This is option (d).`,fig:{w:215,h:150,alt:"A long vertical wire carrying I1 upward and, to its right in the same plane, a square loop of side a carrying I2 anticlockwise; the gap between the wire and the loop is a",d:[["l",30,140,30,20],["l",30,77,30,63,"a"],["t",22,28,"I\u2081","e"],["r",90,50,60,60],["l",127,50,113,50,"a"],["l",90,73,90,87,"a"],["l",113,110,127,110,"a"],["l",150,87,150,73,"a"],["t",120,42,"I\u2082","m"],["l",170,50,170,110,"bd"],["t",178,84,"a"],["l",30,128,90,128,"bd"],["t",60,122,"a","m"]]}},{pn:228,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force between parallel currents",q:"Two wires A and B are carrying currents I\u2081 and I\u2082 as shown in the figure. The separation between them is d. A third wire C carrying a current I is to be kept parallel to them at a distance x from A such that the net force acting on it is zero. The possible values of x are",o:["x = (I\u2081/(I\u2081 \u2212 I\u2082)) d and x = (I\u2082/(I\u2081 + I\u2082)) d","x = (I\u2082/(I\u2081 + I\u2082)) d and x = (I\u2082/(I\u2081 \u2212 I\u2082)) d","x = \xB1 I\u2081d/(I\u2081 \u2212 I\u2082)","x = (I\u2081/(I\u2081 + I\u2082)) d and x = (I\u2082/(I\u2081 \u2212 I\u2082)) d"],c:2,s:`The force on C is zero where the total magnetic field of A and B is zero.
A and B carry opposite currents, so between them the two fields point the same way and add; the field can vanish only outside the pair, nearer the wire with the smaller current.
Suppose I\u2081 > I\u2082. The point is beyond B, at distance x from A and x \u2212 d from B: I\u2081/x = I\u2082/(x \u2212 d), so I\u2081(x \u2212 d) = I\u2082x and x = I\u2081d/(I\u2081 \u2212 I\u2082).
Suppose I\u2082 > I\u2081. The point is on the far side of A, at distance |x| from A and |x| + d from B: I\u2081/|x| = I\u2082/(|x| + d), so |x| = I\u2081d/(I\u2082 \u2212 I\u2081) = I\u2081d/|I\u2081 \u2212 I\u2082|.
Measured from A towards B this point is at x = \u2212|x| = \u2212I\u2081d/(I\u2082 \u2212 I\u2081) = +I\u2081d/(I\u2081 \u2212 I\u2082), the same expression as in the first case. In both cases the distance from A is I\u2081d/|I\u2081 \u2212 I\u2082|, which is written x = \xB1 I\u2081d/(I\u2081 \u2212 I\u2082).
Option (c).`,fig:{w:230,h:170,alt:"Parallel wires A (current I1 upward) and B (current I2 downward) a distance d apart, and a third wire C, dashed, at distance x from A",d:[["l",40,25,40,150],["l",40,97,40,83,"a"],["t",34,90,"I\u2081","e"],["t",40,16,"A","m"],["l",190,25,190,150],["l",190,83,190,97,"a"],["t",198,90,"I\u2082"],["t",190,16,"B","m"],["l",115,25,115,150,"d"],["t",115,16,"C","m"],["l",40,45,115,45,"bd"],["t",78,40,"x","m"],["l",40,138,190,138,"bd"],["t",115,155,"d","m"]]}},{pn:229,y:"JEE Main 2015",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force between parallel currents",q:"Two long current carrying thin wires, both with current I, are held by insulating threads of length L and are in equilibrium as shown in the figure, with threads making an angle \u03B8 with the vertical. If wires have mass \u03BB per unit length then the value of I is (g = gravitational acceleration)",o:["2\u221A(\u03C0gL tan\u03B8/\u03BC\u2080)","\u221A(\u03C0\u03BBgL tan\u03B8/\u03BC\u2080)","sin\u03B8 \u221A(\u03C0\u03BBgL/(\u03BC\u2080 cos\u03B8))","2 sin\u03B8 \u221A(\u03C0\u03BBgL/(\u03BC\u2080 cos\u03B8))"],c:3,s:`The threads hang apart, so the wires repel each other. Take 1 m of each wire: weight \u03BBg, magnetic force F, thread tension T.
The distance between the wires is d = 2L sin\u03B8.
Repulsive force per metre: F = \u03BC\u2080I\xB2/(2\u03C0d) = \u03BC\u2080I\xB2/(4\u03C0L sin\u03B8).
Equilibrium of each wire: T cos\u03B8 = \u03BBg and T sin\u03B8 = F, so F = \u03BBg tan\u03B8.
\u03BC\u2080I\xB2/(4\u03C0L sin\u03B8) = \u03BBg tan\u03B8, so I\xB2 = 4\u03C0\u03BBgL sin\u03B8 tan\u03B8/\u03BC\u2080.
I = 2\u221A(\u03C0\u03BBgL sin\u03B8 tan\u03B8/\u03BC\u2080) = 2 sin\u03B8 \u221A(\u03C0\u03BBgL/(\u03BC\u2080 cos\u03B8)), option (d).`,fig:{w:230,h:160,alt:"Two parallel wires hung by threads of length L from one point; each thread makes angle theta with the vertical dashed line; each wire carries current I",d:[["l",115,14,60.1,131.8],["l",115,14,169.9,131.8],["l",115,14,115,131.8,"d"],["c",60.1,131.8,6],["l",56.1,135.8,64.1,127.80000000000001],["c",169.9,131.8,6],["l",165.9,135.8,173.9,127.80000000000001],["t",48.1,135.8,"I","e"],["t",181.9,135.8,"I"],["p","M115 59 A45 45 0 0 0 96 54.8"],["t",107,76,"\u03B8","e"],["t",155,74,"L"]]}},{pn:230,y:"JEE Main 2015",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force on a current-carrying wire",q:"Two coaxial solenoids of different radii carry current I in the same direction. Let F\u2081 be the magnetic force on the inner solenoid due to the outer one and F\u2082 be the magnetic force on the outer solenoid due to the inner one. Then",o:["F\u2081 is radially inwards and F\u2082 = 0","F\u2081 is radially outwards and F\u2082 = 0","F\u2081 = F\u2082 = 0","F\u2081 is radially inwards and F\u2082 = 0 is radially outwards"],c:2,s:`Outside a long solenoid the field is almost zero, so the inner solenoid produces no field at the outer solenoid: F\u2082 = 0.
The outer solenoid produces a uniform axial field at the inner one. Each small element of the inner winding feels a force I dl \xD7 B that is radial.
These radial forces on opposite elements point in opposite directions and cancel, so the total (net) force on the inner solenoid is zero as well.
So F\u2081 = F\u2082 = 0, option (c).
Note: the individual elements are pushed radially (the winding is stretched), but the force on the solenoid as a whole is zero.`},{pn:231,y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force between parallel currents",q:"Two long straight parallel wires, carrying (adjustable) currents I\u2081 and I\u2082, are kept at a distance d apart. If the force F between the two wires is taken as positive when the wires repel each other and negative when the wires attract each other, the graph showing the dependence of F, on the product I\u2081I\u2082, would be",o:["(a)","(b)","(c)","(d)"],c:3,s:`The force between the wires is F = \u03BC\u2080I\u2081I\u2082/(2\u03C0d) in size, so it is proportional to the product I\u2081I\u2082.
When I\u2081I\u2082 is positive (same direction) the wires attract, which is taken as negative F; when I\u2081I\u2082 is negative (opposite directions) they repel, which is positive F.
So F = \u2212kI\u2081I\u2082: a straight line through the origin with a negative slope.
Only graph (d) is a straight line sloping down to the right through O.
The answer is (d).`,fig:{w:390,h:280,alt:"Four graphs of the force F against the product I1 I2: (a) an inverted V through O, (b) a V through O, (c) a straight line of positive slope through O, (d) a straight line of negative slope through O",d:[["l",90,125,90,15,"a"],["l",30,70,160,70,"a"],["t",84,14,"F","e"],["t",164,74,"I\u2081I\u2082"],["t",84,83,"O","e"],["t",20,40,"(a)","m"],["l",45,115,90,70],["l",90,70,135,115],["l",300,125,300,15,"a"],["l",240,70,370,70,"a"],["t",294,14,"F","e"],["t",374,74,"I\u2081I\u2082"],["t",294,83,"O","e"],["t",230,40,"(b)","m"],["l",255,25,300,70],["l",300,70,345,25],["l",90,255,90,145,"a"],["l",30,200,160,200,"a"],["t",84,144,"F","e"],["t",164,204,"I\u2081I\u2082"],["t",84,213,"O","e"],["t",20,170,"(c)","m"],["l",45,245,135,155],["l",300,255,300,145,"a"],["l",240,200,370,200,"a"],["t",294,144,"F","e"],["t",374,204,"I\u2081I\u2082"],["t",294,213,"O","e"],["t",230,170,"(d)","m"],["l",255,155,345,245]]}},{pn:232,y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force on a current-carrying wire",q:"A wire carrying current I is tied between points P and Q and is in the shape of a circular arch of radius R due to a uniform magnetic field B (perpendicular to the plane of the paper, shown by \xD7\xD7\xD7) in the vicinity of the wire. If the wire subtends an angle 2\u03B8\u2080 at the centre of the circle (of which it forms an arch) then the tension in the wire is",o:["IBR","IBR/sin\u03B8\u2080","IBR/(2 sin\u03B8\u2080)","IBR\u03B8\u2080/sin\u03B8\u2080"],c:0,s:`In a uniform field the force on the arc equals the force on its chord: F = IB \xD7 (2R sin\u03B8\u2080), directed away from the centre.
The chord PQ has length 2R sin\u03B8\u2080 because the arc subtends 2\u03B8\u2080 at the centre.
At each end the tension T acts along the tangent, which makes angle \u03B8\u2080 with the chord.
The components of the two tensions along the force direction add: 2T sin\u03B8\u2080.
Balance: 2T sin\u03B8\u2080 = 2IBR sin\u03B8\u2080, so T = IBR.
This is option (a).`,fig:{w:260,h:180,alt:"A wire in the shape of a circular arc PQ of radius R subtending angle 2 theta-zero at the centre, carrying current I, in a magnetic field into the page shown by crosses",d:[["p","M58.3 62.6 A125 125 0 0 1 201.7 62.6"],["l",123,40,137,40,"a"],["l",130,165,58.3,62.6,"d"],["l",130,165,201.7,62.6,"d"],["l",130,165,130,40,"d"],["p","M117.38131840027698 147 A22 22 0 0 1 130 143"],["t",124,139,"\u03B8\u2080","e"],["t",48.3,68.6,"P","e"],["t",211.7,68.6,"Q"],["t",138,95,"R"],["t",136,24,"I"],["t",215,160,"B into page","s"],["l",97,67,103,73],["l",97,73,103,67],["l",152,59,158,65],["l",152,65,158,59],["l",172,69,178,75],["l",172,75,178,69],["l",102,89,108,95],["l",102,95,108,89],["l",157,92,163,98],["l",157,98,163,92],["l",102,49,108,55],["l",102,55,108,49],["l",162,47,168,53],["l",162,53,168,47],["l",182,89,188,95],["l",182,95,188,89]]}},{pn:233,y:"JEE Main 2014",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force on a current-carrying wire",q:"A conductor lies along the z-axis at \u22121.5 \u2264 z < 1.5 m and carries a fixed current of 10.0 A in \u2212a_z direction (see figure). For a field B = 3.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 e^(\u22120.2x) a_y T, find the power required to move the conductor at constant speed to x = 2.0 m, y = 0 m in 5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 s. Assume parallel motion along the x-axis.",o:["29.7 W","1.57 W","2.97 W","14.85 W"],c:2,s:`Force on the conductor of length 3 m: F = I l B = 10 \xD7 3 \xD7 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 e^(\u22120.2x) = 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 e^(\u22120.2x) N, along the x-axis.
The force changes with x, so find the work as an integral: W = \u222B F dx from x = 0 to x = 2 m (the conductor starts at x = 0).
W = 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 [(1 \u2212 e^(\u22120.4))/0.2].
1 \u2212 e^(\u22120.4) = 1 \u2212 0.670 = 0.330, so W = 9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 1.648 = 1.48 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 J.
Power = W/t = 1.48 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 / (5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3) \u2248 2.97 W, option (c).
Note: with constant speed the agent does work equal in size to the work of the magnetic force, so the power asked is this value.`,fig:{w:320,h:185,alt:"A conductor along the z axis from z = -1.5 m to 1.5 m carrying 10 A in the -z direction, in a field B along y that falls off with x; it is moved along x to x = 2.0 m",d:[["l",190,170,190,10,"a"],["t",196,14,"z"],["l",190,100,285,100,"a"],["t",290,104,"y"],["l",190,100,120,150,"a"],["t",112,156,"x","e"],["l",185,40,185,160,"B"],["l",185,93,185,107,"a"],["t",193,50,"1.5"],["t",193,164,"\u22121.5"],["t",200,86,"I"],["l",150,86,260,86,"a"],["l",150,100,260,100,"a"],["l",150,114,260,114,"a"],["t",270,92,"B"],["l",105,40,105,130,"B"],["l",105,40,185,40,"d"],["l",105,130,185,130,"d"],["t",98,96,"2.0","e"],["l",185,100,108,128,"a"]]}},{pn:234,y:"AIEEE 2012 \xB7 Online",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force on a current-carrying wire",q:"The circuit in figure consists of wires at the top and bottom and identical springs as the left and right sides. The wire at the bottom has a mass of 10 g and is 5 cm long. The wire is hanging as shown in the figure. The springs stretch 0.5 cm under the weight of the wire and the circuit has a total resistance of 12 \u03A9. When the lower wire is subjected to a static magnetic field, the springs stretch an additional 0.3 cm. The magnetic field is",o:["0.6 T and directed out of page","1.2 T and directed into the plane of page","0.6 T and directed into the plane of page","1.2 T and directed out of page"],c:0,s:`Note: the printed key says (2); working gives (a).
Current: I = V/R = 24/12 = 2 A.
Springs: the weight mg = 0.01 \xD7 10 = 0.1 N stretches them 0.5 cm, so an extra stretch of 0.3 cm needs an extra force 0.3/0.5 \xD7 0.1 = 0.06 N downward.
This extra force is the magnetic force: BIl = 0.06, so B = 0.06/(2 \xD7 0.05) = 0.6 T.
Direction: the long plate (+) of the cell is on the left, so current goes left, down the left spring and then left to right through the bottom wire.
The force on the bottom wire must be downward: x\u0302 \xD7 B = \u2212\u0177 needs B along +z, which is out of the page.
So B = 0.6 T out of the page, option (a).`,fig:{w:330,h:200,alt:"A circuit with a 24 V cell and resistance in the top wire, two identical springs as left and right sides, and a 5 cm long wire at the bottom lying in a rectangular magnetic field region",d:[["l",40,40,70,40],["l",70,40,120,40],["p","M120 40 l5 0 l3 -7 l6 14 l6 -14 l6 14 l3 -7 l5 0"],["l",155,40,172,40],["l",172,31,172,49],["l",178,35,178,45,"B"],["l",184,40,230,40],["l",230,40,260,40],["l",40,32,40,48,"B"],["l",260,32,260,48,"B"],["l",70,40,70,70],["l",230,40,230,70],["p","M70 70 a7 4 0 1 1 0 8 a7 4 0 1 1 0 8 a7 4 0 1 1 0 8 a7 4 0 1 1 0 8 a7 4 0 1 1 0 8 a7 4 0 1 1 0 8"],["p","M230 70 a7 4 0 1 1 0 8 a7 4 0 1 1 0 8 a7 4 0 1 1 0 8 a7 4 0 1 1 0 8 a7 4 0 1 1 0 8 a7 4 0 1 1 0 8"],["l",70,118,70,128],["l",230,118,230,128],["r",70,128,160,16,"f"],["l",70,128,230,128],["r",35,125,230,60],["t",160,152,"\u2190 5 cm \u2192","m"],["t",172,30,"24 V","m"],["t",260,95,"Magnetic","s"],["t",260,106,"field region","s"]]}},{pn:235,y:"AIEEE 2012 \xB7 Online",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force between parallel currents",q:"Currents of a 10 ampere and 2 ampere are passed through two parallel thin wires A and B respectively in opposite directions. Wire A is infinitely long and the length of the wire B is 2 m. The force acting on the conductor B, which is situated at 10 cm distance from A will be",o:["8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N","5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N","8\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 N","4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 N"],c:0,s:`Note: the printed key says (2); working gives (a).
Field of the long wire A at wire B: B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0d) = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 10/0.1 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T.
Force on B: F = B I l = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 2 \xD7 2 = 8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N.
Opposite currents, so the force is repulsive; the size is 8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N, option (a).`},{pn:236,y:"IIT-JEE 2011",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:"A long insulated copper wire is closely wound as a spiral of N turns. The spiral has inner radius a and outer radius b. The spiral lies in the X-Y plane and a steady current I flows through the wire. The Z-component of the magnetic field at the centre of the spiral is",o:["(\u03BC\u2080NI/(2(b \u2212 a))) ln(b/a)","(\u03BC\u2080NI/(2(b \u2212 a))) ln((b + a)/(b \u2212 a))","(\u03BC\u2080NI/(2b)) ln(b/a)","(\u03BC\u2080NI/(2b)) ln((b + a)/(b \u2212 a))"],c:0,s:`Cut the spiral into thin rings of radius r and thickness dr. The number of turns in it is N dr/(b \u2212 a).
Each turn of radius r gives \u03BC\u2080I/(2r) at the centre, so dB = (\u03BC\u2080I/(2r)) \xD7 N dr/(b \u2212 a).
All the rings produce a field along the same axis (z), so add them by integration from r = a to r = b.
B = (\u03BC\u2080NI/(2(b \u2212 a))) \u222B dr/r = (\u03BC\u2080NI/(2(b \u2212 a))) ln(b/a).
This is option (a).`,fig:{w:250,h:200,alt:"A flat spiral of N turns in the X-Y plane with inner radius a and outer radius b, carrying current I",d:[["p","M130 100 L130.1 97.9 L129.7 95.7 L128.8 93.6 L127.5 91.7 L125.7 90.1 L123.6 88.9 L121.3 88.1 L118.7 87.8 L116.1 88 L113.6 88.9 L111.2 90.2 L109.1 92.1 L107.5 94.4 L106.3 97.1 L105.7 100 L105.7 103 L106.4 106 L107.7 108.9 L109.7 111.5 L112.1 113.6 L115.1 115.2 L118.3 116.2 L121.7 116.5 L125.2 116 L128.6 114.8 L131.7 113 L134.3 110.4 L136.4 107.3 L137.9 103.8 L138.6 100 L138.4 96.1 L137.5 92.2 L135.7 88.6 L133.2 85.3 L130 82.7 L126.3 80.7 L122.2 79.5 L117.8 79.3 L113.5 79.9 L109.3 81.4 L105.5 83.9 L102.2 87.1 L99.6 90.9 L97.9 95.3 L97.1 100 L97.4 104.8 L98.6 109.5 L100.8 113.9 L103.9 117.8 L107.9 121 L112.4 123.4 L117.4 124.7 L122.6 125 L127.9 124.2 L132.9 122.3 L137.4 119.3 L141.3 115.5 L144.3 110.8 L146.3 105.6 L147.1 100 L146.8 94.3 L145.3 88.7 L142.7 83.5 L138.9 79 L134.3 75.3 L128.9 72.6 L123 71 L116.9 70.7 L110.8 71.7 L105 74 L99.7 77.5 L95.3 82 L91.8 87.4 L89.5 93.5 L88.6 100 L89 106.6 L90.8 113 L93.9 119 L98.2 124.2 L103.6 128.5 L109.8 131.5 L116.5 133.2 L123.5 133.5 L130.5 132.3 L137.1 129.7 L143.1 125.7 L148.2 120.5 L152.1 114.3 L154.7 107.4 L155.7 100 L155.2 92.5 L153.1 85.2 L149.6 78.5 L144.7 72.6 L138.6 67.8 L131.6 64.4 L123.9 62.5 L116 62.2 L108.2 63.6 L100.7 66.6 L94 71.1 L88.3 77 L84 84 L81.2 91.7 L80 100 L80.6 108.4 L82.9 116.5 L86.9 124 L92.5 130.6 L99.3 135.9 L107.1 139.7 L115.6 141.8 L124.4 142.1 L133.2 140.5 L141.4 137.1 L148.9 132.1 L155.1 125.5 L159.9 117.8 L163 109.1 L164.3 100 L163.6 90.7 L161 81.8 L156.5 73.5 L150.4 66.2 L142.9 60.4 L134.2 56.3 L124.8 54 L115.1 53.7 L105.5 55.4 L96.4 59.2 L88.3 64.8 L81.4 72 L76.1 80.5 L72.8 90 L71.4 100 L72.2 110.2 L75.1 120 L80 129.1 L86.7 136.9 L95 143.3 L104.5 147.8 L114.7 150.3 L125.3 150.6 L135.8 148.6 L145.7 144.5 L154.6 138.4 L162.1 130.6 L167.8 121.3 L171.4 110.9 L172.9 100 L172 89 L168.8 78.3 L163.5 68.4 L156.1 59.9 L147.1 53 L136.9 48.1 L125.7 45.4 L114.2 45.2 L102.9 47.3 L92.1 51.8 L82.5 58.4 L74.5 66.9 L68.3 77 L64.4 88.2 L62.9 100 L63.8 111.9 L67.3 123.5 L73.1 134.1 L81 143.3 L90.7 150.7 L101.8 156 L113.8 158.8 L126.2 159.1 L138.5 156.8 L150 152 L160.3 144.8 L169 135.6 L175.6 124.8 L179.8 112.7 L181.4 100 L180.4 87.2 L176.6 74.8 L170.4 63.4 L161.9 53.5 L151.4 45.6 L139.5 39.9 L126.6 36.9 L113.3 36.6 L100.2 39.1 L87.9 44.3 L76.8 52 L67.5 61.9 L60.5 73.5 L56 86.4 L54.3 100 L55.4 113.7 L59.4 127 L66.1 139.1 L75.3 149.7 L86.4 158.1 L99.2 164.1 L112.9 167.3 L127.1 167.6 L141.1 164.9 L154.3 159.4 L166.1 151.2 L175.9 140.6 L183.4 128.2 L188.2 114.5 L190 100"],["l",120,10,120,190],["l",10,100,230,100],["t",124,12,"Y"],["t",228,96,"X"],["t",14,92,"I"],["c",120,100,2,"F"],["l",120,100,130,100,"d"],["t",134,114,"a","s"],["l",120,100,190,100,"d"],["t",160,116,"b","s"]]}},{pn:237,y:"IIT-JEE 2010",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force on a current-carrying wire",q:"A thin flexible wire of length L is connected to two adjacent fixed points and carries a current I in the clockwise direction, as shown in the figure. When the system is put in a uniform magnetic field of strength B going into plane of the paper, the wire takes the shape of a circle. The tension in the wire is",o:["IBL","IBL/\u03C0","IBL/(2\u03C0)","IBL/(4\u03C0)"],c:2,s:`The wire of length L forms a circle of radius r with 2\u03C0r = L, so r = L/(2\u03C0).
Take a small piece of length dl = r d\u03B8. The magnetic force on it is I dl B, directed outward (for a clockwise current and B into the page).
The two tensions T at its ends have an inward resultant T d\u03B8.
Balance: T d\u03B8 = I B r d\u03B8, so T = IBr.
T = IB \xD7 L/(2\u03C0) = IBL/(2\u03C0), option (c).`,fig:{w:310,h:160,alt:"A flexible wire of length L joined to two close fixed points carrying current I clockwise, and the same wire as a circle in a field B into the page (crosses)",d:[["p","M30 105 C10 80 30 30 70 30 C110 30 130 60 110 85 C95 100 85 90 75 105 L60 105"],["c",58,106,3,"F"],["c",72,106,3,"F"],["l",63,31,77,31,"a"],["l",119.9,65.1,110.1,74.9,"a"],["l",167,17,173,23],["l",167,23,173,17],["l",167,39,173,45],["l",167,45,173,39],["l",167,61,173,67],["l",167,67,173,61],["l",167,83,173,89],["l",167,89,173,83],["l",167,105,173,111],["l",167,111,173,105],["l",167,127,173,133],["l",167,133,173,127],["l",191,17,197,23],["l",191,23,197,17],["l",191,39,197,45],["l",191,45,197,39],["l",191,61,197,67],["l",191,67,197,61],["l",191,83,197,89],["l",191,89,197,83],["l",191,105,197,111],["l",191,111,197,105],["l",191,127,197,133],["l",191,133,197,127],["l",215,17,221,23],["l",215,23,221,17],["l",215,39,221,45],["l",215,45,221,39],["l",215,61,221,67],["l",215,67,221,61],["l",215,83,221,89],["l",215,89,221,83],["l",215,105,221,111],["l",215,111,221,105],["l",215,127,221,133],["l",215,133,221,127],["l",239,17,245,23],["l",239,23,245,17],["l",239,39,245,45],["l",239,45,245,39],["l",239,61,245,67],["l",239,67,245,61],["l",239,83,245,89],["l",239,89,245,83],["l",239,105,245,111],["l",239,111,245,105],["l",239,127,245,133],["l",239,133,245,127],["l",263,17,269,23],["l",263,23,269,17],["l",263,39,269,45],["l",263,45,269,39],["l",263,61,269,67],["l",263,67,269,61],["l",263,83,269,89],["l",263,89,269,83],["l",263,105,269,111],["l",263,111,269,105],["l",263,127,269,133],["l",263,133,269,127],["l",287,17,293,23],["l",287,23,293,17],["l",287,39,293,45],["l",287,45,293,39],["l",287,61,293,67],["l",287,67,293,61],["l",287,83,293,89],["l",287,89,293,83],["l",287,105,293,111],["l",287,111,293,105],["l",287,127,293,133],["l",287,133,293,127],["c",230,75,40],["l",223,35,237,35,"a"],["l",270,68,270,82,"a"],["t",150,150,"B into the page","s"]]}},{pn:238,y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force between parallel currents",q:"Two thin long, parallel wires, separated by a distance d carry a current of i A in the same direction. They will",o:["attract each other with a force of \u03BC\u2080i\xB2/(2\u03C0d\xB2)","repel each other with a force of \u03BC\u2080i\xB2/(2\u03C0d\xB2)","attract each other with a force of \u03BC\u2080i\xB2/(2\u03C0d)","repel each other with a force of \u03BC\u2080i\xB2/(2\u03C0d)"],c:2,s:`Currents in the same direction create fields that pull the wires together: they attract.
Force per unit length: F/l = \u03BC\u2080I\u2081I\u2082/(2\u03C0d) with both currents i.
So F/l = \u03BC\u2080i\xB2/(2\u03C0d); the force falls as 1/d, not 1/d\xB2.
Attract with \u03BC\u2080i\xB2/(2\u03C0d), option (c).
Note: this is the force on each metre of the wires.`},{pn:239,y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force between parallel currents",q:"Two long conductors, separated by a distance d carry current I\u2081 and I\u2082 in the same direction. They exert a force F on each other. Now the current in one of them is increased to two times and its direction is reversed. The distance is also increased to 3d. The new value of the force between them is",o:["\u22122F","F/3","\u22122F/3","\u2212F/3"],c:2,s:`Force: F = \u03BC\u2080I\u2081I\u2082l/(2\u03C0d); it is proportional to I\u2081I\u2082/d (positive means attraction).
One current becomes 2 times and reverses, so I\u2081I\u2082 becomes \u22122I\u2081I\u2082.
The distance becomes 3d, so 1/d becomes 1/3.
F\u2032 = (\u22122)(1/3) F = \u22122F/3.
The minus sign means repulsion; the answer is option (c).`},{pn:240,y:"IIT-JEE 2003",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force on a current-carrying wire",q:"A conducting loop, carrying a current I, is placed in a uniform magnetic field pointing into the plane of the paper as shown. The loop will have a tendency to",o:["contract","expand","move towards +ve x-axis","move towards \u2212ve x-axis."],c:1,s:`The force on each small piece of the loop is dF = I dl \xD7 B, perpendicular to the piece and to B, so it lies in the plane of the loop.
The current in the figure is clockwise and B points into the page. At the right-hand point dl points down, and (\u2212\u0177) \xD7 (\u2212\u1E91) = +x\u0302.
That is outward from the centre. By symmetry the force is outward at every point of the loop.
Forces on opposite pieces cancel in the net, so the loop does not move, but it is pulled outward everywhere.
So it has a tendency to expand, option (b).`,fig:{w:260,h:185,alt:"A circular conducting loop in the xy plane carrying current I clockwise, in a uniform magnetic field into the page",d:[["l",20,100,230,100,"a"],["l",125,170,125,15,"a"],["t",236,104,"x"],["t",131,20,"y"],["t",20,90,"O","e"],["c",125,100,40],["l",85,107,85,93,"a"],["t",72,112,"I","e"],["l",47,32,53,38],["l",47,38,53,32],["c",50,35,6],["t",36,30,"B","e"]]}},{pn:241,y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force between parallel currents",q:"If a current is passed through a spring then the spring will",o:["expand","compress","remains same","none of these"],c:1,s:`Neighbouring turns of a spring carry current in the same direction.
Parallel wires carrying current in the same direction attract each other.
So every turn pulls the next turn towards it and the turns come closer together.
The spring therefore compresses, which is option (b).`},{pn:242,y:"IIT-JEE 1983",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force on a current-carrying wire",q:"A conducting circular loop of radius r carries a constant current i. It is placed in a uniform magnetic field B\u2080 such that B\u2080 is perpendicular to the plane of the loop. The magnetic force acting on the loop is",o:["irB\u2080","2\u03C0irB\u2080","zero","\u03C0irB\u2080"],c:2,s:`Take a small element dl of the loop. The force on it is i dl \xD7 B\u2080, and it points along the radius, outward or inward in the plane of the loop.
Elements on opposite ends of a diameter feel equal and opposite forces, so the forces cancel in pairs.
The net force on a closed current loop in a uniform field is i (\u222Edl) \xD7 B\u2080, and \u222Edl = 0 for a closed loop.
So the net force is zero, option (c).
Note: the forces still try to stretch the loop (tension), but the total force on it is zero.`},{pn:243,y:"JEE Advanced 2015",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force on a current-carrying wire",q:"A conductor (shown in the figure) carrying constant current I is kept in the x-y plane in a uniform magnetic field B. If F is the magnitude of the total magnetic force acting on the conductor, then the correct statement(s) is(are)",o:["If B is along \u1E91, F \u221D (L + R)","If B is along x\u0302, F = 0","If B is along \u0177, F \u221D (L + R)","If B is along \u1E91, F = 0"],c:[0,1,2],s:`Note: the key is not printed for this question; working gives (a), (b) and (c).
In a uniform field the force on a wire of any shape is F = I (\u2113 \xD7 B), where \u2113 is the straight vector from the start of the wire to its end.
In the figure the wire starts at the left end and finishes on the x-axis after L + R + R + L, so \u2113 = 2(L + R) in the x direction.
B along \u1E91: \u2113 \xD7 B = 2(L + R)B (x\u0302 \xD7 \u1E91), which is non-zero and proportional to (L + R). So (a) is true and (d) is false.
B along x\u0302: \u2113 is also along x\u0302, so \u2113 \xD7 B = 0 and F = 0. (b) is true.
B along \u0177: \u2113 \xD7 B = 2(L + R)B \u1E91, again proportional to (L + R). (c) is true.
So the correct statements are (a), (b) and (c).`,fig:{w:340,h:150,alt:"A conductor in the x-y plane: straight length L, then a bent part made of two pieces of horizontal extent R each (angles pi/6 and pi/4 marked), then straight length L. Current I flows left to right. Axes x and y shown.",d:[["l",20,100,90,100],["l",50,100,72,100,"a"],["t",52,90,"I","m"],["p","M 90 100 A 30 30 0 0 1 112 66 L 150 100"],["l",126,84,134,90,"a"],["p","M 150 100 L 188 62 A 30 30 0 0 1 210 100"],["l",164,86,172,78,"a"],["l",210,100,280,100],["l",234,100,256,100,"a"],["l",150,100,90,100,"d"],["l",150,100,210,100,"d"],["t",136,56,"R","m"],["t",172,50,"R","m"],["t",108,96,"\u03C0/6","m","s"],["t",176,96,"\u03C0/4","m","s"],["l",20,124,90,124,"b"],["l",90,124,150,124,"b"],["l",150,124,210,124,"b"],["l",210,124,280,124,"b"],["t",55,140,"L","m"],["t",120,140,"R","m"],["t",180,140,"R","m"],["t",245,140,"L","m"],["l",305,110,305,66,"a"],["l",305,110,335,110,"a"],["t",313,70,"y"],["t",328,126,"x"]]}},{pn:244,y:"IIT-JEE 1986",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force between parallel currents",q:"Two thin long parallel wires separated by a distance b are carrying a current i ampere each. The magnitude of the force per unit length exerted by one wire on the other is",o:["(\u03BC\u2080i\xB2)/(b\xB2)","(\u03BC\u2080i\xB2)/(2\u03C0b)","(\u03BC\u2080i)/(2\u03C0b)","(\u03BC\u2080i)/(2\u03C0b\xB2)"],c:1,s:`One wire makes a field B = \u03BC\u2080i/(2\u03C0b) at the position of the other wire.
The force on a length l of the second wire is F = B i l, since the field is perpendicular to it.
Force per unit length = B i = (\u03BC\u2080i/(2\u03C0b)) \xB7 i = \u03BC\u2080i\xB2/(2\u03C0b).
This is option (b).`},{pn:245,y:"IIT-JEE 1985",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force between parallel currents",q:"A rectangular loop carrying a current i is situated near a long straight wire such that the wire is parallel to one of the sides of the loop and is in the plane of the loop. If steady current I is established in the wire as shown in the figure, the loop will",o:["rotate about an axis parallel to the wire","move away from the wire","move towards the wire","remain stationary."],c:2,s:`Look at the loop side nearest the wire. In the figure its current is upward, the same direction as I in the wire.
Parallel currents in the same direction attract, so this near side is pulled towards the wire.
The far side carries current in the opposite direction, so it is repelled, but it is farther away and the field there is weaker.
The forces on the top and bottom sides are equal and opposite, so they cancel.
The near-side attraction is stronger, so the net force pulls the loop towards the wire, option (c).`,fig:{w:300,h:170,alt:"A long straight wire carrying current I upward, with a rectangular loop carrying current i beside it in the same plane. The left side of the loop is parallel to the wire; the loop current goes up on that side, right along the top, down on the right side and left along the bottom.",d:[["l",50,15,50,155],["l",50,95,50,70,"a"],["t",62,86,"I"],["r",110,40,150,90],["l",170,40,200,40,"a"],["l",260,75,260,90,"a"],["l",200,130,170,130,"a"],["l",110,95,110,80,"a"],["t",188,32,"i","m"]]}},{pn:246,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force on a current-carrying wire",q:"A 4.0 cm long straight wire carrying a current of 8 A is placed perpendicular to a uniform magnetic field of strength 0.15 T. The magnetic force on the wire is ______ mN.",n:48,s:`The wire is perpendicular to the field, so sin 90\xB0 = 1 and F = B I l.
l = 4.0 cm = 0.04 m.
F = 0.15 \xD7 8 \xD7 0.04 = 0.048 N.
In millinewtons, F = 48 mN.`},{pn:247,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force on a current-carrying wire",q:"The magnetic field existing in a region is given by B = 0.2(1 + 2x) k\u0302 T. A square loop of edge 50 cm carrying 0.5 A current is placed in x-y plane with its edges parallel to the x-y axes, as shown in figure. The magnitude of the net magnetic force experienced by the loop is ______ mN.",n:50,s:`The field is along z, perpendicular to the loop, and changes only with x.
The top and bottom sides are at the same x range and carry opposite currents, so their forces are equal and opposite and cancel.
The two vertical sides carry opposite currents but sit at different x, where the fields differ, so their forces do not cancel. The loop spans x = 1.5 m to x = 2 m (see the figure).
B(1.5) = 0.2(1 + 3) = 0.8 T and B(2) = 0.2(1 + 4) = 1.0 T.
Net force = I l (B(2) \u2212 B(1.5)) = 0.5 \xD7 0.5 \xD7 0.2 = 0.05 N.
So the net force is 50 mN.`,fig:{w:320,h:190,alt:"Square loop of edge 50 cm with sides parallel to the axes, its right side at x = 2 m, so it spans x = 1.5 m to 2 m. Axes x and y; the field is along z and grows with x.",d:[["l",30,160,300,160,"a"],["l",30,160,30,15,"a"],["t",36,22,"y"],["t",298,176,"x","m"],["t",24,174,"O","e"],["r",190,40,50,50],["l",205,40,225,40,"a"],["l",240,55,240,75,"a"],["l",225,90,205,90,"a"],["l",190,75,190,55,"a"],["l",240,90,240,160,"d"],["t",240,176,"2 m","m"],["l",190,90,190,160,"d"],["t",190,176,"1.5 m","m"],["t",215,32,"0.5 m","m","s"]]}},{pn:248,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbs",st:"Superposition and symmetry",q:"Two parallel long current carrying wires separated by a distance 2r are shown in the figure. The ratio of magnetic field at A to the magnetic field produced at C is x/7. The value of x is ______.",n:5,s:`Each long wire gives B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0 d), where d is the distance of the point from that wire. A and C are on the dashed line through both wires, and the currents point the same way (up).
At A, the point is to the left of both wires, so both fields point the same way and add. Distances: r from the left wire (current I) and 3r from the right wire (current 2I).
B_A = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0)(1/r + 2/(3r)) = (\u03BC\u2080I/2\u03C0r)(5/3).
At C, the point is to the right of both wires, again both fields point the same way. Distances: 3r from the left wire and r from the right wire.
B_C = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0)(1/(3r) + 2/r) = (\u03BC\u2080I/2\u03C0r)(7/3).
B_A/B_C = (5/3)/(7/3) = 5/7, so x = 5.`,fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"Two parallel long wires, the left carrying I upward and the right carrying 2I upward, a distance 2r apart. Point O midway between them, A at distance r to the left of the left wire and C at distance r to the right of the right wire, all on one dashed line.",d:[["l",20,100,320,100,"d"],["l",100,40,100,150],["l",100,80,100,58,"a"],["t",90,52,"I","e"],["l",220,40,220,150],["l",220,80,220,58,"a"],["t",212,52,"2I","e"],["t",30,94,"A","m"],["t",310,94,"C","m"],["c",160,100,4],["l",156,96,164,104],["l",156,104,164,96],["t",160,92,"O","m"],["l",40,126,100,126,"b"],["t",70,140,"r","m"],["l",100,126,160,126,"b"],["t",130,140,"r","m"],["l",160,126,220,126,"b"],["t",190,140,"r","m"],["l",220,126,280,126,"b"],["t",250,140,"r","m"],["l",100,162,220,162,"b"],["t",160,178,"2r","m"]]}},{pn:249,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force on a current-carrying wire",q:"A 2 A current carrying straight metal wire of resistance 1 \u03A9, resistivity 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \u03A9m, area of cross-section 10 mm\xB2 and mass 500 g is suspended horizontally in mid air by applying a uniform magnetic field B. The magnitude of B is ______ \xD7 10\u207B\xB9 T. (Given, g = 10 m/s\xB2)",n:5,s:`First find the length of the wire from R = \u03C1l/A, so l = RA/\u03C1.
A = 10 mm\xB2 = 10 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB2 = 10\u207B\u2075 m\xB2. l = (1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075)/(2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) = 5 m.
In mid air the magnetic force upward balances the weight: B I l = mg.
B \xD7 2 \xD7 5 = 0.5 \xD7 10, so B = 5/10 = 0.5 T.
That is 5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9 T, so the answer is 5.`},{pn:250,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force on a current-carrying wire",q:"A square loop of edge length 2 m carrying current of 2 A is placed with its edges parallel to the x-y axis. A magnetic field is passing through the x-y plane and expressed as B = B\u2080(1 + 4x)k\u0302, where B\u2080 = 5 T. The net magnetic force experienced by the loop is ______ N.",n:160,s:`The field is along z, perpendicular to the plane of the loop, and varies only with x.
Forces on the two sides parallel to the x-axis are equal and opposite (same x range, opposite currents), so they cancel.
The two sides parallel to the y-axis are 2 m apart in x, so the field difference between them is \u0394B = 4B\u2080 \xD7 2 = 4 \xD7 5 \xD7 2 = 40 T.
Their forces are opposite in direction but unequal, so net force = I l \u0394B = 2 \xD7 2 \xD7 40 = 160 N.
Note: the answer does not depend on where the loop sits, only on how far apart its two vertical sides are.`},{pn:251,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force on a current-carrying wire",q:"The horizontal component of earth's magnetic field at a place is 3.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T. A very long straight conductor carrying current of \u221A2 A in the direction from South East to North West is placed. The force per unit length experienced by the conductor is ______ \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 N/m.",n:35,s:`The horizontal field points North. The wire runs from South East to North West, which is 45\xB0 to the North direction.
Force per unit length is F/l = I B sin \u03B8, where \u03B8 is the angle between the wire and the field.
F/l = \u221A2 \xD7 3.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 sin 45\xB0 = \u221A2 \xD7 3.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 (1/\u221A2) = 3.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N/m.
In units of 10\u207B\u2076 N/m, this is 35.`},{pn:252,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force on a current-carrying wire",q:"A straight wire AB of mass 40 g and length 50 cm is suspended by a pair of flexible leads in uniform magnetic field of magnitude 0.40 T as shown in the figure. The magnitude of the current required in the wire to remove the tension in the supporting leads is ______ A. (Take g = 10 m s\u207B\xB2)",n:2,s:`The tension in the leads vanishes when the upward magnetic force on the wire balances its weight (see the second figure).
Weight: mg = 0.040 \xD7 10 = 0.4 N.
Magnetic force: B I l = 0.40 \xD7 I \xD7 0.50 = 0.2 I.
Equate: 0.2 I = 0.4, so I = 2 A.`,fig:{w:340,h:184,alt:"A straight wire AB hung by two flexible leads from a rod, in a uniform magnetic field pointing into the page (crosses).",d:[["l",50,18,290,18,"B"],["p","M 80 18 L 74 24 L 86 30 L 74 36 L 86 42 L 74 48 L 86 54 L 80 60 L 80 72"],["p","M 260 18 L 254 24 L 266 30 L 254 36 L 266 42 L 254 48 L 266 54 L 260 60 L 260 72"],["l",80,72,260,72,"B"],["t",72,88,"A","e"],["t",268,88,"B"],["t",110,50,"\xD7","m"],["t",140,50,"\xD7","m"],["t",170,50,"\xD7","m"],["t",200,50,"\xD7","m"],["t",230,50,"\xD7","m"],["t",110,110,"\xD7","m"],["t",140,110,"\xD7","m"],["t",170,110,"\xD7","m"],["t",200,110,"\xD7","m"],["t",230,110,"\xD7","m"],["t",110,142,"\xD7","m"],["t",140,142,"\xD7","m"],["t",170,142,"\xD7","m"],["t",200,142,"\xD7","m"],["t",230,142,"\xD7","m"],["t",110,172,"\xD7","m"],["t",140,172,"\xD7","m"],["t",170,172,"\xD7","m"],["t",200,172,"\xD7","m"],["t",230,172,"\xD7","m"],["t",310,112,"B = 0.40 T","e","s"]]},sfig:{w:300,h:170,alt:"For zero tension the upward magnetic force on the wire balances its weight: BIl up, mg down.",d:[["l",60,85,240,85,"B"],["t",52,90,"A","e"],["t",248,90,"B"],["l",150,85,150,28,"a"],["t",160,40,"BIl (up)"],["l",150,85,150,145,"a"],["t",160,140,"mg (down)"],["t",150,12,"B into the page, current from A to B","m","s"]]}},{pn:253,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 24 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force on a current-carrying wire",q:"A single turn current loop in the shape of a right angle triangle with sides 5 cm, 12 cm, 13 cm is carrying a current of 2 A. The loop is in a uniform magnetic field of magnitude 0.75 T whose direction is parallel to the current in the 13 cm side of the loop. The magnitude of the magnetic force on the 5 cm side will be (x/130) N. The value of x is ______.",n:9,s:`The field is along the 13 cm side. The angle \u03B8 between the 5 cm side and the 13 cm side has cos \u03B8 = 5/13, so sin \u03B8 = 12/13.
Force on a straight side: F = B I l sin \u03B8.
F = 0.75 \xD7 2 \xD7 0.05 \xD7 (12/13) = 0.075 \xD7 12/13 = 0.9/13 N.
Write it over 130: 0.9/13 = 9/130 N.
So x = 9.`},{pn:254,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force between parallel currents",q:"Two 10 cm long, straight wires, each carrying a current of 5 A are kept parallel to each other. If each wire experienced a force of 10\u207B\u2075 N, then separation between the wire is ______ cm.",n:5,s:`Force between two parallel wires of length l is F = \u03BC\u2080 I\u2081 I\u2082 l/(2\u03C0 d).
Using \u03BC\u2080/(2\u03C0) = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077: F = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 5 \xD7 5 \xD7 0.1/d = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077/d.
Set this equal to 10\u207B\u2075 N: d = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077/10\u207B\u2075 = 0.05 m.
So the separation is 5 cm.`},{pn:255,y:"JEE Main (year not printed)",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:`Comprehension. Advanced countries are making use of powerful electromagnets to move trains at very high speed. These trains are called maglev trains (abbreviated from magnetic levitation). These trains float on a guideway and do not run on steel tail tracks. Instead of using an engine based on fossil fuels, they make use of magnetic field forces. The magnetized coils are arranged in the guideway which repel the strong magnets placed in the train's under carriage. This helps train move over the guideway, a technic called electro-dynamic suspension. When current passes in the coils of guideway, a typical magnetic field is set up between the undercarriage of train and guideway which pushes and pulls the train along the guideway depending on the requirement. The lack of friction and its aerodynamic style allows the train to move at very high speed.
The levitation of the train is due to`,o:["mechanical force","electrostatic attraction","electrostatic repulsion","magnetic repulsion"],c:3,s:`The passage says the magnetised coils in the guideway repel the strong magnets in the train's undercarriage.
The train floats because of this repulsion between magnetic fields, not because of any electric charge or mechanical contact.
So the levitation is due to magnetic repulsion, option (d).`},{pn:256,y:"JEE Main (year not printed)",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:`Comprehension. Advanced countries are making use of powerful electromagnets to move trains at very high speed. These trains are called maglev trains (abbreviated from magnetic levitation). These trains float on a guideway and do not run on steel tail tracks. Instead of using an engine based on fossil fuels, they make use of magnetic field forces. The magnetized coils are arranged in the guideway which repel the strong magnets placed in the train's under carriage. This helps train move over the guideway, a technic called electro-dynamic suspension. When current passes in the coils of guideway, a typical magnetic field is set up between the undercarriage of train and guideway which pushes and pulls the train along the guideway depending on the requirement. The lack of friction and its aerodynamic style allows the train to move at very high speed.
The disadvantage of maglev trains is`,o:["more friction","less pollution","less wear and tear","high initial cost"],c:3,s:`Maglev trains float, so there is almost no friction (option a is false), and less pollution and less wear and tear are advantages, not disadvantages.
Powerful electromagnets, a special guideway and coils make the first cost of building such a system large.
So the disadvantage is the high initial cost, option (d).`},{pn:257,y:"IIT-JEE 2006",tp:"mfrc",st:"Lorentz force",q:`Comprehension. Advanced countries are making use of powerful electromagnets to move trains at very high speed. These trains are called maglev trains (abbreviated from magnetic levitation). These trains float on a guideway and do not run on steel tail tracks. Instead of using an engine based on fossil fuels, they make use of magnetic field forces. The magnetized coils are arranged in the guideway which repel the strong magnets placed in the train's under carriage. This helps train move over the guideway, a technic called electro-dynamic suspension. When current passes in the coils of guideway, a typical magnetic field is set up between the undercarriage of train and guideway which pushes and pulls the train along the guideway depending on the requirement. The lack of friction and its aerodynamic style allows the train to move at very high speed.
The force which makes maglev move is`,o:["gravitational","magnetic","nuclear forces","air drag."],c:1,s:`The passage says the current in the guideway coils sets up a magnetic field between the guideway and the undercarriage.
This field pushes and pulls the train along the guideway.
So the force that moves the train is the magnetic force, option (b).`},{pn:258,y:"IIT-JEE 2000",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force on a current-carrying wire",q:`A circular loop of radius R is bent along a diameter and given a shape as shown in the figure. One of the semicircles (KNM) lies in the x-z plane and the other one (KLM) in the y-z plane with their centres at the origin. Current I is flowing through each of the semi circles as shown in figure.
(a) A particle of charge q is released at the origin with a velocity v = \u2212v\u2080 \xEE. Find the instantaneous force F on the particle. Assume that space is gravity free.
(b) If an external uniform magnetic field B\u2080 \u0135 is applied, determine the force F\u2081 and F\u2082 on the semicircles KLM and KNM due to the field and the net force F on the loop.`,a:"(a) F = \u2212(\u03BC\u2080Iqv\u2080/(4R)) k\u0302  (b) F\u2081 = F\u2082 = 2B\u2080IR \xEE, so F = 4B\u2080IR \xEE",s:`(a) Each semicircle gives B = \u03BC\u2080I/(4R) at the centre O, perpendicular to its own plane.
KLM (in the y-z plane) gives a field along \u2212x\u0302 and KNM (in the x-z plane) gives a field along +\u0177, by the right-hand rule for the current direction K to M. So B = (\u03BC\u2080I/4R)(\u2212\xEE + \u0135).
Force on the charge: F = q(v \xD7 B) = q(\u2212v\u2080 \xEE) \xD7 (\u03BC\u2080I/4R)(\u2212\xEE + \u0135). The \xEE \xD7 \xEE part is zero and \xEE \xD7 \u0135 = k\u0302.
F = \u2212(\u03BC\u2080Iqv\u2080/(4R)) k\u0302.
(b) In a uniform field, the force on a curved wire equals the force on the straight line joining its ends. Both semicircles run from K to M, so each is equivalent to the diameter KM, of length 2R, carrying I.
F\u2081 = F\u2082 = I(2R)B\u2080 and the direction is \xEE (current along \u2212z, field along +y).
The two forces are in the same direction, so F = F\u2081 + F\u2082 = 4B\u2080IR \xEE.
Note: the direction of each current is read from the arrows of the figure.`,fig:{w:360,h:190,alt:"A circular loop bent along a diameter KM (along the z-axis): semicircle KLM in the y-z plane and semicircle KNM in the x-z plane, centres at the origin O. Current I flows from K to M along each semicircle. Axes: y up, x right, z toward the lower left.",d:[["p","M 108 152 L 108.2 144.5 L 108.9 136.5 L 110.1 128.3 L 111.6 119.9 L 113.6 111.4 L 116 102.8 L 118.8 94.4 L 121.9 86.1 L 125.3 78.1 L 129 70.4 L 132.9 63.1 L 137 56.4 L 141.3 50.3 L 145.6 44.8 L 150 40 L 154.4 36 L 158.7 32.8 L 163 30.4 L 167.1 29 L 171 28.4 L 174.7 28.7 L 178.1 29.9 L 181.2 31.9 L 184 34.9 L 186.4 38.6 L 188.4 43.2 L 189.9 48.4 L 191.1 54.4 L 191.8 60.9 L 192 68"],["p","M 108 152 L 115.5 151.8 L 123.5 151.1 L 131.7 149.9 L 140.1 148.4 L 148.6 146.4 L 157.2 144 L 165.6 141.2 L 173.9 138.1 L 181.9 134.7 L 189.6 131 L 196.9 127.1 L 203.6 123 L 209.7 118.7 L 215.2 114.4 L 220 110 L 224 105.6 L 227.2 101.3 L 229.6 97 L 231 92.9 L 231.6 89 L 231.3 85.3 L 230.1 81.9 L 228.1 78.8 L 225.1 76 L 221.4 73.6 L 216.8 71.6 L 211.6 70.1 L 205.6 68.9 L 199.1 68.2 L 192 68"],["l",108,152,192,68,"d"],["l",122,85.9,125.9,76.7,"a"],["l",174.1,138,183.3,134.1,"a"],["l",176.1,29,179.8,30.9,"a"],["l",231,83.9,229.1,80.2,"a"],["t",102,166,"K","e"],["t",198,62,"M"],["t",146,32,"L","e"],["t",228,114,"N"],["c",150,110,2,"F"],["t",156,124,"O"],["t",120,76,"I","m"],["t",206,148,"I","m"],["l",300,130,300,70,"a"],["l",300,130,350,130,"a"],["l",300,130,270,160,"a"],["t",308,76,"y"],["t",348,146,"x","e"],["t",266,176,"z","m"]]}},{pn:259,y:"IIT-JEE 1994",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force between parallel currents",q:"A long horizontal wire AB, which is free to move in a vertical plane and carries a steady current of 20 A, is in equilibrium at a height of 0.01 m over another parallel long wire CD which is fixed in a horizontal plane and carries a steady current of 30 A, as shown in figure. Show that when AB is slightly depressed, it executes simple harmonic motion. Find the period of oscillations.",a:"T = 2\u03C0\u221A(d/g) \u2248 0.2 s",s:`For AB to hang in equilibrium over CD, the magnetic force must push it up against its weight. That needs opposite currents in AB and CD (repulsion), as drawn in the second figure.
Force per unit length on AB at height x: F = \u03BC\u2080 i\u2081i\u2082/(2\u03C0x). At equilibrium x = d = 0.01 m: \u03BC\u2080 i\u2081i\u2082/(2\u03C0d) = \u03BBg, where \u03BB is the mass per unit length.
Push AB down by a small y, so the gap is x = d \u2212 y. The upward force per unit length becomes \u03BC\u2080 i\u2081i\u2082/(2\u03C0(d \u2212 y)) = \u03BBgd/(d \u2212 y).
Net upward force per unit length = \u03BBg(d/(d \u2212 y) \u2212 1) = \u03BBg\xB7y/(d \u2212 y) \u2248 \u03BBg y/d for small y.
The force is upward when the wire is below equilibrium and proportional to y, so it acts as a restoring force: SHM.
Acceleration = (g/d) y, so \u03C9\xB2 = g/d and T = 2\u03C0\u221A(d/g).
T = 2\u03C0\u221A(0.01/9.8) = 0.2 s.
Note: the period does not depend on the currents or the mass, because the equilibrium condition already fixed them.`,fig:{w:320,h:170,alt:"Two long horizontal parallel wires: AB above, free to move vertically, and CD below, fixed, with the gap x = 0.01 m between them.",d:[["l",60,45,250,45],["t",52,50,"A","e"],["t",258,50,"B"],["l",60,115,250,115],["t",52,120,"C","e"],["t",258,120,"D"],["l",290,45,290,115,"b"],["t",298,84,"x"],["t",160,36,"AB (20 A)","m","s"],["t",160,132,"CD (30 A)","m","s"]]},sfig:{w:320,h:170,alt:"AB carries current to the right, CD to the left (opposite currents repel). The repulsion Fm pushes AB up, its weight Fg pulls it down.",d:[["l",60,50,250,50],["l",120,50,150,50,"a"],["t",52,55,"i\u2081","e"],["l",60,125,250,125],["l",190,125,160,125,"a"],["t",52,130,"i\u2082","e"],["l",205,50,205,12,"a"],["t",213,24,"Fm"],["l",105,50,105,88,"a"],["t",113,82,"Fg = mg"],["l",290,50,290,125,"b"],["t",298,92,"d"]]}},{pn:260,y:"IIT-JEE 1992",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force on a current-carrying wire",q:"A straight segment OC (of length L metre) of a circuit carrying a current I ampere is placed along the x-axis (figure). Two infinitely long straight wires A and B, each extending from z = \u2212\u221E to z = +\u221E, are fixed at y = \u2212a metre and y = +a metre respectively, as shown in the figure. If the wires A and B each carry a current I ampere into the plane of the paper, obtain the expression for the force acting on the segment OC. What will be the force on OC if the current in the wire B is reversed?",a:"F = (\u03BC\u2080I\xB2/2\u03C0) ln((L\xB2 + a\xB2)/a\xB2) along \u2212z; zero if the current in B is reversed",s:`Take a point P on OC at distance x from O. Wires A and B are each at distance \u221A(a\xB2 + x\xB2) from P, so their fields are equal in size: B\u2081 = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0\u221A(a\xB2 + x\xB2)). Each is at right angles to the line joining the wire to P (see the second figure).
Their x components cancel and the y components add, along \u2212y: B = 2B\u2081 cos \u03B8, with cos \u03B8 = x/\u221A(a\xB2 + x\xB2).
B = \u03BC\u2080I x/(\u03C0(a\xB2 + x\xB2)), directed along \u2212y.
Force on a small piece dx of OC: dF = I B dx, with direction x\u0302 \xD7 (\u2212\u0177) = \u2212\u1E91.
F = (\u03BC\u2080I\xB2/\u03C0) \u222B\u2080\u1D38 x dx/(x\xB2 + a\xB2) = (\u03BC\u2080I\xB2/2\u03C0) ln((L\xB2 + a\xB2)/a\xB2), along \u2212z.
If the current in B is reversed, the y components of the two fields cancel and the x components add, so the resultant field at P is along x, the same line as the current in OC.
A current parallel to the field feels no force, so the force on OC is zero.`,fig:{w:340,h:200,alt:"Top view in the plane of the paper: segment OC of length L along the x-axis with current I from O to C, and two long wires A (at y = -a) and B (at y = +a) perpendicular to the paper, each carrying current I into the paper.",d:[["l",20,110,320,110,"a"],["l",60,200,60,10,"a"],["t",68,16,"y"],["t",316,126,"x","m"],["l",60,110,250,110,"B"],["l",140,110,170,110,"a"],["t",155,100,"I","m"],["t",56,126,"O","e"],["t",252,126,"C"],["c",60,50,9],["l",54,44,66,56],["l",54,56,66,44],["t",76,46,"B"],["c",60,170,9],["l",54,164,66,176],["l",54,176,66,164],["t",76,176,"A"],["l",20,110,20,50,"b"],["t",14,84,"a","e"],["l",30,110,30,170,"b"],["t",36,144,"a"]]},sfig:{w:300,h:210,alt:"At a point P on OC the fields of A and B have equal size, are at right angles to AP and BP, and add up to a field along the negative y-axis.",d:[["l",20,110,280,110],["t",14,114,"O","e"],["c",60,40,3,"F"],["t",50,36,"B","e"],["c",60,180,3,"F"],["t",50,186,"A","e"],["c",160,110,2.5,"F"],["t",166,102,"P"],["l",60,40,160,110,"d"],["l",60,180,160,110,"d"],["l",160,110,190,153,"a"],["t",196,166,"field of A"],["l",160,110,130,153,"a"],["t",152,172,"field of B","e"],["l",160,110,160,178,"a"],["t",168,196,"net field (\u2212y)"]]}},{pn:261,y:"IIT-JEE 1987",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force between parallel currents",q:`Two long straight parallel wires are 2 metre apart, perpendicular to the plane of the paper (see figure). The wire A carries current of 9.6 ampere, directed into the plane of the paper. The wire B carries a current such that the magnetic field of induction at the point P, at distance of (10/11) metre from the wire B, is zero. Find
(i) The magnitude and direction of the current in B.
(ii) The magnitude of the magnetic field of induction at the point S.
(iii) The force per unit length on the wire B.
(In the figure AS = 1.6 m and BS = 1.2 m; P lies on the line AB beyond B.)`,a:"(i) 3 A, directed out of the plane of the paper  (ii) 1.3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 T  (iii) 2.88 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 N/m (repulsion, away from A)",s:`(i) P is on the line AB beyond B. For the fields to cancel, they must be opposite, so the current in B must be opposite to that in A: out of the paper.
Distance of P from A is 2 + 10/11 = 32/11 m and from B is 10/11 m.
Set the field sizes equal: \u03BC\u2080(9.6)/(2\u03C0 \xD7 32/11) = \u03BC\u2080I_B/(2\u03C0 \xD7 10/11).
I_B = 9.6 \xD7 (10/11)/(32/11) = 9.6 \xD7 10/32 = 3 A.
(ii) Because 1.6\xB2 + 1.2\xB2 = 2\xB2, the angle ASB is 90\xB0. At S, B\u2081 (from A) = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 9.6/1.6 = 12 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T and B\u2082 (from B) = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 3/1.2 = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T.
B\u2081 is perpendicular to AS and B\u2082 is perpendicular to BS, so B\u2081 and B\u2082 are at right angles (see the second figure).
B = \u221A(12\xB2 + 5\xB2) \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 = 13 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 = 1.3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 T.
(iii) Force per unit length on B: F/l = \u03BC\u2080 I_A I_B/(2\u03C0 d) = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 9.6 \xD7 3/2 = 2.88 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 N/m. The currents are opposite, so the wires repel, and B is pushed away from A.`,fig:{w:300,h:240,alt:"Wire A (current into the paper) at the top and wire B at the bottom, 2 m apart on a vertical line. P lies on that line 10/11 m below B. S is a point to the right with AS = 1.6 m and BS = 1.2 m.",d:[["c",110,40,8],["l",105,35,115,45],["l",105,45,115,35],["t",100,36,"A","e"],["c",110,160,8],["c",110,160,2,"F"],["t",100,176,"B","e"],["l",110,40,110,225,"d"],["c",110,215,2.5,"F"],["t",118,222,"P"],["l",110,40,168,117,"d"],["l",110,160,168,117,"d"],["c",168,117,2.5,"F"],["t",176,114,"S"],["t",150,70,"1.6 m"],["t",148,150,"1.2 m"],["l",78,40,78,160,"b"],["t",70,104,"2 m","e"],["l",66,160,66,215,"b"],["t",60,192,"10/11 m","e","s"]]},sfig:{w:300,h:170,alt:"At S the field of A (12 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T) and the field of B (5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T) are at right angles; their resultant is 13 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 T.",d:[["c",200,70,2.5,"F"],["t",208,62,"S"],["l",200,70,152,106,"a"],["t",146,116,"B\u2081 = 12","e","s"],["l",200,70,185,50,"a"],["t",185,42,"B\u2082 = 5","e","s"],["l",200,70,137,86,"aB"],["t",131,84,"B = 13","e","s"],["t",150,156,"(units of 10\u207B\u2077 T)","m","s"]]}},{pn:262,y:"IIT-JEE 1990",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force on a current-carrying wire",q:"A wire ABCDEF (with each side of length L) bent as shown in figure and carrying a current I is placed in a uniform magnetic induction B parallel to the positive y-direction. The force experienced by the wire is ______ in the ______ direction.",a:"ILB in the +z direction",s:`In the figure the current flows F \u2192 E \u2192 D \u2192 C \u2192 B \u2192 A. FE and BA are parallel to y, ED and CB are along z, and DC is along x.
Force on a straight side is I l \xD7 B, which is zero when the side is parallel to B. So FE and BA feel no force.
ED and CB are perpendicular to B and carry current in opposite directions (up on ED, down on CB), so their forces are equal in size ILB but opposite, and cancel.
Only DC is left. Its current goes from D to C, along +x, and B is along +y.
Force = I L (x\u0302 \xD7 \u0177) B = ILB along +\u1E91, by the right-hand rule (Fleming's left-hand rule).
So the force is ILB in the +z direction.`,fig:{w:300,h:200,alt:"Wire ABCDEF, each side L: current flows F to E, E up to D, D to C, C down to B, B to A. A and BA, FE are along y; ED and CB along z; DC along x. Uniform B points along +y. Axes: x toward lower left, y right, z up.",d:[["p","M 212 137 L 152 137 L 152 77 L 110 110 L 110 170 L 170 170"],["l",190,137,172,137,"a"],["l",152,110,152,98,"a"],["l",136,93,126,101,"a"],["l",110,130,110,146,"a"],["l",130,170,148,170,"a"],["t",176,180,"A"],["t",106,184,"B","e"],["t",104,110,"C","e"],["t",156,73,"D"],["t",146,151,"E","e"],["t",218,141,"F"],["t",158,100,"I"],["l",200,60,260,60,"a"],["t",230,52,"B","m"],["l",240,175,240,125,"a"],["l",240,175,290,175,"a"],["l",240,175,215,196,"a"],["t",246,128,"+z"],["t",290,190,"+y","e"],["t",206,192,"+x","e"]]}},{pn:263,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 3 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbar",st:"Dipole in a uniform field",q:"A magnetic dipole experiences a torque of 80\u221A3 Nm when placed in uniform magnetic field in such a way that dipole moment makes angle of 60\xB0 with magnetic field. The potential energy of the dipole is",o:["\u221280 J","80 J","\u221260 J","\u221240\u221A3 J"],c:0,s:`Torque on a dipole: \u03C4 = MB sin \u03B8.
80\u221A3 = MB sin 60\xB0 = MB (\u221A3/2), so MB = 160 J.
Potential energy: U = \u2212MB cos \u03B8 = \u2212160 \xD7 cos 60\xB0 = \u2212160 \xD7 1/2.
U = \u221280 J, option (a).`},{pn:264,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"In an electromagnetic system, a quantity defined as the ratio of electric dipole moment and magnetic dipole moment has dimension of [M\u1D3EL\u1D60T\u1D3FA\u02E2]. The value of P and Q are",o:["\u22121, 0","1, \u22121","\u22121, 1","0, \u22121"],c:3,s:`Electric dipole moment = charge \xD7 length = (A T)(L), dimension [L T A].
Magnetic dipole moment = current \xD7 area = A \xD7 L\xB2, dimension [L\xB2 A].
Ratio = (L T A)/(L\xB2 A) = [L\u207B\xB9 T].
So M has power 0 (P = 0) and L has power \u22121 (Q = \u22121), with R = 1 and S = 0.
So P, Q = 0, \u22121, option (d).`},{pn:265,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A uniform magnetic field of 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 T acts along positive Y-direction. A rectangular loop of sides 20 cm and 10 cm with current of 5 A is in Y-Z plane. The current is in anticlockwise sense with reference to negative X axis. Magnitude and direction of the torque is",o:["2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 N\xB7m along negative X-direction","2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 N\xB7m along negative Z-direction","2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 N\xB7m along positive Y-direction","2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 N\xB7m along positive Z-direction"],c:1,s:`Magnetic moment of the loop: m = I \xD7 area = 5 \xD7 (0.20 \xD7 0.10) = 0.10 A\xB7m\xB2.
The loop is in the Y-Z plane, so m is along the X axis. Anticlockwise as seen from the negative X axis means (right-hand rule) m points towards that viewer, along \u2212X: m = \u22120.10 x\u0302.
Torque \u03C4 = m \xD7 B with B = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \u0177 T.
Size: \u03C4 = mB sin90\xB0 = 0.10 \xD7 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 N\xB7m.
Direction: (\u2212x\u0302) \xD7 \u0177 = \u2212\u1E91.
So the torque is 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 N\xB7m along the negative Z-direction, option (b).`},{pn:266,y:"JEE Advanced 2024",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A thin stiff insulated metal wire is bent into a circular loop with its two ends extending tangentially from the same point of the loop. The wire loop has mass m and radius r and it is in a uniform vertical magnetic field B\u2080, as shown in the figure. Initially, it hangs vertically downwards, because of acceleration due to gravity g, on two conducting supports at P and Q. When a current I is passed through the loop, the loop turns about the line PQ by an angle \u03B8 given by",o:["tan\u03B8 = \u03C0rIB\u2080/(mg)","tan\u03B8 = 2\u03C0rIB\u2080/(mg)","tan\u03B8 = \u03C0rIB\u2080/(2mg)","tan\u03B8 = mg/(\u03C0rIB\u2080)"],c:0,s:`The loop can turn about the horizontal line PQ at its top. In the final position two torques about PQ balance.
Weight: the centre of mass is at the loop centre, a distance r below PQ. After a turn by \u03B8 the weight torque is mg \xB7 r sin\u03B8.
Magnetic torque: the loop has moment \u03C0r\xB2I, perpendicular to its plane. At the start the plane is vertical, so the moment is horizontal and B\u2080 (vertical) is perpendicular to it. After turning by \u03B8 the angle between them is (90\xB0 \u2212 \u03B8).
So the magnetic torque is \u03C0r\xB2I B\u2080 sin(90\xB0 \u2212 \u03B8) = \u03C0r\xB2I B\u2080 cos\u03B8.
Balance: mg r sin\u03B8 = \u03C0r\xB2I B\u2080 cos\u03B8.
tan\u03B8 = \u03C0rIB\u2080/(mg), option (a).`,fig:{w:300,h:200,alt:"A circular wire loop of radius r hangs from a horizontal line PQ that rests on two supports at P and Q; a vertical magnetic field B0 points upward beside the loop and gravity g acts downward",d:[["l",40,30,260,30],["l",40,30,40,175],["l",260,30,260,175],["l",25,175,55,175],["l",245,175,275,175],["t",32,24,"P","e"],["t",268,24,"Q"],["c",150,100,65],["l",150,35,150,165,"d"],["l",150,100,192,56,"a"],["t",176,92,"r"],["t",208,150,"m"],["l",60,95,60,60,"a"],["l",70,95,70,60,"a"],["t",65,116,"B\u2080","m"],["l",150,175,150,195,"a"],["t",158,192,"g"]]}},{pn:267,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"The magnetic moments associated with two closely wound circular coils A and B of radius r_A = 10 cm and r_B = 20 cm respectively are equal if (Where N_A, I_A and N_B, I_B are number of turn and current of A and B respectively)",o:["2N_A I_A = N_B I_B","N_A = 2N_B","N_A I_A = 4N_B I_B","4N_A I_A = N_B I_B"],c:2,s:`The magnetic moment of a coil is m = N I A, where A = \u03C0r\xB2 is the area of one turn.
m_A = N_A I_A \u03C0 r_A\xB2 and m_B = N_B I_B \u03C0 r_B\xB2.
Set m_A = m_B: N_A I_A r_A\xB2 = N_B I_B r_B\xB2.
r_B = 2r_A, so r_B\xB2 = 4r_A\xB2.
N_A I_A = 4 N_B I_B.
This is option (c).`},{pn:268,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbar",st:"Dipole in a uniform field",q:"A bar magnet with a magnetic moment 5.0 Am\xB2 is placed in parallel position relative to a magnetic field of 0.4 T. The amount of required work done in turning the magnet from parallel to antiparallel position relative to the field direction is",o:["4 J","1 J","2 J","zero"],c:0,s:`The potential energy of a dipole is U = \u2212mB cos\u03B8.
Parallel (\u03B8 = 0\xB0): U\u2081 = \u2212mB. Antiparallel (\u03B8 = 180\xB0): U\u2082 = +mB.
Work done = U\u2082 \u2212 U\u2081 = 2mB.
W = 2 \xD7 5.0 \xD7 0.4 = 4 J.
This is option (a).`},{pn:269,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"Two concentric circular loops of radii r\u2081 = 30 cm and r\u2082 = 50 cm are placed in X-Y plane as shown in the figure. A current I = 7 A is flowing through them in the direction as shown in figure. The net magnetic moment of this system of two circular loops is approximately",o:["(7/2) k\u0302 Am\xB2","\u2212(7/2) k\u0302 Am\xB2","7 k\u0302 Am\xB2","\u22127 k\u0302 Am\xB2"],c:1,s:`Each loop has magnetic moment m = I\u03C0r\xB2, with direction from the right-hand rule.
The outer loop (r\u2082 = 0.5 m) carries the current clockwise (as seen from +z), so its moment is along \u2212\u1E91: m\u2082 = \u22127\u03C0(0.5)\xB2 = \u22121.75\u03C0.
The inner loop (r\u2081 = 0.3 m) carries it anticlockwise, so its moment is along +\u1E91: m\u2081 = +7\u03C0(0.3)\xB2 = +0.63\u03C0.
Net m = (\u22121.75\u03C0 + 0.63\u03C0) = \u22121.12\u03C0 \u2248 \u22123.52 Am\xB2.
7/2 = 3.5, so m \u2248 \u2212(7/2) k\u0302 Am\xB2, option (b).`,fig:{w:260,h:220,alt:"Two concentric circles in the X-Y plane with radii r1 inside and r2 outside; the current I flows clockwise in the outer circle and anticlockwise in the inner circle; the radii are drawn from the common centre",d:[["c",120,110,40],["c",120,110,90],["l",120,110,92,82],["l",120,110,160,190],["t",94,104,"r\u2081","s"],["t",122,170,"r\u2082","e"],["c",120,110,2,"F"],["l",161,100,161,88,"a"],["t",168,90,"I"],["l",208,100,208,118,"a"],["t",216,114,"I"]]}},{pn:270,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 26 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbar",st:"Dipole in a uniform field",q:"A bar magnet having a magnetic moment of 2.0 \xD7 10\u2075 JT\u207B\xB9, is placed along the direction of uniform magnetic field of magnitude B = 14 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T. The work done in rotating the magnet slowly through 60\xB0 from the direction of field is",o:["14 J","8.4 J","4 J","1.4 J"],c:0,s:`Work done in turning a dipole slowly from \u03B8\u2081 to \u03B8\u2082 is W = mB(cos\u03B8\u2081 \u2212 cos\u03B8\u2082).
Here \u03B8\u2081 = 0\xB0 and \u03B8\u2082 = 60\xB0, so W = mB(1 \u2212 cos60\xB0) = mB/2.
mB = 2.0 \xD7 10\u2075 \xD7 14 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 = 28 J.
W = 28 \xD7 \xBD = 14 J.
This is option (a).`},{pn:271,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 2 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A wire carrying current I is bent in the shape ABCDEFA as shown, where rectangle ABCDA and ADEFA are perpendicular to each other. If the sides of the rectangles are of lengths a and b, then the magnitude and direction of magnetic moment of the loop ABCDEFA is",o:["abI, along (\u0135/\u221A5 + 2k\u0302/\u221A5)","\u221A2 abI, along (\u0135/\u221A2 + k\u0302/\u221A2)","\u221A2 abI, along (\u0135/\u221A5 + 2k\u0302/\u221A5)","abI, along (\u0135/\u221A2 + k\u0302/\u221A2)"],c:1,s:`Think of the loop as two rectangular loops, ABCDA and ADEFA, each carrying I. Their currents in the shared side AD are opposite and cancel, leaving exactly the given wire.
So the net magnetic moment is the vector sum of the two moments, each of size I \xD7 area = abI.
Loop ABCDA lies in the horizontal X-Y plane. By the right-hand rule its moment is along +\u1E91: abI k\u0302.
Loop ADEFA lies in the vertical X-Z plane. By the right-hand rule its moment is along +\u0177: abI \u0135.
The two moments are perpendicular, so the magnitude is \u221A((abI)\xB2 + (abI)\xB2) = \u221A2 abI.
The direction is (\u0135 + k\u0302)/\u221A2.
This is option (b).`,fig:{w:320,h:250,alt:"A wire made of two perpendicular rectangles sharing side AD: the base rectangle ABCD lies flat in the X-Y plane with A on the X axis, AB of length a along Y and BC of length b; the upright rectangle ADEF has E above D and F above A. Current I flows A to B to C to D, then up to E, across to F and down to A",d:[["l",70,200,70,80,"a"],["l",70,200,250,200,"a"],["l",70,200,40,223,"a"],["t",76,84,"Z","s"],["t",252,204,"Y","s"],["t",28,236,"X","s"],["l",92.7,181.1,148.7,181.1],["l",116.19999999999999,181.1,126.30000000000001,181.1,"a"],["l",148.7,181.1,176,160],["l",160.2,172.2,165.1,168.4,"a"],["l",176,160,120,160],["l",152.5,160,142.4,160,"a"],["l",120,160,120,104],["l",120,136.5,120,126.4,"a"],["l",120,104,92.7,125.1],["l",108.5,112.9,103.6,116.7,"a"],["l",92.7,125.1,92.7,181.1],["l",92.7,148.6,92.7,158.7,"a"],["t",84.7,183.1,"A","e"],["t",152.7,193.1,"B"],["t",184,158,"C"],["t",116,174,"D"],["t",126,102,"E"],["t",86.7,123.1,"F","e"],["t",128.7,195.1,"a","s"],["t",178.35,172.55,"b","s"],["t",110,134,"I","e"],["t",80.7,153.1,"I","e"],["t",58,214,"O","e"]]}},{pn:272,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A circular coil has moment of inertia 0.8 kg m\xB2 around any diameter and is carrying current to produce a magnetic moment of 20 Am\xB2. The coil is kept initially in a vertical position and it can rotate freely around a horizontal diameter. When a uniform magnetic field of 4 T is applied along the vertical, it starts rotating around its horizontal diameter. The angular speed the coil acquires after rotating by 60\xB0 will be",o:["20 rad s\u207B\xB9","10\u03C0 rad s\u207B\xB9","10 rad s\u207B\xB9","20\u03C0 rad s\u207B\xB9"],c:2,s:`Note: the printed key says (c); the numbers work out to 10 rad/s only if the magnetic moment starts along the field (the coil's axis vertical) and turns by 60\xB0. If the moment started at 90\xB0 to B, the same method gives about 13 rad/s, which is not an option.
Energy method: the work done by the field becomes rotational kinetic energy, \xBDI\u03C9\xB2 = mB(cos\u03B8\u2081 \u2212 cos\u03B8\u2082).
The moment m = 20 Am\xB2, B = 4 T, so mB = 80 J.
Turning through 60\xB0 from the field direction: mB(cos0\xB0 \u2212 cos60\xB0) = 80 \xD7 \xBD = 40 J.
\xBD \xD7 0.8 \xD7 \u03C9\xB2 = 40, so \u03C9\xB2 = 100.
\u03C9 = 10 rad/s, option (c).`},{pn:273,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 5 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A square loop of side 2a, and carrying current I, is kept in XZ plane with its centre at origin. A long wire carrying the same current I is placed parallel to the z-axis and passing through the point (0, b, 0), (b >> a). The magnitude of the torque on the loop about Z-axis is given by",o:["\u03BC\u2080I\xB2a\xB2/(2\u03C0b)","\u03BC\u2080I\xB2a\xB3/(2\u03C0b\xB2)","2\u03BC\u2080I\xB2a\xB2/(\u03C0b)","2\u03BC\u2080I\xB2a\xB3/(\u03C0b\xB2)"],c:2,s:`Because b \u226B a, the field of the long wire is almost uniform over the small loop: B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0b).
At the origin that field is along the x-axis (perpendicular to both the wire and the line from the wire to the origin).
The loop is in the XZ plane, so its moment m = I(2a)\xB2 = 4a\xB2I points along y, perpendicular to B.
Torque \u03C4 = mB sin90\xB0 = 4a\xB2I \xD7 \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0b).
\u03C4 = 2\u03BC\u2080I\xB2a\xB2/(\u03C0b), about the z-axis.
This is option (c).`},{pn:274,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 6 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A charged particle going around in a circle can be considered to be a current loop. A particle of mass m carrying charge q is moving in a plane with speed v under the influence of magnetic field B. The magnetic moment of this moving particle",o:["(mv\xB2B)/(2B\xB2)","\u2212(mv\xB2B)/(2\u03C0B\xB2)","\u2212(mv\xB2B)/(B\xB2)","\u2212(mv\xB2B)/(2B\xB2)"],c:3,s:`The particle circles with radius r = mv/(qB) and period T = 2\u03C0r/v.
Equivalent current I = q/T = qv/(2\u03C0r). Area A = \u03C0r\xB2.
Magnitude of the moment: \u03BC = IA = (qv/2\u03C0r)(\u03C0r\xB2) = qvr/2.
Put r = mv/(qB): \u03BC = qv(mv/qB)/2 = mv\xB2/(2B).
Direction: the circulating charge always gives a field opposite to the applied B (like Lenz's law), so the moment points opposite to B. In vector form \u03BC = \u2212(mv\xB2/2B\xB2) B.
This is option (d).`},{pn:275,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 6 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A square loop of side 2a, and carrying current I, is kept in XZ plane with its centre at origin. A long wire carrying the same current I is placed parallel to the z-axis and passing through the point (0, b, 0), (b >> a). The magnitude of the torque on the loop about Z-axis is given by",o:["2\u03BC\u2080I\xB2a\xB2/(\u03C0b)","2\u03BC\u2080I\xB2a\xB2b/(\u03C0(a\xB2 + b\xB2))","\u03BC\u2080I\xB2a\xB2b/(2\u03C0(a\xB2 + b\xB2))","\u03BC\u2080I\xB2a\xB2/(2\u03C0b)"],c:0,s:`Because b \u226B a, the field of the long wire is almost uniform over the small loop: B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0b).
At the origin that field is along the x-axis (perpendicular to both the wire and the line from the wire to the origin).
The loop is in the XZ plane, so its moment m = I(2a)\xB2 = 4a\xB2I points along y, perpendicular to B.
Torque \u03C4 = mB sin90\xB0 = 4a\xB2I \xD7 \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0b).
\u03C4 = 2\u03BC\u2080I\xB2a\xB2/(\u03C0b), about the z-axis.
This is option (a).`},{pn:276,y:"JEE Advanced 2020",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A circular coil of radius R and N turns has negligible resistance. As shown in the schematic figure, its two ends are connected to two wires and it is hanging by those wires with its plane being vertical. The wires are connected to a capacitor with charge Q through a switch. The coil is in a horizontal uniform magnetic field B\u2080 parallel to the plane of the coil. When the switch is closed, the capacitor gets discharged through the coil in a very short time. By the time the capacitor is discharged fully, magnitude of the angular momentum gained by the coil will be (Assume that the discharge time is so short that the coil has hardly rotated during this time.)",o:["(\u03C0/2) N Q B\u2080 R\xB2","\u03C0 N Q B\u2080 R\xB2","2\u03C0 N Q B\u2080 R\xB2","4\u03C0 N Q B\u2080 R\xB2"],c:1,s:`While current i flows, the coil (area \u03C0R\xB2, N turns) feels a torque \u03C4 = N i \u03C0R\xB2 B\u2080 sin90\xB0, because its moment is perpendicular to B\u2080 (the field lies in the plane of the coil).
The coil has hardly rotated, so the torque keeps this size during the whole discharge.
Angular momentum gained = \u222B\u03C4 dt = N\u03C0R\xB2B\u2080 \u222B i dt.
\u222B i dt is the total charge that flows, which is Q.
L = \u03C0 N Q B\u2080 R\xB2.
This is option (b).`,fig:{w:300,h:220,alt:"A circular coil hanging from two wires connected to a charged capacitor and a switch in a rectangular circuit; horizontal magnetic field lines B0 pass through the plane of the coil",d:[["l",80,20,142,20],["l",142,10,142,30],["l",150,10,150,30],["l",150,20,190,20],["l",205,12,190,20],["l",205,20,230,20],["l",80,20,80,110],["l",230,20,230,110],["t",146,48,"C","m"],["t",196,40,"S","m"],["c",155,150,40],["l",80,110,125,126],["l",230,110,185,126],["l",50,128,265,128,"a"],["l",50,140,265,140,"a"],["l",50,152,265,152,"a"],["l",50,164,265,164,"a"],["l",50,176,265,176,"a"],["t",272,154,"B\u2080"]]}},{pn:277,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A circular coil having N turns and radius r carries a current I. It is held in the XZ plane in a magnetic field B \xEE. The torque on the coil due to the magnetic field is",o:["(B\u03C0r\xB2I)/(N)","(Br\xB2I)/(\u03C0N)","B\u03C0r\xB2IN","zero"],c:2,s:`The coil has N turns, so its magnetic moment is m = N I \u03C0r\xB2.
The coil is in the XZ plane, so m is along \xB1y, perpendicular to B \xEE.
Torque \u03C4 = mB sin90\xB0 = N I \u03C0r\xB2 B.
\u03C4 = B\u03C0r\xB2IN.
This is option (c).`},{pn:278,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A rectangular coil (Dimension 5 cm \xD7 2.5 cm) with 100 turns, carrying a current of 3 A in the clock-wise direction, is kept centered at the origin and in the X-Z plane. A magnetic field of 1 T is applied along X-axis. If the coil is tilted through 45\xB0 about Z-axis, then the torque on the coil is",o:["0.27 N m","0.42 N m","0.55 N m","0.38 N m"],c:0,s:`Moment of the coil: m = N I A = 100 \xD7 3 \xD7 (0.05 \xD7 0.025) = 0.375 A\xB7m\xB2.
The coil starts in the XZ plane, so m is along y, perpendicular to B (along x). The torque is \u03C4 = mB sin(angle between m and B).
Tilting by 45\xB0 about the z-axis turns m in the x-y plane to 45\xB0 from the x-axis, so the angle between m and B is 45\xB0.
\u03C4 = 0.375 \xD7 1 \xD7 sin45\xB0 = 0.375 \xD7 0.707 = 0.265 N\xB7m.
\u03C4 \u2248 0.27 N\xB7m, option (a).`},{pn:279,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A square loop is carrying a steady current I and the magnitude of its magnetic dipole moment is m. If this square loop is changed to a circular loop and it carries the same current, the magnitude of the magnetic dipole moment of circular loop will be",o:["3m/\u03C0","4m/\u03C0","m/\u03C0","2m/\u03C0"],c:1,s:`Let the square have side s: m = I s\xB2.
The wire length is the perimeter 4s. Made into a circle of radius R: 2\u03C0R = 4s, so R = 2s/\u03C0.
Circle moment: m\u2032 = I \u03C0R\xB2 = I \u03C0 (4s\xB2/\u03C0\xB2) = 4Is\xB2/\u03C0.
m\u2032 = 4m/\u03C0.
This is option (b).`},{pn:280,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force between parallel currents",q:"An infinitely long current carrying wire and a small current carrying loop are in the plane of the paper as shown. The radius of the loop is a and distance of its centre from the wire is d (d \u226B a). If the loop applies a force F on the wire then",o:["F = 0","F \u221D (a/d)","F \u221D (a\xB2/d\xB3)","F \u221D (a/d)\xB2"],c:3,s:`By Newton's third law the force of the loop on the wire equals the force of the wire on the loop, so find the force on the loop.
The wire's field is B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0d), perpendicular to the paper, and it changes with d.
Because d \u226B a, the loop acts as a dipole of moment m = I\u2032\u03C0a\xB2, and the field is almost uniform across it.
A dipole in a non-uniform field feels a net force F = m (dB/dd) (the field is strongest on the side near the wire, so the forces on the two sides do not cancel).
dB/dd = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0d\xB2), so F = I\u2032\u03C0a\xB2 \xD7 \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0d\xB2) = \u03BC\u2080I I\u2032a\xB2/(2d\xB2).
So F \u221D a\xB2/d\xB2 = (a/d)\xB2, option (d).`,fig:{w:300,h:170,alt:"A long straight wire carrying current drawn vertically on the left and a small circular loop of radius a at distance d to the right, both in the plane of the paper; d is marked by a double arrow between the wire and the centre of the loop",d:[["l",50,160,50,10,"a"],["t",38,40,"I","e"],["t",140,78,"d","m"],["c",230,85,24],["l",230,85,247,68],["t",252,64,"a","s"],["l",52,85,230,85,"d"],["l",238,109,222,109,"a"]]}},{pn:281,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"An insulating thin rod of length l has a linear charge density \u03C1(x) = \u03C1\u2080 x/l on it. The rod is rotated about an axis passing through the origin (x = 0) and perpendicular to the rod. If the rod makes n rotations per second, then the time averaged magnetic moment of the rod is",o:["\u03C0 n \u03C1\u2080 l\xB3","(\u03C0/3) n \u03C1\u2080 l\xB3","(\u03C0/4) n \u03C1\u2080 l\xB3","n \u03C1\u2080 l\xB3"],c:2,s:`Cut the rod into small elements of length dx at distance x. Its charge is dq = \u03C1\u2080(x/l) dx.
The element goes round n times per second, so its current is dI = n dq, and it encloses area \u03C0x\xB2.
Moment of the element: dm = dI \xD7 \u03C0x\xB2 = n \u03C0 x\xB2 \u03C1\u2080 (x/l) dx = (n\u03C0\u03C1\u2080/l) x\xB3 dx.
Add up from x = 0 to l: m = (n\u03C0\u03C1\u2080/l) \xD7 (l\u2074/4).
m = (\u03C0/4) n \u03C1\u2080 l\xB3.
This is option (c).`},{pn:282,y:"JEE Main 2018",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"The dipole moment of a circular loop carrying a current I is m and the magnetic field at the centre of the loop is B\u2081. When the dipole moment is doubled by keeping the current constant, the magnetic field at the centre of the loop is B\u2082. The ratio B\u2081/B\u2082 is",o:["2","\u221A3","\u221A2","1/\u221A2"],c:2,s:`Dipole moment m = I\u03C0r\xB2, and the field at the centre B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2r).
With I fixed, doubling m means r\xB2 doubles, so r becomes \u221A2 r.
B is proportional to 1/r, so B\u2082 = B\u2081/\u221A2.
B\u2081/B\u2082 = \u221A2.
This is option (c).`},{pn:283,y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A charge q is spread uniformly over an insulated loop of radius r. If it is rotated with an angular velocity \u03C9 with respect to normal axis then the magnetic moment of the loop is",o:["(1/2) q \u03C9 r\xB2","q \u03C9 r\xB2","(3/2) q \u03C9 r\xB2","(4/3) q \u03C9 r\xB2"],c:0,s:`The charge goes round once in T = 2\u03C0/\u03C9, so the equivalent current is I = q/T = q\u03C9/(2\u03C0).
Area of the loop: \u03C0r\xB2.
Magnetic moment m = I A = (q\u03C9/2\u03C0)(\u03C0r\xB2) = q\u03C9r\xB2/2.
This is option (a).`},{pn:284,y:"JEE Main 2017 \xB7 Online",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A uniform magnetic field B of 0.3 T is along the positive Z-direction. A rectangular loop (abcd) of sides 10 cm \xD7 5 cm carries a current I of 12 A. Out of the following different orientations which one corresponds to stable equilibrium?",o:["Figure (a)","Figure (b)","Figure (c)","Figure (d)"],c:3,s:`Stable equilibrium of a loop in a magnetic field means the magnetic moment m points along B (here +z), so the torque is zero and any small turn brings it back.
That needs the loop to lie in the X-Y plane with the current anticlockwise as seen from +z (right-hand rule gives m along +z).
In (b) and (c) the loop is in a vertical plane, so m is perpendicular to B and the torque is not zero.
In (a) the loop is flat, but the current goes a \u2192 d \u2192 c \u2192 b, which is clockwise from +z, so m is along \u2212z (unstable).
In (d) the loop is flat and the current goes a \u2192 b \u2192 c \u2192 d, anticlockwise from +z, so m is along +z.
So (d) is the stable orientation.`,fig:{w:400,h:350,alt:"Four orientations of a rectangular loop abcd with current I in axes X, Y, Z with B along Z: (a) flat in the XY plane, current a to d to c to b; (b) upright in the YZ plane; (c) upright in the XZ plane; (d) flat in the XY plane, current a to b to c to d",d:[["l",70,110,70,40,"a"],["l",70,110,180,110,"a"],["l",70,110,26.6,143.6,"a"],["t",75,44,"Z","s"],["t",184,113,"Y","s"],["t",16.6,151.6,"X","s"],["t",64,62,"B","es"],["t",10,30,"(a)"],["l",70,110,120,110],["l",91,110,100,110,"a"],["l",120,110,101.4,124.4],["l",112.2,116,108.8,118.6,"a"],["l",101.4,124.4,51.4,124.4],["l",80.4,124.4,71.4,124.4,"a"],["l",51.4,124.4,70,110],["l",59.2,118.4,62.6,115.8,"a"],["t",76,106,"a","s"],["t",124,106,"d","s"],["t",104,138,"c","s"],["t",42,138,"b","s"],["l",270,110,270,40,"a"],["l",270,110,380,110,"a"],["l",270,110,226.6,143.6,"a"],["t",275,44,"Z","s"],["t",384,113,"Y","s"],["t",216.6,151.6,"X","s"],["t",264,62,"B","es"],["t",210,30,"(b)"],["l",270,110,320,110],["l",291,110,300,110,"a"],["l",320,110,320,72],["l",320,94,320,87.2,"a"],["l",320,72,270,72],["l",299,72,290,72,"a"],["l",270,72,270,110],["l",270,88,270,94.8,"a"],["t",264,121,"a","s"],["t",326,121,"b","s"],["t",326,68,"c","s"],["t",276,67,"d","s"],["l",70,285,70,215,"a"],["l",70,285,180,285,"a"],["l",70,285,26.6,318.6,"a"],["t",75,219,"Z","s"],["t",184,288,"Y","s"],["t",16.6,326.6,"X","s"],["t",64,237,"B","es"],["t",10,205,"(c)"],["l",70,285,51.4,299.4],["l",62.2,291,58.8,293.6,"a"],["l",51.4,299.4,51.4,261.4],["l",51.4,283.4,51.4,276.6,"a"],["l",51.4,261.4,70,247],["l",59.2,255.4,62.6,252.8,"a"],["l",70,247,70,285],["l",70,263,70,269.8,"a"],["t",74,298,"a","s"],["t",57.4,310.4,"b","s"],["t",40,258,"c","s"],["t",76,244,"d","s"],["l",270,285,270,215,"a"],["l",270,285,380,285,"a"],["l",270,285,226.6,318.6,"a"],["t",275,219,"Z","s"],["t",384,288,"Y","s"],["t",216.6,326.6,"X","s"],["t",264,237,"B","es"],["t",210,205,"(d)"],["l",270,285,251.4,299.4],["l",262.2,291,258.8,293.6,"a"],["l",251.4,299.4,301.4,299.4],["l",272.4,299.4,281.4,299.4,"a"],["l",301.4,299.4,320,285],["l",309.2,293.4,312.6,290.8,"a"],["l",320,285,270,285],["l",299,285,290,285,"a"],["t",276,296,"a","s"],["t",257.4,310.4,"b","s"],["t",304,310,"c","s"],["t",316,274,"d","s"]]}},{pn:285,y:"JEE Main 2015",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A rectangular loop of sides 10 cm and 5 cm carrying a current I of 12 A is placed in different orientations as shown in the figure below. If there is a uniform magnetic field of 0.3 T in the positive z direction, in which orientations the loop would be in (i) stable equilibrium and (ii) unstable equilibrium.",o:["(2) and (4), respectively","(2) and (3), respectively","(1) and (2), respectively","(1) and (3), respectively"],c:0,s:`Equilibrium needs the torque m \xD7 B to be zero, so m must be along B (stable) or opposite to B (unstable).
Loops (1) and (3) are in vertical planes, so m is horizontal and perpendicular to B. They have maximum torque and are not in equilibrium.
Loops (2) and (4) are flat in the X-Y plane, so m is along \xB1z.
In (2) the current is anticlockwise when seen from +z, so m is along +z (the same as B): stable equilibrium.
In (4) the current is clockwise when seen from +z, so m is along \u2212z (opposite to B): unstable equilibrium.
So (i) is (2) and (ii) is (4), option (a).`,fig:{w:400,h:350,alt:"Four orientations of a rectangular loop with current I in axes X, Y, Z with B along Z: (1) upright in the YZ plane; (2) flat in the XY plane, anticlockwise from above; (3) upright in the XZ plane; (4) flat in the XY plane, clockwise from above",d:[["l",70,110,70,40,"a"],["l",70,110,180,110,"a"],["l",70,110,26.6,143.6,"a"],["t",75,44,"Z","s"],["t",184,113,"Y","s"],["t",16.6,151.6,"X","s"],["t",64,62,"B","es"],["t",10,30,"(1)"],["l",70,110,120,110],["l",91,110,100,110,"a"],["l",120,110,120,72],["l",120,94,120,87.2,"a"],["l",120,72,70,72],["l",99,72,90,72,"a"],["l",70,72,70,110],["l",70,88,70,94.8,"a"],["l",270,110,270,40,"a"],["l",270,110,380,110,"a"],["l",270,110,226.6,143.6,"a"],["t",275,44,"Z","s"],["t",384,113,"Y","s"],["t",216.6,151.6,"X","s"],["t",264,62,"B","es"],["t",210,30,"(2)"],["l",320,110,270,110],["l",299,110,290,110,"a"],["l",270,110,251.4,124.4],["l",262.2,116,258.8,118.6,"a"],["l",251.4,124.4,301.4,124.4],["l",272.4,124.4,281.4,124.4,"a"],["l",301.4,124.4,320,110],["l",309.2,118.4,312.6,115.8,"a"],["l",70,285,70,215,"a"],["l",70,285,180,285,"a"],["l",70,285,26.6,318.6,"a"],["t",75,219,"Z","s"],["t",184,288,"Y","s"],["t",16.6,326.6,"X","s"],["t",64,237,"B","es"],["t",10,205,"(3)"],["l",70,247,70,285],["l",70,263,70,269.8,"a"],["l",70,285,51.4,299.4],["l",62.2,291,58.8,293.6,"a"],["l",51.4,299.4,51.4,261.4],["l",51.4,283.4,51.4,276.6,"a"],["l",51.4,261.4,70,247],["l",59.2,255.4,62.6,252.8,"a"],["l",270,285,270,215,"a"],["l",270,285,380,285,"a"],["l",270,285,226.6,318.6,"a"],["t",275,219,"Z","s"],["t",384,288,"Y","s"],["t",216.6,326.6,"X","s"],["t",264,237,"B","es"],["t",210,205,"(4)"],["l",270,285,320,285],["l",291,285,300,285,"a"],["l",320,285,301.4,299.4],["l",312.2,291,308.8,293.6,"a"],["l",301.4,299.4,251.4,299.4],["l",280.4,299.4,271.4,299.4,"a"],["l",251.4,299.4,270,285],["l",259.2,293.4,262.6,290.8,"a"]]}},{pn:286,y:"IIT-JEE 2000",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A particle of charge q and mass m moves in a circular orbit of radius r with angular speed \u03C9. The ratio of the magnitude of its magnetic moment to that of its angular momentum depends on",o:["\u03C9 and q","\u03C9, q and m","q and m","\u03C9 and m"],c:2,s:`Current: I = q/T = q\u03C9/(2\u03C0). Magnetic moment \u03BC = I\u03C0r\xB2 = q\u03C9r\xB2/2.
Angular momentum: L = mvr = m\u03C9r\xB2.
Ratio \u03BC/L = (q\u03C9r\xB2/2)/(m\u03C9r\xB2) = q/(2m).
The \u03C9 and r cancel, so the ratio depends only on q and m.
This is option (c).`},{pn:287,y:"IIT-JEE 2006",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force on a current-carrying wire",q:"A long current carrying wire, carrying current I\u2081 such that I\u2081 is flowing out from the plane of paper is placed at O. A steady state current I\u2082 is flowing in the loop ABCD.",o:["the net force is zero","the net torque is zero","As seen from O, the loop will rotate in clockwise along OO\u2032 axis.","As seen from O, the loop will rotate in anticlockwise direction along OO\u2032 axis."],c:[0,2],s:`The wire at O is perpendicular to the paper, so its field circles O in the plane of the paper: B depends only on the distance from O and is tangent to circles about O.
The arcs AB and CD are along circles about O, so the current there is along B and the force on them is zero.
The straight sides BC and DA are radial, so B is perpendicular to them, and the force is perpendicular to the paper. On BC the current goes outward and on DA inward, so the two forces (same size, since they have the same length and field) are opposite: out of the page on BC and into the page on DA.
Net force = 0, so (a) is true.
The two forces are equal and opposite but act at different places on either side of OO\u2032, so they form a couple. Its torque axis is along OO\u2032 (the force is out of the page on BC and into the page on DA, so the couple turns the loop about OO\u2032). So (b) is false.
Seen from O, looking along OO\u2032 away from O, the sense of rotation about OO\u2032 is clockwise: (c) is true, (d) is false.
So the correct options are (a) and (c).`,fig:{w:310,h:200,alt:"A long wire at O carrying current I1 out of the paper, with a dashed axis OO prime. Loop ABCD: inner arc A to B and outer arc C to D, with radial sides B to C and D to A; current I2 flows A to B to C to D",d:[["c",40,100,8],["c",40,100,1.5,"F"],["l",48,100,290,100,"d"],["l",40,100,117.9,55,"d"],["l",40,100,117.9,145,"d"],["p","M117.9,145 A90,90 0 0 0 117.9,55"],["l",117.9,55,161.2,30],["p","M161.2,30 A140,140 0 0 1 161.2,170"],["l",161.2,170,117.9,145],["l",130,108,130,92,"a"],["l",134,43,141,39,"a"],["l",181,92,181,108,"a"],["l",144,160,136,155,"a"],["t",30,90,"O","e"],["t",26,122,"I\u2081","e"],["t",296,104,"O\u2032"],["t",108,52,"B","e"],["t",166,30,"C"],["t",108,152,"A","e"],["t",166,176,"D"],["t",190,100,"I\u2082"]]}},{pn:288,y:"IIT-JEE 1992",tp:"mfrc",st:"Force on a current-carrying wire",q:"A tube of length L is filled completely with an incompressible liquid of mass M and closed at both the ends. The tube is then rotated in a horizontal plane about one of its ends with a uniform angular velocity \u03C9. The force exerted by the liquid at the other end is",o:["M\u03C9\xB2L/2","M\u03C9\xB2L","M\u03C9\xB2L/4","M\u03C9\xB2L\xB2/2"],c:0,s:`The liquid moves in circles about the fixed end. A small slice dm at distance x needs a centripetal force dm \u03C9\xB2 x.
Mass per unit length is M/L, so dm = (M/L) dx.
Total force needed = \u222B (M/L) \u03C9\xB2 x dx from 0 to L = (M/L) \u03C9\xB2 L\xB2/2 = M\u03C9\xB2L/2.
The other end of the tube must supply this force through the liquid (the force the liquid exerts there is the same size, by the third law).
So the force is M\u03C9\xB2L/2, option (a).`},{pn:289,y:"IIT-JEE 1982",tp:"mbar",st:"Force and torque on magnets",q:"A magnetic needle is kept in a non-uniform magnetic field. It experiences",o:["a force and a torque","a force but not a torque","a torque but not a force","neither a force nor a torque"],c:0,s:`A magnetic needle is a dipole: it has a north pole and a south pole of equal strength.
In a non-uniform field the two poles are in fields of different strength, so the forces on them are not equal and opposite. A net force remains.
The two forces also act at different points and are not along one line, so they give a torque about the centre unless the needle lies exactly along the field.
So the needle experiences both a force and a torque, option (a).`},{pn:290,y:"JEE Advanced 2025",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A conducting solid sphere of radius R and mass M carries a charge Q. The sphere is rotating about an axis passing through its center with a uniform angular speed \u03C9. The ratio of the magnitudes of the magnetic dipole moment to the angular momentum about the same axis is given as \u03B1 Q/(2M). The value of \u03B1 is _____.",n:1.67,s:`A conductor holds its charge on the surface, so the charge Q is spread uniformly over a spherical shell of radius R.
Cut the shell into rings. A ring at angle \u03B8 has charge dq = Q sin \u03B8 d\u03B8/2 and area of loop \u03C0(R sin \u03B8)\xB2. Its current is dI = dq \u03C9/(2\u03C0).
d\u03BC = dI \xD7 \u03C0 R\xB2 sin\xB2\u03B8 = (Q \u03C9 R\xB2/4) sin\xB3\u03B8 d\u03B8, so \u03BC = (Q \u03C9 R\xB2/4) \u222B\u2080^\u03C0 sin\xB3\u03B8 d\u03B8 = (Q \u03C9 R\xB2/4)(4/3) = Q \u03C9 R\xB2/3.
The sphere is a solid body of mass M, so L = I\u03C9 = (2/5)MR\xB2\u03C9.
\u03BC/L = (Q\u03C9R\xB2/3)/((2/5)MR\xB2\u03C9) = 5Q/(6M) = \u03B1 Q/(2M), so \u03B1 = 5/3 \u2248 1.67.`},{pn:291,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A circular coil having 200 turns, 2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2 area and carrying 100 \u03BCA current is placed in a uniform magnetic field of 1 T. Initially the magnetic dipole moment (M) was directed along B. Amount of work, required to rotate the coil through 90\xB0 from its initial orientation such that M becomes perpendicular to B, is _____ \u03BCJ.",n:5,s:`The magnetic moment of the coil is m = NIA = 200 \xD7 100 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 A m\xB2.
The potential energy of a dipole is U = \u2212mB cos \u03B8. Initially \u03B8 = 0, U = \u2212mB. At 90\xB0, U = 0.
Work required = U_final \u2212 U_initial = 0 \u2212 (\u2212mB) = mB = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 1 = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 J.
So the work is 5 \u03BCJ.`},{pn:292,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A coil of 100 turns, area of 5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB2, carrying current of 1 mA is placed in uniform magnetic field of 0.20 T such a way that plane of coil is perpendicular to the magnetic field. The work done in turning the coil through 90\xB0 is _____ \u03BCJ.",n:100,s:`The plane of the coil is perpendicular to B, so the magnetic moment m is parallel to B (\u03B8 = 0).
m = NIA = 100 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 A m\xB2.
Turning the coil by 90\xB0 takes U from \u2212mB to 0, so the work is W = mB(cos 0\xB0 \u2212 cos 90\xB0) = mB.
W = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 0.20 = 10\u207B\u2074 J = 100 \u03BCJ.`},{pn:293,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbar",st:"Field of a bar magnet",q:"A straight magnetic strip has a magnetic moment of 44 Am\xB2. If the strip is bent in a semicircular shape, its magnetic moment will be _____ Am\xB2. (Given \u03C0 = 22/7)",n:28,s:`A bar magnet has poles of strength p separated by its length L: m = p L = 44.
When the strip is bent into a semicircle, the poles stay at the two ends. Their separation becomes the diameter 2R, where the arc length is \u03C0R = L, so R = L/\u03C0.
New moment m\u2032 = p \xD7 2R = p \xD7 2L/\u03C0 = (2/\u03C0) m.
m\u2032 = (2 \xD7 7/22) \xD7 44 = 28 A m\xB2.`},{pn:294,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbar",st:"Field of a bar magnet",q:"The magnetic potential due to a magnetic dipole at a point on its axis situated at a distance of 20 cm from its center is 1.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 Tm. The magnetic moment of the dipole is _____ Am\xB2. (Given: \u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0 = 10\u207B\u2077 Tm A\u207B\xB9)",n:6,s:`On the axis of a dipole the magnetic potential is V = (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0) m/r\xB2.
m = V r\xB2/(\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0) = (1.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 (0.2)\xB2)/10\u207B\u2077.
= (1.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 0.04)/10\u207B\u2077 = 6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077/10\u207B\u2077 = 6.
The magnetic moment is 6 A m\xB2.`},{pn:295,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A wire of length 314 cm carrying current of 14 A is bent to form a circle. The magnetic moment of the coil is _____ A-m\xB2. [Given \u03C0 = 3.14]",n:11,s:`The wire makes one circle, so 2\u03C0r = 3.14 m, giving r = 3.14/(2 \xD7 3.14) = 0.5 m.
Magnetic moment m = I \xD7 area = I \u03C0 r\xB2.
m = 14 \xD7 3.14 \xD7 (0.5)\xB2 = 14 \xD7 3.14 \xD7 0.25 = 10.99.
So m \u2248 11 A m\xB2.`},{pn:296,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A coil in the shape of an equilateral triangle of side 10 cm lies in a vertical plane between the pole pieces of permanent magnet producing a horizontal magnetic field 20 mT. The torque acting on the coil when a current of 0.2 A is passed through it and its plane becomes parallel to the magnetic field will be \u221Ax \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N m. The value of x is _____.",n:3,s:`Torque on a loop is \u03C4 = mB sin \u03B8, where \u03B8 is the angle between m (normal to the plane) and B. When the plane is parallel to B, \u03B8 = 90\xB0 and the torque is largest: \u03C4 = mB.
Area of the triangle = (\u221A3/4) a\xB2 = (\u221A3/4)(0.1)\xB2 = \u221A3 \xD7 2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB2.
m = IA = 0.2 \xD7 2.5\u221A3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 = \u221A3 \xD7 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 A m\xB2.
\u03C4 = mB = \u221A3 \xD7 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 20 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 = \u221A3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N m.
So x = 3.`},{pn:297,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A uniform conducting wire of length 24a, and resistance R is wound up as a current carrying coil in the shape of an equilateral triangle of side 'a' and then in the form of a square of side 'a'. The coil is connected to a voltage source V\u2080. The ratio of magnetic moment of the coils in case of equilateral triangle to that for square is 1 : \u221Ay where y is _____.",n:3,s:`The same wire and the same source give the same current I = V\u2080/R in both cases, so only turns and area matter. m = N I A.
Triangle of side a: perimeter 3a, so N = 24a/3a = 8 turns, and A = (\u221A3/4)a\xB2. m\u2081 = 8 I (\u221A3/4)a\xB2 = 2\u221A3 I a\xB2.
Square of side a: perimeter 4a, so N = 6 turns and A = a\xB2. m\u2082 = 6 I a\xB2.
m\u2081/m\u2082 = 2\u221A3/6 = 1/\u221A3, so the ratio is 1 : \u221A3.
Hence y = 3.`},{pn:298,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 23 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
List-I: (A) Permeability of free space  (B) Magnetic field  (C) Magnetic moment  (D) Torsional constant
List-II: (I) [ML\xB2T\u207B\xB2]  (II) [MT\u207B\xB2A\u207B\xB9]  (III) [MLT\u207B\xB2A\u207B\xB2]  (IV) [L\xB2A]
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["(A)-(IV), (B)-(III), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)","(A)-(III), (B)-(II), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I)","(A)-(II), (B)-(I), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(I), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(III)"],c:1,s:`Permeability \u03BC\u2080: from B = \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0r) or F = \u03BC\u2080I\u2081I\u2082L/(2\u03C0d), \u03BC\u2080 = F d/(I\xB2 L) has dimension MLT\u207B\xB2 A\u207B\xB2 (force per length is MT\u207B\xB2, divided by A\xB2). So (A)-(III).
Magnetic field: F = qvB gives B = F/(qv) = MLT\u207B\xB2/(A T \xD7 L T\u207B\xB9) = MT\u207B\xB2A\u207B\xB9. So (B)-(II).
Magnetic moment = current \xD7 area = A \xD7 L\xB2 = [L\xB2A]. So (C)-(IV).
Torsional constant = torque per unit angle, with the dimension of torque: ML\xB2T\u207B\xB2. So (D)-(I).
The matching (A)-(III), (B)-(II), (C)-(IV), (D)-(I) is option (b).`},{pn:299,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmat",st:"Hysteresis and permeability",q:`Match List-I with List-II.
List-I: (A) Magnetic induction  (B) Magnetic intensity  (C) Magnetic flux  (D) Magnetic moment
List-II: (I) Ampere meter\xB2  (II) Weber  (III) Gauss  (IV) Ampere meter
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["(A)-(III), (B)-(II), (C)-(I), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(I), (B)-(II), (C)-(III), (D)-(IV)","(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(I), (D)-(II)","(A)-(III), (B)-(IV), (C)-(II), (D)-(I)"],c:3,s:`Magnetic induction B (the magnetic field) is measured in tesla in SI and in gauss in the CGS system. So (A)-(III).
Magnetic intensity H = B/\u03BC\u2080 = (ampere turns)/length, with unit ampere per metre, which is ampere meter\u207B\xB9; among the given choices the unit written "Ampere meter" is the only one left for it, so (B)-(IV).
Magnetic flux \u03A6 = B \xD7 area has the unit weber. So (C)-(II).
Magnetic moment = current \xD7 area, unit ampere meter\xB2. So (D)-(I).
Note: the printed unit for (IV) should be ampere per metre (A/m); it is written without the "per".
The matching is option (d).`},{pn:300,y:"IIT-JEE 2009",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:`Six point charges, each of the same magnitude q, are arranged in different manners as shown in Column II. In each case, a point M and a line PQ passing through M are shown. Let E be the electric field and V be the electric potential at M (potential at infinity is zero) due to the given charge distribution when it is at rest. Now, the whole system is set into rotation with a constant angular velocity about the line PQ. Let B be the magnetic field at M and \u03BC be the magnetic moment of the system in this condition. Assume each rotating charge to be equivalent to a steady current.
Column I: (A) E = 0  (B) V \u2260 0  (C) B = 0  (D) \u03BC \u2260 0
Column II: (p) Charges are at the corners of a regular hexagon. M is at the centre of the hexagon. PQ is perpendicular to the plane of the hexagon.
(q) Charges are on a line perpendicular to PQ at equal intervals. M is the mid-point between the two innermost charges.
(r) Charges are placed on two coplanar insulating rings at equal intervals. M is the common centre of the rings. PQ is perpendicular to the plane of the rings.
(s) Charges are placed at the corners of a rectangle of sides a and 2a and at the mid points of the longer sides. M is at the centre of the rectangle. PQ is parallel to the longer sides.
(t) Charges are placed on two coplanar, identical insulating rings at equal intervals. M is the mid point between the centres of the rings. PQ is perpendicular to the line joining the centres and coplanar to the rings.`,fig:{w:420,h:270,alt:"Five arrangements of six equal point charges: (p) a regular hexagon with alternating signs, PQ perpendicular to its plane; (q) a line of charges \u2212 + \u2212 + \u2212 + with M at the middle, PQ perpendicular to the line; (r) two concentric rings, three negative charges on the inner and three positive on the outer, PQ perpendicular to the plane; (s) a rectangle with charges \u2212 at the four corners and + at the midpoints of the longer sides, PQ parallel to the longer sides; (t) two coplanar rings, three positive charges on the left ring and three negative on the right, PQ perpendicular to the line joining the centres",d:[["p","M100 60L85 34L55 34L40 60L55 86L85 86Z"],["c",100,60,2,"F"],["t",111,63,"+","m"],["c",85,34,2,"F"],["t",90.5,27.5,"\u2212","m"],["c",55,34,2,"F"],["t",49.5,27.5,"+","m"],["c",40,60,2,"F"],["t",29,63,"\u2212","m"],["c",55,86,2,"F"],["t",49.5,98.5,"+","m"],["c",85,86,2,"F"],["t",90.5,98.5,"\u2212","m"],["c",70,60,2,"F"],["t",74,74,"M","s"],["l",46,82,100,36,"d"],["t",40,94,"P","s"],["t",106,30,"Q","s"],["t",70,112,"(p)","m"],["l",133,60,277,60,"d"],["c",145,60,2,"F"],["t",145,52,"\u2212","m"],["c",169,60,2,"F"],["t",169,52,"+","m"],["c",193,60,2,"F"],["t",193,52,"\u2212","m"],["c",217,60,2,"F"],["t",217,52,"+","m"],["c",241,60,2,"F"],["t",241,52,"\u2212","m"],["c",265,60,2,"F"],["t",265,52,"+","m"],["l",205,34,205,90],["t",210,36,"P","s"],["t",210,94,"Q","s"],["t",199,72,"M","es"],["t",205,118,"(q)","m"],["c",340,60,15],["c",340,60,32],["c",340,45,2,"F"],["t",340,38,"\u2212","m"],["c",327.0096189432334,67.5,2,"F"],["t",321,78,"\u2212","m"],["c",352.9903810567666,67.5,2,"F"],["t",359,78,"\u2212","m"],["c",312.28718707889794,44,2,"F"],["t",304.5,42.5,"+","m"],["c",367.71281292110206,44,2,"F"],["t",375.5,42.5,"+","m"],["c",340,92,2,"F"],["t",340,104,"+","m"],["c",340,60,2,"F"],["t",321,63,"M","es"],["l",310,94,376,30,"d"],["t",304,104,"P","s"],["t",382,30,"Q","s"],["t",340,122,"(r)","m"],["r",65,168,90,44],["c",65,168,2,"F"],["t",65,162,"\u2212","m"],["c",110,168,2,"F"],["t",110,162,"+","m"],["c",155,168,2,"F"],["t",155,162,"\u2212","m"],["c",65,212,2,"F"],["t",65,224,"\u2212","m"],["c",110,212,2,"F"],["t",110,224,"+","m"],["c",155,212,2,"F"],["t",155,224,"\u2212","m"],["l",48,190,172,190],["t",48,202,"P","s"],["t",172,202,"Q","s"],["c",110,190,2,"F"],["t",114,200,"M","s"],["t",110,242,"(s)","m"],["c",264,190,16],["c",264,174,2,"F"],["t",264,168,"+","m"],["c",250.14359353944897,198,2,"F"],["t",242.3,205.5,"+","m"],["c",277.856406460551,198,2,"F"],["t",276,214,"+","m"],["c",316,190,16],["c",316,174,2,"F"],["t",316,168,"\u2212","m"],["c",302.143593539449,198,2,"F"],["t",304,214,"\u2212","m"],["c",329.856406460551,198,2,"F"],["t",337.7,205.5,"\u2212","m"],["l",228,190,352,190,"d"],["l",290,160,290,220],["t",294,164,"P","s"],["t",294,224,"Q","s"],["c",290,190,2,"F"],["t",284,226,"M","es"],["t",290,248,"(t)","m"]]},a:"(A) \u2192 (p), (r), (s);  (B) \u2192 (r), (s);  (C) \u2192 (p), (q), (t);  (D) \u2192 (r), (s)",s:`Work out E, V, B and \u03BC at M for each arrangement (signs as in the figure), then read off the columns.
(p) Hexagon with alternating signs: the three + charges (120\xB0 apart) give fields that cancel at M, and so do the three \u2212 charges, so E = 0. V = 0 (three + and three \u2212 at equal distance). On rotation the + and \u2212 charges on the same circle give opposite currents: B = 0 and \u03BC = 0.
(q) Charges \u2212 + \u2212 | + \u2212 + on a line, antisymmetric about M (the charge at \u2212x is opposite to that at +x): E \u2260 0 (the two fields add), V = 0. A pair at \xB1x moves on circles of the same radius around PQ with opposite currents: B = 0, \u03BC = 0.
(r) Inner ring all negative, outer ring all positive, three charges each at 120\xB0: E = 0 (they cancel in each ring). V = k(3q)(1/R\u2082 \u2212 1/R\u2081) \u2260 0. The rings carry opposite currents with different radii, so B \u2260 0 and \u03BC \u2260 0.
(s) Four negative corners and two positive midpoints placed symmetrically about M: E = 0 (point symmetry). Net charge is \u22122q, so V \u2260 0. Negative corners and positive midpoints do not cancel in B or in \u03BC: B \u2260 0, \u03BC \u2260 0.
(t) Left ring positive, right ring negative and mirror images: E \u2260 0 (fields add), V = 0 by antisymmetry. Mirror charges are opposite and circle PQ with equal radius, so B = 0 and \u03BC = 0.
So (A) E = 0: (p), (r), (s). (B) V \u2260 0: (r), (s). (C) B = 0: (p), (q), (t). (D) \u03BC \u2260 0: (r), (s).`},{pn:301,y:"IIT-JEE 2006",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:`Match the following columns:
Column I: (a) Dielectric ring uniformly charged  (b) Dielectric ring uniformly charged rotating with angular velocity \u03C9  (c) Constant current in ring i  (d) A variable current in a ring, i = i\u2080 cos \u03C9t
Column II: (p) Constant electrostatic field out of system  (q) Magnetic field strength  (r) Electric field (induced)  (s) Magnetic dipole moment`,a:"(a) \u2192 (p);  (b) \u2192 (p), (q), (s);  (c) \u2192 (q), (s);  (d) \u2192 (q), (r), (s)",s:`Note: the book prints (b) \u2192 (q, s); the ring is still charged so (p) also applies.
Decide for each system which of the four effects exist.
(a) A uniformly charged ring at rest: a constant electrostatic field outside. No current, so no magnetic field or moment. (a) \u2192 (p).
(b) A charged ring rotating at constant \u03C9 is a steady current loop and also has charge: a constant electrostatic field (p), a magnetic field (q) and a magnetic dipole moment (s). (b) \u2192 (p), (q), (s).
(c) A constant current in a ring: magnetic field (q) and magnetic moment (s). Nothing changes with time, so no induced field. (c) \u2192 (q), (s).
(d) A current i\u2080 cos \u03C9t: its magnetic field changes with time, so an induced electric field exists (r), along with the magnetic field (q) and moment (s). (d) \u2192 (q), (r), (s).`},{pn:302,y:"IIT-JEE 2014",tp:"mbs",st:"Circular loop and coil",q:`Comprehension: The figure shows a circular loop of radius a with two long parallel wires (no. 1 and 2) all in the plane of the paper. The distance of each wire from the centre of the loop is d. The loop and the wires are carrying the same current I. The current in the loop is in the counterclockwise direction if seen from above.
When d \u2248 a but wires are not touching the loop, it is found that the net magnetic field on the axis of the loop is zero at a height h above the loop. In that case`,fig:{w:300,h:230,alt:"A circular loop of radius a at the centre between two long parallel wires in the plane of the paper: wire 1 (P at the bottom, Q at the top) on the left and wire 2 (R at the bottom, S at the top) on the right, each at distance d from the centre",d:[["l",70,25,70,205,"d"],["l",230,25,230,205,"d"],["l",150,60,150,200,"d"],["c",150,115,28],["p","M150 87 A28 28 0 0 0 122 115"],["l",150,115,168,102],["t",166,114,"a","s"],["l",72,45,148,45,"b"],["t",110,38,"d","m"],["l",152,45,228,45,"b"],["t",190,38,"d","m"],["t",70,18,"Q","m"],["t",70,222,"P","m"],["t",230,18,"S","m"],["t",230,222,"R","m"],["t",62,120,"wire 1","e"],["t",238,120,"wire 2"]]},o:["current in wire 1 and wire 2 is the direction PQ and RS, respectively and h \u2248 a","current in wire 1 and wire 2 is the direction PQ and SR, respectively and h \u2248 a","current in wire 1 and wire 2 is the direction PQ and SR, respectively and h \u2248 1.2a","current in wire 1 and wire 2 is the direction PQ and RS, respectively and h \u2248 1.2a"],c:2,s:`Take the loop in the plane of the paper, current counterclockwise as seen from above: on the axis the loop field points up, with B_loop = \u03BC\u2080Ia\xB2/(2(a\xB2+h\xB2)^(3/2)).
Each wire is at distance \u221A(d\xB2 + h\xB2) from the point on the axis, and gives a field perpendicular to the line joining them. Its vertical component is \u03BC\u2080I d/(2\u03C0(d\xB2 + h\xB2)), and the horizontal components point along the line of the wires.
If the currents are in the same direction (PQ and RS), the vertical components of the two wires cancel, so nothing can cancel the loop field. So the currents must be opposite (PQ and SR): the vertical components add and point downward, while the horizontal parts cancel.
Zero field: \u03BC\u2080Ia\xB2/(2(a\xB2+h\xB2)^(3/2)) = 2 \xD7 \u03BC\u2080I d/(2\u03C0(d\xB2+h\xB2)). With d \u2248 a: a\xB2/(2(a\xB2+h\xB2)^(3/2)) = a/(\u03C0(a\xB2+h\xB2)).
This gives \u221A(a\xB2+h\xB2) = \u03C0a/2 = 1.57a, so h\xB2 = (2.47 \u2212 1)a\xB2 = 1.47a\xB2 and h \u2248 1.2a.
Opposite currents PQ and SR with h \u2248 1.2a, option (c).`},{pn:303,y:"IIT-JEE 2014",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:`Comprehension: The figure shows a circular loop of radius a with two long parallel wires (no. 1 and 2) all in the plane of the paper. The distance of each wire from the centre of the loop is d. The loop and the wires are carrying the same current I. The current in the loop is in the counterclockwise direction if seen from above.
Consider d >> a, and the loop is rotated about its diameter parallel to the wires by 30\xB0 from the position shown in the figure. If the currents in the wires are in the opposite directions, the torque on the loop at its new position will be (assume that the net field due to the wires is constant over the loop)`,fig:{w:300,h:230,alt:"A circular loop of radius a at the centre between two long parallel wires in the plane of the paper: wire 1 (P at the bottom, Q at the top) on the left and wire 2 (R at the bottom, S at the top) on the right, each at distance d from the centre",d:[["l",70,25,70,205,"d"],["l",230,25,230,205,"d"],["l",150,60,150,200,"d"],["c",150,115,28],["p","M150 87 A28 28 0 0 0 122 115"],["l",150,115,168,102],["t",166,114,"a","s"],["l",72,45,148,45,"b"],["t",110,38,"d","m"],["l",152,45,228,45,"b"],["t",190,38,"d","m"],["t",70,18,"Q","m"],["t",70,222,"P","m"],["t",230,18,"S","m"],["t",230,222,"R","m"],["t",62,120,"wire 1","e"],["t",238,120,"wire 2"]]},o:["\u03BC\u2080I\xB2a\xB2/d","\u03BC\u2080I\xB2a\xB2/(2d)","\u221A3 \u03BC\u2080I\xB2a\xB2/d","\u221A3 \u03BC\u2080I\xB2a\xB2/(2d)"],c:1,s:`With opposite currents, the two wires give fields in the same direction at the loop, each \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0d). So B = 2 \xD7 \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0d) = \u03BC\u2080I/(\u03C0d), perpendicular to the plane of the paper.
The loop has magnetic moment m = I \u03C0 a\xB2, perpendicular to its plane. In the position shown m is parallel (or opposite) to B, so there is no torque.
After turning by 30\xB0 about a diameter parallel to the wires, the angle between m and B is 30\xB0 (or 150\xB0), so sin \u03B8 = 1/2 either way.
\u03C4 = m B sin 30\xB0 = (I \u03C0 a\xB2)(\u03BC\u2080I/(\u03C0 d))(1/2) = \u03BC\u2080I\xB2a\xB2/(2d).
Option (b).`},{pn:304,y:"IIT-JEE 2004",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A ring of radius R having uniformly distributed charge Q is mounted on a rod suspended by two identical strings. The tension in strings in equilibrium is T\u2080. Now a vertical magnetic field is switched on and the ring is rotated at constant angular velocity \u03C9. Find the maximum \u03C9 with which the ring can be rotated if the strings can withstand a maximum tension of 3T\u2080/2.",fig:{w:340,h:230,alt:"A ring of radius R on a horizontal rod MN hanging from the ceiling on two vertical strings a distance D apart; a vertical magnetic field B points upward",d:[["l",60,20,280,20],["l",60,20,62,12],["l",82,20,84,12],["l",104,20,106,12],["l",126,20,128,12],["l",148,20,150,12],["l",170,20,172,12],["l",192,20,194,12],["l",214,20,216,12],["l",236,20,238,12],["l",258,20,260,12],["l",280,20,282,12],["l",100,20,100,130],["l",260,20,260,130],["l",100,130,260,130],["t",92,146,"M","e"],["t",268,146,"N"],["l",102,35,258,35,"b"],["t",180,30,"D","m"],["p","M180 80 A16 50 0 1 0 180 180 A16 50 0 1 0 180 80"],["l",180,130,180,84,"a"],["t",186,110,"R"],["p","M215 112 A18 18 0 0 0 215 150"],["t",232,106,"\u03C9"],["l",110,215,110,185,"a"],["l",150,215,150,185,"a"],["l",210,215,210,185,"a"],["l",250,215,250,185,"a"],["t",268,200,"B"]]},a:"\u03C9_max = T\u2080 D/(Q R\xB2 B)",s:`At the start the two strings share the weight equally, so each has tension T\u2080 and the weight is 2T\u2080.
Rotating the charged ring makes a current I = Q\u03C9/(2\u03C0) and a magnetic moment m = I \u03C0 R\xB2 = Q \u03C9 R\xB2/2, along the rod MN (horizontal).
In the vertical field B, the torque on the ring is \u03C4 = m B = Q \u03C9 R\xB2 B/2, about a horizontal axis perpendicular to the rod. The rod tries to tip: one string pulls more, the other less.
The strings are D apart. The extra tension on one string and the lost tension on the other balance the torque: \u0394T \xD7 D = \u03C4, so \u0394T = \u03C4/D, and the tensions become T\u2080 + \u0394T and T\u2080 \u2212 \u0394T.
The largest allowed tension is 3T\u2080/2, so \u0394T = T\u2080/2: Q \u03C9 R\xB2 B/(2D) = T\u2080/2.
\u03C9_max = T\u2080 D/(Q R\xB2 B).`},{pn:305,y:"IIT-JEE 1998",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:`A uniform, constant magnetic field B is directed at an angle of 45\xB0 to the x-axis in the xy-plane. PQRS is a rigid, square wire frame carrying a steady current I\u2080, with its centre at the origin O. At time t = 0, the frame is at rest in the position as shown in figure, with its sides parallel to the x and y-axis. Each side of the frame is of mass M and length L.
(a) What is the torque \u03C4 about O acting on the frame due to the magnetic field?
(b) Find the angle by which the frame rotates under the action of this torque in a short interval of time \u0394t, and the axis about which rotation occurs. (\u0394t is so short that any variation in the torque during this interval may be neglected). Given: the moment of inertia of the frame about an axis through its centre perpendicular to its plane is (4/3)ML\xB2.`,fig:{w:320,h:250,alt:"Square frame PQRS of side L centred at O with sides parallel to the axes, carrying I\u2080 anticlockwise (arrow from R to S), in a uniform magnetic field B directed at 45\xB0 to the x axis in the xy plane",d:[["l",20,125,300,125,"a"],["l",150,230,150,20,"a"],["t",156,22,"y"],["t",298,141,"x","e"],["r",75,55,150,140],["t",63,52,"S","e"],["t",229,52,"R"],["t",63,198,"P","e"],["t",229,198,"Q"],["t",142,141,"O","e"],["l",190,55,170,55,"a"],["t",180,48,"I\u2080","m"],["l",82,86,110,58,"a"],["l",82,116,110,88,"a"],["l",82,171,110,143,"a"],["l",82,201,110,173,"a"],["l",132,86,160,58,"a"],["l",132,116,160,88,"a"],["l",132,171,160,143,"a"],["l",132,201,160,173,"a"],["l",182,86,210,58,"a"],["l",182,116,210,88,"a"],["l",182,171,210,143,"a"],["l",182,201,210,173,"a"],["l",232,86,260,58,"a"],["l",232,116,260,88,"a"],["l",232,171,260,143,"a"]]},a:"(a) \u03C4 = I\u2080L\xB2B, directed along (\u2212\xEE + \u0135)/\u221A2 (in the plane of the frame, perpendicular to B). (b) \u03B8 = (3I\u2080B/(4M))(\u0394t)\xB2, rotation about the axis through O along (\u2212\xEE + \u0135)/\u221A2 in the plane of the frame.",s:`(a) The frame is a loop with area L\xB2 and current I\u2080 anticlockwise (R to S along the top), so its magnetic moment is m = I\u2080L\xB2, along +z.
Torque \u03C4 = m \xD7 B. With B at 45\xB0 in the xy-plane, B is perpendicular to m, so \u03C4 = I\u2080L\xB2 B in magnitude.
Direction: \u1E91 \xD7 (\xEE + \u0135)/\u221A2 = (\u0135 \u2212 \xEE)/\u221A2, so \u03C4 lies in the plane of the frame along the line through O at 135\xB0 to the x-axis (the line y = \u2212x), perpendicular to B.
(b) For a square frame, the moment of inertia about any axis in its plane through the centre is half of that about the perpendicular axis: I = \xBD \xD7 (4/3)ML\xB2 = (2/3)ML\xB2.
Angular acceleration \u03B1 = \u03C4/I = I\u2080L\xB2B/((2/3)ML\xB2) = 3I\u2080B/(2M). It starts from rest and acts for the short time \u0394t.
Angle \u03B8 = \xBD \u03B1 (\u0394t)\xB2 = (3I\u2080B/(4M)) (\u0394t)\xB2, about the axis through O along the direction (\u0135 \u2212 \xEE)/\u221A2 (the line y = \u2212x in the plane of the frame).`},{pn:306,y:"IIT-JEE 1996",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:`An electron in the ground state of hydrogen atom is revolving in anticlock-wise direction in a circular orbit of radius R.
(i) Obtain an expression for the orbital magnetic dipole moment of the electron.
(ii) The atom is placed in a uniform magnetic induction B such that the plane-normal of the electron-orbit makes an angle of 30\xB0 with the magnetic induction. Find the torque experienced by the orbiting electron.`,fig:{w:300,h:260,alt:"An electron orbit (anticlockwise) of radius R with the plane normal n making 30 degrees with the magnetic induction B",d:[["p","M110 190 A60 24 0 1 0 230 190 A60 24 0 1 0 110 190"],["l",170,190,170,80,"a"],["t",176,92,"n"],["l",170,190,240,60,"a"],["t",248,60,"B"],["p","M170 140 A50 50 0 0 1 188 144"],["t",196,134,"30\xB0","s"],["t",170,232,"R","m"]]},a:"(i) \u03BC = e v R/2 = e h/(4\u03C0 m) (ground state). (ii) \u03C4 = \u03BC B sin 30\xB0 = e h B/(8\u03C0 m)",s:`(i) The orbiting electron is a current loop: I = e/T = e v/(2\u03C0R), and the area is \u03C0R\xB2. So \u03BC = I A = e v R/2.
In the ground state of hydrogen, the angular momentum is mvR = h/(2\u03C0) (Bohr). So vR = h/(2\u03C0m), and \u03BC = (e/2)(h/(2\u03C0m)) = e h/(4\u03C0 m).
(ii) A dipole in a field feels a torque \u03C4 = \u03BC B sin \u03B8, with \u03B8 the angle between the dipole moment (along the orbit normal) and B.
\u03B8 = 30\xB0, so \u03C4 = (e h/(4\u03C0 m)) B sin 30\xB0 = e h B/(8\u03C0 m).
The torque tends to turn the orbit normal towards the field direction.`},{pn:307,y:"IIT-JEE 1991",tp:"mbs",st:"Superposition and symmetry",q:`A wire loop carrying a current I is placed in the x-y plane as shown in figure.
(i) If a particle with charge +Q and mass m is placed at the centre P and given a velocity v along NP (see figure), find its instantaneous acceleration.
(ii) If an external uniform magnetic induction field B is applied find the force and the torque acting on the loop due to this field.`,fig:{w:300,h:230,alt:"A loop made of an arc MN of a circle centred at P with radius a, subtending 120 degrees at P, and the straight chord NM; current I flows down along the arc and up along the chord; a charge +Q at P has velocity v along NP",d:[["p","M125 22 A90 90 0 0 0 125 178"],["l",125,178,125,22,"a"],["l",125,22,170,100,"d"],["l",125,178,170,100,"d"],["c",170,100,3,"F"],["t",176,116,"P"],["t",123,18,"M","e"],["t",123,194,"N","e"],["l",172,98,222,40,"a"],["t",226,36,"v"],["t",196,90,"+Q"],["t",153,66,"a","s"],["p","M161 84 A18 18 0 0 0 161 116"],["t",143,104,"120\xB0","m","s"],["t",66,104,"I","e"],["l",80,90,80,114,"a"]]},a:"(i) acceleration = \u03BC\u2080IQv(3\u221A3 \u2212 \u03C0)/(6\u03C0 m a), perpendicular to v and to the field at P. (ii) net force = 0; torque \u03C4 = m \xD7 B, with m = (I a\xB2/2)(2\u03C0/3 \u2212 \u221A3/2) along +z",s:`(i) The loop is the arc MN (radius a, angle 120\xB0 = 2\u03C0/3 at P) and the chord MN. Find the field at P.
Arc: B\u2081 = \u03BC\u2080I\u03B8/(4\u03C0a) = \u03BC\u2080I(2\u03C0/3)/(4\u03C0a) = \u03BC\u2080I/(6a). The current goes down on the left of P, so B\u2081 points out of the page.
Chord: it is at distance a cos 60\xB0 = a/2 from P and subtends 60\xB0 on each side of the perpendicular, so B\u2082 = (\u03BC\u2080I/(4\u03C0(a/2)))(sin 60\xB0 + sin 60\xB0) = \u221A3 \u03BC\u2080I/(2\u03C0a). Its current goes up, so B\u2082 points into the page.
Net field at P: B = B\u2082 \u2212 B\u2081 = (\u03BC\u2080I/a)(\u221A3/(2\u03C0) \u2212 1/6) = \u03BC\u2080I(3\u221A3 \u2212 \u03C0)/(6\u03C0a), into the page.
The velocity v is in the plane, perpendicular to B, so F = QvB and the acceleration is a = QvB/m = \u03BC\u2080IQv(3\u221A3 \u2212 \u03C0)/(6\u03C0 a m), perpendicular to v (directed up and to the left in the figure).
(ii) In a uniform field the forces on a closed current loop add up to zero: the net force is 0.
The torque is \u03C4 = m \xD7 B, with m = I \xD7 area along +z (current down on the arc, up on the chord is anticlockwise), where the area is the circular segment A = (a\xB2/2)(2\u03C0/3 \u2212 \u221A3/2). So \u03C4 = I A B sin \u03C6 in magnitude, \u03C6 being the angle between the normal (z) and B; for B in the plane of the loop, \u03C4 = I(a\xB2/2)(2\u03C0/3 \u2212 \u221A3/2) B.`},{pn:308,y:"IIT-JEE 1981",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"True or False: No net force acts on a rectangular coil carrying a steady current when suspended freely in a uniform magnetic field.",o:["True","False"],c:0,s:`On a straight segment of wire carrying current I in a uniform field, F = I l \xD7 B.
For a closed loop the sum of the displacement vectors around it is zero: \u03A3 l = 0.
So the total force F = I (\u03A3 l) \xD7 B = 0. Only a torque can act.
The statement is True.`},{pn:309,y:"IIT-JEE 1988",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"In a hydrogen atom, the electron moves in an orbit of radius 0.5 \xC5 making 10\xB9\u2076 revolutions per second. The magnetic moment associated with the orbital motion of the electron is _____.",a:"\u2248 1.26 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3 A m\xB2",s:`The revolving electron is a current loop: I = e f = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 10\xB9\u2076 = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 A.
Area of the orbit = \u03C0 r\xB2 = 3.14 \xD7 (0.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2070)\xB2 = 7.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB9 m\xB2.
Magnetic moment \u03BC = I A = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 7.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB9 \u2248 1.26 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3 A m\xB2.`},{pn:310,y:"IIT-JEE 1987",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A wire of length L metre carrying a current i ampere is bent in the form of a circle. The magnitude of its magnetic moment is _____ in MKS unit.",a:"i L\xB2/(4\u03C0) A m\xB2",s:`One circle is made from the wire, so 2\u03C0r = L and r = L/(2\u03C0).
Magnetic moment = current \xD7 area = i \u03C0 r\xB2.
\u03BC = i \u03C0 L\xB2/(4\u03C0\xB2) = i L\xB2/(4\u03C0).
The unit is A m\xB2 (ampere metre squared).`},{pn:311,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"In a moving coil galvanometer, two moving coils M\u2081 and M\u2082 have the following particulars: R\u2081 = 5 \u03A9, N\u2081 = 15, A\u2081 = 3.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB2, B\u2081 = 0.25 T; R\u2082 = 7 \u03A9, N\u2082 = 21, A\u2082 = 1.8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB2, B\u2082 = 0.50 T. Assuming that torsional constant of the springs are same for both coils, what will be the ratio of voltage sensitivity of M\u2081 and M\u2082?",o:["1 : 2","1 : 1","1 : 4","1 : 3"],c:1,s:`Voltage sensitivity = deflection per unit voltage = NBA/(kR), where k is the torsional constant.
With the same k, the sensitivity is proportional to NBA/R.
Coil 1: 15 \xD7 0.25 \xD7 3.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3/5 = 1.35 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2/5 = 2.7 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3.
Coil 2: 21 \xD7 0.50 \xD7 1.8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3/7 = 1.89 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2/7 = 2.7 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3.
The ratio is 1 : 1, option (b).`},{pn:312,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 23 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:`Consider a moving coil galvanometer (MCG):
A. The torsional constant in moving coil galvanometer has dimensions [ML\xB2T\u207B\xB2]
B. Increasing the current sensitivity may not necessarily increase the voltage sensitivity.
C. If we increase number of turns (N) to its double (2N), then the voltage sensitivity doubles.
D. MCG can be converted into an ammeter by introducing a shunt resistance of large value in parallel with galvanometer.
E. Current sensitivity of MCG depends inversely on number of turns of coil.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["B, D, E only","A, D only","A, B, E only","A, B only"],c:3,s:`A: torsional constant = torque per unit angle, dimension ML\xB2T\u207B\xB2 (angle has no dimension). True.
B: current sensitivity is NBA/k and voltage sensitivity is NBA/(kR). Raising current sensitivity by adding turns also raises R, so the voltage sensitivity need not rise. True.
C: doubling N doubles the coil resistance R too (twice as much wire), so NBA/(kR) stays the same. False.
D: an ammeter needs a small shunt resistance in parallel, not a large one. False.
E: current sensitivity NBA/k is directly proportional to N. False.
Only A and B are correct, option (d).`},{pn:313,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 23 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A galvanometer having a coil of resistance 30 \u03A9 need 20 mA of current for full-scale deflection. If a maximum current of 3 A is to be measured using this galvanometer, the resistance of the shunt to be added to the galvanometer should be 30/X \u03A9, where X is",o:["596","298","447","149"],c:3,s:`To measure a larger current, connect a small resistance S (a shunt) in parallel with the galvanometer.
The galvanometer takes only I_g = 20 mA = 0.02 A; the shunt carries the rest, 3 \u2212 0.02 = 2.98 A.
Both have the same voltage: I_g G = (I \u2212 I_g) S.
S = 0.02 \xD7 30/2.98 = 0.6/2.98 \u03A9.
Write 0.6/2.98 = 30/(2.98/0.02) = 30/149 \u03A9.
So X = 149, which is option (d).`},{pn:314,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A galvanometer of resistance 100 \u03A9 when connected in series with 400 \u03A9 measures a voltage of upto 10 V. The value of resistance required to convert the galvanometer into ammeter to read upto 10 A is x \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \u03A9. The value of x is",o:["800","20","200","2"],c:1,s:`First find the full-scale current of the galvanometer from the voltmeter data: total resistance 100 + 400 = 500 \u03A9 at 10 V.
I_g = 10/500 = 0.02 A = 20 mA.
For the ammeter, a shunt S is in parallel: I_g G = (I \u2212 I_g) S.
S = 0.02 \xD7 100/(10 \u2212 0.02) = 2/9.98 = 0.2004 \u03A9 \u2248 0.20 \u03A9.
0.20 \u03A9 = 20 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \u03A9, so x = 20, option (b).`},{pn:315,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A galvanometer has a coil of resistance 200 \u03A9 with a full scale deflection at 20 \u03BCA. The value of resistance to be added to use it as an ammeter of range (0-20) mA is",o:["0.40 \u03A9","0.10 \u03A9","0.50 \u03A9","0.20 \u03A9"],c:3,s:`An ammeter needs a small resistance (shunt) in parallel with the galvanometer.
Full-scale current I_g = 20 \u03BCA = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 A; range I = 20 mA = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 A.
The shunt carries I \u2212 I_g = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \u2212 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \u2248 1.998 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 A.
S = I_g G/(I \u2212 I_g) = (2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 200)/(1.998 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2) = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3/1.998 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \u2248 0.20 \u03A9.
This is option (d).`},{pn:316,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A galvanometer has resistance of 50 \u03A9 and it allows maximum current of 5 mA. It can be converted into voltmeter to measure upto 100 V by connecting in series a resistor of resistance",o:["20050 \u03A9","19500 \u03A9","5975 \u03A9","19950 \u03A9"],c:3,s:`A voltmeter needs a large resistance R in series so that the full-scale current I_g flows at the full range V.
V = I_g (G + R), so R = V/I_g \u2212 G.
R = 100/(5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3) \u2212 50 = 20000 \u2212 50.
R = 19950 \u03A9, option (d).`},{pn:317,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"In an ammeter, 5% of the main current passes through the galvanometer. If resistance of the galvanometer is G, the resistance of ammeter will be (a) 199G (b) 200G (c) G/200 (d) G/199",s:`Note: the book key is (*), meaning none of the options is correct. With 5% as printed the working gives G/20, which is not among the options.
The galvanometer (G) and the shunt (S) are in parallel, so they have the same voltage: I_g G = (I \u2212 I_g) S.
For a fraction f of the current in the galvanometer, S = fG/(1 \u2212 f).
The ammeter resistance is the parallel combination: R_A = GS/(G + S) = fG.
For f = 0.05 as printed: R_A = G/20, which is not listed (it would be G/200 only for 0.5%).`,a:"None of the options is correct (the book key is *); with 5% the result is G/20"},{pn:318,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 27 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A current of 200 \u03BCA deflects the coil of a moving coil galvanometer through 60\xB0. The current to cause deflection through \u03C0/10 radian is",o:["120 \u03BCA","30 \u03BCA","180 \u03BCA","60 \u03BCA"],c:3,s:`In a moving coil galvanometer the deflection is proportional to the current (NIAB = k\u03B8).
Convert the angle: \u03C0/10 rad = 180\xB0/10 = 18\xB0.
I/200 \u03BCA = 18\xB0/60\xB0, so I = 200 \xD7 0.3 \u03BCA.
I = 60 \u03BCA, option (d).`},{pn:319,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"The deflection in moving coil galvanometer falls from 25 divisions to 5 division when a shunt of 24 \u03A9 is applied. The resistance of galvanometer coil will be",o:["96 \u03A9","12 \u03A9","100 \u03A9","48 \u03A9"],c:0,s:`Deflection is proportional to the current through the coil.
With the shunt, the fraction of the total current through the galvanometer is S/(G + S).
This fraction equals the ratio of deflections: 5/25 = 1/5.
24/(G + 24) = 1/5, so G + 24 = 120.
G = 96 \u03A9, option (a).`},{pn:320,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 29 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A galvanometer having coil resistance 10 \u03A9 shows a full scale deflection for a current of 3 mA. For it to measure a current of 8 A, the value of the shunt should be",o:["4.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \u03A9","3.75 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \u03A9","2.75 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \u03A9","3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \u03A9"],c:1,s:`Use a shunt S in parallel: I_g G = (I \u2212 I_g) S.
I_g = 3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 A, G = 10 \u03A9, I = 8 A.
S = (3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 10)/(8 \u2212 0.003) = 0.03/7.997.
S = 3.75 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \u03A9, option (b).`},{pn:321,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A student is provided with a variable voltage source V, a test resistor R_T = 10 \u03A9, two identical galvanometers G\u2081 and G\u2082 and two additional resistors, R\u2081 = 10 M\u03A9 and R\u2082 = 0.001 \u03A9. For conducting an experiment to verify ohm's law, the most suitable circuit is",o:["(a)","(b)","(c)","(d)"],c:0,s:`To verify Ohm's law we need a voltmeter across R_T and an ammeter in series with it.
A galvanometer becomes a voltmeter with a very large resistance in series: G\u2081 with R\u2081 = 10 M\u03A9, connected across R_T.
A galvanometer becomes an ammeter with a very small resistance in parallel (shunt): G\u2082 with R\u2082 = 0.001 \u03A9, connected in the main line in series with R_T.
Circuit (a) has exactly this: G\u2081 in series with R\u2081 across R_T, and G\u2082 shunted by R\u2082 in the main line.
In (b), (c), (d) the large resistor is in parallel with a galvanometer or the small one is in series, so they do not make a voltmeter or an ammeter.
The answer is (a).`,fig:{w:600,h:340,alt:"Four circuits with a variable source V, test resistor R_T and two galvanometers: (a) G1 in series with R1 across R_T and G2 shunted by R2 in the main line; (b) G1 shunted by R1 across R_T and G2 in series with R2; (c) G1 shunted by R2 across R_T and G2 in series with R1; (d) G1 shunted by R2 across R_T and G2 shunted by R1",d:[["t",14,75,"(a)","m"],["l",40,70,60,70],["p","M60 70 l3 -5 l6 10 l6 -10 l6 10 l6 -10 l3 5"],["l",90,70,130,70],["l",130,70,270,70],["l",40,70,40,130],["l",270,70,270,130],["l",40,130,142,130],["l",142,122,142,138],["l",148,126,148,134,"B"],["l",148,130,270,130],["t",145,148,"V","m"],["t",75,88,"R_T","m"],["c",40,70,2,"F"],["c",130,70,2,"F"],["l",40,70,40,20],["l",130,20,130,70],["l",40,20,55,20],["c",64,20,9],["t",64,23,"G\u2081","ms"],["l",73,20,85,20],["p","M85 20 l3 -5 l6 10 l6 -10 l6 10 l6 -10 l3 5"],["l",115,20,130,20],["t",100,36,"R\u2081","m"],["l",150,70,160,70],["l",160,70,178,70],["c",187,70,9],["t",187,73,"G\u2082","ms"],["l",196,70,235,70],["l",160,70,160,92],["l",235,70,235,92],["l",160,92,180,92],["p","M180 92 l3 -5 l6 10 l6 -10 l6 10 l6 -10 l3 5"],["l",210,92,235,92],["t",195,108,"R\u2082","ms"],["t",314,75,"(b)","m"],["l",340,70,360,70],["p","M360 70 l3 -5 l6 10 l6 -10 l6 10 l6 -10 l3 5"],["l",390,70,430,70],["l",430,70,570,70],["l",340,70,340,130],["l",570,70,570,130],["l",340,130,442,130],["l",442,122,442,138],["l",448,126,448,134,"B"],["l",448,130,570,130],["t",445,148,"V","m"],["t",375,88,"R_T","m"],["c",340,70,2,"F"],["c",430,70,2,"F"],["l",340,70,340,20],["l",430,20,430,70],["l",340,20,350,20],["l",350,20,368,20],["c",377,20,9],["t",377,23,"G\u2081","ms"],["l",386,20,420,20],["l",420,20,430,20],["l",350,20,350,44],["l",420,20,420,44],["l",350,44,370,44],["p","M370 44 l3 -5 l6 10 l6 -10 l6 10 l6 -10 l3 5"],["l",400,44,420,44],["t",385,60,"R\u2081","ms"],["l",450,70,460,70],["c",469,70,9],["t",469,73,"G\u2082","ms"],["l",478,70,505,70],["p","M505 70 l3 -5 l6 10 l6 -10 l6 10 l6 -10 l3 5"],["t",520,88,"R\u2082","m"],["t",14,245,"(c)","m"],["l",40,240,60,240],["p","M60 240 l3 -5 l6 10 l6 -10 l6 10 l6 -10 l3 5"],["l",90,240,130,240],["l",130,240,270,240],["l",40,240,40,300],["l",270,240,270,300],["l",40,300,142,300],["l",142,292,142,308],["l",148,296,148,304,"B"],["l",148,300,270,300],["t",145,318,"V","m"],["t",75,258,"R_T","m"],["c",40,240,2,"F"],["c",130,240,2,"F"],["l",40,240,40,190],["l",130,190,130,240],["l",40,190,50,190],["l",50,190,68,190],["c",77,190,9],["t",77,193,"G\u2081","ms"],["l",86,190,120,190],["l",120,190,130,190],["l",50,190,50,214],["l",120,190,120,214],["l",50,214,70,214],["p","M70 214 l3 -5 l6 10 l6 -10 l6 10 l6 -10 l3 5"],["l",100,214,120,214],["t",85,230,"R\u2082","ms"],["l",150,240,160,240],["c",169,240,9],["t",169,243,"G\u2082","ms"],["l",178,240,205,240],["p","M205 240 l3 -5 l6 10 l6 -10 l6 10 l6 -10 l3 5"],["t",220,258,"R\u2081","m"],["t",314,245,"(d)","m"],["l",340,240,360,240],["p","M360 240 l3 -5 l6 10 l6 -10 l6 10 l6 -10 l3 5"],["l",390,240,430,240],["l",430,240,570,240],["l",340,240,340,300],["l",570,240,570,300],["l",340,300,442,300],["l",442,292,442,308],["l",448,296,448,304,"B"],["l",448,300,570,300],["t",445,318,"V","m"],["t",375,258,"R_T","m"],["c",340,240,2,"F"],["c",430,240,2,"F"],["l",340,240,340,190],["l",430,190,430,240],["l",340,190,350,190],["l",350,190,368,190],["c",377,190,9],["t",377,193,"G\u2081","ms"],["l",386,190,420,190],["l",420,190,430,190],["l",350,190,350,214],["l",420,190,420,214],["l",350,214,370,214],["p","M370 214 l3 -5 l6 10 l6 -10 l6 10 l6 -10 l3 5"],["l",400,214,420,214],["t",385,230,"R\u2082","ms"],["l",450,240,460,240],["l",460,240,478,240],["c",487,240,9],["t",487,243,"G\u2082","ms"],["l",496,240,535,240],["l",460,240,460,262],["l",535,240,535,262],["l",460,262,480,262],["p","M480 262 l3 -5 l6 10 l6 -10 l6 10 l6 -10 l3 5"],["l",510,262,535,262],["t",495,278,"R\u2081","ms"]]}},{pn:322,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"The current sensitivity of moving coil galvanometer is increased by 25%. This increase is achieved only by changing in the number of turns of coils and area of cross section of the wire while keeping the resistance of galvanometer coil constant. The percentage change in the voltage sensitivity will be",o:["+25%","\u221250%","zero","\u221225%"],c:0,s:`Current sensitivity is \u03B8/I = NAB/k.
Voltage sensitivity is \u03B8/V = (\u03B8/I)/R, because V = IR for the coil.
The coil resistance R is kept constant, so voltage sensitivity changes in the same ratio as current sensitivity.
So it also increases by 25%, option (a).`},{pn:323,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 15 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:`For designing a voltmeter of range 50 V and an ammeter of range 10 mA using a galvanometer which has a coil of resistance 54 \u03A9 showing a full scale deflection for 1 mA as in figure.
(A) For voltmeter R \u2248 50 k\u03A9
(B) For ammeter r \u2248 0.2 \u03A9
(C) For ammeter r \u2248 6 \u03A9
(D) For voltmeter R \u2248 5 k\u03A9
(E) For voltmeter R \u2248 500 \u03A9
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["(C) and (E)","(C) and (D)","(A) and (B)","(A) and (C)"],c:3,s:`Voltmeter: a large resistance R in series. R = V/I_g \u2212 G = 50/(1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3) \u2212 54 = 49 946 \u03A9 \u2248 50 k\u03A9, so (A) is true.
Ammeter: a small resistance r in parallel. The shunt carries 10 \u2212 1 = 9 mA at the same voltage as the galvanometer.
r = I_g G/(I \u2212 I_g) = 1 \xD7 54/9 = 6 \u03A9, so (C) is true.
(B), (D) and (E) do not match these values.
The correct pair is (A) and (C), option (d).`,fig:{w:260,h:255,alt:"Top: a galvanometer of 54 ohm carrying 1 mA in series with a resistance R across a 50 V source. Bottom: the same galvanometer with a resistance r in parallel with it, current entering and leaving",d:[["l",20,40,50,40,"a"],["t",25,30,"1 mA","s"],["c",65,40,9],["t",65,43,"G","ms"],["t",66,58,"54 \u03A9","ms"],["l",74,40,100,40],["p","M100 40 l3 -5 l6 10 l6 -10 l6 10 l6 -10 l3 5"],["t",115,58,"R","m"],["l",130,40,200,40],["l",200,40,200,100],["l",20,40,20,100],["l",20,100,95,100],["l",95,92,95,108],["l",101,96,101,104,"B"],["l",101,100,200,100],["t",100,120,"50 V","m"],["l",20,170,50,170,"a"],["t",25,160,"1 mA","s"],["c",65,170,9],["t",65,173,"G","ms"],["t",66,188,"54 \u03A9","ms"],["l",74,170,235,170,"a"],["l",20,170,20,225],["l",20,225,95,225],["p","M95 225 l3 -5 l6 10 l6 -10 l6 10 l6 -10 l3 5"],["l",125,225,200,225],["l",200,170,200,225],["t",110,243,"r","m"],["t",185,243,"50 V","m"]]}},{pn:324,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"For a moving coil galvanometer, the deflection in the coil is 0.05 rad when a current of 10 mA is passed through it. If the torsional constant of suspension wire is 4.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N m rad\u207B\xB9, the magnetic field is 0.01 T and the number of turns in the coil is 200, the area of each turn (in cm\xB2) is",o:["2.0","1.0","1.5","0.5"],c:1,s:`In balance, the magnetic torque NIAB equals the restoring torque k\u03B8.
A = k\u03B8/(NIB).
A = (4.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 0.05)/(200 \xD7 10 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 0.01).
Numerator = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076; denominator = 200 \xD7 0.01 \xD7 0.01 = 0.02.
A = 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2 = 1.0 cm\xB2, option (b).`},{pn:325,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 31 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmat",st:"Magnetic materials and Gauss\u2019s law",q:"Which of the following correctly represents the variation of electric potential (V) of a charged spherical conductor of radius (R) with radial distance (r) from the center?",o:["Graph (a): V rises linearly from 0 up to r = R, then falls as 1/r","Graph (b): V falls steadily from r = 0 onwards","Graph (c): V is 0 up to r = R, then falls as 1/r","Graph (d): V is constant up to r = R, then falls as 1/r"],c:3,s:`Note: this question is about electrostatics, not magnetism, but it is printed in this chapter\u2019s set, so it is included.
Inside a charged conductor the electric field is zero, so the potential does not change: V is constant (equal to its surface value) for r \u2264 R.
Outside, the sphere acts like a point charge Q at its centre, so V = kQ/r, which falls as 1/r.
At r = R the two parts meet without a jump, V = kQ/R.
So the graph is flat up to R and then decreases like 1/r. This is graph (d).`,fig:{w:400,h:270,alt:"Four graphs of potential V against r: (a) rises linearly to R then falls; (b) falls smoothly from the axis; (c) zero up to R, then jumps and falls; (d) constant up to R, then falls",d:[["l",45,110,45,25,"a"],["l",45,110,175,110,"a"],["t",39,30,"V","e"],["t",181,114,"r"],["t",37,124,"0","e"],["t",13,70,"(a)","m"],["l",45,110,100,50],["l",100,50,100,110,"d"],["t",100,124,"R","m"],["p","M100 50 C114 80 140 102 170 106"],["l",235,110,235,25,"a"],["l",235,110,365,110,"a"],["t",229,30,"V","e"],["t",371,114,"r"],["t",227,124,"0","e"],["t",203,70,"(b)","m"],["p","M235 40 C255 80 285 102 360 106"],["t",310,124,"R","m"],["l",45,240,45,155,"a"],["l",45,240,175,240,"a"],["t",39,160,"V","e"],["t",181,244,"r"],["t",37,254,"0","e"],["t",13,200,"(c)","m"],["l",100,240,100,180],["p","M100 180 C114 210 140 232 170 236"],["t",100,254,"R","m"],["l",235,240,235,155,"a"],["l",235,240,365,240,"a"],["t",229,160,"V","e"],["t",371,244,"r"],["t",227,254,"0","e"],["t",203,200,"(d)","m"],["l",235,180,290,180],["l",290,180,290,240,"d"],["p","M290 180 C304 210 330 232 360 236"],["t",290,254,"R","m"]]}},{pn:326,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:`The current sensitivity of a galvanometer can be increased by
(A) decreasing the number of turns
(B) increasing the magnetic field
(C) decreasing the area of the coil
(D) decreasing the torsional constant of the spring
Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["(B) and (C) only.","(C) and (D) only.","(A) and (C) only.","(B) and (D) only."],c:3,s:`Current sensitivity is \u03B8/I = NAB/k.
It increases with more turns N, larger area A, stronger field B and smaller torsional constant k.
So (B) increasing B helps and (D) decreasing k helps.
(A) fewer turns and (C) smaller area would reduce it.
The answer is (B) and (D) only, option (d).`},{pn:327,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A teacher in his physics laboratory allotted an experiment to determine the resistance (G) of a galvanometer. Students took the observations for 1/3 deflection in the galvanometer. Which of the below is true for measuring value of G?",o:["1/3 deflection method cannot be used for determining the resistance of the galvanometer.","1/3 deflection method can be used and in this case, the G equals to twice the value of shunt resistance(s).","1/3 deflection method can be used and in this case, the G equals to three times the value of shunt resistance(s)","1/3 deflection method can be used and in this case the G value equals to the shunt resistance(s)."],c:1,s:`In the deflection method, a shunt S is put across the galvanometer and the deflection is read before and after; the current through the galvanometer is cut to a known fraction.
Deflection is proportional to the galvanometer current. If the deflection becomes 1/3, the current through G becomes 1/3 of the total.
The current divides as I_G/I = S/(G + S) = 1/3.
So G + S = 3S, giving G = 2S.
G is twice the shunt resistance, option (b); the method works for any known fraction, not only 1/2.`},{pn:328,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"If n represents the actual number of deflection in a converted galvanometer of resistance G and shunt resistance S. Then the total current I when its figure of merit is K will be",o:["KS/(S + G)","(G + S)/(nKS)","nKS/(G + S)","nK(G + S)/S"],c:3,s:`Figure of merit K is the current per division, so the current through the galvanometer is I_g = nK.
The galvanometer and the shunt are in parallel: I_g G = (I \u2212 I_g) S.
So the total current is I = I_g + I_g G/S = I_g (S + G)/S.
I = nK(G + S)/S, option (d).`},{pn:329,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A 72 \u03A9 galvanometer is shunted by a resistance of 8 \u03A9. The percentage of the total current which passes through the galvanometer is",o:["0.1%","10%","25%","0.25%"],c:1,s:`Parallel branches share current in inverse ratio of resistance.
Fraction through the galvanometer = S/(G + S) = 8/(72 + 8) = 8/80.
8/80 = 0.1 = 10%.
The answer is option (b).`},{pn:330,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"For full scale deflection of total 50 divisions, 50 mV voltage is required in galvanometer. The resistance of galvanometer if its current sensitivity is 2 div/mA will be",o:["1 \u03A9","2 \u03A9","5 \u03A9","4 \u03A9"],c:1,s:`Current sensitivity 2 div/mA means 2 divisions for each mA.
Full scale 50 divisions needs I_g = 50/2 = 25 mA.
The voltage across the coil at full scale is 50 mV.
G = V/I_g = 50 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3/25 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 = 2 \u03A9.
This is option (b).`},{pn:331,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 5 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A galvanometer of resistance G is converted into a voltmeter of range 0-1 V by connecting a resistance R\u2081 in series with it. The additional resistance that should be connected in series with R\u2081, to increase the range of the voltmeter to 0-2 V will be",o:["G","R\u2081","R\u2081 \u2212 G","R\u2081 + G"],c:3,s:`For the first voltmeter, full-scale current I_g flows at 1 V: I_g (G + R\u2081) = 1.
For range 2 V the same current must flow, so the total resistance must double: G + R\u2081 + R\u2032 = 2(G + R\u2081).
R\u2032 = G + R\u2081.
This is option (d).`},{pn:332,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 5 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A galvanometer is used in laboratory for detecting the null point in electrical experiments. If, on passing a current of 6 mA it produces a deflection of 2\xB0, its figure of merit is close to",o:["333\xB0 A/div","6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 A/div","666\xB0 A/div","3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 A/div"],c:3,s:`Figure of merit is the current needed for one division (here one degree of deflection).
K = current/deflection = 6 mA/2 = 3 mA per division.
K = 3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 A/div, option (d).`},{pn:333,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 8 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A galvanometer having a coil resistance 100 \u03A9 gives a full scale deflection when a current of 1 mA is passed through it. What is the value of the resistance which can convert this galvanometer into a voltmeter giving full scale deflection for a potential difference of 10 V?",o:["8.9 k\u03A9","10 k\u03A9","9.9 k\u03A9","7.9 k\u03A9"],c:2,s:`Connect a resistance R in series so that 1 mA flows at 10 V.
Total resistance needed = V/I_g = 10/(1 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3) = 10 000 \u03A9.
R = 10 000 \u2212 100 = 9900 \u03A9.
R = 9.9 k\u03A9, option (c).`},{pn:334,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A moving coil galvanometer has resistance 50 \u03A9 and it indicates full deflection at 4 mA current. A voltmeter is made using this galvanometer and a 5 k\u03A9 resistance. The maximum voltage, that can be measured using this voltmeter, will be close to",o:["10 V","20 V","15 V","40 V"],c:1,s:`The 5 k\u03A9 resistance is connected in series, so the total resistance is 50 + 5000 = 5050 \u03A9.
The largest current is the full-scale current 4 mA.
V = I_g \xD7 R_total = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 5050 = 20.2 V.
This is close to 20 V, option (b).`},{pn:335,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"The resistance of a galvanometer is 50 ohm and the maximum current which can be passed through it is 0.002 A. What resistance must be connected to it in order to convert it into an ammeter of range 0 \u2013 0.5 A?",o:["0.002 ohm","0.02 ohm","0.2 ohm","0.5 ohm"],c:2,s:`A shunt S is connected in parallel with the galvanometer.
Shunt current = 0.5 \u2212 0.002 = 0.498 A.
Same voltage across both: 0.002 \xD7 50 = 0.498 \xD7 S.
S = 0.1/0.498 = 0.2008 \u2248 0.2 \u03A9.
This is option (c).`},{pn:336,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A moving coil galvanometer has a coil with 175 turns and area 1 cm\xB2. It uses a torsion band of torsion constant 10\u207B\u2076 N m/rad. The coil is placed in a magnetic field B parallel to its plane. The coil deflects by 1\xB0 for a current of 1 mA. The value of B (in Tesla) is approximately",o:["10\u207B\xB3","10\u207B\xB9","10\u207B\u2074","10\u207B\xB2"],c:0,s:`The field is parallel to the coil plane (radial field), so the torque is NIAB, balanced by k\u03B8.
B = k\u03B8/(NIA).
\u03B8 = 1\xB0 = \u03C0/180 = 0.01745 rad; A = 1 cm\xB2 = 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2; I = 10\u207B\xB3 A.
B = (10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 0.01745)/(175 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074) = 1.745 \xD7 10\u207B\u2078/1.75 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075.
B \u2248 10\u207B\xB3 T, option (a).`},{pn:337,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A moving coil galvanometer allows a full scale current of 10\u207B\u2074 A. A series resistance of 2 M\u03A9 is required to convert the above galvanometer into a voltmeter of range 0 - 5 V. Therefore the value of shunt resistance required to convert the above galvanometer into an ammeter of range 0-10 mA is",o:["10 \u03A9","100 \u03A9","500 \u03A9","200 \u03A9"],c:2,s:`Note: the printed data are inconsistent. A voltmeter of range 5 V with full-scale current 10\u207B\u2074 A needs a total resistance of only 5/10\u207B\u2074 = 50 k\u03A9, so a 2 M\u03A9 series resistor is impossible. The figure that fits the options is a series resistance of 2 k\u03A9; the working below uses that value and gives (c).
Voltmeter: I_g(G + R) = V, so G + R = 5/10\u207B\u2074 = 50 000 \u03A9.
With R = 2000 \u03A9, the galvanometer resistance is G = 50 000 \u2212 2000 = 48 000 \u03A9.
Ammeter: the shunt S carries the rest of the current, so I_g G = (I \u2212 I_g)S, which gives S = I_g G/(I \u2212 I_g).
S = (10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 48 000)/(10 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \u2212 10\u207B\u2074) = 4.8/(9.9 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3) \u2248 485 \u03A9.
The nearest option is 500 \u03A9, option (c).`},{pn:338,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",fig:{w:340,h:400,alt:"Four circuits, each a galvanometer G in series with three resistors R1, R2, R3 with output terminals taken after each resistor, labelled with voltage ranges. (a) 2 V, 10 V, 20 V with 2000, 8000, 10000 ohm; (b) 20 V, 10 V, 2 V with 19900, 9900, 1900 ohm; (c) 2 V, 10 V, 20 V with 1900, 9900, 19900 ohm; (d) 2 V, 10 V, 20 V with 1900, 8000, 10000 ohm",d:[["t",2,18,"(a)"],["c",40,40,10],["t",40,44,"G","m"],["l",30,40,14,40],["l",14,40,14,74],["c",14,74,2,"F"],["l",50,40,50,40],["p","M50 40 L54 40 L56.7 35 L62 45 L67.3 35 L72.7 45 L78 35 L83.3 45 L86 40 L90 40"],["p","M90 40 L94 40 L96.7 35 L102 45 L107.3 35 L112.7 45 L118 35 L123.3 45 L126 40 L130 40"],["p","M130 40 L134 40 L136.7 35 L142 45 L147.3 35 L152.7 45 L158 35 L163.3 45 L166 40 L170 40"],["t",70,30,"R\u2081","m"],["t",110,30,"R\u2082","m"],["t",150,30,"R\u2083","m"],["l",90,40,90,74],["c",90,74,2,"F"],["t",90,88,"2 V","ms"],["l",130,40,130,74],["c",130,74,2,"F"],["t",130,88,"10 V","ms"],["l",170,40,170,74],["c",170,74,2,"F"],["t",170,88,"20 V","ms"],["t",205,36,"R\u2081 = 2000 \u03A9","s"],["t",205,52,"R\u2082 = 8000 \u03A9","s"],["t",205,68,"R\u2083 = 10000 \u03A9","s"],["t",2,110,"(b)"],["c",40,132,10],["t",40,136,"G","m"],["l",30,132,14,132],["l",14,132,14,166],["c",14,166,2,"F"],["l",50,132,50,132],["p","M50 132 L54 132 L56.7 127 L62 137 L67.3 127 L72.7 137 L78 127 L83.3 137 L86 132 L90 132"],["p","M90 132 L94 132 L96.7 127 L102 137 L107.3 127 L112.7 137 L118 127 L123.3 137 L126 132 L130 132"],["p","M130 132 L134 132 L136.7 127 L142 137 L147.3 127 L152.7 137 L158 127 L163.3 137 L166 132 L170 132"],["t",70,122,"R\u2081","m"],["t",110,122,"R\u2082","m"],["t",150,122,"R\u2083","m"],["l",90,132,90,166],["c",90,166,2,"F"],["t",90,180,"20 V","ms"],["l",130,132,130,166],["c",130,166,2,"F"],["t",130,180,"10 V","ms"],["l",170,132,170,166],["c",170,166,2,"F"],["t",170,180,"2 V","ms"],["t",205,128,"R\u2081 = 19900 \u03A9","s"],["t",205,144,"R\u2082 = 9900 \u03A9","s"],["t",205,160,"R\u2083 = 1900 \u03A9","s"],["t",2,202,"(c)"],["c",40,224,10],["t",40,228,"G","m"],["l",30,224,14,224],["l",14,224,14,258],["c",14,258,2,"F"],["l",50,224,50,224],["p","M50 224 L54 224 L56.7 219 L62 229 L67.3 219 L72.7 229 L78 219 L83.3 229 L86 224 L90 224"],["p","M90 224 L94 224 L96.7 219 L102 229 L107.3 219 L112.7 229 L118 219 L123.3 229 L126 224 L130 224"],["p","M130 224 L134 224 L136.7 219 L142 229 L147.3 219 L152.7 229 L158 219 L163.3 229 L166 224 L170 224"],["t",70,214,"R\u2081","m"],["t",110,214,"R\u2082","m"],["t",150,214,"R\u2083","m"],["l",90,224,90,258],["c",90,258,2,"F"],["t",90,272,"2 V","ms"],["l",130,224,130,258],["c",130,258,2,"F"],["t",130,272,"10 V","ms"],["l",170,224,170,258],["c",170,258,2,"F"],["t",170,272,"20 V","ms"],["t",205,220,"R\u2081 = 1900 \u03A9","s"],["t",205,236,"R\u2082 = 9900 \u03A9","s"],["t",205,252,"R\u2083 = 19900 \u03A9","s"],["t",2,294,"(d)"],["c",40,316,10],["t",40,320,"G","m"],["l",30,316,14,316],["l",14,316,14,350],["c",14,350,2,"F"],["l",50,316,50,316],["p","M50 316 L54 316 L56.7 311 L62 321 L67.3 311 L72.7 321 L78 311 L83.3 321 L86 316 L90 316"],["p","M90 316 L94 316 L96.7 311 L102 321 L107.3 311 L112.7 321 L118 311 L123.3 321 L126 316 L130 316"],["p","M130 316 L134 316 L136.7 311 L142 321 L147.3 311 L152.7 321 L158 311 L163.3 321 L166 316 L170 316"],["t",70,306,"R\u2081","m"],["t",110,306,"R\u2082","m"],["t",150,306,"R\u2083","m"],["l",90,316,90,350],["c",90,350,2,"F"],["t",90,364,"2 V","ms"],["l",130,316,130,350],["c",130,350,2,"F"],["t",130,364,"10 V","ms"],["l",170,316,170,350],["c",170,350,2,"F"],["t",170,364,"20 V","ms"],["t",205,312,"R\u2081 = 1900 \u03A9","s"],["t",205,328,"R\u2082 = 8000 \u03A9","s"],["t",205,344,"R\u2083 = 10000 \u03A9","s"]]},q:"A galvanometer of resistance 100 \u03A9 has 50 divisions on its scale and has sensitivity of 20 \u03BCA/division. It is to be converted to a voltmeter with three ranges, of 0-2 V, 0-10 V and 0-20 V. The appropriate circuit to do so is",o:["Circuit (a)","Circuit (b)","Circuit (c)","Circuit (d)"],c:3,s:`Full-scale current of the galvanometer: I_g = 50 divisions \xD7 20 \u03BCA/division = 1 mA.
For a range V the total resistance in series must be V/I_g, including the galvanometer's 100 \u03A9.
2 V range: total 2/10\u207B\xB3 = 2000 \u03A9, so R\u2081 = 2000 \u2212 100 = 1900 \u03A9.
10 V range: total 10 000 \u03A9, so R\u2081 + R\u2082 = 9900 \u03A9, giving R\u2082 = 9900 \u2212 1900 = 8000 \u03A9.
20 V range: total 20 000 \u03A9, so R\u2081 + R\u2082 + R\u2083 = 19 900 \u03A9, giving R\u2083 = 19 900 \u2212 9900 = 10 000 \u03A9.
The 2 V terminal is after R\u2081, the 10 V terminal after R\u2082 and the 20 V terminal after R\u2083. This is circuit (d): R\u2081 = 1900 \u03A9, R\u2082 = 8000 \u03A9, R\u2083 = 10 000 \u03A9.`},{pn:339,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A moving coil galvanometer, having a resistance G, produces full scale deflection when a current I_g flows through it. This galvanometer can be converted into (i) an ammeter of range 0 to I\u2080 (I\u2080 > I_g) by connecting a shunt resistance R_A to it and (ii) into a voltmeter of range 0 to V (V = GI\u2080) by connecting a series resistance R_V to it. Then,",o:["R_A R_V = G\xB2 (I_g)/(I\u2080 \u2212 I_g) and (R_A)/(R_V) = ((I\u2080 \u2212 I_g)/(I_g))\xB2","R_A R_V = G\xB2 (I\u2080 \u2212 I_g)/(I_g) and (R_A)/(R_V) = ((I_g)/(I\u2080 \u2212 I_g))\xB2","R_A R_V = G\xB2 and (R_A)/(R_V) = (I_g)/(I\u2080 \u2212 I_g)","R_A R_V = G\xB2 and (R_A)/(R_V) = ((I_g)/(I\u2080 \u2212 I_g))\xB2"],c:3,s:`Ammeter: the shunt carries I\u2080 \u2212 I_g with the same voltage as the galvanometer, so I_g G = (I\u2080 \u2212 I_g)R_A, giving R_A = I_g G/(I\u2080 \u2212 I_g).
Voltmeter: the total resistance is V/I_g = GI\u2080/I_g, and it includes G. So R_V = GI\u2080/I_g \u2212 G = G(I\u2080 \u2212 I_g)/I_g.
Multiply: R_A R_V = [I_g G/(I\u2080 \u2212 I_g)] \xD7 [G(I\u2080 \u2212 I_g)/I_g] = G\xB2.
Divide: R_A/R_V = [I_g G/(I\u2080 \u2212 I_g)] \xD7 [I_g/(G(I\u2080 \u2212 I_g))] = (I_g/(I\u2080 \u2212 I_g))\xB2.
So R_A R_V = G\xB2 and R_A/R_V = (I_g/(I\u2080 \u2212 I_g))\xB2, which is option (d).`},{pn:340,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A galvanometer having a resistance of 20 \u03A9 and 30 divisions on both sides has figure of merit 0.005 ampere/division. The resistance that should be connected in series such that it can be used as a voltmeter upto 15 volt, is",o:["80 \u03A9","125 \u03A9","120 \u03A9","100 \u03A9"],c:0,s:`Figure of merit = current per division, so full-scale current I_g = 30 divisions \xD7 0.005 A/div = 0.15 A.
For a voltmeter of range V the total resistance must be V/I_g = 15/0.15 = 100 \u03A9.
This total includes the galvanometer's own 20 \u03A9, so the series resistance is R = 100 \u2212 20 = 80 \u03A9.
This is option (a).`},{pn:341,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",fig:{w:300,h:190,alt:"A battery with key K1 drives current through R1 = 220 ohm; then a galvanometer G is in parallel with a branch containing key K2 and a variable resistance R2",d:[["l",30,60,30,150],["l",30,60,50,60],["p","M50 60 L54 60 L57.5 55 L64.5 65 L71.5 55 L78.5 65 L85.5 55 L92.5 65 L96 60 L100 60"],["l",100,60,120,60],["l",120,24,120,100],["l",120,24,142,24],["c",142,24,2.5],["c",156,24,2.5],["l",158.5,24,168,24],["p","M168 24 L172 24 L175 19 L181 29 L187 19 L193 29 L199 19 L205 29 L208 24 L212 24"],["l",212,24,240,24],["l",240,24,240,100],["l",240,60,270,60],["l",270,60,270,150],["l",120,100,170,100],["c",180,100,10],["t",180,104,"G","m"],["l",190,100,240,100],["l",30,150,96,150],["l",96,141,96,159],["l",104,145,104,155,"B"],["l",104,150,190,150],["c",192,150,2.5],["c",206,150,2.5],["l",208.5,150,270,150],["t",75,46,"R\u2081 = 220 \u03A9","m"],["t",149,14,"K\u2082","m"],["t",190,14,"R\u2082","m"],["t",199,172,"K\u2081","m"]]},q:"The galvanometer deflection, when key K\u2081 is closed but K\u2082 is open, equals \u03B8\u2080 (see figure). On closing K\u2082 also and adjusting R\u2082 to 5 \u03A9, the deflection in galvanometer becomes (\u03B8\u2080)/(5). The resistance of the galvanometer is, then, given by [Neglect the internal resistance of battery]",o:["25 \u03A9","22 \u03A9","5 \u03A9","12 \u03A9"],c:1,s:`The deflection is proportional to the current in the galvanometer. Let E be the battery emf, R\u2081 = 220 \u03A9, R\u2082 = 5 \u03A9 and G the galvanometer resistance.
K\u2082 open: only G is in the circuit with R\u2081, so I_g = E/(220 + G).
K\u2082 closed: R\u2082 is in parallel with G. The total current is E/(220 + 5G/(5 + G)), and G gets the part R\u2082/(R\u2082 + G) of it.
So I_g\u2032 = [E/(220 + 5G/(5 + G))] \xD7 5/(5 + G) = 5E/(220(5 + G) + 5G).
The deflection becomes one-fifth: I_g\u2032 = I_g/5, so 5/(220(5 + G) + 5G) = 1/(5(220 + G)).
25(220 + G) = 220(5 + G) + 5G, so 5500 + 25G = 1100 + 225G.
4400 = 200G, so G = 22 \u03A9, which is option (b).`},{pn:342,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A galvanometer, whose resistance is 50 ohm, has 25 divisions in it. When a current of 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 A passes through it, its needle (pointer) deflects by one division. To use this galvanometer as a voltmeter of range 2.5 V, it should be connected to a resistance of",o:["200 ohm","6250 ohm","6200 ohm","250 ohm"],c:0,s:`Full-scale current: I_g = 25 divisions \xD7 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 A per division = 10\u207B\xB2 A = 10 mA.
Total resistance needed for a 2.5 V range: V/I_g = 2.5/10\u207B\xB2 = 250 \u03A9.
The galvanometer already has 50 \u03A9, so the series resistance is 250 \u2212 50 = 200 \u03A9.
This is option (a).`},{pn:343,y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A galvanometer with its coil resistance 25 \u03A9 requires a current of 1 mA for its full deflection. In order to construct an ammeter to read up to a current of 2 A, the approximate value of the shunt resistance should be",o:["1.25 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \u03A9","1.25 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \u03A9","2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 \u03A9","2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \u03A9"],c:1,s:`A shunt S is connected in parallel with the galvanometer. It carries the extra current I \u2212 I_g and has the same voltage as the coil: I_g G = (I \u2212 I_g)S.
S = I_g G/(I \u2212 I_g) = (10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 25)/(2 \u2212 10\u207B\xB3) = 0.025/1.999.
S \u2248 1.25 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \u03A9.
This is option (b).`},{pn:344,y:"JEE Main 2017",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"When a current of 5 mA is passed through a galvanometer having a coil of resistance 15 \u03A9, it shows full scale deflection. The value of the resistance to be put in series with the galvanometer to convert it into a voltmeter of range 0-10 V is",o:["1.985 \xD7 10\xB3 \u03A9","2.045 \xD7 10\xB3 \u03A9","2.535 \xD7 10\xB3 \u03A9","4.005 \xD7 10\xB3 \u03A9"],c:0,s:`For a voltmeter of range V the whole resistance (galvanometer plus series resistor) must be V/I_g.
V/I_g = 10/(5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3) = 2000 \u03A9.
The galvanometer already has 15 \u03A9, so R = 2000 \u2212 15 = 1985 \u03A9 = 1.985 \xD7 10\xB3 \u03A9.
This is option (a).`},{pn:345,y:"JEE Main 2016",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A galvanometer having a coil resistance of 100 \u03A9 gives a full scale deflection, when a current of 1 mA is passed through it. The value of the resistance, which can convert this galvanometer into ammeter giving a full scale deflection for a current of 10 A, is",o:["0.01 \u03A9","2 \u03A9","0.1 \u03A9","3 \u03A9"],c:0,s:`The ammeter needs a shunt S in parallel with the coil, so that I_g G = (I \u2212 I_g)S.
S = I_g G/(I \u2212 I_g) = (10\u207B\xB3 \xD7 100)/(10 \u2212 10\u207B\xB3) = 0.1/9.999.
S \u2248 0.01 \u03A9.
This is option (a).`},{pn:346,y:"JEE Main 2016 \xB7 Online",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"To know the resistance G of a galvanometer by half deflection method, a battery of emf V_E and resistance R is used to deflect the galvanometer by angle \u03B8. If a shunt of resistance S is needed to get half deflection then G, R and S are related by the equation",o:["S(R + G) = RG","2S(R + G) = RG","2G = S","2S = G"],c:0,s:`Without the shunt, the current through the galvanometer is I = V_E/(R + G), giving deflection \u03B8.
With the shunt S across G, the parallel part has resistance GS/(G + S), and G gets the fraction S/(G + S) of the total current.
I\u2032 = [V_E/(R + GS/(G + S))] \xD7 S/(G + S) = V_E S/(R(G + S) + GS).
Half deflection means I\u2032 = I/2: 2S/(R(G + S) + GS) = 1/(R + G).
2S(R + G) = RG + RS + GS, so 2SR + 2SG \u2212 RS \u2212 GS = RG.
This gives SR + SG = RG, that is S(R + G) = RG, option (a).`},{pn:347,y:"JEE Main 2016 \xB7 Online",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A 50 \u03A9 resistance is connected to a battery of 5 V. A galvanometer of resistance 100 \u03A9 is to be used as an ammeter to measure current through the resistance, for this a resistance r_s is connected to the galvanometer. Which of the following connections should be employed if the measured current is within 1% of the current without the ammeter in the circuit?",o:["r_s = 0.5 \u03A9 in series with the galvanometer","r_s = 1 \u03A9 in series with galvanometer","r_s = 1 \u03A9 in parallel with the galvanometer","r_s = 0.5 \u03A9 in parallel with the galvanometer"],c:3,s:`Putting the ammeter in the circuit adds its resistance r_A to the 50 \u03A9. The current changes by less than 1% only if r_A is below 1% of 50 \u03A9, that is r_A < 0.5 \u03A9.
A series resistor would make the ammeter resistance larger than 100 \u03A9. So r_s must be a shunt in parallel with the galvanometer. Options (a) and (b) are out.
Shunt 1 \u03A9: r_A = (100 \xD7 1)/(101) \u2248 0.99 \u03A9, which is more than 0.5 \u03A9. The current would drop by about 2%. Option (c) is out.
Shunt 0.5 \u03A9: r_A = (100 \xD7 0.5)/(100.5) \u2248 0.4975 \u03A9, which is below 0.5 \u03A9. The total resistance is 50.4975 \u03A9, so the change is about 0.99%, within 1%.
So the connection is r_s = 0.5 \u03A9 in parallel with the galvanometer, option (d).`},{pn:348,y:"JEE Main 2016 \xB7 Online",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",fig:{w:230,h:150,alt:"A series circuit of a galvanometer G, a resistance R, a 2 V battery and a key",d:[["l",30,30,60,30],["c",70,30,10],["t",70,34,"G","m"],["l",80,30,120,30],["p","M120 30 L124 30 L127.5 25 L134.5 35 L141.5 25 L148.5 35 L155.5 25 L162.5 35 L166 30 L170 30"],["l",170,30,200,30],["l",200,30,200,110],["l",30,30,30,110],["l",30,110,66,110],["l",66,101,66,119],["l",74,105,74,115,"B"],["l",74,110,128,110],["c",130,110,2.5],["c",144,110,2.5],["l",146.5,110,200,110],["t",145,20,"R","m"],["t",70,132,"2 V","m"]]},q:"A galvanometer has a 50 division scale. Battery has no internal resistance. It is found that there is deflection of 40 divisions when R = 2400 \u03A9. Deflection becomes 20 divisions when resistance taken from resistance box is 4900 \u03A9. Then we can conclude",o:["current sensitivity of galvanometer is 20 \u03BCA/division","resistance of galvanometer is 200 \u03A9","resistance required on R.B. for a deflection of 10 divisions is 9800 \u03A9","full scale deflection current is 2 mA"],c:0,s:`The galvanometer G is in series with the box R and the 2 V cell, so the current is I = 2/(R + G) and the deflection is proportional to I.
Deflections 40 and 20 are in the ratio 2 : 1, so the currents are too: (4900 + G)/(2400 + G) = 2.
4900 + G = 4800 + 2G, so G = 100 \u03A9. Statement (b), 200 \u03A9, is wrong.
At 40 divisions: I = 2/(2400 + 100) = 0.8 mA. Current per division = 0.8 mA/40 = 20 \u03BCA/division. Statement (a) is true.
Full scale (50 divisions) = 50 \xD7 20 \u03BCA = 1 mA, not 2 mA, so (d) is wrong.
For 10 divisions I = 200 \u03BCA, so R + G = 2/(2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074) = 10 000 \u03A9 and R = 9900 \u03A9, not 9800 \u03A9, so (c) is wrong.
The correct statement is (a).`},{pn:349,y:"JEE Main 2013 \xB7 Online",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",fig:{w:300,h:200,alt:"A battery and key in a loop with R1 in series; then R2 in parallel with a branch containing the galvanometer G and R3 in series",d:[["l",30,30,82,30],["l",82,21,82,39],["l",90,25,90,35,"B"],["l",90,30,196,30],["l",100,30,150,30,"a"],["t",118,22,"I","m"],["c",196,30,2.5],["l",198,28,218,14],["c",224,30,2.5],["l",226.5,30,260,30],["l",260,30,260,100],["l",30,30,30,100],["l",30,100,70,100],["r",70,94,34,12],["l",104,100,130,100],["l",130,100,182,100],["r",182,94,34,12],["l",216,100,260,100],["t",87,84,"R\u2081","m"],["t",199,84,"R\u2082","m"],["l",130,100,130,160],["l",130,160,150,160],["c",160,160,10],["t",160,164,"G","m"],["l",170,160,200,160],["r",200,154,34,12],["l",234,160,260,160],["l",260,100,260,160],["c",130,100,2,"F"],["c",260,100,2,"F"],["t",217,182,"R\u2083","m"]]},q:"To find the resistance of a galvanometer by the half deflection method, the following circuit is used with resistances R\u2081 = 9970 \u03A9, R\u2082 = 30 \u03A9 and R\u2083 = 0. The deflection in the galvanometer is d. With R\u2083 = 107 \u03A9, the deflection changed to (d)/(2). The galvanometer resistance is approximately:",o:["107 \u03A9","137 \u03A9","(107)/(2) \u03A9","77 \u03A9"],c:3,s:`In the figure R\u2081 is in series with the cell. After it, R\u2082 (30 \u03A9) is in parallel with the branch G + R\u2083. The deflection is proportional to the current in G.
Let E be the emf. With R\u2083 = 0, the branch has resistance G, so I_g = E R\u2082/(R\u2081(R\u2082 + G) + R\u2082G).
With R\u2083 = 107 \u03A9 the branch has G + 107: I_g\u2032 = E R\u2082/(R\u2081(R\u2082 + G + 107) + R\u2082(G + 107)).
Half the deflection means I_g\u2032 = I_g/2, so the denominator doubles: R\u2081(R\u2082 + G + 107) + R\u2082(G + 107) = 2[R\u2081(R\u2082 + G) + R\u2082G].
Simplify: 107(R\u2081 + R\u2082) = R\u2081(R\u2082 + G) + R\u2082G.
107 \xD7 10 000 = 9970 \xD7 (30 + G) + 30G, so 1 070 000 = 299 100 + 10 000G.
G = 770 900/10 000 \u2248 77 \u03A9, option (d).`},{pn:350,y:"JEE Main 2014 \xB7 Online",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",fig:{w:340,h:300,alt:"Four circuits A to D, each with a cell and key K1 in the bottom wire. A: S in series, G on the main wire with R and key K2 in a branch across G. B: R, G, S in series with K2 shorting S. C: S, G, R in series with K2 shorting R. D: R in series, G on the main wire with S and key K2 in a branch across G",d:[["t",18,70,"(A)","e"],["l",24,50,24,116],["l",154,50,154,116],["l",24,116,60,116],["l",60,107,60,125],["l",68,111,68,121,"B"],["l",68,116,112,116],["c",114,116,2.5],["c",128,116,2.5],["l",130.5,116,154,116],["t",121,134,"K\u2081","m"],["l",24,50,32,50],["p","M32 50 L36 50 L38.3 45 L43 55 L47.7 45 L52.3 55 L57 45 L61.7 55 L64 50 L68 50"],["l",68,50,112,50],["c",120,50,10],["t",120,54,"G","m"],["l",130,50,154,50],["l",84,50,84,28],["p","M84 28 L88 28 L90.3 23 L95 33 L99.7 23 L104.3 33 L109 23 L113.7 33 L116 28 L120 28"],["l",120,28,128,28],["c",130,28,2.5],["l",142,28,142,50],["t",102,18,"R","m"],["t",136,18,"K\u2082","m"],["t",50,40,"S","m"],["l",132.5,28,142,28],["l",112,50,110,50],["t",188,70,"(B)","e"],["l",194,50,194,116],["l",324,50,324,116],["l",194,116,230,116],["l",230,107,230,125],["l",238,111,238,121,"B"],["l",238,116,282,116],["c",284,116,2.5],["c",298,116,2.5],["l",300.5,116,324,116],["t",291,134,"K\u2081","m"],["l",194,50,202,50],["p","M202 50 L206 50 L208.3 45 L213 55 L217.7 45 L222.3 55 L227 45 L231.7 55 L234 50 L238 50"],["c",250,50,10],["t",250,54,"G","m"],["l",260,50,270,50],["p","M270 50 L274 50 L276.3 45 L281 55 L285.7 45 L290.3 55 L295 45 L299.7 55 L302 50 L306 50"],["l",306,50,324,50],["l",264,50,264,28],["l",264,28,284,28],["c",286,28,2.5],["l",288.5,28,310,28],["l",310,28,310,50],["t",220,40,"R","m"],["t",288,70,"S","m"],["t",286,18,"K\u2082","m"],["t",18,220,"(C)","e"],["l",24,200,24,266],["l",154,200,154,266],["l",24,266,60,266],["l",60,257,60,275],["l",68,261,68,271,"B"],["l",68,266,112,266],["c",114,266,2.5],["c",128,266,2.5],["l",130.5,266,154,266],["t",121,284,"K\u2081","m"],["l",24,200,32,200],["p","M32 200 L36 200 L38.3 195 L43 205 L47.7 195 L52.3 205 L57 195 L61.7 205 L64 200 L68 200"],["c",80,200,10],["t",80,204,"G","m"],["l",90,200,100,200],["p","M100 200 L104 200 L106.3 195 L111 205 L115.7 195 L120.3 205 L125 195 L129.7 205 L132 200 L136 200"],["l",136,200,154,200],["l",90,200,90,178],["l",90,178,114,178],["c",116,178,2.5],["l",118.5,178,140,178],["l",140,178,140,200],["t",50,190,"S","m"],["t",118,220,"R","m"],["t",116,168,"K\u2082","m"],["t",188,220,"(D)","e"],["l",194,200,194,266],["l",324,200,324,266],["l",194,266,230,266],["l",230,257,230,275],["l",238,261,238,271,"B"],["l",238,266,282,266],["c",284,266,2.5],["c",298,266,2.5],["l",300.5,266,324,266],["t",291,284,"K\u2081","m"],["l",194,200,202,200],["p","M202 200 L206 200 L208.3 195 L213 205 L217.7 195 L222.3 205 L227 195 L231.7 205 L234 200 L238 200"],["l",238,200,278,200],["c",288,200,10],["t",288,204,"G","m"],["l",298,200,324,200],["l",254,200,254,178],["p","M254 178 L258 178 L259.8 173 L263.5 183 L267.2 173 L270.8 183 L274.5 173 L278.2 183 L280 178 L284 178"],["l",284,178,290,178],["c",292,178,2.5],["l",294.5,178,312,178],["l",312,178,312,200],["t",220,190,"R","m"],["t",269,168,"S","m"],["t",294,168,"K\u2082","m"]]},q:"In the circuit diagrams (A, B, C and D) shown below, R is a high resistance and S is a resistance of the order of galvanometer resistance G. The correct circuit, corresponding to the half deflection method for finding the resistance and figure of merit of the galvanometer, is the circuit labelled as:",o:["Circuit A with G = (RS)/(R \u2212 S)","Circuit B with G = S","Circuit C with G = S","Circuit D with G = (RS)/(R \u2212 S)"],c:3,s:`In the half-deflection method a high resistance R is put in series with the galvanometer G and the cell, which fixes the current at a deflection \u03B8.
Then a key K\u2082 connects a shunt S in parallel with G. Closing K\u2082 and adjusting S until the deflection is \u03B8/2 gives G from S and R.
Only circuit D has this: R in series, G on the main wire, and S with K\u2082 in a branch across G. In A the resistor across G is R, not S; in B and C the key K\u2082 only shorts a series resistor.
For circuit D the condition for half deflection is S(R + G) = RG (the same as the usual shunt relation).
So G(R \u2212 S) = RS, giving G = RS/(R \u2212 S).
This is circuit D with G = RS/(R \u2212 S), option (d).`},{pn:351,y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A moving coil galvanometer has 150 equal divisions. Its current sensitivity is 10 divisions per milliampere and voltage sensitivity is 2 divisions per millivolt. In order that each division reads 1 volt, the resistance in ohms needed to be connected in series with the coil will be",o:["99995","9995","10\xB3","10\u2075"],c:1,s:`Full-scale current: 150 divisions \xF7 10 div/mA = 15 mA.
Full-scale voltage across the coil: 150 divisions \xF7 2 div/mV = 75 mV.
Coil resistance: G = 75 mV/15 mA = 5 \u03A9.
Each division should read 1 V, so the full scale of 150 divisions should read 150 V. Total resistance needed = 150/(15 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3) = 10 000 \u03A9.
Series resistance = 10 000 \u2212 5 = 9995 \u03A9, option (b).`},{pn:352,y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"An ammeter reads upto 1 ampere. Its internal resistance is 0.81 ohm. To increase the range to 10 A the value of the required shunt is",o:["0.03 \u03A9","0.3 \u03A9","0.9 \u03A9","0.09 \u03A9"],c:3,s:`The ammeter's full scale current is I_g = 1 A and its resistance is G = 0.81 \u03A9.
To measure 10 A the shunt S must carry 10 \u2212 1 = 9 A, with the same voltage as the ammeter: I_g G = (I \u2212 I_g)S.
S = (1 \xD7 0.81)/9 = 0.09 \u03A9.
This is option (d).`},{pn:353,y:"AIEEE 2002",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"If an ammeter is to be used in place of a voltmeter, then we must connect with the ammeter a",o:["low resistance in parallel","high resistance in parallel","high resistance in series","low resistance in series."],c:2,s:`A voltmeter reads the voltage across two points, so it must have a very high resistance and draw almost no current.
An ammeter has a very low resistance. To make it behave like a voltmeter, we must add a large resistance in series with it, so that its total resistance is large and the current through the coil stays within its full scale value.
So the answer is a high resistance in series, option (c).`},{pn:354,y:"JEE Advanced 2019",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"Two identical moving coil galvanometers have 10 \u03A9 resistance and full scale deflection at 2 \u03BCA current. One of them is converted into a voltmeter of 100 mV full scale reading and the other into an ammeter of 1 mA full scale current using appropriate resistors. These are then used to measure the voltage and current in the Ohm's law experiment with R = 1000 \u03A9 resistor by using an ideal cell. Which of the following statement(s) is/are correct?",o:["The resistance of the Voltmeter will be 100 k\u03A9","The resistance of the Ammeter will be 0.02 \u03A9 (round off to 2nd decimal place)","If ideal cell is replaced by a cell having internal resistance of 5 \u03A9 then the measured value of R will be more than 1000 \u03A9","The measured value of R will be 978 \u03A9 < R < 982 \u03A9"],c:[1,3],s:`Voltmeter: its total resistance is V/I_g = 100 mV/2 \u03BCA = 50 k\u03A9 (a series resistor of 49 990 \u03A9 added to the 10 \u03A9). So (a), which says 100 k\u03A9, is false.
Ammeter: the shunt is S = I_g G/(I \u2212 I_g) = (2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 10)/(10\u207B\xB3 \u2212 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076) \u2248 0.0200 \u03A9. The ammeter resistance is G in parallel with S \u2248 0.02 \u03A9, so (b) is true.
The usual set-up is assumed (the question gives no figure): the voltmeter is across R, and the ammeter reads the whole current, including what flows through the voltmeter.
Then the measured value is V/I = R in parallel with R_V = (1000 \xD7 50 000)/(51 000) \u2248 980.4 \u03A9. This lies between 978 \u03A9 and 982 \u03A9, so (d) is true.
The measured value is below 1000 \u03A9. A cell with internal resistance changes the current but not the ratio V/I, so (c) is false.
Correct statements: (b) and (d).`},{pn:355,y:"JEE Advanced 2016",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"Consider two identical galvanometers and two identical resistors with resistance R. If the internal resistance of the galvanometers R_C < R/2, which of the following statement(s) about any one of the galvanometers is(are) true?",o:["The maximum voltage range is obtained when all the components are connected in series","The maximum voltage range is obtained when the two resistors and one galvanometer are connected in series, and the second galvanometer is connected in parallel to the first galvanometer","The maximum current range is obtained when all the components are connected in parallel","The maximum current range is obtained when the two galvanometers are connected in series and the combination is connected in parallel with both the resistors"],c:[1,2],s:`Note: the printed key says a,c; working gives b,c.
Let I_g be the full-scale current of one galvanometer. Voltage range = (largest current that keeps each galvanometer within I_g) \xD7 (total resistance).
(a) All four in series: current I_g, resistance 2R + 2R_C, range I_g(2R + 2R_C).
(b) Two resistors in series with the pair of galvanometers in parallel: the two equal galvanometers share the current, so the total current is 2I_g, and the resistance is 2R + R_C/2. Range = 2I_g(2R + R_C/2) = I_g(4R + R_C).
Compare: 4R + R_C > 2R + 2R_C because 2R > R_C (true, since R_C < R/2). So (b) gives the larger range, and (a) is false.
Current range: (c) all four in parallel: the voltage across all is I_g R_C. Each resistor takes I_g R_C/R. Total current = 2I_g + 2I_g R_C/R = I_g(2 + 2x) with x = R_C/R.
(d) The galvanometers in series (resistance 2R_C, carrying I_g) in parallel with the two resistors: voltage 2I_g R_C, each resistor takes 2I_g R_C/R, total = I_g(1 + 4x).
(c) minus (d) = I_g(1 \u2212 2x) > 0 because x < 1/2. So (c) is larger and (d) is false.
Correct statements: (b) and (c).`},{pn:356,y:"IIT-JEE 1991",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A microammeter has a resistance of 100 \u03A9 and a full scale range of 50 \u03BCA. It can be used as a voltmeter or as a higher range ammeter provided a resistance is added to it. Pick the correct range and resistance combination(s)",o:["50 V range with 10 k\u03A9 resistance in series","10 V range with 200 k\u03A9 resistance in series","5 mA range with 1 \u03A9 resistance in parallel","10 mA range with 1 \u03A9 resistance in parallel."],c:[1,2],s:`Voltmeter with series resistance R: range V = I_g(G + R), with I_g = 50 \u03BCA and G = 100 \u03A9.
(a) R = 10 k\u03A9: V = 50 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 10 100 \u2248 0.5 V, not 50 V. False.
(b) R = 200 k\u03A9: V = 50 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 200 100 \u2248 10 V. True.
Ammeter with shunt S in parallel: range I = I_g(1 + G/S).
(c) S = 1 \u03A9: I = 50 \u03BCA \xD7 (1 + 100) = 5.05 mA \u2248 5 mA. True.
(d) The same 1 \u03A9 shunt gives about 5 mA, not 10 mA. False.
Correct combinations: (b) and (c).`},{pn:357,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 5 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",fig:{w:290,h:190,alt:"Graph of 1/\u03B8 (per division) against R (ohm): a straight line starting at 1/2 on the vertical axis and passing through 1 at R = 2, 1.5 at R = 4 and 2 at R = 6, with dashed guide lines",d:[["l",50,160,50,16,"a"],["l",50,150,240,150,"a"],["t",50,12,"1/\u03B8 (div\u207B\xB9)","m"],["t",246,154,"R (\u03A9)"],["t",44,154,"0","e"],["l",50,125,170,50,"B"],["c",50,125,3,"F"],["c",90,100,3,"F"],["c",130,75,3,"F"],["c",170,50,3,"F"],["l",50,100,90,100,"d"],["l",50,75,130,75,"d"],["l",50,50,170,50,"d"],["l",90,100,90,150,"d"],["l",130,75,130,150,"d"],["l",170,50,170,150,"d"],["t",44,129,"1/2","e"],["t",44,104,"1","e"],["t",44,79,"1\xBD","e"],["t",44,54,"2","e"],["t",90,166,"2","m"],["t",130,166,"4","m"],["t",170,166,"6","m"]]},q:"In the experiment to determine the galvanometer resistance by half-deflection method, the plot of 1/\u03B8 vs the resistance (R) of the resistance box is shown in the figure. The figure of merit of the galvanometer is ______ \xD7 10\u207B\xB9 A/division. [The source has emf 2 V]",n:5,s:`In the half-deflection set-up the galvanometer is in series with the resistance box R and the source E. The current is I = E/(R + G).
If k is the figure of merit (current per division), then I = k\u03B8, so \u03B8 = E/(k(R + G)) and 1/\u03B8 = (k/E)R + kG/E.
This is a straight line: slope = k/E and intercept = kG/E.
From the graph: the line passes through (R = 2, 1) and (R = 4, 1.5), so the slope is (1.5 \u2212 1)/(4 \u2212 2) = 0.25 div\u207B\xB9 per \u03A9 (the intercept is 1/2 at R = 0).
k = slope \xD7 E = 0.25 \xD7 2 = 0.5 A/division = 5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9 A/division.
So the missing number is 5.
Note: the intercept 1/2 = kG/E = 0.5G/2 would give the galvanometer resistance G = 2 \u03A9, a useful check but not needed here.`},{pn:358,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 6 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",fig:{w:300,h:150,alt:"A 24 V cell, a resistor of 140.4 ohm and an ammeter A in series",d:[["l",30,30,100,30],["p","M100 30 L104 30 L109.2 25 L119.5 35 L129.8 25 L140.2 35 L150.5 25 L160.8 35 L166 30 L170 30"],["l",170,30,260,30],["l",260,30,260,110],["l",30,30,30,110],["l",30,110,76,110],["l",76,101,76,119],["l",84,105,84,115,"B"],["l",84,110,190,110],["c",200,110,10],["t",200,114,"A","m"],["l",210,110,260,110],["t",135,18,"140.4 \u03A9","m"],["t",80,132,"24 V","m"]]},q:"In the given figure an ammeter A consists of a 240 \u03A9 coil connected in parallel to a 10 \u03A9 shunt. The reading of the ammeter is ______ mA.",n:160,s:`The ammeter is made of the 240 \u03A9 coil in parallel with the 10 \u03A9 shunt, so its resistance is (240 \xD7 10)/(240 + 10) = 9.6 \u03A9.
It is in series with the 140.4 \u03A9 resistor and the 24 V cell. Total resistance = 140.4 + 9.6 = 150 \u03A9.
Current in the circuit: I = 24/150 = 0.16 A.
The ammeter is designed to read the total current that enters it (the shunt takes most of it and the coil a small fixed part, but the scale is marked for the total).
Reading = 0.16 A = 160 mA.`},{pn:359,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A moving coil galvanometer has 100 turns and each turn has an area of 2.0 cm\xB2. The magnetic field produced by the magnet is 0.01 T and the deflection in the coil is 0.05 radian when a current of 10 mA is passed through it. The torsional constant of the suspension wire is x \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N-m/rad. The value of x is ______ .",n:4,s:`In a moving coil galvanometer the magnetic torque on the coil, NIAB, is balanced by the restoring torque of the wire, k\u03B8.
So k = NIAB/\u03B8, where N = 100, I = 10 mA = 10\u207B\xB2 A, A = 2.0 cm\xB2 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2, B = 0.01 T and \u03B8 = 0.05 rad.
NIAB = 100 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \xD7 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 0.01 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 N\xB7m.
k = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076/0.05 = 4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N\xB7m/rad.
So x = 4.`},{pn:360,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 30 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"Two resistance of 100 \u03A9 and 200 \u03A9 are connected in series with a battery of 4 V and negligible internal resistance. A voltmeter is used to measure voltage across 100 \u03A9 resistance, which gives reading as 1 V. The resistance of voltmeter must be ______ \u03A9.",n:200,s:`Let the voltmeter have resistance R_V. It is in parallel with the 100 \u03A9 resistor, so that part has resistance R_p = 100R_V/(100 + R_V).
The 4 V battery drives current through R_p and the 200 \u03A9 in series, so the voltage across R_p is 4 \xD7 R_p/(R_p + 200).
The reading is 1 V: 4R_p = R_p + 200, so R_p = 200/3 \u03A9 \u2248 66.7 \u03A9.
100R_V/(100 + R_V) = 200/3, so 300R_V = 200(100 + R_V) = 20 000 + 200R_V.
100R_V = 20 000, so R_V = 200 \u03A9.
Check: without the voltmeter the 100 \u03A9 would read 4/3 V; the voltmeter pulls the reading down to 1 V.`},{pn:361,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 13 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"When a resistance of 5 \u03A9 is shunted with a moving coil galvanometer, it shows a full scale deflection for a current of 250 mA, however when 1050 \u03A9 resistance is connected with it in series, it gives full scale deflection for 25 volt. The resistance of galvanometer is ______ \u03A9.",n:50,s:`Let the galvanometer have resistance G and full-scale current I_g.
With the 5 \u03A9 shunt, the galvanometer and shunt have the same voltage: I_g G = (0.25 \u2212 I_g) \xD7 5.
With 1050 \u03A9 in series, I_g (G + 1050) = 25, so I_g = 25/(G + 1050).
Put this in the first equation: 25G/(G + 1050) = 5(0.25 \u2212 25/(G + 1050)).
Multiply by (G + 1050): 25G = 1.25(G + 1050) \u2212 125.
25G = 1.25G + 1312.5 \u2212 125, so 23.75 G = 1187.5.
G = 50 \u03A9.`},{pn:362,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A galvanometer coil has 500 turns and each turn has an average area 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2. If a torque of 1.5 N m is required to keep this coil parallel to a magnetic field when a current of 0.5 A is flowing through it, the strength of the field (in T) is ______.",n:20,s:`Torque on a coil: \u03C4 = N I A B sin \u03B1, where \u03B1 is the angle between the coil's axis and the field.
The coil is parallel to the field, so its plane is along B and its axis is at 90\xB0 to B: sin \u03B1 = 1, the largest torque.
1.5 = 500 \xD7 0.5 \xD7 3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 B = 0.075 B.
B = 1.5/0.075 = 20 T.`},{pn:363,y:"JEE Advanced 2018",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A moving coil galvanometer has 50 turns and each turn has an area 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 m\xB2. The magnetic field produced by the magnet inside the galvanometer is 0.02 T. The torsional constant of the suspension wire is 10\u207B\u2074 N m rad\u207B\xB9. When a current flows through the galvanometer, a full scale deflection occurs if the coil rotates by 0.2 rad. The resistance of the coil of the galvanometer is 50 \u03A9. This galvanometer is to be converted into an ammeter capable of measuring current in the range 0 - 1.0 A. For this purpose, a shunt resistance is to be added in parallel to the galvanometer. The value of this shunt resistance, in ohms, is ______.",n:5.56,s:`Full-scale current from the balance of torques: N I_g A B = k \u03C6.
I_g = k\u03C6/(NAB) = (10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 0.2)/(50 \xD7 2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 0.02) = (2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075)/(2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074) = 0.1 A.
For the ammeter, the total current is 1.0 A. The shunt carries 1.0 \u2212 0.1 = 0.9 A.
Shunt and galvanometer have the same voltage: I_g G = (1.0 \u2212 I_g) S.
0.1 \xD7 50 = 0.9 S, so S = 5/0.9 = 5.56 \u03A9.`},{pn:364,y:"JEE Advanced 2014",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:"A galvanometer gives full scale deflection with 0.006 A current. By connecting it to a 4990 \u03A9 resistance, it can be converted into a voltmeter of range 0 - 30 V. If connected to a (2n/249) \u03A9 resistance, it becomes an ammeter of range 0 - 1.5 A. Find the value of n.",a:"n = 5",s:`Note: the key is not printed for this question; working gives n = 5.
As a voltmeter: I_g (G + 4990) = 30, so G + 4990 = 30/0.006 = 5000 and G = 10 \u03A9.
As an ammeter with shunt S = 2n/249 \u03A9: I_g G = (1.5 \u2212 I_g) S.
0.006 \xD7 10 = 1.494 \xD7 S, so S = 0.06/1.494 = 10/249 \u03A9.
Compare with S = 2n/249: 2n = 10, so n = 5.`},{pn:365,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: If the number of turns in the coil of a moving coil galvanometer is doubled then the current sensitivity becomes double.
Statement II: Increasing current sensitivity of a moving coil galvanometer by only increasing the number of turns in the coil will also increase its voltage sensitivity in the same ratio.
In the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Statement I is true but Statement II is false","Both Statement I and Statement II are false","Both Statement I and Statement II are true","Statement I is false but Statement II is true."],c:0,s:`Current sensitivity = deflection per unit current = NAB/k. It is proportional to N, so doubling N doubles it. Statement I is true.
Voltage sensitivity = deflection per unit voltage = NAB/(kR), where R is the coil resistance.
More turns means more wire, so R also grows in proportion to N. Then N/R stays about the same.
So voltage sensitivity does not increase in the same ratio. Statement II is false.
So option (a).`},{pn:366,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 1 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:`Given below are two statements: One is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.
Assertion A: For measuring the potential difference across a resistance of 600 \u03A9, the voltmeter with resistance 1000 \u03A9 will be preferred over voltmeter with resistance 4000 \u03A9.
Reason R: Voltmeter with higher resistance will draw smaller current than voltmeter with lower resistance.
In the light of above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below`,o:["Both A and R are correct and R is not the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are correct but R is the correct explanation of A.","A is incorrect but R is correct.","A is correct but R is incorrect."],c:2,s:`A voltmeter is connected in parallel with the resistance. It should draw as little current as possible so that it does not disturb the circuit.
A voltmeter of high resistance draws less current, so Reason R is correct.
The 4000 \u03A9 voltmeter in parallel with 600 \u03A9 changes the combination much less (about 522 \u03A9) than the 1000 \u03A9 one (375 \u03A9). So the 4000 \u03A9 voltmeter is preferred.
So Assertion A, which prefers the 1000 \u03A9 voltmeter, is incorrect.
A is incorrect and R is correct, option (c).`},{pn:367,y:"JEE Main 2013",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:`This question has Statement-I and Statement-II. Of the four choices given after the Statements, choose the one that best describes the two Statements.
Statement-I: Higher the range, greater is the resistance of ammeter.
Statement-II: To increase the range of ammeter, additional shunt needs to be used across it.`,o:["Statement-I is false, Statement-II is true.","Statement-I is true, Statement-II is true, Statement-II is the correct explanation of Statement-I.","Statement-I is true, Statement-II is true, Statement-II is not the correct explanation of Statement-I.","Statement-I is true, Statement-II is false."],c:0,s:`An ammeter is a galvanometer with a small shunt resistance S in parallel. Its resistance is G S/(G + S), which is a little less than S.
To measure a larger current, a smaller shunt is needed, so the resistance of the ammeter becomes smaller. A higher range means a lower resistance. Statement I is false.
To extend the range, a shunt is put across the galvanometer, so Statement II is true.
So option (a).`},{pn:368,y:"IIT-JEE 2005",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:`In a moving coil galvanometer, torque on the coil can be expressed as \u03C4 = k i, where i is current through the wire and k is constant. The rectangular coil of the galvanometer having number of turns N, area A and moment of inertia I is placed in magnetic field B. Find
(a) k in terms of given parameters N, I, A and B
(b) the torsion constant of the spring, if a current i\u2080 produces a deflection of \u03C0/2 in the coil.
(c) the maximum angle through which the coil is deflected, if charge Q is passed through the coil almost instantaneously. (Ignore the damping in mechanical oscillations)`,a:"(a) k = NAB  (b) \u03BA = 2NABi\u2080/\u03C0  (c) \u03B8_max = Q \u221A(\u03C0NAB/(2 I i\u2080))",s:`(a) The torque on a coil of N turns, area A carrying current i in a field B (coil plane parallel to B in the radial field) is \u03C4 = NiAB. Comparing with \u03C4 = k i gives k = NAB.
(b) In steady deflection, the magnetic torque balances the spring torque: NAB i\u2080 = \u03BA \u03C6, with \u03C6 = \u03C0/2.
So \u03BA = 2NABi\u2080/\u03C0.
(c) A charge Q passes almost instantly, so the angular impulse is \u222B\u03C4 dt = NAB \u222Bi dt = NAB Q. It gives the coil an initial angular speed \u03C9\u2080 with I \u03C9\u2080 = NABQ, so \u03C9\u2080 = NABQ/I.
The kinetic energy then goes into the spring: \xBD I \u03C9\u2080\xB2 = \xBD \u03BA \u03B8_max\xB2, with no damping.
\u03B8_max = \u03C9\u2080 \u221A(I/\u03BA) = (NABQ/I) \u221A(I \u03C0/(2NABi\u2080)) = Q \u221A(\u03C0NAB/(2 I i\u2080)).`},{pn:369,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 28 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbar",st:"Field of a bar magnet",q:"A bar magnet has total length 2l = 20 units and the field point P is at a distance d = 10 units from the centre of the magnet (on the perpendicular bisector, see the figure). If the relative uncertainty of length measurement is 1%, then uncertainty of the magnetic field at point P is",o:["3%","5%","4%","10%"],c:2,s:`Take the bar magnet as two poles \xB1m a distance 2l apart, with m fixed. At P on the perpendicular bisector:
B = (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0)\xB7m(2l)/(d\xB2 + l\xB2)^(3/2).
Every length (d, l, 2l) has the same relative uncertainty of 1%.
The factor 2l contributes 1%. The factor (d\xB2 + l\xB2)^(3/2) is a length cubed, so it contributes 3 \xD7 1% = 3%.
\u0394B/B = 1% + 3% = 4%.
The answer is option (c).`,fig:{w:300,h:160,alt:"A bar magnet with south pole on the left and north pole on the right, total length 2l. Point P lies a distance d above the centre of the magnet, on the perpendicular bisector.",d:[["r",50,105,200,24],["t",66,122,"S"],["t",226,122,"N"],["l",150,105,150,30,"d"],["c",150,26,2.5,"F"],["t",158,28,"P"],["t",142,70,"d","e"],["l",50,146,250,146,"b"],["t",150,158,"2l","m"]]}},{pn:370,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 4 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbar",st:"Dipole in a uniform field",q:"The magnetic moment of a bar magnet is 0.5 A m\xB2. It is suspended in a uniform magnetic field of 8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 T. The work done in rotating it from its most stable to most unstable position is",o:["4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 J","8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 J","16 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 J","zero"],c:1,s:`Potential energy of a dipole: U = \u2212MB cos \u03B8.
Most stable: \u03B8 = 0, U = \u2212MB. Most unstable: \u03B8 = 180\xB0, U = +MB.
Work done = change in U = MB \u2212 (\u2212MB) = 2MB.
W = 2 \xD7 0.5 \xD7 8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 = 8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 J, option (b).`},{pn:371,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 16 Mar \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbar",st:"Field of a bar magnet",q:"A bar magnet of length 14 cm is placed in the magnetic meridian with its north pole pointing towards the geographic north pole. A neutral point is obtained at a distance of 18 cm from the center of the magnet. If B_H = 0.4 G, the magnetic moment of the magnet is (1 G = 10\u207B\u2074 T)",o:["28.80 J T\u207B\xB9","2.880 J T\u207B\xB9","2.880 \xD7 10\xB3 J T\u207B\xB9","2.880 \xD7 10\xB2 J T\u207B\xB9"],c:1,s:`With the magnet's north pole pointing to geographic north, the earth's field (north) is opposed by the magnet's field on its equatorial line, so the neutral points lie on the equatorial line.
On the equatorial line, B = (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0) M/(d\xB2 + l\xB2)^(3/2). Set it equal to B_H.
Half-length l = 7 cm, d = 18 cm: d\xB2 + l\xB2 = 324 + 49 = 373 cm\xB2 = 3.73 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 m\xB2.
(3.73 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2)^(3/2) = 7.20 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB3.
M = B_H (d\xB2 + l\xB2)^(3/2)/10\u207B\u2077 = 0.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 7.20 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3/10\u207B\u2077 = 2.88 J T\u207B\xB9, option (b).`},{pn:372,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbar",st:"Dipole in a uniform field",q:"A small bar magnet placed with its axis at 30\xB0 with an external field of 0.06 T experiences a torque of 0.018 N m. The minimum work required to rotate it from its stable to unstable equilibrium position is",o:["7.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 J","6.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 J","9.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 J","11.7 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 J"],c:0,s:`Torque \u03C4 = MB sin \u03B8, so M = 0.018/(0.06 \xD7 sin 30\xB0) = 0.018/0.03 = 0.6 A m\xB2.
Work from stable (\u03B8 = 0) to unstable (\u03B8 = 180\xB0) is 2MB.
W = 2 \xD7 0.6 \xD7 0.06 = 0.072 J = 7.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 J, option (a).`},{pn:373,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mtrq",st:"Torque on a current loop and magnetic moment",q:"A small circular loop of conducting wire has radius a and carries current I. It is placed in a uniform magnetic field B perpendicular to its plane such that when rotated slightly about its diameter and released, it starts performing simple harmonic motion of time period T. If the mass of the loop is m then",o:["T = \u221A(2m/(IB))","T = \u221A(\u03C0m/(IB))","T = \u221A(\u03C0m/(2IB))","T = \u221A(2\u03C0m/(IB))"],c:3,s:`Magnetic moment of the loop: M = I \u03C0 a\xB2.
The loop turns about its diameter, and its moment of inertia about a diameter is I_d = \xBD m a\xB2.
For a small turn, the restoring torque is M B \u03B8, so T = 2\u03C0 \u221A(I_d/(MB)).
T = 2\u03C0 \u221A((\xBD m a\xB2)/(I \u03C0 a\xB2 B)) = 2\u03C0 \u221A(m/(2\u03C0 I B)).
Square under the root: T = \u221A(4\u03C0\xB2 m/(2\u03C0 I B)) = \u221A(2\u03C0 m/(IB)), option (d).`},{pn:374,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbar",st:"Field of a bar magnet",q:"Two magnetic dipoles X and Y are placed at a separation d, with their axes perpendicular to each other. The dipole moment of Y is twice that of X. A particle of charge q is passing through their mid-point P, at angle \u03B8 = 45\xB0 with the horizontal line, as shown in figure. What would be the magnitude of force on the particle at that instant? (d is much larger than the dimensions of the dipole)",o:["0","(\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0) (2M)/((d/2)\xB3) \xD7 qv","\u221A2 (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0) (M)/((d/2)\xB3) \xD7 qv","(\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0) (M)/((d/2)\xB3) \xD7 qv"],c:0,s:`P is at distance d/2 from each dipole. Find the field of each at P.
P lies on the axis of X, so B_X = (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0) 2M/(d/2)\xB3, along the moment of X (to the right).
P lies on the equatorial line of Y, so B_Y = (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0) M_Y/(d/2)\xB3 = (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0) 2M/(d/2)\xB3, opposite to Y's moment. Y's moment points down (S to N), so B_Y points up.
The two fields are equal and perpendicular, so the resultant is at 45\xB0 above the horizontal line, the same direction as the velocity in the figure.
The magnetic force is q v \xD7 B. The velocity is parallel to B, so sin 0\xB0 = 0.
The force is zero, option (a).`,fig:{w:340,h:170,alt:"Dipole X (moment M) lies horizontally with its south pole on the left and north pole on the right. Dipole Y (moment 2M) lies vertically with south pole on top and north pole at the bottom. P is midway between them, a distance d apart. A charged particle passes P at 45 degrees to the horizontal line.",d:[["r",20,70,70,22],["t",36,86,"S"],["t",68,86,"N"],["t",55,112,"X (M)","m"],["r",270,40,22,90],["t",281,60,"S","m"],["t",281,118,"N","m"],["t",281,148,"Y (2M)","m"],["l",90,81,170,81,"d"],["l",170,81,268,81,"d"],["c",170,81,2.5,"F"],["t",166,98,"P"],["l",170,81,222,29,"a"],["t",198,72,"\u03B8"],["l",20,34,150,34,"d"],["l",50,34,270,34,"b"],["t",160,26,"d","m"]]}},{pn:375,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbar",st:"Dipole in a uniform field",q:"A magnet of total magnetic moment 10\u207B\xB2 \xEE A m\xB2 is placed in a time varying magnetic field, B \xEE (cos \u03C9t) where B = 1 T and \u03C9 = 0.125 rad/s. The work done for reversing the direction of the magnetic moment at t = 1 second, is",o:["0.007 J","0.01 J","0.028 J","0.014 J"],c:2,s:`Note: the printed key says (c) 0.028 J; working gives about 0.0198 J, which is close to 0.02 J and matches none of the options.
Work needed to reverse a dipole in a field B is W = 2MB (from U = \u2212MB to +MB).
At t = 1 s: B(t) = 1 \xD7 cos(0.125 rad) = 0.992 T.
W = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \xD7 0.992 = 0.0198 J.
The printed answer is (c), which I keep as the marked option.`},{pn:376,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mbar",st:"Dipole in a uniform field",q:"A hoop and a solid cylinder of same mass and radius are made of a permanent magnetic material with their magnetic moment parallel to their respective axes. But the magnetic moment of hoop is twice of solid cylinder. They are placed in a uniform magnetic field in such a manner that their magnetic moments make a small angle with the field. If the oscillation periods of hoop and cylinder are T_h and T_c respectively, then",o:["T_h = 0.5 T_c","T_h = 2 T_c","T_h = 1.5 T_c","T_h = T_c"],c:3,s:`For small angle oscillation of a magnet, T = 2\u03C0 \u221A(I/(MB)), where I is the moment of inertia about the axis of rotation (a diameter).
Hoop, mass m, radius R: I_h = \xBD m R\xB2. Solid cylinder (treated as a disc, short compared with R): I_c = \xBC m R\xB2.
M_h = 2 M_c.
T_h \u221D \u221A(\xBD m R\xB2/(2M_c)) = \u221A(m R\xB2/(4M_c)) and T_c \u221D \u221A(\xBC m R\xB2/M_c) = \u221A(m R\xB2/(4M_c)).
They are equal, so T_h = T_c, option (d).
Note: the cylinder must be short (disc-like) for I_c = \xBCmR\xB2; this is the intended assumption.`},{pn:377,y:"JEE Main 2017",tp:"mbar",st:"Dipole in a uniform field",q:"A magnetic needle of magnetic moment 6.7 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 A m\xB2 and moment of inertia 7.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 kg m\xB2 is performing simple harmonic oscillations in a magnetic field of 0.01 T. Time taken for 10 complete oscillations is",o:["6.65 s","8.89 s","6.98 s","8.76 s"],c:0,s:`For small oscillations, T = 2\u03C0 \u221A(I/(MB)).
MB = 6.7 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \xD7 0.01 = 6.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 J.
I/(MB) = 7.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076/6.7 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 = 1.119 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 s\xB2.
\u221A = 0.1058 s, so T = 2\u03C0 \xD7 0.1058 = 0.665 s.
Ten oscillations take 10 \xD7 0.665 = 6.65 s, option (a).`},{pn:378,y:"JEE Main 2017 \xB7 Online",tp:"mbar",st:"Dipole in a uniform field",q:"A magnetic dipole in a constant magnetic field has",o:["zero potential energy when the torque is maximum","minimum potential energy when the torque is maximum","maximum potential energy when the torque is maximum","zero potential energy when the torque is minimum."],c:0,s:`Torque: \u03C4 = MB sin \u03B8. Potential energy: U = \u2212MB cos \u03B8.
The torque is maximum at \u03B8 = 90\xB0. There U = \u2212MB cos 90\xB0 = 0.
So the potential energy is zero when the torque is maximum. At the minimum torque (\u03B8 = 0 or 180\xB0) U is \u2212MB or +MB, not zero.
So option (a).`},{pn:379,y:"JEE Main 2016 \xB7 Online",tp:"mbar",st:"Dipole in a uniform field",q:"A magnetic dipole is acted upon by two magnetic fields which are inclined to each other at an angle of 75\xB0. One of the fields has a magnitude of 15 mT. The dipole attains stable equilibrium at an angle of 30\xB0 with this field. The magnitude of the other field (in mT) is close to",o:["1","11","36","1060"],c:1,s:`The dipole settles along the resultant of the two fields. So the resultant makes 30\xB0 with the 15 mT field B\u2081.
The other field B\u2082 is at 75\xB0 to B\u2081. For the resultant direction: tan 30\xB0 = B\u2082 sin 75\xB0/(B\u2081 + B\u2082 cos 75\xB0).
0.5774 (15 + 0.2588 B\u2082) = 0.9659 B\u2082.
8.660 + 0.1494 B\u2082 = 0.9659 B\u2082, so B\u2082 = 8.660/0.8165 = 10.6 mT.
That is close to 11 mT, option (b).`},{pn:380,y:"AIEEE 2005",tp:"mbar",st:"Force and torque on magnets",q:"A magnetic needle is kept in a non-uniform magnetic field. It experiences",o:["a force and a torque","a force but not a torque","a torque but not a force","neither a force nor a torque."],c:0,s:`The two poles of the needle feel forces q\u2098B, equal in size to the pole strength times the local field.
In a non-uniform field the field at the N pole and at the S pole differ, so the two forces do not cancel and there is a net force.
The forces also do not act along the same line unless the needle is along the field, so in general there is a torque as well.
So the needle feels both a force and a torque, option (a).`},{pn:381,y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"mbar",st:"Dipole in a uniform field",q:"The length of a magnet is large compared to its width and breadth. The time period of its oscillation in a vibration magnetometer is 2 s. The magnet is cut along its length into three equal parts and three parts are then placed on each other with their like poles together. The time period of this combination will be",o:["2 s","(2/3) s","(2\u221A3) s","(2/\u221A3) s"],c:1,s:`T = 2\u03C0 \u221A(I/(MB)). Find how M and I change for the stack.
Take the three equal parts as three short pieces of length L/3, each of mass m/3 and pole strength unchanged, so each has moment M/3 (moment = pole strength \xD7 length).
Placed on each other with like poles together, the moments add: M\u2032 = 3 \xD7 M/3 = M.
Moment of inertia of a thin rod about its centre: I = mL\xB2/12. For each piece, (m/3)(L/3)\xB2/12 = I/27. Three pieces together (axis through the centre of the stack): I\u2032 = 3 \xD7 I/27 = I/9.
T\u2032 = T \u221A(I\u2032/I) = T \u221A(1/9) = T/3 = 2/3 s, option (b).
Note: this is the reading that gives the printed key. If the magnet were sliced lengthwise into thin strips of the same length, the stack would give I\u2032 = I and M\u2032 = M, and T\u2032 = T.`},{pn:382,y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"mbar",st:"Dipole in a uniform field",q:"A thin rectangular magnet suspended freely has a period of oscillation equal to T. Now it is broken into two equal halves (each having half of the original length) and one piece is made to oscillate freely in the same field. If its period of oscillation is T\u2032, the ratio T\u2032/T is",o:["1/(2\u221A2)","1/2","2","1/4"],c:1,s:`The piece has half the length, half the mass, and half the pole strength, so its magnetic moment is M\u2032 = M/2.
Moment of inertia of a thin rod about its centre: I = mL\xB2/12. For the piece I\u2032 = (m/2)(L/2)\xB2/12 = I/8.
T\u2032/T = \u221A((I\u2032/I)(M/M\u2032)) = \u221A((1/8)(2)) = \u221A(1/4) = 1/2.
So option (b).`},{pn:383,y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"mmat",st:"Gauss's law for magnetism",q:"The magnetic lines of force inside a bar magnet",o:["are from north-pole to south-pole of the magnet","do not exist","depend upon the area of cross-section of the bar magnet","are from south-pole to north-pole of the magnet."],c:3,s:`Magnetic field lines are closed loops, with no beginning and no end (Gauss's law for magnetism).
Outside the magnet they go from the north pole to the south pole.
To close the loop, they must go back inside the magnet from the south pole to the north pole.
So option (d).`},{pn:384,y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"mbar",st:"Dipole in a uniform field",q:"A magnetic needle lying parallel to a magnetic field requires W units of work to turn it through 60\xB0. The torque needed to maintain the needle in this position will be",o:["\u221A3 W","W","(\u221A3/2) W","2W"],c:0,s:`Work to turn the needle from \u03B8 = 0 to \u03B8 = 60\xB0: W = MB(1 \u2212 cos 60\xB0) = MB/2.
So MB = 2W.
Torque at 60\xB0: \u03C4 = MB sin 60\xB0 = 2W \xD7 \u221A3/2 = \u221A3 W.
So option (a).`},{pn:385,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 26 Aug \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbar",st:"Force and torque on magnets",q:"Two short magnetic dipoles m\u2081 and m\u2082 each having magnetic moment of 1 A m\xB2 are placed at point O and P respectively. The distance between OP is 1 meter. The torque experienced by the magnetic dipole m\u2082 due to the presence of m\u2081 is ______ \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 N m.",n:1,s:`P lies on the equatorial line of m\u2081 (m\u2081 is vertical, OP is horizontal), at distance r = 1 m.
Field of a short dipole on its equatorial line: B = (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0) m\u2081/r\xB3 = 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 1/1\xB3 = 10\u207B\u2077 T, directed opposite to m\u2081 (downward).
m\u2082 is horizontal, so the angle between m\u2082 and this field is 90\xB0.
Torque \u03C4 = m\u2082 B sin90\xB0 = 1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 = 1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 N m.
The blank is 1.`,fig:{w:300,h:170,alt:"Short dipole m1 at O pointing up and short dipole m2 at P pointing to the right, with P one metre to the right of O on the same horizontal line",d:[["l",60,125,60,70,"a"],["t",50,98,"m\u2081","e"],["t",60,148,"O","m"],["l",230,100,230,100],["l",205,100,255,100,"a"],["t",230,88,"m\u2082","m"],["t",230,122,"P","m"],["l",60,100,205,100,"d"],["t",132,114,"1 m","m"]]}},{pn:386,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbar",st:"Dipole in a uniform field",q:"In a uniform magnetic field, the magnetic needle has a magnetic moment 9.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 A m\xB2 and moment of inertia 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 kg m\xB2. If it performs 10 complete oscillations in 5 seconds then the magnitude of the magnetic field is ______ mT. [Take \u03C0\xB2 as 9.85]",n:8,s:`Time period of one oscillation: T = 5 s / 10 = 0.5 s.
For a magnet oscillating in a field, T = 2\u03C0\u221A(I/(mB)), so B = 4\u03C0\xB2I/(mT\xB2).
B = 4 \xD7 9.85 \xD7 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 / (9.85 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \xD7 0.25).
The 9.85 cancels: B = (4 \xD7 5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076)/(0.25 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2) = 20 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076/(2.5 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3) = 8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 T.
B = 8 mT.`},{pn:387,y:"IIT-JEE 1979",tp:"mbar",st:"Field of a bar magnet",q:"A bar magnet is placed with its north pole pointing north and its south pole pointing south. Draw a figure to show the location of neutral points.",a:"There are two neutral points, on the east-west line through the centre of the magnet (its equatorial line), one on each side, at equal distances d from the centre. For a short magnet, d = (\u03BC\u2080M/(4\u03C0B_H))^(1/3), where M is the magnetic moment and B_H the horizontal field of the earth.",s:`A neutral point is where the magnet's field exactly cancels the earth's horizontal field.
In the solution figure the earth's horizontal field points towards geographic north.
On the axial line of the magnet the field points along M (north on one side, south on the other). On the equatorial line it points opposite to M, that is towards the south.
Along the east-west line (the equatorial line of this magnet) the magnet's field points south while the earth's field points north, so they can cancel.
They cancel where (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0) M/d\xB3 = B_H. This gives one point on the east and one on the west.
On the north-south line (the axis) the magnet's field points north on both sides, the same way as the earth's field, so they add and there is no neutral point there.
The solution figure marks the two neutral points on the east-west line.`,sfig:{w:300,h:200,alt:"A vertical bar magnet with its north pole on top and south pole at the bottom; a dashed east-west line through its centre has two neutral points marked with crosses at equal distances on either side; compass directions North, South, East and West are shown",d:[["r",138,60,24,40,"f"],["r",138,100,24,40],["t",150,86,"N","m"],["t",150,126,"S","m"],["l",20,100,280,100,"d"],["l",64,94,76,106],["l",64,106,76,94],["l",224,94,236,106],["l",224,106,236,94],["t",70,124,"neutral point","m"],["t",230,124,"neutral point","m"],["l",150,50,150,16,"a"],["t",150,12,"North","m"],["l",150,150,150,184,"a"],["t",150,198,"South","m"],["t",288,104,"E"],["t",12,104,"W","e"]]}},{pn:388,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmot",st:"Circular and helical motion",q:"An electron with energy 0.1 keV moves at right angle to the earth's magnetic field of 1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 Wb m\u207B\xB2. The frequency of revolution of the electron will be (Take mass of electron = 9.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9 kg)",o:["1.6 \xD7 10\u2075 Hz","5.6 \xD7 10\u2075 Hz","2.8 \xD7 10\u2076 Hz","1.8 \xD7 10\u2076 Hz"],c:2,s:`At right angles to the field the electron moves on a circle: evB = mv\xB2/r.
The frequency f = v/(2\u03C0r) = eB/(2\u03C0m). It does not depend on the energy (0.1 keV is not needed).
f = (1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB9\u2079 \xD7 1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074)/(2\u03C0 \xD7 9.0 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\xB9).
f = 1.6 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2\xB3 / (5.65 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3\u2070) \u2248 2.8 \xD7 10\u2076 Hz.
This is option (c).`},{pn:389,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mbar",st:"Dipole in a uniform field",q:"Two bar magnets oscillate in a horizontal plane in earth's magnetic field with time periods of 3 s and 4 s respectively. If their moments of inertia are in the ratio of 3 : 2, then the ratio of their magnetic moments will be",o:["2 : 1","8 : 3","1 : 3","27 : 16"],c:1,s:`For a magnet oscillating in the earth's field, T = 2\u03C0\u221A(I/(MB_H)), so M = 4\u03C0\xB2I/(B_H T\xB2).
B_H is the same for both, so M \u221D I/T\xB2.
M\u2081/M\u2082 = (I\u2081/I\u2082) \xD7 (T\u2082/T\u2081)\xB2 = (3/2) \xD7 (4/3)\xB2 = (3/2)(16/9).
M\u2081/M\u2082 = 8/3.
This is option (b).`},{pn:390,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mear",st:"Earth's field and dip",q:"A magnet hung at 45\xB0 with magnetic meridian makes an angle of 60\xB0 with the horizontal. The actual value of the angle of dip is",o:["tan\u207B\xB9(\u221A(3/2))","tan\u207B\xB9(\u221A6)","tan\u207B\xB9(\u221A(2/3))","tan\u207B\xB9(1/\u221A2)"],c:0,s:`When a dip circle is not in the meridian but at angle \u03B1 to it, it shows the apparent dip \u03B4\u2032 with tan\u03B4\u2032 = tan\u03B4 / cos\u03B1.
Here \u03B4\u2032 = 60\xB0 and \u03B1 = 45\xB0.
tan\u03B4 = tan\u03B4\u2032 cos\u03B1 = tan60\xB0 \xD7 cos45\xB0 = \u221A3 \xD7 (1/\u221A2) = \u221A(3/2).
So the true dip is \u03B4 = tan\u207B\xB9(\u221A(3/2)).
Reason for the formula: the vertical component V is unchanged, but the horizontal component seen in the tilted plane is only H cos\u03B1, so tan\u03B4\u2032 = V/(H cos\u03B1).
This is option (a).`},{pn:391,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mear",st:"Earth's field and dip",q:"A compass needle of oscillation magnetometer oscillates 20 times per minute at a place P of dip 30\xB0. The number of oscillations per minute become 10 at another place Q of 60\xB0 dip. The ratio of the total magnetic field at the two places (B_Q : B_P) is",o:["\u221A3 : 4","4 : \u221A3","\u221A3 : 2","2 : \u221A3"],c:0,s:`A compass needle swings in a horizontal plane, so only the horizontal component B_H = B cos\u03B4 acts, and the frequency f \u221D \u221AB_H.
So B cos\u03B4 \u221D f\xB2, giving B \u221D f\xB2/cos\u03B4.
Place P: B_P \u221D 20\xB2/cos30\xB0 = 400/(\u221A3/2) = 800/\u221A3.
Place Q: B_Q \u221D 10\xB2/cos60\xB0 = 100/(1/2) = 200.
B_Q/B_P = 200 \xD7 \u221A3/800 = \u221A3/4.
So B_Q : B_P = \u221A3 : 4, option (a).`},{pn:392,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mear",st:"Earth's field and dip",q:"The vertical component of the earth's magnetic field is 6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T at any place where the angle of dip is 37\xB0. The earth's resultant magnetic field at that place will be (Given tan37\xB0 = 3/4)",o:["8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T","6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 T","5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T","1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T"],c:3,s:`The vertical component is B_V = B sin\u03B4.
tan37\xB0 = 3/4 means the sides are 3 and 4 with hypotenuse 5, so sin37\xB0 = 3/5.
B = B_V/sin\u03B4 = 6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 / (3/5) = 10 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075.
B = 1 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T.
This is option (d).`},{pn:393,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 29 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mear",st:"Earth's field and dip",q:"At a certain place the angle of dip is 30\xB0 and the horizontal component of earth's magnetic field is 0.5 G. The earth's total magnetic field (in G), at that certain place, is",o:["1/\u221A3","1/2","\u221A3","1"],c:0,s:`The horizontal component is B_H = B cos\u03B4.
B = B_H/cos\u03B4 = 0.5/cos30\xB0 = 0.5/(\u221A3/2) = 1/\u221A3 G.
This is option (a).`},{pn:394,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mear",st:"Earth's field and dip",q:"At an angle of 30\xB0 to the magnetic meridian, the apparent dip is 45\xB0. Find the true dip.",o:["tan\u207B\xB9(1/\u221A3)","tan\u207B\xB9(\u221A3)","tan\u207B\xB9(\u221A3/2)","tan\u207B\xB9(2/\u221A3)"],c:2,s:`For a dip circle in a plane at angle \u03B1 to the meridian: tan\u03B4\u2032 = tan\u03B4 / cos\u03B1.
tan\u03B4 = tan\u03B4\u2032 cos\u03B1 = tan45\xB0 \xD7 cos30\xB0 = 1 \xD7 \u221A3/2.
True dip \u03B4 = tan\u207B\xB9(\u221A3/2).
This is option (c).`},{pn:395,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 22 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mear",st:"Earth's field and dip",q:"Choose the correct option.",o:["True dip is always greater than the apparent dip.","True dip is less than the apparent dip.","True dip is not mathematically related to apparent dip.","True dip is always equal to apparent dip."],c:1,s:`The two are related by tan\u03B4\u2032 = tan\u03B4 / cos\u03B1, where \u03B1 is the angle between the dip circle's plane and the meridian.
Since cos\u03B1 is less than 1 (for \u03B1 other than 0\xB0), tan\u03B4\u2032 is greater than tan\u03B4, so \u03B4\u2032 > \u03B4.
So the true dip is less than the apparent dip (equal only when \u03B1 = 0\xB0).
This is option (b).`},{pn:396,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mear",st:"Earth's field and dip",q:"A magnetic compass needle oscillates 30 times per minute at a place where the dip is 45\xB0, and 40 times per minute where the dip is 30\xB0. If B\u2081 and B\u2082 are respectively the total magnetic field due to the earth at the two places, then the ratio B\u2081/B\u2082 is best given by",o:["0.7","3.6","1.8","2.2"],c:0,s:`The compass needle oscillates in a horizontal plane, so f \u221D \u221A(B cos\u03B4), which gives B \u221D f\xB2/cos\u03B4.
B\u2081 \u221D 30\xB2/cos45\xB0 = 900/0.7071 = 1272.8.
B\u2082 \u221D 40\xB2/cos30\xB0 = 1600/0.8660 = 1847.5.
B\u2081/B\u2082 = 1272.8/1847.5 \u2248 0.69.
The nearest option is 0.7, option (a).`},{pn:397,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mear",st:"Earth's field and dip",q:"At some location on earth the horizontal component of earth's magnetic field is 18 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 T. At this location, magnetic needle of length 0.12 m and pole strength 1.8 A m is suspended from its mid-point using a thread, it makes 45\xB0 angle with horizontal in equilibrium. To keep this needle horizontal, the vertical force that should be applied at one of its ends is",o:["1.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N","3.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N","6.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N","1.3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 N"],c:2,s:`The needle dips at 45\xB0, so tan45\xB0 = V/H, which gives V = H = 18 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 T.
The vertical field acts on the two poles with forces pV, opposite in direction on the two ends. They form a couple that tilts the needle.
For a horizontal needle, this couple has moment pV \xD7 L = 1.8 \xD7 18 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 \xD7 0.12 = 3.89 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 N m.
A single vertical force F at one end, with the needle pivoted at its mid-point, has moment F \xD7 (L/2).
F \xD7 0.06 = 3.89 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076, so F = 6.5 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 N.
This is option (c).`},{pn:398,y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"mbar",st:"Field of a bar magnet",q:"A short bar magnet is placed in the magnetic meridian of the earth with north pole pointing north. Neutral points are found at a distance of 30 cm from the magnet on the East - West line, drawn through the middle point of the magnet. The magnetic moment of the magnet in A m\xB2 is close to (Given \u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0 = 10\u207B\u2077 in SI units and B_H = Horizontal component of earth's magnetic field = 3.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 Tesla.)",o:["9.7","4.9","19.4","14.6"],c:0,s:`The East-West line is the equatorial line of the magnet. A neutral point is where the magnet's equatorial field cancels B_H.
Equatorial field of a short magnet: B = (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0) M/d\xB3.
Set (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0) M/d\xB3 = B_H: M = B_H d\xB3/10\u207B\u2077.
d = 0.30 m, d\xB3 = 0.027 m\xB3.
M = 3.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 0.027 / 10\u207B\u2077 = 9.72 A m\xB2.
This is close to 9.7, option (a).`},{pn:399,y:"JEE Main 2013",tp:"mbar",st:"Field of a bar magnet",q:"Two short bar magnets of length 1 cm each have magnetic moments 1.20 A m\xB2 and 1.00 A m\xB2 respectively. They are placed on a horizontal table parallel to each other with their N poles pointing towards the South. They have a common magnetic equator and are separated by a distance of 20.0 cm. The value of the resultant horizontal magnetic induction at the mid-point O of the line joining their centres is close to (Horizontal component of earth's magnetic induction is 3.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 Wb/m\xB2)",o:["5.80 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 Wb/m\xB2","3.6 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 Wb/m\xB2","2.56 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 Wb/m\xB2","3.50 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 Wb/m\xB2"],c:2,s:`Point O is on the equatorial line of both magnets, at r = 10 cm = 0.10 m from each.
Equatorial field of a short magnet: B = (\u03BC\u2080/4\u03C0) M/r\xB3, directed opposite to M. Each M points south (from S pole to N pole), so each equatorial field points north.
The earth's horizontal field also points north, so all three add.
B\u2081 = 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 1.20/10\u207B\xB3 = 1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T and B\u2082 = 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 1.00/10\u207B\xB3 = 1.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 T.
Total = 1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 + 1.0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 + 0.36 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 = 2.56 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 Wb/m\xB2.
This is option (c).`},{pn:400,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mear",st:"Earth's field and dip",q:"A compass needle oscillates 20 times per minute at a place where the dip is 30\xB0 and 30 times per minute where the dip is 60\xB0. The ratio of total magnetic field due to the earth at two places respectively is 4/\u221Ax. The value of x is ______.",n:243,s:`The compass needle swings in a horizontal plane, so f \u221D \u221A(B cos\u03B4), giving B \u221D f\xB2/cos\u03B4.
First place: B\u2081 \u221D 20\xB2/cos30\xB0 = 400/(\u221A3/2) = 800/\u221A3.
Second place: B\u2082 \u221D 30\xB2/cos60\xB0 = 900/(1/2) = 1800.
B\u2081/B\u2082 = (800/\u221A3)/1800 = 4/(9\u221A3).
Write it as 4/\u221Ax: 9\u221A3 = \u221A(81 \xD7 3) = \u221A243.
So x = 243.`},{pn:401,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 2 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmat",st:"Hysteresis and permeability",q:"The relationship between the magnetic susceptibility (\u03C7) and the magnetic permeability (\u03BC) is given by: (\u03BC\u2080 is the permeability of free space and \u03BC_r is relative permeability)",o:["\u03C7 = \u03BC_r + 1","\u03C7 = 1 \u2212 \u03BC/\u03BC\u2080","\u03C7 = \u03BC_r/\u03BC\u2080 + 1","\u03C7 = \u03BC/\u03BC\u2080 \u2212 1"],c:3,s:`Relative permeability and susceptibility are related by \u03BC_r = 1 + \u03C7, so \u03C7 = \u03BC_r \u2212 1.
Also \u03BC_r = \u03BC/\u03BC\u2080.
So \u03C7 = \u03BC/\u03BC\u2080 \u2212 1.
This is option (d).`},{pn:402,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 7 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmat",st:"Hysteresis and permeability",q:"The percentage increase in magnetic field (B) when space within a current carrying solenoid is filled with magnesium (magnetic susceptibility \u03C7_Mg = 1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075) is",o:["(5/6) \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 %","(6/5) \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 %","(5/6) \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 %","(5/3) \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 %"],c:1,s:`In vacuum B\u2080 = \u03BC\u2080nI. With the material, B = \u03BC_r \u03BC\u2080nI, where \u03BC_r = 1 + \u03C7.
Fractional increase = (B \u2212 B\u2080)/B\u2080 = \u03BC_r \u2212 1 = \u03C7 = 1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075.
Percentage increase = 1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 100 = 1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 %.
And 1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 = (6/5) \xD7 10\u207B\xB3.
This is option (b).`},{pn:403,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmat",st:"Dia-, para- and ferromagnetism",q:`Paramagnetic substances:
(A) align themselves along the direction of external magnetic field.
(B) attract strongly towards external magnetic field.
(C) has susceptibility little more than zero.
(D) move from a region of strong magnetic field to weak magnetic field.
Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below`,o:["A, B, C, D","A, C only","B, D only","A, B, C only"],c:1,s:`(A) is true: the atomic dipoles of a paramagnet line up with the applied field.
(B) is false: the attraction is weak, not strong (strong attraction is ferromagnetic).
(C) is true: \u03C7 is small and positive.
(D) is false: a paramagnet moves from weak to strong field regions (it is attracted), and the reverse is true for diamagnets.
So only A and C are correct, option (b).`},{pn:404,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 11 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmat",st:"Hysteresis and permeability",q:"The free space inside a current carrying toroid is filled with a material of susceptibility 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2. The percentage increase in the value of magnetic field inside the toroid will be",o:["2%","0.2%","0.1%","1%"],c:0,s:`With the material, B = \u03BC_r B\u2080 with \u03BC_r = 1 + \u03C7 = 1.02.
Fractional increase = \u03BC_r \u2212 1 = \u03C7 = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2.
Percentage increase = 2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2 \xD7 100 = 2%.
This is option (a).`},{pn:405,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 25 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mamp",st:"Solenoid and toroid",q:"A solenoid of 1200 turns is wound uniformly in a single layer on a glass tube 2 m long and 0.2 m in diameter. The magnetic intensity at the center of the solenoid when a current of 2 A flows through it is",o:["2.4 \xD7 10\xB3 A m\u207B\xB9","1.2 \xD7 10\xB3 A m\u207B\xB9","1 A m\u207B\xB9","2.4 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 A m\u207B\xB9"],c:1,s:`The magnetic intensity inside a long solenoid is H = nI, where n is turns per metre.
n = 1200 turns / 2 m = 600 turns/m.
H = 600 \xD7 2 = 1200 A/m.
H = 1.2 \xD7 10\xB3 A m\u207B\xB9.
The diameter is not needed (the tube is long enough compared with it).
This is option (b).`},{pn:406,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 24 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmat",st:"Hysteresis and permeability",q:"The soft-iron is a suitable material for making electromagnet. This is because soft-iron has",o:["low coercivity and high retentivity.","low coercivity and low permeability.","high permeability and low retentivity.","high permeability and high retentivity."],c:2,s:`An electromagnet must magnetise strongly when current flows (high permeability) and lose its magnetism when the current is switched off (low retentivity).
Soft iron has a narrow hysteresis loop: low retentivity and low coercivity, and a high permeability.
Among the options, high permeability with low retentivity is option (c).
Low permeability (b) or high retentivity (a, d) would make a poor electromagnet.`},{pn:407,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 27 Jun \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmat",st:"Hysteresis and permeability",q:"The susceptibility of a paramagnetic material is 99. The permeability of the material in Wb/A-m, is [Permeability of free space \u03BC\u2080 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 Wb/A-m]",o:["4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077","4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074","4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075","4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076"],c:2,s:`Relative permeability \u03BC_r = 1 + \u03C7 = 1 + 99 = 100.
Permeability \u03BC = \u03BC_r \u03BC\u2080 = 100 \xD7 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077.
\u03BC = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 Wb/A-m.
This is option (c).`},{pn:408,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 28 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmat",st:"Hysteresis and permeability",q:"The space inside a straight solenoid is filled with a magnetic material having magnetic susceptibility equal to 1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075. What is fractional increase in the magnetic field inside solenoid with respect to air as medium inside the solenoid?",o:["1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075","1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3","1.8 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3","2.4 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075"],c:0,s:`B with the material = \u03BC_r \u03BC\u2080nI and B in air = \u03BC\u2080nI (air is nearly vacuum).
Fractional increase = (B \u2212 B\u2080)/B\u2080 = \u03BC_r \u2212 1.
\u03BC_r = 1 + \u03C7, so the fractional increase is \u03C7 = 1.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075.
This is option (a).`},{pn:409,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 1 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmat",st:"Dia-, para- and ferromagnetism",q:`Following plots show Magnetization (M) vs Magnetising field (H) and magnetic susceptibility (\u03C7) vs Temperature (T) graph.
Which of the following combination will be represented by a diamagnetic material?`,fig:{w:340,h:285,alt:"Four graphs: (A) magnetization M against H, a straight line of negative slope through the origin; (B) M against H, a straight line of positive slope through the origin; (C) susceptibility \u03C7 against T, a horizontal line below the T axis (constant negative); (D) \u03C7 against T, a curve falling like 1/T above the axis",d:[["l",80,105,80,15,"a"],["l",35,60,135,60,"a"],["t",75,18,"M","e"],["t",134,74,"H","e"],["l",45,25,115,95],["t",80,128,"(A)","m"],["l",250,105,250,15,"a"],["l",205,60,305,60,"a"],["t",245,18,"M","e"],["t",304,74,"H","e"],["l",215,95,285,25],["t",250,128,"(B)","m"],["l",80,245,80,155,"a"],["l",35,200,135,200,"a"],["t",75,158,"\u03C7","e"],["t",134,214,"T","e"],["l",45,225,125,225],["t",80,268,"(C)","m"],["l",250,245,250,155,"a"],["l",205,200,305,200,"a"],["t",245,158,"\u03C7","e"],["t",304,214,"T","e"],["p","M262 160 C264 185 276 197 305 200"],["t",250,268,"(D)","m"]]},o:["(B), (C)","(B), (D)","(A), (D)","(A), (C)"],c:3,s:`A diamagnetic material gets magnetised opposite to the applied field, so M = \u03C7H with \u03C7 negative: the M\u2013H graph is a straight line with negative slope. That is graph (A), not (B).
Diamagnetic susceptibility is small, negative and does not depend on temperature, so the \u03C7\u2013T graph is a horizontal line below the T axis. That is graph (C).
Graph (D), \u03C7 falling like 1/T, is the Curie law of a paramagnetic material.
So the diamagnetic combination is (A) and (C), option (d).`},{pn:410,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 20 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmat",st:"Hysteresis and permeability",q:"The magnetic susceptibility of a material of a rod is 499. Permeability in vacuum is 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 H/m. Absolute permeability of the material of the rod is",o:["\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 H/m","2\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 H/m","3\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 H/m","4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 H/m"],c:1,s:`Relative permeability \u03BC\u1D63 = 1 + \u03C7 = 1 + 499 = 500.
Absolute permeability \u03BC = \u03BC\u2080\u03BC\u1D63 = 4\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 \xD7 500.
= 2000\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2077 = 2\u03C0 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 H/m, option (b).`},{pn:411,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 18 Mar \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmat",st:"Gauss's law for magnetism",q:`Which of the following statements are correct?
(A) Electric monopoles do not exist whereas magnetic monopoles exist.
(B) Magnetic field lines due to a solenoid at its ends and outside cannot be completely straight and confined.
(C) Magnetic field lines are completely confined within a toroid.
(D) Magnetic field lines inside a bar magnet are not parallel.
(E) \u03C7 = \u22121 is the condition for a perfect diamagnetic material, where \u03C7 is its magnetic susceptibility.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below`,o:["(A) and (B) only","(B) and (D) only","(B) and (C) only","(C) and (E) only"],c:3,s:`(A) is wrong: it is the other way round. Isolated electric charges (monopoles) exist, but no isolated magnetic monopole has been found.
(C) is correct: in an ideal toroid the field is entirely inside the winding and the lines are closed circles within it.
(E) is correct: \u03BC\u1D63 = 1 + \u03C7 = 0 for a perfect diamagnet (field is expelled), which gives \u03C7 = \u22121.
Note: key (d) as printed. (B) is also a true statement, but no option lists B, C and E together, so (d) is the accepted answer. (D) is doubtful: inside a bar magnet the lines run roughly parallel from S to N.
So the key is (C) and (E) only, option (d).`},{pn:412,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 24 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmat",st:"Hysteresis and permeability",q:"A soft ferromagnetic material is placed in an external magnetic field. The magnetic domains",o:["decrease in size and changes orientation.","increase in size but no change in orientation.","may increase or decrease in size and change its orientation.","have no relation with external magnetic field."],c:2,s:`A ferromagnet is made of small regions called domains, each magnetised in its own direction.
In an external field, domains pointing along the field grow in size, and domains pointing against it shrink. Domains may also turn towards the field direction.
A soft ferromagnetic material has easily movable domain walls, so both changes happen.
So the domains may increase or decrease in size and change orientation, option (c).`},{pn:413,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Feb \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmat",st:"Dia-, para- and ferromagnetism",q:"In a ferromagnetic material, below the curie temperature, a domain is defined as",o:["a macroscopic region with consecutive magnetic dipoles oriented in opposite direction.","a macroscopic region with randomly oriented magnetic dipoles.","a macroscopic region with saturation magnetization.","a macroscopic region with zero magnetization."],c:2,s:`Below the Curie temperature, neighbouring atomic dipoles in a ferromagnet line up in the same direction by a strong exchange effect.
A domain is a small macroscopic region in which all these dipoles are aligned, so it is magnetised to saturation.
Opposite or random dipoles would give zero magnetisation, which is not a domain.
So the answer is option (c).`},{pn:414,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 2 Sep \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmat",st:"Hysteresis and permeability",q:"Magnetic materials used for making permanent magnets (P) and magnets in a transformer (T) have different properties of the following, which property best matches for the type of magnet required?",o:["T : Large retentivity, small coercivity.","P : Small retentivity, large coercivity.","T : Large retentivity, large coercivity.","P : Large retentivity, large coercivity."],c:3,s:`A permanent magnet must stay magnetised and must resist demagnetisation: large retentivity and large coercivity.
A transformer core is magnetised and demagnetised 50 times per second. It needs a narrow hysteresis loop (small retentivity and small coercivity) to waste little energy.
Only (d) states a correct pair: P with large retentivity and large coercivity.`},{pn:415,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 3 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmat",st:"Dia-, para- and ferromagnetism",q:"A perfectly diamagnetic sphere has a small spherical cavity at its centre, which is filled with a paramagnetic substance. The whole system is placed in a uniform magnetic field B. Then the field inside the paramagnetic substance is",fig:{w:260,h:200,alt:"A large perfectly diamagnetic sphere with a small spherical cavity at its centre; the cavity contains a paramagnetic substance (point P)",d:[["c",130,100,80],["c",130,100,16],["c",130,100,2.5,"F"],["t",130,76,"P","m"]]},o:["B","Zero","much larger than |B| and parallel to B.","much larger than |B| and but opposite to B."],c:1,s:`A perfectly diamagnetic body has \u03C7 = \u22121, so \u03BC\u1D63 = 0: it expels the magnetic field from its own volume and does not let it enter.
The paramagnetic substance sits inside a cavity completely surrounded by this body, so the external field cannot reach it. The field lines go around the sphere.
So the field inside the cavity, and inside the paramagnetic substance, is zero, option (b).`},{pn:416,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 4 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmat",st:"Dia-, para- and ferromagnetism",q:"A paramagnetic sample shows a net magnetisation of 6 A/m when it is placed in an external magnetic field of 0.4 T at a temperature of 4 K. When the sample is placed in an external magnetic field of 0.3 T at a temperature of 24 K, then the magnetisation will be",o:["0.75 A/m","2.25 A/m","4 A/m","1 A/m"],c:0,s:`Curie law: magnetisation M \u221D B/T for a paramagnet.
M\u2082/M\u2081 = (B\u2082/B\u2081) \xD7 (T\u2081/T\u2082) = (0.3/0.4) \xD7 (4/24) = 0.75 \xD7 (1/6) = 1/8.
M\u2082 = 6/8 = 0.75 A/m, option (a).`},{pn:417,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 5 Sep \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmat",st:"Hysteresis and permeability",q:"An iron rod of volume 10\u207B\xB3 m\xB3 and relative permeability 1000 is placed as core in a solenoid with 10 turns/cm. If a current of 0.5 A is passed through the solenoid, then the magnetic moment of the rod will be",o:["50 \xD7 10\xB2 A m\xB2","5 \xD7 10\xB2 A m\xB2","500 \xD7 10\xB2 A m\xB2","0.5 \xD7 10\xB2 A m\xB2"],c:1,s:`Magnetising field H = nI, with n = 10 turns/cm = 1000 turns/m. H = 1000 \xD7 0.5 = 500 A/m.
Magnetisation M = \u03C7H = (\u03BC\u1D63 \u2212 1)H = 999 \xD7 500 \u2248 5 \xD7 10\u2075 A/m.
Magnetic moment = M \xD7 volume = 5 \xD7 10\u2075 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 = 500 A m\xB2 = 5 \xD7 10\xB2 A m\xB2, option (b).`},{pn:418,y:"JEE Main 2020 \xB7 7 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmat",st:"Hysteresis and permeability",q:"The figure gives experimentally measured B vs H variation in a ferromagnetic material. The retentivity, coercivity and saturation, respectively, of the material are",fig:{w:310,h:270,alt:"Hysteresis (B against H) curve of a ferromagnet: B axis marked 1.0, 2.0, \u22121.0, \u22122.0 tesla and H axis marked \u2212150, \u221250, 50, 150 A/m; the loop crosses the B axis at 1.0 T and \u22121.0 T, crosses the H axis at \xB150 A/m and levels off near 1.5 T",d:[["l",150,250,150,22,"a"],["l",50,150,285,150,"a"],["t",142,28,"B (T)","e"],["t",300,142,"H (A/m)","e"],["p","M240 67.5 C186 67.5 159 95 150 95 C132 106 120 136.3 120 150 C117 172 78 232.5 60 232.5","d"],["p","M60 232.5 C114 232.5 141 205 150 205 C168 194 180 163.8 180 150 C183 128 222 67.5 240 67.5","d"],["p","M150 150 C160.8 108.8 172.5 84 204 71.6"],["l",147,95,153,95],["t",134,92,"1.0","es"],["l",147,40,153,40],["t",144,44,"2.0","es"],["l",147,205,153,205],["t",134,198,"\u22121.0","es"],["l",147,260,153,260],["t",138,264,"\u22122.0","es"],["l",60,147,60,153],["t",60,142,"\u2212150","ms"],["l",120,147,120,153],["t",106,142,"\u221250","ms"],["l",180,147,180,153],["t",193,166,"50","ms"],["l",240,147,240,153],["t",240,166,"150","ms"],["t",144,164,"O","e"]]},o:["1.0 T, 50 A/m and 1.5 T","1.5 T, 50 A/m and 1.0 T","150 A/m, 1.0 T and 1.5 T","1.5 T, 50 A/m and 1.0 T"],c:0,s:`Note: the printed key in the book list is (a); the choices (b) and (d) are printed identically, so only (a) fits the graph.
Retentivity is the flux density left when the field H is brought back to zero: the loop cuts the B axis at 1.0 T.
Coercivity is the reverse field needed to bring B to zero: the loop cuts the H axis at 50 A/m.
Saturation is the highest value B levels off at: about 1.5 T.
So (1.0 T, 50 A/m, 1.5 T), option (a).`},{pn:419,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 9 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmat",st:"Hysteresis and permeability",q:"A bar magnet is demagnetized by inserting it inside a solenoid of length 0.2 m, 100 turns, and carrying a current of 5.2 A. The coercivity of the bar magnet is",o:["520 A/m","1200 A/m","2600 A/m","285 A/m"],c:2,s:`The reverse field that just demagnetises the magnet is its coercivity: H_c = nI.
n = 100/0.2 = 500 turns/m.
H_c = 500 \xD7 5.2 = 2600 A/m, option (c).`},{pn:420,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 11 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmat",st:"Dia-, para- and ferromagnetism",q:"A paramagnetic substance in the form of a cube with sides 1 cm has a magnetic dipole moment of 20 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076 J/T when a magnetic intensity of 60 \xD7 10\xB3 A/m is applied. Its magnetic susceptibility is",o:["3.3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2","4.3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2","3.3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074","2.3 \xD7 10\u207B\xB2"],c:2,s:`Magnetisation = dipole moment per unit volume. V = (1 cm)\xB3 = 10\u207B\u2076 m\xB3.
M = (20 \xD7 10\u207B\u2076)/10\u207B\u2076 = 20 A/m.
Susceptibility \u03C7 = M/H = 20/(60 \xD7 10\xB3) = 3.3 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074, option (c).`},{pn:421,y:"JEE Main 2019 \xB7 12 Jan \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmat",st:"Dia-, para- and ferromagnetism",q:"A paramagnetic material has 10\xB2\u2078 atoms/m\xB3. Its magnetic susceptibility at temperature 350 K is 2.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074. Its susceptibility at 300 K is",o:["3.267 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074","3.672 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074","2.672 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074","3.726 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074"],c:0,s:`Curie law: for a paramagnet, \u03C7 \u221D 1/T (the number of atoms is the same and does not matter).
\u03C7\u2082 = \u03C7\u2081 \xD7 (T\u2081/T\u2082) = 2.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 (350/300).
= 2.8 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074 \xD7 1.1667 = 3.267 \xD7 10\u207B\u2074, option (a).`},{pn:422,y:"JEE Main 2018 \xB7 Online",tp:"mmat",st:"Hysteresis and permeability",q:"The B-H curve for a ferromagnet is shown in the figure. The ferromagnet is placed inside a long solenoid with 1000 turns/cm. The current that should be passed in the solenoid to demagnetise the ferromagnet completely is",fig:{w:310,h:260,alt:"B\u2013H curve of a ferromagnet: B axis in tesla marked 1.0, 2.0, \u22121.0, \u22122.0 and H axis in A/m marked \u2212200, \u2212100, 100, 200; the loop crosses the H axis at \xB1100 A/m",d:[["l",150,245,150,15,"a"],["l",45,130,265,130,"a"],["t",142,22,"B (T)","e"],["t",300,122,"H (A/m)","e"],["p","M240 29.2 C186 29.2 159 34 150 34 C123 53.2 105 106 105 130 C100.5 168.4 78 230.8 60 230.8","d"],["p","M60 230.8 C114 230.8 141 226 150 226 C177 206.8 195 154 195 130 C199.5 91.6 222 29.2 240 29.2","d"],["l",147,82,153,82],["t",144,86,"1.0","es"],["l",147,34,153,34],["t",158,26,"2.0","s"],["l",147,178,153,178],["t",144,182,"\u22121.0","es"],["l",147,226,153,226],["t",138,244,"\u22122.0","es"],["l",60,127,60,133],["t",60,122,"\u2212200","ms"],["l",105,127,105,133],["t",92,122,"\u2212100","ms"],["l",195,127,195,133],["t",209,146,"100","ms"],["l",240,127,240,133],["t",240,146,"200","ms"],["t",144,144,"O","e"]]},o:["1 mA","20 \u03BCA","2 mA","40 \u03BCA"],c:0,s:`To demagnetise, we need a reverse field equal to the coercivity, that is the H at which B = 0 on the loop.
From the graph the loop crosses the H axis at 100 A/m, so H_c = 100 A/m.
H = nI with n = 1000 turns/cm = 10\u2075 turns/m, so I = H/n = 100/10\u2075 = 10\u207B\xB3 A.
I = 1 mA, option (a).`},{pn:423,y:"JEE Main 2016",tp:"mmat",st:"Hysteresis and permeability",q:"Hysteresis loops for two magnetic materials A and B are given below: These materials are used to make magnets for electric generators, transformer core and electromagnet core. Then it is proper to use :",fig:{w:340,h:190,alt:"Two hysteresis loops: loop A is wide and almost rectangular (large coercivity and retentivity); loop B is thin and slanted (small coercivity and retentivity)",d:[["l",80,140,80,20,"a"],["l",30,80,135,80,"a"],["t",74,24,"B","e"],["t",135,94,"H","e"],["p","M100 46 L100 80 L100 112 M60 114 L60 80 L60 48 M100 46 H62 M60 114 H98"],["t",80,152,"(A)","m"],["l",250,140,250,20,"a"],["l",200,80,305,80,"a"],["t",244,24,"B","e"],["t",305,94,"H","e"],["p","M262 52 C256 56 252 66 249 80 C246 94 242 106 234 110 C228 112 224 112 222 112 M222 112 C228 110 236 98 246 80 C252 66 258 54 268 50"],["t",250,152,"(B)","m"]]},o:["A for electric generators and transformers.","A for electromagnets and B for electric generators.","A for transformers and B for electric generators.","B for electromagnets and transformers."],c:3,s:`Loop A is wide and almost rectangular: large retentivity and large coercivity. This suits a permanent magnet (the magnet of a generator).
Loop B is narrow and thin: small coercivity and small loop area, so little energy is lost in each cycle. This suits cores that are magnetised again and again: transformer cores and electromagnets.
So B should be used for electromagnets and transformers, option (d).`},{pn:424,y:"JEE Main 2015 \xB7 Online",tp:"mamp",st:"Solenoid and toroid",q:"A 25 cm long solenoid has radius 2 cm and 500 total number of turns. It carries a current of 15 A. If it is equivalent to a magnet of the same size and magnetisation M (magnetic moment/volume), then |M| is",o:["3\u03C0 A m\u207B\xB9","30000 A m\u207B\xB9","300 A m\u207B\xB9","30000\u03C0 A m\u207B\xB9"],c:1,s:`Magnetic moment of the solenoid: m = N I A, where A is the cross-section area.
Volume of the solenoid: V = A L.
M = m/V = N I A/(A L) = N I/L = 500 \xD7 15/0.25 = 30000 A/m.
The area cancels, so the radius is not needed. Option (b).`},{pn:425,y:"JEE Main 2014",tp:"mmat",st:"Hysteresis and permeability",q:"The coercivity of a small magnet where the ferromagnet gets demagnetized is 3 \xD7 10\xB3 A m\u207B\xB9. The current required to be passed in a solenoid of length 10 cm and number of turns 100, so that the magnet gets demagnetized when inside the solenoid, is",o:["6 A","30 mA","60 mA","3 A"],c:3,s:`The reverse field needed equals the coercivity: H = nI = 3 \xD7 10\xB3 A/m.
n = 100/0.1 m = 1000 turns/m.
I = H/n = 3000/1000 = 3 A, option (d).`},{pn:426,y:"JEE Main 2014 \xB7 Online",tp:"mmat",st:"Dia-, para- and ferromagnetism",q:"Three identical bars A, B and C are made of different magnetic materials. When kept in a uniform magnetic field, the field lines around them look as follows. Make the correspondence of these bars with their material being diamagnetic (D), ferromagnetic (F) and paramagnetic (P)",fig:{w:320,h:230,alt:"Magnetic field lines near three identical bars in a uniform field: around A the lines crowd into the bar, around B they bend away from it, around C they bend into it only slightly",d:[["r",45,51,60,18],["t",75,64,"A","m"],["p","M5 12 C25 12 35 54 45 54","a"],["p","M105 54 C115 54 125 12 145 12","a"],["p","M5 34.4 C25 34.4 35 56.8 45 56.8","a"],["p","M105 56.8 C115 56.8 125 34.4 145 34.4","a"],["p","M5 60 C25 60 35 60 45 60","a"],["p","M105 60 C115 60 125 60 145 60","a"],["p","M5 85.6 C25 85.6 35 63.2 45 63.2","a"],["p","M105 63.2 C115 63.2 125 85.6 145 85.6","a"],["p","M5 108 C25 108 35 66 45 66","a"],["p","M105 66 C115 66 125 108 145 108","a"],["r",45,161,60,18],["t",75,174,"B","m"],["p","M5 142 C40 142 45 126 75 126 C105 126 110 142 145 142","a"],["p","M5 156 C40 156 45 148 75 148 C105 148 110 156 145 156","a"],["p","M5 184 C40 184 45 192 75 192 C105 192 110 184 145 184","a"],["p","M5 198 C40 198 45 214 75 214 C105 214 110 198 145 198","a"],["r",205,106,60,18],["t",235,119,"C","m"],["p","M165 76 C185 76 195 94 205 94","a"],["p","M265 94 C275 94 285 76 305 76","a"],["p","M165 94.2 C185 94.2 195 103.8 205 103.8","a"],["p","M265 103.8 C275 103.8 285 94.2 305 94.2","a"],["p","M165 115 C185 115 195 115 205 115","a"],["p","M265 115 C275 115 285 115 305 115","a"],["p","M165 135.8 C185 135.8 195 126.2 205 126.2","a"],["p","M265 126.2 C275 126.2 285 135.8 305 135.8","a"],["p","M165 154 C185 154 195 136 205 136","a"],["p","M265 136 C275 136 285 154 305 154","a"]]},o:["A \u2194 D, B \u2194 P, C \u2194 F","A \u2194 F, B \u2194 D, C \u2194 P","A \u2194 F, B \u2194 P, C \u2194 D","A \u2194 P, B \u2194 F, C \u2194 D"],c:1,s:`A diamagnetic material pushes the field lines out of itself, so the lines bend away from the bar: this is B.
A ferromagnet has very large permeability and draws in many lines, so lines crowd strongly into the bar: this is A.
A paramagnetic material has small positive susceptibility, so it draws in the lines only slightly: this is C.
So A \u2194 F, B \u2194 D, C \u2194 P, option (b).`},{pn:427,y:"AIEEE 2008",tp:"mmat",st:"Dia-, para- and ferromagnetism",q:"Relative permittivity and permeability of a material are \u03B5_r and \u03BC_r, respectively. Which of the following values of these quantities are allowed for a diamagnetic material?",o:["\u03B5_r = 1.5, \u03BC_r = 1.5","\u03B5_r = 0.5, \u03BC_r = 1.5","\u03B5_r = 1.5, \u03BC_r = 0.5","\u03B5_r = 0.5, \u03BC_r = 0.5"],c:2,s:`For any material, \u03B5_r = K \u2265 1 (a dielectric is never less than vacuum). So \u03B5_r = 0.5 is not allowed, which removes (b) and (d).
A diamagnetic material has negative \u03C7, so \u03BC_r = 1 + \u03C7 is slightly below 1.
Only \u03B5_r = 1.5 and \u03BC_r = 0.5 fits both conditions, option (c).`},{pn:428,y:"AIEEE 2006",tp:"mmat",st:"Dia-, para- and ferromagnetism",q:"Needles N\u2081, N\u2082 and N\u2083 are made of a ferromagnetic, a paramagnetic and a diamagnetic substance respectively. A magnet when brought close to them will",o:["attract all three of them","attract N\u2081 and N\u2082 strongly but repel N\u2083","attract N\u2081 strongly, N\u2082 weakly and repel N\u2083 weakly","attract N\u2081 strongly, but repel N\u2082 and N\u2083 weakly"],c:2,s:`Ferromagnetic material: strongly attracted.
Paramagnetic material: weakly attracted (small positive susceptibility).
Diamagnetic material: weakly repelled (small negative susceptibility).
So N\u2081 strongly attracted, N\u2082 weakly attracted, N\u2083 weakly repelled, option (c).`},{pn:429,y:"AIEEE 2004",tp:"mmat",st:"Hysteresis and permeability",q:"The materials suitable for making electromagnets should have",o:["high retentivity and high coercivity","low retentivity and low coercivity","high retentivity and low coercivity","low retentivity and high coercivity"],c:1,s:`An electromagnet is switched on and off, so the core must be magnetised easily and lose its magnetism when the current stops.
Easy magnetising and demagnetising needs low coercivity, and losing the magnetism when the current stops needs low retentivity.
So low retentivity and low coercivity (soft iron), option (b).`},{pn:430,y:"AIEEE 2003",tp:"mmat",st:"Dia-, para- and ferromagnetism",q:"Curie temperature is the temperature above which",o:["a ferromagnetic material becomes paramagnetic","a paramagnetic material becomes diamagnetic","a ferromagnetic material becomes diamagnetic","a paramagnetic material becomes ferromagnetic."],c:0,s:`Ferromagnetism comes from domains in which the dipoles are lined up.
Heating above the Curie temperature shakes this alignment apart: the domains are destroyed.
The material then behaves like a paramagnet (\u03C7 \u221D 1/T), so a ferromagnetic material becomes paramagnetic, option (a).`},{pn:431,y:"JEE Main 2024 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmat",st:"Hysteresis and permeability",q:"The coercivity of a magnet is 5 \xD7 10\xB3 A/m. The amount of current required to be passed in a solenoid of length 30 cm and the number of turns 150, so that the magnet gets demagnetised when inside the solenoid is _____ A.",n:10,s:`The solenoid must give a reverse field equal to the coercivity: nI = 5 \xD7 10\xB3 A/m.
n = 150/0.30 = 500 turns/m.
I = 5000/500 = 10 A.`},{pn:432,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Jan \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmat",st:"Hysteresis and permeability",q:"The current required to be passed through a solenoid of 15 cm length and 60 turns in order to demagnetise a bar magnet of magnetic intensity 2.4 \xD7 10\xB3 Am\u207B\xB9 is _____ A.",n:6,s:`To demagnetise, the solenoid field must equal the magnetic intensity H of the magnet: nI = 2.4 \xD7 10\xB3 A/m.
n = 60/0.15 = 400 turns/m.
I = 2400/400 = 6 A.`},{pn:433,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 31 Aug \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmat",st:"Hysteresis and permeability",q:"A long solenoid with 1000 turns/m has a core material with relative permeability 500 and volume 10\xB3 cm\xB3. If the core material is replaced by another material having relative permeability of 750 with same volume maintaining same current of 0.75 A in the solenoid, the fractional change in the magnetic moment of the core would be approximately (x/499). Find the value of x.",n:250,s:`Magnetic moment of the core = M V, with magnetisation M = \u03C7 H = (\u03BC\u1D63 \u2212 1) n I. The volume, n and I are unchanged.
So the moment is proportional to (\u03BC\u1D63 \u2212 1): 499 for the first material and 749 for the second.
Fractional change = (749 \u2212 499)/499 = 250/499.
So x = 250.`},{pn:434,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 25 Jul \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmat",st:"Dia-, para- and ferromagnetism",q:"The value of aluminium susceptibility is 2.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075. The percentage increase in the magnetic field if space within a current carrying toroid is filled with Aluminium is x/10\u2074. Then the value of x is _____.",n:22,s:`With vacuum inside, B\u2080 = \u03BC\u2080 n I. With aluminium inside, B = \u03BC\u2080 \u03BC\u1D63 n I = (1 + \u03C7) B\u2080.
Fractional increase = (B \u2212 B\u2080)/B\u2080 = \u03C7 = 2.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075.
Percentage increase = 2.2 \xD7 10\u207B\u2075 \xD7 100 = 2.2 \xD7 10\u207B\xB3 = 22/10\u2074.
So x = 22.`},{pn:435,y:"IIT-JEE 2010",tp:"mmat",st:"Dia-, para- and ferromagnetism",q:`Comprehension: Electrical resistance of certain materials, known as superconductors, changes abruptly from a nonzero value to zero as their temperature is lowered below a critical temperature T_C(0). An interesting property of superconductors is that their critical temperature becomes smaller than T_C(0) if they are placed in a magnetic field, i.e., the critical temperature T_C(B) is a function of the magnetic field strength B. The dependence of T_C(B) on B is shown in the figure.
In the graphs below, the resistance R of a superconductor is shown as a function of its temperature T for two different magnetic fields B\u2081 (solid line) and B\u2082 (dashed line). If B\u2082 is larger than B\u2081, which of the following graphs shows the correct variation of R with T in these fields?`,fig:{w:360,h:470,alt:"Top: critical temperature T_C(B) against B, falling from T_C(0) on the temperature axis as B increases. Below: four R\u2013T graphs for fields B\u2081 (solid) and B\u2082 (dashed): in (1) B\u2082 becomes superconducting at a lower temperature than B\u2081; in (2) the two fall at the same temperature and B\u2082 rises higher; in (3) and (4) B\u2082 falls at a higher temperature than B\u2081",d:[["l",100,180,100,20,"a"],["l",100,180,260,180,"a"],["t",96,24,"T_C(B)","e"],["t",260,196,"B","e"],["t",94,196,"O","e"],["l",96,50,100,50],["t",92,54,"T_C(0)","e"],["p","M100 50 C190 52 232 80 236 180"],["l",30,300,30,252,"a"],["l",30,300,118,300,"a"],["t",26,256,"R","e"],["t",116,313,"T","e"],["t",24,312,"O","e"],["l",75,300,75,284,"d"],["p","M75 284 C80 280 84 278 88 276","d"],["l",90,300,90,282],["l",90,282,115,262],["t",66,290,"B\u2082","e","s"],["t",96,296,"B\u2081","s"],["t",60,332,"(1)","m"],["l",210,300,210,252,"a"],["l",210,300,298,300,"a"],["t",206,256,"R","e"],["t",296,313,"T","e"],["t",204,312,"O","e"],["l",252,300,252,262,"d"],["p","M252 262 C258 254 264 248 272 244","d"],["l",254,300,254,284],["l",254,284,270,274],["t",246,258,"B\u2082","e","s"],["t",256,296,"B\u2081","s"],["t",240,332,"(2)","m"],["l",30,430,30,382,"a"],["l",30,430,118,430,"a"],["t",26,386,"R","e"],["t",116,443,"T","e"],["t",24,442,"O","e"],["l",75,430,75,412],["l",75,412,92,402],["t",69,426,"B\u2081","e","s"],["l",90,430,90,396,"d"],["p","M90 396 C100 386 108 380 116 376","d"],["t",96,426,"B\u2082","s"],["t",60,462,"(3)","m"],["l",210,430,210,382,"a"],["l",210,430,298,430,"a"],["t",206,386,"R","e"],["t",296,443,"T","e"],["t",204,442,"O","e"],["l",245,430,245,414],["l",245,414,262,404],["t",239,426,"B\u2081","e","s"],["l",255,430,255,420,"d"],["l",255,420,274,410,"d"],["t",261,426,"B\u2082","s"],["t",240,462,"(4)","m"]]},o:["Graph (1)","Graph (2)","Graph (3)","Graph (4)"],c:0,s:`The T_C(B) graph falls as B rises: a larger field gives a lower critical temperature.
B\u2082 > B\u2081, so T_C(B\u2082) < T_C(B\u2081): the resistance of the superconductor in the field B\u2082 drops to zero at a lower temperature than for B\u2081.
On the temperature axis, the sudden drop of the dashed line (B\u2082) must therefore be to the left of that of the solid line (B\u2081).
Only graph (1) has the dashed B\u2082 jump to the left of the B\u2081 jump. In (2) the B\u2082 jump starts at the same temperature as B\u2081, and in (3) and (4) it is to the right of B\u2081.
Option (a).`},{pn:436,y:"IIT-JEE 2010",tp:"mmat",st:"Dia-, para- and ferromagnetism",q:"A superconductor has T_C(0) = 100 K. When a magnetic field of 7.5 Tesla is applied, its T_C decreases to 75 K. For this material one can definitely say that when",o:["B = 5 Tesla, T_C(B) = 80 K","B = 5 Tesla, 75 K < T_C(B) < 100 K","B = 10 Tesla, 75 K < T_C(B) < 100 K","B = 10 Tesla, T_C(B) = 70 K"],c:1,s:`T_C(B) keeps falling as B rises: the graph goes down from 100 K at B = 0 to 75 K at B = 7.5 T.
For B = 5 T, which lies between 0 and 7.5 T, T_C lies between 100 K and 75 K. So (b) is certain. The exact value (80 K in (a)) cannot be fixed without the curve.
For B = 10 T, which is larger than 7.5 T, T_C must be below 75 K, which rules out (c); the exact 70 K in (d) cannot be fixed either.
Option (b).`},{pn:437,y:"JEE Main 2025 \xB7 7 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmat",st:"Gauss's law for magnetism",q:`Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion (A) and the other is labelled as Reason (R).
Assertion (A): Magnetic monopoles do not exist.
Reason (R): Magnetic field lines are continuous and form closed loops.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:`,o:["Both (A) and (R) are correct and (R) is the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is not correct but (R) is correct","Both (A) and (R) are correct but (R) is not the correct explanation of (A)","(A) is correct but (R) is not correct"],c:0,s:`Gauss law for magnetism: the net flux through any closed surface is zero, \u222EB\xB7dA = 0.
This is because field lines never begin or end: they form closed loops. A magnetic monopole would act as a source or sink of lines.
Since the lines are continuous and closed, there is no source, which is exactly why monopoles do not exist as far as we know.
Both statements are true and R explains A, option (a).`},{pn:438,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 8 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmat",st:"Hysteresis and permeability",q:`Given below are two statements: one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.
Assertion A: Electromagnets are made of soft iron.
Reason R: Soft iron has high permeability and low retentivity.
In the light of above statements choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both A and R are correct and R is NOT the correct explanation of A.","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A.","A is not correct but R is correct.","A is correct but R is not correct."],c:1,s:`Assertion A is true: electromagnet cores are made of soft iron.
Reason R is true: soft iron has high permeability, so it is magnetised strongly by a small current, and low retentivity, so it loses its magnetism when the current is switched off.
These are exactly the properties an electromagnet needs, so R explains A: option (b).`},{pn:439,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 10 Apr \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmat",st:"Dia-, para- and ferromagnetism",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: For diamagnetic substance, \u22121 \u2264 \u03C7 < 0, where \u03C7 is the magnetic susceptibility.
Statement II: Diamagnetic substances when placed in an external magnetic field, tend to move from stronger to weaker part of the field.
In the light of above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Statement I is correct but Statement II is false.","Both statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true."],c:0,s:`Statement I: \u03BC\u1D63 = 1 + \u03C7 cannot be negative, so \u03C7 \u2265 \u22121, and a diamagnet has \u03C7 < 0. True.
Statement II: a diamagnet gets magnetised opposite to the field, so it is repelled from the stronger field region and moves towards the weaker part. True.
Both are true, option (a).`},{pn:440,y:"JEE Main 2023 \xB7 12 Apr \xB7 Shift 1",tp:"mmat",st:"Dia-, para- and ferromagnetism",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement I: The diamagnetic property depends on temperature.
Statement II: The induced magnetic dipole moment in a diamagnetic sample is always opposite to the magnetizing field.
In the light of given statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["Statement I is correct but Statement II is false.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is true."],c:3,s:`Statement I: diamagnetism comes from the induced change in electron orbits and does not depend on temperature (unlike paramagnetism). So I is false.
Statement II: the induced moment of a diamagnetic sample is always opposite to the field. True.
So I is incorrect but II is true, option (d).`},{pn:441,y:"JEE Main 2022 \xB7 25 Jun \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmat",st:"Dia-, para- and ferromagnetism",q:`Given below are two statements:
Statement-I: Susceptibilities of paramagnetic and ferromagnetic substances increase with decrease in temperature.
Statement-II: Diamagnetism is a result of orbital motions of electrons developing magnetic moments opposite to the applied magnetic field.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below`,o:["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are true.","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are false.","Statement-I is true but Statement-II is false.","Statement-I is false but Statement-II is true."],c:0,s:`Statement I: by Curie law (paramagnets) and Curie\u2013Weiss law (ferromagnets above T_C), \u03C7 \u221D 1/T, so \u03C7 increases as the temperature falls. True.
Statement II: in an applied field the orbital motion of electrons changes so as to produce a moment opposite to the field. This is diamagnetism. True.
Both are true, option (a).`},{pn:442,y:"JEE Main 2021 \xB7 22 Jul \xB7 Shift 2",tp:"mmat",st:"Dia-, para- and ferromagnetism",q:`Statement-I: The ferromagnetic property depends on temperature. At high temperature, ferromagnet becomes paramagnet.
Statement-II: At high temperature, the domain wall area of a ferromagnetic substance increases.`,o:["Both Statement-I and Statement-II are true.","Both Statement-I and Statement-II are false.","Statement-I is true but Statement-II is false.","Statement-I is false but Statement-II is true."],c:2,s:`Statement I is true: above the Curie temperature a ferromagnet turns into a paramagnet.
Statement II: heating destroys the ordered domains; the alignment inside them is lost, so there are no domains with walls and the domain wall area does not increase. False.
So I is true and II is false, option (c).`},{pn:443,y:"IIT-JEE 2008",tp:"mtrq",st:"Moving-coil galvanometer, ammeter and voltmeter",q:`Statement-1: The sensitivity of a moving coil galvanometer is increased by placing a suitable magnetic material as a core inside the coil.
Statement-2: Soft iron has a high magnetic permeability and cannot be easily magnetized or demagnetized.`,o:["Statements-1 and 2 are true and statement-2 is a correct explanation for statement-1","Statements-1 and 2 are true and statement-2 is not a correct explanation for statement-1","Statement-1 is true, statement-2 is false","Statement-1 is false, statement-2 is true."],c:2,s:`Statement 1: a soft iron core inside the coil concentrates the field lines and makes B larger in the gap, so the torque per unit current, NBA, grows and the sensitivity increases. True.
Statement 2: soft iron does have high permeability, but it is easily magnetised and demagnetised (low retentivity and coercivity). The second half of the statement is false.
So statement 1 is true and statement 2 is false, option (c).`}]})();
